1 OCCU aka Cellach Cualann; King of Laigin; of Ui Mail; eponym of Ui Ceallaig Cuallan (O'KELLY)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Cellach Cualann'''
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Cellach Cualann mac Gerthidi (died 715) was the last Uí Máil king of Leinster. Cellach's byname is derived from the land of Cualu which lay around Glendalough.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Uí Máil may mean "grandsons of the princes". This, along with their ownership of certain objects symbolic of the kingship of Leinster, has suggested that they may once have been considered the rightful rulers of Leinster,
2 CONC  at least by themselves. Alternatively, Uí Máil may have derived their name from the Glen of Imaal, in the west of modern County Wicklow, which lay in the centre of their historic lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cellach's father was not king of Leinster, but his second cousin Fiannamail mac Máele Tuile (died 680) was. Cellach succeeded his cousin as king of Uí Máil when Fiannamail was murdered by a servant named Fochsechán, probably on th
2 CONC e orders of Fínsnechta Fledach.[1] The kingship of Leinster passed to the Uí Dúnlainge in the person of Bran Mut mac Conaill who died in 693, to be followed as king by Cellach.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Reign 2 Family 3 Notes 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reign'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Immediately after the death of Bran Mut a battle between the Leinstermen and their western neighbours the kingdom of Osraige is recorded, but Cellach is not associated with this.[2] Cellach was one of the guarantors of the Cáin Ad
2 CONC omnáin (Law of Adomnán) in 697 promulgated at the Synod of Birr.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 704 he warred with his northern neighbours, the Uí Néill of Clann Cholmáin, led by Bodbchath mac Diarmata Déin, and of Síl nÁedo Sláine, led by Fogartach mac Néill. The Battle of Claenath, fought near Clane in modern County Kil
2 CONC dare, was a victory for Cellach, with Bodbchath killed and Fogartach fled.[3] In 707 the Uí Néill High King of Ireland Congal Cennmagair campaigned in Leinster and obtained Cellach's submission to his authority.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 709, aided by British allies—usually presumed to be mercenaries, exiled members of the warbands of the British kingdoms conquered by expansionist Northumbria—he fought a battle at Serg where his sons Fiachra and Fiannamail were
2 CONC  killed. Serg is unidentified, Whitley Stokes translates it as "the battle of the Hunt". The Annals of Tigernach locate it somewhere in the fortuatha of Leinster, that part of the province east of the Wicklow Mountains.[5] His adv
2 CONC ersaries are not named. Cellach's death in 715 is reported without comment by the Irish annals.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT His first wife was Mugain ingen Failbe of Uí Bairrche. Their daughter Conchenn (died 743) married Murchad mac Brain Mut (died 727), his successor as king of Leinster. Mugain was also the mother of his sons Fiachra and Fianamail sl
2 CONC ain in 709.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cellach's wife Bé Fáil, the third of four, was the daughter of Sechnassach, the High King of Ireland. Several of his sons died in his lifetime. His daughters married well. Muirenn (died 748) was the mother of High King Cináed mac 
2 CONC Írgalaig and perhaps of Cináed's successor Flaithbertach mac Loingsig by a second marriage. Derbforgaill, wife of Fínsnechta Fledach, was probably Cellach's daughter. His daughter Caintigern (Saint Kentigerna) was by his fourth wi
2 CONC fe, also Caintigern, daughter of Conaing Cuirre of the Síl nÁedo Sláine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Cellach's surviving sons, Áed was killed in 719 near Fennor, County Kildare, Crimthann died in battle, "at an immature age" according to the Annals of Ulster in 726, and Eterscél in 727 fighting against Fáelán mac Murchado. His
2 CONC  two grandsons Cathal and Ailill were slain in 744. '''Notes'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Annals of Tigernach AT 680.3
2 CONT * Annals of Ulster AU 693.4
2 CONT * Annals of Ulster AU 704.4
2 CONT * Annals of Ulster AU 707.7
2 CONT
2 CONT Annals of Tigernach AT 709.2
2 CONT
