2 CONT From Genealogical Library book "House of Adam".
1 NOTE The Picts: A member of a possibly non-Celtic people who once occupiedGreat Britian, carried on continual border wars with the Romans, andabout the 9th century became amalgamated with the Scots.
1 NOTE King of the Picts
2 CONT Continued bu Dan Heagren
2 CONT
2 CONT From Genealogical Library book "House of Adam".
1 NOTE Acceded to throne about 490. Killed in 501. Fergus moved the seat of theDal Raida from Ulster, Ireland to Scotland. He is the father of theScottish royal lines.[FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Acceded 490.  The Dal Riata, the people from which the Scottish kingsare descended from, were originally settled on the north east coast ofIreland. Perhaps as early as the third century, and no later than thefifth century, they
2 CONC  began to settle on the west coast of what is nowScotland. It is in the late fifth century that the names begin to take onsome historical credibility. In any case, the ruling dynasty of the DalRiata had established itself in the a
2 CONC rea corresponding to modern Argyllby the late fifth century. Almost all historians, with one or two notableexceptions, accept unbroken descent from Fergus Mor, the first king ofDal Riata, to the present day.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Acceded 490. From the seventh century onwards, Irish monks wereinvolved in manufacturing a history for themselves to fill the gapbetween biblical history and (their) modern history. As a result, anycompetent medieval Irish hist
2 CONC orian could trace the supposed genealogy ofany of the modern kings of Irish descent back to Adam, through fabulous,then biblical antecedents.
2 CONT    The Dal Riata, the people from which the Scottish kings are descendedfrom, were originally settled on the north east coast of Ireland. Perhapsas early as the third century, and no later than the fifth century, theybegan to sett
2 CONC le on the west coast of what is now Scotland. It is in thelate fifth century that the names begin to take on some historicalcredibility. In any case, the ruling dynasty of the Dal Riata hadestablished itself in the area correspond
2 CONC ing to modern Argyll by the latefifth century.
2 CONT    Almost all historians, with one or two notable exceptions, acceptunbroken descent from Fergus Mor, the first king of Dal Riata, to thepresent day.
