1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Saint Columbanus (540 – 23 November 615; Irish: Columban, meaning the white dove.) was an Irish missionary notable for founding a number of monasteries on the European continent from around 590 in the Frankish and 
2 CONC Lombard kingdoms, most notably Luxeuil (in present-day France) and Bobbio (Italy), and stands as an exemplar of Irish missionary activity in early medieval Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He spread among the Franks a Celtic monastic rule and Celtic penitential practices for those repenting of sins, which emphasized private confession to a priest, followed by penances levied by the priest in reparation for the sin. 
2 CONC He is also one of the earliest identifiable Hiberno-Latin writers.
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Columbanus is the patron saint of motorcyclists and is the favored saint of the Columban Fathers, a missionary society.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Catholic_Encyclopedia_%281913%29/St._Columbanus
