1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Colmán mac Diarmato  (Colmán Bec)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Konge av  Uisnech fra ? døde i 587'''
2 CONT * sønn av Diarmait mac Cerbaill og Brea  
2 CONT * Colmán kone var ukjent, de hadde sønnen  Óengus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Begge Colmáns ble ansett som grunnleggerne av senere dynastier. Colmán Már, som slektsforskerne ga to sønner, Suibne og Fergus , var den ensomme forfederen til Clann Cholmáin , et dynasti som dominert den sørlige Uí Néill fra det 
2 CONC 8. århundre til det 11. århundre, og som ga mange konger av Tara . Colmán Bec ble ansett som forfader til Clann Cholmáin Bic, mye mindre viktige dynasti, senere Caille Follamain, gjennom en sønn Óengus
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colm%C3%A1n_Bec
2 CONT
2 CONT =Colmán Bec (?)=
2 CONT  (1) M, #284105, d. 587 Last Edited=20 May 2008
2 CONT *'''Colmán Bec (?)''' was the son of '''Diarmait I, High King of Ireland'''. (1) 
2 CONT *He died in 587. (1)
2 CONT *Forrás / Source: http://www.thepeerage.com/p28411.htm#i284105
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colm%C3%A1n_Bec
2 CONT =Colmán Bec=
2 CONT Colmán mac Diarmato (died c. 587) was an Irish king, son of Diarmait mac Cerbaill. Early sources and older scholarship distinguish two sons of Diarmait, Colmán Bec (Colmán the Younger) and Colmán Már (Colmán the Elder), although i
2 CONC t is now supposed that only Colmán Bec was a historical figure, Colmán Már being a later genealogical invention. There are some traces of Colmán Bec in the Irish annals, but so far as Colmán Már is concerned only his putative deat
2 CONC h is recorded.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the traditional account, found in genealogical sources, Diarmait mac Cerbaill had three known sons, two of whom were called Colmán. Colmán Bec's mother is said to have been Brea daughter of the Conmaicne, a Connacht p
2 CONC eople.[1] Colmán Már's mother, Eithne, daughter of Brénainn Dall of the Conmaicne. Other sources claim that Eithne was also a wife of Diarmait's son Áed Sláine, and yet others say that she also married Áed's son Blathmac.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Colmáns were regarded as the founders of later dynasties. Colmán Már, to whom the genealogists gave two sons, Suibne and Fergus, was the eponymous ancestor of Clann Cholmáin, a dynasty which dominated the southern Uí Néill fr
2 CONC om the 8th century to the early 11th century, and which supplied many kings of Tara. Colmán Bec was regarded as the ancestor of the much less important dynasty of Clann Cholmáin Bic, later Caille Follamain, through a son Óengus.[3
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT The only record of Colmán Már in the annals is a report of his death in the 550s.[4] A number of difficulties have been noted with the chronology of Colmán Már's death in relation to the floruit of his supposed sons and brothers, 
2 CONC with the early appearance of his Latinate name, and with the record of the annals. Ailbhe Mac Shamhráin concludes "...Colmán Már is a hollow figure and looks suspiciously like an artificial creation...".[5] It is suggested that Co
2 CONC lmán Már was added to the genealogies in the time of Domnall Midi (died 763).[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT The first record of Colmán Bec in the annals is in the 560s, when he is reported to have undertaken an expedition to Iardoman—glossed as "Seil and Islay", but sometimes understood to mean the Inner Hebrides more generally—along wi
2 CONC th Conall mac Comgaill.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 570s, the annals record Colmán Bec's defeat at a battle at Femen. Some sources add that he was defeated by Coirpre Cromm mac Crimthainn, who is recorded as the provincial overking of Munster.[8] There were at least two nota
2 CONC ble places named Femen, one near to the Hill of Tara, the other near to Cashel.[9] Some historians have supposed that this battle in fact concerned internal Uí Néill disputes.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Colmán Bec's final appearances in the historical record are in the 580s, perhaps 586 and 587. In 586, the annals report the killing of Báetán mac Ninneda, whom they claim to have been king of Tara, "according to Colmán Bec's plan"
2 CONC  by Colmán's son Cumméne and a kinsman of the same name, a grandson of Diarmait's brother Illand. The following year there is a report of Colmán Bec's death, fighting against Áed mac Ainmuirech, at the unlocated Belach Dathí.[11]
