1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Máel Dúin mac Máele Fithrich (625 – 681) was a highly resilient early medieval sovereign, King of Ailech, and head of the Cenél maic Ercae branch of the Northern Uí Néill. Following the battlefield defeat and political marginaliza
2 CONC tion of his father, Máel Dúin successfully re-established his branch’s ancestral dominance over northwestern Ireland, ruling from roughly 668 until his own violent death in 681.
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Dúin’s early life was defined by the long shadow of the Battle of Leitheirbe (630), where his father was killed and his lineage stripped of power by the rival Cenél Feradaig branch. Through decades of calculated local skirmis
2 CONC hes and strategic tribal diplomacy, Máel Dúin successfully reversed his family’s fortunes. By 668, he broke the stranglehold of his rivals and seized the stone fortress at the Grianan of Ailech, restoring the Cenél maic Ercae to a
2 CONC bsolute regional hegemony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once secured on the throne of Ailech, Máel Dúin aggressively expanded his border zones. In 677, he launched a sudden assault near the coastlines of Lough Foyle, directly slaying Dúnchad mac Ultáin, the King of Airgialla, at the st
2 CONC ronghold of Dún Forgo. His growing influence in western Ulster drew the immediate hostility of the Southern Uí Néill High King, Fínsnechta Fledach, who launched a massive punitive invasion northward in 676, aggressively destroying
2 CONC  the outer fortifications of Ailech to assert southern authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT The final chapter of Máel Dúin’s martial career was defined by extreme violence. In the summer of 681, he orchestrated a terrifying psychological strike known in early Irish annals as the "Burning of the Kings" at Dún Ceithirn. Al
2 CONC ongside his northern allies, Máel Dúin trapped his bitter regional enemies—including Dúngal Eilni mac Scandail of Dal nAraide and Cenn Faelad mac Suibni—inside a royal fortress and burned it to the ground. This sparked a desperate
2 CONC , immediate counter-offensive by the surviving Ulster coalitions later that same year. The two forces collided at the Battle of Bla Sléibe (County Londonderry), where Máel Dúin was ultimately overwhelmed and slain on the battlefie
2 CONC ld.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prolific Legacy and DescendantsMáel 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dúin’s marriage to Cacht ingen Cellaig—the daughter of the powerful Cenél Conaill High King Cellach mac Máele Coba—solidified a vital northern alliance:Fergal mac Máele Dúin (d. 722) – His primary son and successor, who completely
2 CONC  avenged his father's death, achieved absolute dominance over Ulster, and claimed the High Kingship of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the ancient patrilineal lineages preserved by Clan MacNeil, Máel Dúin mac Máele Fithrich is formally cataloged as the 8th Ancestral Chief. His successful re-conquest of the Kingdom of Ailech represents a critical survival m
2 CONC ilestone, ensuring that the royal patrilineal line survived to anchor the genealogical tree before migrating centuries later.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
