1 OCCU High King of Ireland 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fergal mac Máele Dúin (c. 665 – 11 December 722) was the 156th High King of Ireland and King of Ailech. A ruthlessly aggressive military imperialist from the Cenél nEógain branch of the Northern Uí Néill, his reign
2 CONC  was defined by unrelenting, bloody expansion. He terrorized the southern halves of Ireland to enforce absolute tribute payments, culminating in a catastrophic, legendary battlefield defeat where he was slain and decapitated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father:  Máel Dúin mac Máele Fithrich, King of Ailech (slain 681).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Cacht ingen Chellaig, a high-ranking princess of the rival Cenél Conaill branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Partner: Ailpin ingen Comgaill, a royal princess of the Scottish kingdom of Dál Riata.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fergal seized the Kingship of Ailech in 700 and aggressively pushed his authority southward. In 707, he led a brutal vanguard against the men of Connacht, slaughtering their king, Indrechtach mac Dúnchado, in a cold act of ancestr
2 CONC al blood-vengeance. Following the death of High King Congal Cendmagair in 710, Fergal seized the High Kingship of Ireland by force.
2 CONT
2 CONT His imperial rule was characterized by merciless suppression of internal rivalries. In 718, during a vicious internal feud among the southern Uí Néill at the Battle of Kells, the warlord Conall Grant emerged victorious. Viewing Co
2 CONC nall’s rising power as an immediate threat to his own sovereignty, Fergal intervened directly two months later, tracking Conall down and assassinating him to firmly settle northern authority over the midlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fergal's primary ambition was the absolute subjugation of the Kingdom of Leinster to extract the Bórama—a crushing, ancient tribute of cattle, silver, and goods. In 719, Fergal marched his imperial armies south, executing local ch
2 CONC ieftains and laying waste to the agrarian plains of Kildare.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Leinstermen formed a desperate alliance with Cathal mac Finguine, the King of Munster, launching massive guerrilla counter-strikes that ravaged the plain of Brega in 721. Fergal retaliated immediately with scorched-earth campa
2 CONC igns. He invaded, plundered, and burned entire southern communities until the Leinster king capitulated, handing over massive wealth tributes and noble hostages to satisfy Fergal's demands.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In the winter of 722, enraged that the Leinster tribute was once again being withheld, Fergal broke an active winter truce and launched a massive, full-scale invasion force of 21,000 men into the south. According to the historical
2 CONC  saga Cath Almaine (The Battle of Allen), Fergal's campaign was spiritually cursed from the outset. His vanguard soldiers callously pillaged a Christian hermit's church and stole his only cow, an act of sacrilege that the metaphys
2 CONC ical worldview of the era deemed a fatal moral failure of the king's righteousness (fír flathemon).
2 CONT
2 CONT On 11 December 722, at the Hill of Allen, a vastly outnumbered but desperate Leinster army ambushed Fergal's northern forces. The battle turned into a total massacre. Fergal fought with brutal ferocity but was surrounded and slain
2 CONC  alongside 160 of his royal bodyguards. To complete his ultimate humiliation, the victorious Leinster chieftains severed Fergal's head from his body directly on the battlefield. In a grim, metaphysical ritual recorded in early lit
2 CONC erature, his severed head was carried south to the King of Munster, who treated the skull with great reverence, binding it with satin and gold before it was eventually returned north for royal burial.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left behind two powerful sons who would both carry on his violent legacy:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Niall Frossach mac Fergaile (718 – 778) – High King of Ireland whose birth brought miraculous showers but whose reign collapsed into famine and survivor cannibalism.
2 CONT #Áed Allán (d. 743) – A fiercely violent High King of Ireland who later avenged his father's death by completely massacring the Leinstermen at the Battle of Uchbad in 738.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official traditions of Clan MacNeil, Fergal is recognized as the 9th Ancestral Chief. While his historical title was High King of Ireland rather than a Scottish clan chief, he represents a crucial link in their traditio
2 CONC nal lineage connecting the clan to the royal Uí Néill dynasty of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
