1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blathmac_mac_M%C3%A1ele_Cobo
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Blathmac mac Máele Cobo (died 670)[1] was a Dál Fiatach ruler of the over-kingdom of Ulaid. He was the son of Máel Cobo mac Fiachnai (died 647).[2] He ruled from 647 to 670.
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT The Dál Fiatach dominated the kingship of Ulster from 637 to 674. Family strife was a common theme among the dynasty at this time. His father was slain or killed by Blathmac's cousin Congal Cennfota mac Dúnchada in 647.[3] The Iri
2 CONC sh annals mention Blathmac's death as king prior to Congal's with that title so it can be assumed that Blathmac acquired the title despite Congal's killing of his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 668 the Battle of Fertas (near Belfast) was fought between the Ulaid and the Cruithne. Here, Cathussach mac Luirgéne, their king, was defeated and slain.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Blathmac had seven sons including Bécc Bairrche mac Blathmaic, a king of Ulaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT  Annals of Ulster AU 670.3; Annals of Tigernach AT 670.3
2 CONT  Byrne, Table 6; Charles-Edwards, appendix XXI; Mac Niocaill, pg.155
2 CONT  AU 647.1; AT 648.1; Mac Niocaill, pg.100
2 CONT  AU 668.2; AT 666.6; Mac Niocaill, pg.101
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT Kings of Ulster
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT Annals of Ulster at [1] at University College Cork
2 CONT Annals of Tigernach at [2] at University College Cork
