1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Máel Fithrich mac Aedo Uaridnaig (560 – 630) was a prominent early medieval chieftain, Lord of the Northern Uí Néill, and head of the Cenél maic Ercae branch of the Cenél nÉogain dynasty. Ruling as King of Ailech f
2 CONC rom 628 until his violent death in 630, his brief reign was consumed by a brutal, internecine civil war fought entirely over which ancestral branch would hold territorial supremacy over the kingdoms of the north.
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Fithrich's entire political life was overshadowed by an intense, multi-generational factional feud within his own extended family network. For generations, his specific branch, the Cenél maic Ercae, had exercised undisputed c
2 CONC ontrol over the royal stone fortress at the Grianan of Ailech. However, their supremacy was shattered by the rise of Suibne Menn, a ruthless rival chieftain from the Cenél Feradaig branch who seized the local throne and eventually
2 CONC  claimed the High Kingship of Ireland. Following Suibne's assassination in 628, Máel Fithrich aggressively mobilized his warriors to reclaim his ancestral birthright, taking control of Ailech but fracturing the kingdom into warrin
2 CONC g camps.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bitter dynastic feud reached its violent climax in 630 at the historic Battle of Leitheirbe. Refusing to cede control back to the Cenél maic Ercae, the rival branch rallied under the command of Suibne’s brother, Ernaine mac Fi
2 CONC achnai. The two heavily armed tribal coalitions collided in a fierce engagement. Máel Fithrich was slain on the battlefield while fighting at the head of his frontline shield-wall. His decisive battlefield defeat resulted in total
2 CONC  political marginalization for his lineage; the rival Cenél Feradaig branch completely swept the field, seizing the throne of Ailech and dominating northern kingships for nearly the remainder of the 7th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his violent demise and the temporary loss of the kingdom, Máel Fithrich's line survived through his strategic marriage to Cacht of Dal Fiatach (a prominent eastern Ulster princess):
2 CONT
2 CONT #Máel Dúin mac Máele Fithrich (d. 681) – His primary son, who later re-established the family's fortunes by successfully fighting his way back onto the throne as King of Ailech.
2 CONT #Fergal mac Máele Dúin – His grandson, who eventually restored the line to absolute greatness, completely crushing their old rivals and claiming the High Kingship of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the ancient, patrilineal genealogies held by Clan MacNeil, Máel Fithrich mac Aedo Uaridnaig is formally cataloged as the 7th Ancestral Chief. He represents a critical link of survival in the early medieval Irish chain, pres
2 CONC erving the royal bloodline during an era of extreme dynastic collapse before his descendants laid the groundwork for future expansions.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---- 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
