1 OCCU 162nd MONARCH of IRELAND; `of the Showers', monk
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Niall Frossach mac Fergaile (718 – 778), widely known as Niall of the Showers, was the 162nd High King of Ireland and King of Ailech. A prominent ruler of the Cenél nÉogain branch of the Northern Uí Néill dynasty, 
2 CONC his highly volatile eight-year imperial reign was deeply intertwined with the mystical worldview of the medieval Gaels, marked by miraculous birth omens, devastating natural catastrophes, widespread cannibalism, and an eventual po
2 CONC litical collapse that forced his abdication.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Fergal mac Máele Dúin, High King of Ireland (slain 722).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Ailpin ingen Comgaill, a royal princess of the Scottish kingdom of Dál Riata.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Dunfhlaith ingen Flaithbertaig, an elite princess of the rival Cenél Conaill branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall's entire life was defined by the deep-seated Gaelic belief in fír flathemon (the "justice of the ruler"), a metaphysical concept stating that the heavens would physically manifest signs to validate a true king. According to 
2 CONC the Annals of Ulster, on the exact night Niall was born in 718 at Fahan, Inishowen, the sky opened and dropped three miraculous showers upon his birthplace: a shower of pure silver, a shower of sweet honey, and a shower of wheat. 
2 CONC This extraordinary cosmic omen earned him his permanent historical nickname Frossach (of the Showers) and caused the realm to view him from infancy as a spiritually ordained sovereign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Famine, Catastrophe, and CannibalismNiall ascended the throne of Ailech in 743 after his violently aggressive brother, High King Áed Allán, was slaughtered in battle. In 763, Niall claimed the High Kingship of Ireland. However, th
2 CONC e cosmic favor of his birth soon gave way to an apocalyptic dark age.B
2 CONT
2 CONT etween 764 and 767, Ireland was struck by an unprecedented, back-to-back sequence of severe winter freezes and summer droughts. In the psychological worldview of the 8th century, this ecological ruin was seen as a terrifying sign 
2 CONC of divine displeasure and a collapse of the king's spiritual purity. The contemporary Annals of Ulster explicitly record that the devastation across the landscape became so absolute that the social contract utterly dissolved and "
2 CONC man ate man." Historians note that this horrific survivor cannibalism—eating the corpses of those who had already perished—was the ultimate consequence of the total societal collapse that struck his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unable to reverse the famine or maintain control over the desperate provincial kingdoms, Niall's imperial authority rapidly disintegrated. In 770, the aggressive southern Uí Néill ruler, Donnchad Midi, launched a massive military 
2 CONC invasion into the north to strip Niall of his power. Recognizing that his starving northern armies could not withstand the assault, and unwilling to plunge the realm into a suicidal civil war, Niall chose to abdicate the High King
2 CONC ship in 770 rather than face a slaughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stripped of his crown, Niall spent his remaining years in religious exile. He traveled to the kingdom of his mother's people in Scotland, entering the famous monastery on the holy island of Iona as a penitent monk, seeking to rest
2 CONC ore his soul after the dark years of his reign. He passed away there in 778 and was buried in the Royal Cemetery of Iona.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left behind his chosen heir:
2 CONT
2 CONT Áed Oirdnide mac Néill (c. 769 – 819) – High King of Ireland who later enacted brutal vengeance against the southern kingdoms and plundered the clergy.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official traditions of Clan MacNeil, Niall is recognized as the 10th Ancestral Chief. While his historical title was High King of Ireland rather than a Scottish clan chief, he represents a crucial link in their traditio
2 CONC nal lineage connecting the clan to the royal Uí Néill dynasty of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
2 CONT 4. Jorge O'Neill, styled Count of Tyrone & Clannaboy (1848-1925).
2 CONT 5. Jorge Torlades O'Neill (1817-1890).
2 CONT 6. Jose-Maria O'Neill (b. 1788).
2 CONT 7. Carlos O'Neill (1760-1835).
2 CONT 8. Shane or Joao O'Neill, immigrated to Portugal (fl. 1740-1750).
2 CONT 9. Constantine O'Neill in Dublin.
