1 NAME Áed Ordnidhe /mac Néill/ Ard-rí na h'Éireann
2 GIVN Áed Ordnidhe
2 SURN mac Néill
2 NSFX Ard-rí na h'Éireann
2 _MARNM mac Néill
2 NICK High King of Ireland
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 769
1 DEAT
2 DATE 20 NOV 819
2 PLAC Ath da Ferta, Mag Choba (near modern County Down)
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Ireland
1 OCCU aka Aedh V `the Dignified'; 164th MONARCH of IRELAND; aka Aodh Oirdnide
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT Áed Oirdnide mac Néill (c. 769 – 819), historically known as Áed the Dignified or Áed the Ordained, was the 164th High King of Ireland and King of Ailech. A commanding ruler of the Cenél nÉogain branch of the Northern Uí Néill, hi
2 CONC s violent 22-year reign was defined by unrelenting internal civil wars, the forced subjugation of provincial kingdoms, and complex, often bloody interactions with the medieval Irish Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Niall Frossach mac Fergaile, High King of Ireland (d. 778).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Dunfhlaith ingen Flaithbertaig, a royal princess of the Cenél Conaill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Euginis ingen Donnchada, daughter of High King Donnchad Midi. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Áed claimed the Kingship of Ailech in 788 after defeating and killing his rival cousins in a bloody succession feud. Following the death of High King Donnchad Midi in 797, Áed plunged Ireland into a fierce civil war to seize the H
2 CONC igh Throne. He won the decisive Battle of Druim Ríg, where he slaughtered two of Donnchad Midi's sons and took the High Kingship by force.
2 CONT
2 CONT His famous epithet Oirdnide (meaning "the Ordained") stems from a calculated political move at the Synod of Áth Da Ferta in 804. There, Áed had himself officially anointed with holy oil by Condmach, the Archbishop of Armagh. This 
2 CONC event marked a major integration of royal power and church authority, cementing a mutually beneficial alliance between the High King and the prominent northern clergy.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his northern borders secure, Áed launched a brutal campaign of conquest against the Kingdom of Leinster to force their total submission. In 804 and 805, Áed systematically plundered, burned, and laid waste to the territories 
2 CONC of the Laigin. The Annals of Ulster record that he devastated and plundered Leinster twice within the span of a single month. When the Leinster king fell from power, Áed forcefully partitioned the kingdom into two sub-realms and i
2 CONC nstalled puppet rulers loyal only to his throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT His violence was not reserved solely for external enemies. In 815, during a fit of dynastic rage to crush a rebellion by the southern Uí Néill branch, Áed marched his armies into the Boyne Valley. He initiated a severe purge, expl
2 CONC icitly hunting down and massacring the noblemen of Clann Cholmáin directly within their own ancestral lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Áed famously passed a decree in 804 exempting Christian monks and priests from mandatory military service, his relationship with the Church was volatile. In 817, the monks of the sacred monastery of Tallaght and the Céili Dé
2 CONC  religious reformers were so outraged by Áed’s atrocities and violations of sanctuary that they organized an unprecedented religious boycott against the High King.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monks successfully barred Áed and his royal court from holding the historic Games of Tailtiu—Ireland’s ancient national assembly. Angered by this blow to his royal prestige, Áed retaliated by launching a military assault direc
2 CONC tly on the borders of the monastery's territories, plundering the local populations who supported the boycott.
2 CONT
2 CONT After over two decades of heavy warfare, Áed fell ill while leading a military expedition into the south. He died on 20 November 819 at Ath da Ferta in Mag Choba (near modern County Down). Despite his intense conflicts with certai
2 CONC n monastic houses, his primary allies in the Church honored his royal anointment, burying him with full imperial honors at the sacred capital of Armagh.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left behind several children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Niall Caille mac Áedo (c. 791 – 846) – Celebrated High King of Ireland who later violently sacked Armagh in 831 before drowning in 846.
2 CONT #Máel Dúin mac Áeda (King of Ailech, d. 867)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official traditions of Clan MacNeil, Áed is recognized as the 11th Ancestral Chief. While his historical title was High King of Ireland rather than a Scottish clan chief, he represents a crucial link in their traditiona
2 CONC l lineage connecting the clan to the royal Uí Néill dynasty of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---- 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
2 CONT 4. Jorge O'Neill, styled Count of Tyrone & Clannaboy (1848-1925).
2 CONT 5. Jorge Torlades O'Neill (1817-1890).
2 CONT 6. Jose-Maria O'Neill (b. 1788).
2 CONT 7. Carlos O'Neill (1760-1835).
2 CONT 8. Shane or Joao O'Neill, immigrated to Portugal (fl. 1740-1750).
2 CONT 9. Constantine O'Neill in Dublin.
2 CONT 10. Felim O'Neill (d. 1709).
2 CONT 11. Capt. Ever O'Neill (fl. ca. 1670).
2 CONT 12. Feidhlimidh Dubh O Neill (fl. 1640-1650).
