2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A DNA test can show if a man is related to Niall: 
2 CONT
2 CONT A recent study conducted at Trinity College Dublin, Ireland, found that a striking percentage of men in Ireland (and quite a few in Scotland) share the same Y chromosome, suggesting that the 5th-century warlord known as "Niall of 
2 CONC the Nine Hostages" may be the ancestor of one in 12 Irishmen. Niall established a dynasty of powerful chieftains that dominated the island for six centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the study scientists found an area in northwest Ireland where they claim 21.5% carry Niall’s genetic fingerprint, says Brian McVoy, one of the team at Trinity. The same area of Ireland has previously been the subject of anthrop
2 CONC ological study…and has shown a strikingly high percentage of men from Haplogroup R1b (98%) versus 90% in southeast Ireland. According to McVoy this area was the main powerbase of the Ui Neill kings, which literally translated mean
2 CONC s "descendants of Niall".
2 CONT
2 CONT McVoy says the Y chromosome appeared to trace back to one person. Following the genealogists' trail McVoy comments: "There are certain surnames that seem to have come from Ui Neill. We studied if there was any association between 
2 CONC those surnames and the genetic profile. It is his (Niall's) family."
2 CONT
2 CONT Several testing companies can do this test: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Of note to Family Tree DNA customers, this signature is found in .6 of one percent of the entire family Tree DNA database. More info:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familytreedna.com/landing/matching-niall.aspx
2 CONT
2 CONT While the signature is typical for R1b European males in general, it is characterized by 11,13 at DYS 385a/b and 14 at DYS 392. Within our second panel of markers the most distinctive difference from the R1b Modal is the 15,16,16,
2 CONC 17 at DYS 464.
2 CONT
2 CONT McEvoy states: "As in other polygynous societies, the siring of offspring was related to power and prestige." The study mentions that just one of the O'Neill dynasty chieftains who died in 1423 had 18 sons with nearly a dozen wome
2 CONC n and claimed 59 grandsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall of the Nine Hostages received his name from the taking of hostages as a strategy for playing mental havoc upon his opponent chieftains. He is known in folklore as a raider of the British and French coasts. Supposedly slain i
2 CONC n the English Channel or in Scotland, his descendants were the most powerful rulers of Ireland until the 11th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Modern surnames tracing their ancestry to Niall include (O')Neill, (O')Gallagher, (O')Boyle, (O')Doherty, O'Donnell, Connor, Cannon, Bradley, O'Reilly, Flynn, (Mc)Kee, Campbell, Devlin, Donnelly, Egan, Gormley, Hynes, McCaul, McGo
2 CONC vern, McLoughlin, McManus, McMenamin, Molloy, O'Kane, O'Rourke and Quinn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Journal reference: American Journal of Human Genetics (February issue)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thegeneticgenealogist.com/2007/12/28/famous-dna-review-part-iii-niall-of-the-nine-hostages/
2 CONT
2 CONT Article in The Times: "High King Niall: the most fertile man in Ireland"
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/world/ireland/article788652.ece
