1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT Eógan mac Néill (c. 395 – 465) was a pivotal early medieval warlord, King of Ailech, and a primary son of Niall of the Nine Hostages. As the dynamic founder of the Cenél nÉogain dynasty, his ruthless military campaigns permanently
2 CONC  reshaped the geopolitics of northern Ireland. Alongside his brothers, Eógan spearheaded the brutal Gaelic conquest of western Ulster, reducing the ancient kingdom of the Ulaid and forging a tribal dominion that would dominate Iri
2 CONC sh politics for centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Conquest of Inishowen and Ulster. Eógan’s career was defined by aggressive territorial expansion. Operating out of the family's ancestral lands in Connacht, Eógan, his brother Conall Gulban, and their kin launched a massive, c
2 CONC oordinated invasion into the northern province of Ulster. They shattered the defenses of the ancient Ulaid kingdoms, seizing vast swaths of land. Eógan claimed the massive, rugged northern peninsula that still bears his name today
2 CONC : Inis Éogain (Inishowen, County Donegal). He established his supreme seat of power at the Grianan of Ailech, a towering stone ringfort overlooking Loch Swilly and Loch Foyle, effectively creating the Kingdom of Ailech.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eógan was one of the first major Gaelic rulers to engage with the Christian missionary Saint Patrick. According to the late 7th-century Vita tripartita Sancti Patricii (Tripartite Life of Saint Patrick), the saint traveled to Eóga
2 CONC n's stronghold at Ailech. Recognizing the immense political utility of a spiritual alliance, Eógan converted to Christianity and was baptized by Patrick. The historical texts note that Patrick blessed Eógan and his seed, physicall
2 CONC y touching the king's shield. This highly symbolic ritual transformed Eógan's martial arms into a consecrated instrument of the new faith, cementing a powerful alliance between the Cenél nÉogain dynasties and the early Irish Churc
2 CONC h.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eógan's life ended in profound grief, deeply tied to the intense tribal warfare of the era. In 464, his beloved brother Conall Gulban was ambushed and slain by the Luigni people at Dunadh Loch Deas. The early annals record that Eó
2 CONC gan was utterly consumed by heartbreak over his brother’s violent demise. Plunged into a deep melancholy, Eógan survived Conall by only one year, dying of sorrow and illness in 465. He was buried at Eskaheen (Uisce Chaoin, Inishow
2 CONC en), close to his royal fortress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prolific Legacy and Descendants. Eógan's lineages consolidated absolute political control over northwestern Ireland, ruling as Kings of Ailech and frequently claiming the High Kingship of Tara.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Muiredach mac Éogain – His eldest son and successor, who continued the dynasty's expansion.
2 CONT #Muirchertach mac Ercae – His grandson, who became one of the most powerful and ruthless High Kings of early Christian Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The O'Neill Clan – Eógan stands as the direct patrilineal ancestor of the great O'Neill (Ó Néill) dynasties of Tyrone, securing his place as a foundational patriarch of Irish history.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The traditional genealogical records of Clan MacNeil, Eógan mac Néill is recognized as the 2nd Ancestral Chief. He forms the crucial second generation in the ancient lineage, bridging the gap between his father, Niall of the Nine 
2 CONC Hostages, and the subsequent generations that traditional clan history claims eventually migrated across the Irish Sea to the Western Isles of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
