1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Maine (?) (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #284107
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=20 May 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT      Maine (?) is the son of Niall 'of the Nine Hostages', High King of Ireland. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p28411.htm#i284107
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Máiné of Tethbae or Máiné mac Néill was a supposed son of Niall Noigiallach. Writing of him in 1973, Irish historian Francis John Byrne stated his belief that:
2 CONT
2 CONT     We may suspect then that eastern Uí Máiné was so successfully absorbed into the Uí Néill ambit that their kings, by a polite fiction, were accepted into the dominant dynasty circle ... The fact that the annalistic obit of Máin
2 CONC é mac Néill in 440 is so much earlier than that of any of his supposed brothers also suggests that he was adopted into the dynasty some time after the synthetic historians had agreed to push back the date of Niall's reign by a gen
2 CONC eration or more. [Byrne 1973:92–93]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is actually far more likely that Máiné Mór was the ancestor of the Uí Maine, whose kingdom once covered a region over the entire of what is now County Galway north and east of Athenry, all of south County Roscommon, and stretch
2 CONC ing over the River Shannon into the regions called Cenél Máiné, Cuircni, Calraige and Delbhna Bethra. Cenél Máiné lay in the south-west portion of the kingdom of Tethbae – on the east shore of Lough Ree – and when it was taken ove
2 CONC r by the Uí Néill, its ruler's ancestor was given a pedigree making him a son of Niall.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ui Máiné remained a powerful independent kingdom in its own right for several further centuries.
