1 OCCU of Ireland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Áed Uaridnach mac Domnall Ilchelgach (530 – 612), recorded in ancient genealogies as Áed son of Domnall or Áed Allán, was a powerful early medieval monarch, King of Ailech, and High King of Ireland. Representing th
2 CONC e Cenél nÉogain branch of the Northern Uí Néill, his reign was characterized by brutal cattle-tribute extortions and aggressive expansion into rival territories, cementing his branch's ancestral right to the sovereign throne at Ta
2 CONC ra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aed is uniquely distinguished by his stark historical moniker, Uaridnach. While middle-Irish writers occasionally interpreted this to mean "warlike" or "martial," contemporary scholars and early translation notes link the title to
2 CONC  a literal physical affliction: "of the cold chills" or "the shivering sickness". This signifies that Áed suffered from a chronic, severe ague or repeating fever—likely a lingering variant of malaria or post-plague neural damage c
2 CONC ommon along the damp coastlines of early medieval northwestern Europe—which he endured throughout his active military career.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the traumatic leadership vacuum left by his father's sudden death via pandemic, Áed slowly reconsolidated power within the north. He governed as King of Ailech from 604 until 612. Upon the violent deaths of the joint rul
2 CONC ers Áed Sláine and Colmán Rímid, Áed forcefully asserted his patrilineal claim to the Overkingship of Tara, successfully expanding his tax boundaries into the midlands and bringing local minor kings under direct northern tribute.
2 CONT
2 CONT The definitive event of Áed’s high kingship occurred in 605 during his punitive campaign against the southern territories. Seeking to enforce the ancient, highly contentious Bóroma (the compulsory cattle-tribute) upon the province
2 CONC  of Leinster, Áed led a massive, heavily armored northern host southwards. He encountered the forces of Brandub mac Echach, King of Leinster, at the bloody Battle of Slabra. Áed’s warriors completely crushed the Leinster defensive
2 CONC  lines, thoroughly routing the southern coalition and securing unprecedented spoils in livestock, human collateral, and territorial dominance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aed Uaridnach passed away in 612 after an intense eight-year reign as High King. Unlike his father, he did not fall to sudden plague, instead succumbing to natural complications likely exacerbated by his lifetime struggle with the
2 CONC  shivering ague. He was buried with full royal honors within the sacred precinct of the Fahan Old Graveyard in Inishowen, County Donegal, a church site historically intertwined with his own ancestral family lineages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prolific Legacy and Descendants. Áed's marriage to Damnat ingen Murchada guaranteed the sovereign survival of the Cenél nÉogain lines:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Máel Fithrich mac Áedo Uaridnaig (d. 630) – His primary son and direct successor as King of Ailech.
2 CONT #Dáire mac Áedo (d. 624) – A prominent prince who died of wounds sustained during the territorial conflicts with the Cenél Lóegaire.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official, unyielding traditions of Clan MacNeil, Áed Uaridnach is formally recognized as the 6th Ancestral Chief. He stands as a vital link in the ancient patrilineal sequence, anchoring the prehistoric genealogical cha
2 CONC in to the absolute height of the historic Uí Néill dynasties before the family line shifted outward.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
2 CONT 4. Jorge O'Neill, styled Count of Tyrone & Clannaboy (1848-1925).
