2 CONT Various Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Mac Ruaidri, from RUAIDHRI Earl of Moray, was the father of Finlay, and grandfather of MacBeth. There are a number of Gaelic names throughout Ireland which do not belong to any Irish clans. Mac Ruaidhri was leader of the Irish and
2 CONC  lord of Cruach Brandon (this place is a lofty mountain of Saint Brendan, a wonderful hermit, and nine cantreds around it). In the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, as evidenced by the Tudor Fiants, by the census of 1659 and ot
2 CONC her records, the name MacRory was both numerous and ubiquitous; now it is rare. It can also be ascribed to the ephemeral nature, outside its own proper territory, to which reference will be made hereunder, of the surname MacRory, 
2 CONC I.e Mac Ruaidhri, son of Rory. This like MacTeige and MacCormac, was, at least up to the middle of the seventeenth century, frequently used for one generation only. In Co. Clare, for example, many of the people who appear in the r
2 CONC ecords as MacRory were O'Briens, MacNamaras and MacMahons. Later some resumed their real patronymic but a larger number became Rodgers, thus obscuring their Dalcassian origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The true Gaelic sept of MacRory belongs properly to Co. Tyrone. A branch of this was established in Co. Derry where they became erenaghs of Ballynascreen in the barony of Loughlinsholin.
2 CONT
