1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT Niall Caille mac Áedo (c. 791 – 846) was the 166th High King of Ireland and King of Ailech. A dominant ruler from the Cenél nÉogain branch of the Northern Uí Néill dynasty, his reign marked a major turning point as Viking raids tr
2 CONC ansformed from local coastal plundering into deep inland conquests. He earned his famous historical title Caille directly from the river where he met his violent end.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Áed Oirdnide mac Néill, High King of Ireland (d. 819).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Medb ingen Indregataig, a royal princess of Connacht.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Gormflaith ingen Donnchada, daughter of High King Donnchad Midi.Life and ReignNiall did not automatically take the throne due to strict rules that rotated power between northern and southern kingly branches. He fought his wa
2 CONC y to power, seizing the Kingship of Ailech in 823 by deposing his rival cousin. In 827, he launched a massive army to protect his personal confessor, Eoghan, winning a bloody battle near Armagh to force his ally onto the archbisho
2 CONC p's throne. He officially claimed the High Kingship of Ireland in 833.
2 CONT
2 CONT His imperial reign was defined by aggressive warfare. In 844, he won a historic victory over a massive invading Viking fleet at Mag Itha, completely shattering their army to save the local populations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though celebrated for fighting Vikings, Niall was utterly ruthless against his fellow Irishmen when his imperial power was threatened. In 831, during a bitter political feud over church wealth, Niall marched his army directly into
2 CONC  the holy city of Armagh, plundering the sacred sanctuary and taking the local population hostage to force absolute submission.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 846, while on a military march, Niall witnessed one of his personal bodyguards pulled under by the flooded, raging currents of the River Callan. Disregarding his own safety, the High King spurred his horse directly into the vio
2 CONC lent torrent to save the man but was swept from his mount and drowned.
2 CONT
2 CONT He left behind several children who carried on his lineage, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Áed Findliath mac Néill (c. 830 – 879) – Celebrated High King of Ireland who crushed Viking expansion.
2 CONT #Flaithbertach mac Néill (d. 855)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official traditions of Clan MacNeil, Niall is recognized as the 12th Ancestral Chief. While his historical title was High King of Ireland rather than a Scottish clan chief, he represents a crucial link in their traditio
2 CONC nal lineage connecting the clan to the royal Uí Néill dynasty of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
2 CONT 4. Jorge O'Neill, styled Count of Tyrone & Clannaboy (1848-1925).
