1 NOTE Máel Sechnaill mac Maíl Ruanaid var konge av Mide fra 845 og overkonge av Irland fra 846 til sin død 27. november 862. Han tilhørte klanen Clann Cholmáin av det sørlige Ui Neill dynastiet, og var sønn av Máel Ruanaid mac Donnc
2 CONC hada (konge av Mide fra 833 til 843) og sønnesønn av Donnchad Midi mac Domnaill (overkonge av Irland fra 766 til 797). Máel Sechnaill regnes som den første kongen i middelalderen som virkelig var nær et reellt overherredømme over
2 CONC  hele Irland. I motsetning til sine forgjengere som kalles overkonge (Ard rí) bare i senere kilder som Annalene av de fire mesterne og Geoffrey Keatings Irlands historie, kalles Máel Sechnaill også konge over hele Irland i Ulst
2 CONC erannalene [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Bakgrunn
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill nevnes ved to anledninger i annalene før han blir konge, ved begge drepte han utfordrere til hans far Máel Ruanaids herredømme i Mide - Crunnmael mac Fiannamail i 839 og Diarmait mac Conchobar (sønn av Conchobar m
2 CONC ac Donnchada) i 841.
2 CONT
2 CONT I 845 tok han livet av sin egen bror Flann og ble konge i Mide. Samme år klarte han å fange den norrøne høvdingen Torgest som hadde herjet den midtre delen av Irland, og druknet ham senere i Loch Uair, en innsjø i dagens grevska
2 CONC p Westmeath. Keating utmaler i sin Irlands historie denne historien, og framstiller det som om Máel Sechnaill ved list og snarrådighet klarer å overmanne en overmektig tyrann, og ved denne dåden befri Irland fra den norrøne un
2 CONC dertrykkelsen. Dette er nok betydelig overdrevet, men seieren over Torgest bidro nok både til at Máel Sechnaill sikret grepet om Mide, og at han utpekte seg som den naturlige etterfølgeren til overkongen Niall Caille mac Áeda d
2 CONC a han døde i 846.
2 CONT
2 CONT Overkonge
2 CONT Det norrøne nærværet i Irland var blitt tydeligere, og tilsynelatende mer organisert. Nå opptrer også lederene for de norrønne fremmede ved navn i annalene, og disse deltok i den irske maktkampen i skiftende allianser. I 850 gjo
2 CONC rde Cináed mac Conaing, leder for Sil nAeda Slaine i det nordlige Brega, også kalt Knowth, opprør mot Máel Sechnaill, ?med støtte av de fremmede?. Cinaed plyndret store deler av Mide, men ble drept av Máel Sechnaill og Tigerna
2 CONC ch av Lagore i 851. Også ved senere anledninger allierte Máel Sechnaills irske rivaler seg med de nye norrøne lederne, både Cerball mac Dúnlainge av Osraige og Aed Finliath hadde i følge annalene allianser med Olav Kvite i det
2 CONC  nye norrøne kongeriket Dublin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Annalene forteller om flere slag mellom Máel Sechnaill og de fremmede, men også om at han hadde dem som allierte. I 856 forteller Ulsterannalene om ?stor krigføring mellom hedningene og Máel Sechnaill, støttet av norrøn-gælere?
2 CONC [2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Referanser
2 CONT Fotnoter
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ AU 862.5 
2 CONT ^ AU 856.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT Litteratur
2 CONT Donnchadh Ó Corraín, ?Vikings in Ireland and Scotland in the Ninth Century?. I: Peritia 1998. Årbok for Medieval Academy of Ireland. issn 0332-1592 pdf 
2 CONT Donnchadh Ó Corraín, The Vikings & Ireland, 
2 CONT Annalene av de fire mesterne AFM 
2 CONT Inisfallen-annalene AI 
2 CONT Ulster-annalene AU 
2 CONT Irlands fragmentariske annaler FA
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill mac Máele Ruanaid (died 27 November 862) was High King of Ireland. The Annals of Ulster use the Old Irish title rí hÉrenn uile, that is "king of all Ireland", when reporting his death, distinguishing Máel Sechn
2 CONC aill from the usual Kings of Tara who are only called High Kings of Ireland in late sources such as the Annals of the Four Masters or Geoffrey Keating's Foras Feasa ar Éirinn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill was a grandson of Donnchad Midi mac Domnaill of Clann Cholmáin, who was King of Tara from around 778 to 797. Clann Cholmáin was a sept of the Uí Néill which ruled as Kings of Mide in east central Ireland. While
2 CONC  the southern Uí Néill had been dominated by the Síl nÁedo Sláine Kings of Brega in the 7th and early 8th centuries, the Clann Cholmáin were dominant from the time of Máel Sechnaill's great-grandfather Domnall Midi. The Kin
2 CONC gship of Tara, a largely symbolic title, alternated between Clann Cholmáin as representatives of the southern Uí Néill and the Cenél nEógain as representatives of the northern Uí Néill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill became king of Mide and head of Clann Cholmáin after killing his brother Flann in 845, and king of Tara in 846 on the death of Niall Caille mac Áeda of the Cenél nEógain, who drowned in the Callan River close t
2 CONC o Armagh.[1] He had appeared in the Irish annals some years earlier, being noticed in 839, and again 841 as a result of fighting among the chiefs of Clann Cholmáin when he killed his cousin Diarmait, son of Conchobar mac Donnchad
2 CONC a, when Diarmait had tried to depose Máel Sechnaill's father as king of Mide.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to Máel Sechnaill's coming to power, the southern Uí Néill had been disunited, and until Niall Caille defeated Feidlimid mac Crimthainn, king of Munster, at Mag nÓchtair (County Kildare) in 841, the midlands had been rep
2 CONC eatedly ravaged by the Munstermen.[3] At the same time, Ireland was a target for Viking raids, although these appear to have been of minor significance. Niall Caille apparently inflicted a heavy defeat on the Norsemen in 845 at Ma
2 CONC g Itha shortly before Máel Sechnaill became king of Mide.[4] Late in 845 the Norse chieftain Thorgest or Turgesius, who had emulated Feidlimid mac Crimthainn by attacking Clonmacnoise and Clonfert, was captured by Máel Sechnaill
2 CONC , and drowned in Lough Owel.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT King of All Ireland
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill's reign was portrayed in later sources as being frequently a matter of war with the Vikings and Norse-Gaels, thanks largely to works such as the Cogadh Gaedhil re Gallaibh, a panegyric written for Muircheartach Ua 
2 CONC Briain, grandson of Brian Boru. The annals tell of frequent battles between Máel Sechnaill and the Viking, both when they were acting on their own and as allies to Cináed mac Conaing or Cerball mac Dúnlainge. But he was also on
2 CONC  occasions allied to the Norse-Gaels. In 856 "[g]reat warfare between the heathens [the Norse or Danes] and Máel Sechnaill with the Norse-Irish" is reported by the Annals of Ulster.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill's real achievements, although they were to be lost on his death, were in Ulster and Munster. Shortly after killing Cináed with the aid of Tigernach mac Fócartai, Máel Sechnaill met with the king of Ulster, Matud
2 CONC án mac Muiredaig, and the chief cleric of Ulster, Diarmait, Abbot of Armagh. Here Máel Sechnaill was acknowledged as High King by the Ulstermen. This did not end strife between the Uí Néill and the kings of Ulster as Armagh wa
2 CONC s raided by Máel Sechnaill in 852. However, Ulster provided troops for Máel Sechnaill, whose army is called "the men of Ireland" in 858.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The annals record expeditions to Munster to obtain tribute and hostages in 854, 856 and in 858, when his army killed several kings, wasted the land and marched south to the sea. Máel Sechnaill's attempts to obtain the submission 
2 CONC to the Munster kings of the Eóganachta were obstructed by the ambitious king of Osraige in Leinster, Cerball mac Dúnlainge. Cerball, known to Icelanders' sagas as Kjarvalr Írakonungr, raided Munster and obtained allies and merc
2 CONC enaries from among the Norse and Norse-Gaels of southern Ireland. The Fragmentary Annals of Ireland, a combination of annals and history written in the 11th century for Donnchad mac Gilla Pátraic king of Osraige and Leinster, say
2 CONC  that the expedition of 854 was led by Cerball on Máel Sechnaill's orders, although Máel Sechnaill himself appears also to have raided into Munster that year. The Annals of Innisfallen are alone in reporting an expedition by Cer
2 CONC ball with allies from Munster against Máel Sechnaill in 859, which is said to have reached as far north as Armagh. The Annals of Ulster however, states that Cerball entered Mide with a great army, supported by Norse allies, Amlai
2 CONC ́b and Ivar. A general assembly of kings and clerics in 859 at Rahugh in County Westmeath settled matters by detaching Osraige from Munster and combining it with Leinster as a subject kingdom. Máel Gualae mac Donngaile of Munster
2 CONC  and Cerball both consented to the change which was little loss to the Eóganachta who had but rarely exercised any control over Osraige.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill's successes raised more opposition from his Uí Néill kinsmen than from subject kings or the Norse and Norse-Gaels, and the latter part of his reign was spent in conflict with the northern Uí Neill, led by Áed F
2 CONC indliath, son of Niall Caille. In 860 Máel Sechnaill led an army raised from Munster, Leinster and Connacht against the northern Uí Néill. The annals say that Áed Findliath and Flann mac Conaing, brother of Cináed, led a nigh
2 CONC t attack on Máel Sechnaill's camp near Armagh which was beaten off with heavy loss to Áed and Flann. Further fighting between Áed and Máel Sechnaill is reported in 861, and again in 862.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill died peacefully on 27 November 862. His obituary in the Annals of Ulster states:
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnailll son of Máel Ruanaid son of Donnchad son of Domnall son of Murchad of Mide son of Diarmait the Harsh son of Airmedach the One-eyed son of Conall of the Sweet Voice, son of Suibne son of Colmán the great son of Di
2 CONC armait the red son of Fergus Wrymouth, king of all Ireland, died on the third feria, the second of the Kalends of December, in the 16th year of his reign.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fragmentary Annals quote a lament for Máel Sechnaill:
2 CONT
2 CONT There is much sorrow everywhere;
2 CONT there is a great misfortune among the Irish.
2 CONT Red wine has been spilled down the valley;
2 CONT the only King of Ireland has been slain.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill's achievements did not outlast him, and Áed Finnliath was unable to maintain peace within the Uí Néill, nor to receive submission for Munster. Áed's incessant warfare with the Norse and Norse-Gaels, while milit
2 CONC arily successful, proved to unexpected consequences in the long term for the position of the northern Uí Néill. Power and influence in the 10th and 11th centuries rested increasingly with kings who, like Máel Sechnaill, could e
2 CONC xploit the wealth of new trading towns and the forces of the Norse and the Norse-Gaels.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Sechnaill's son Flann Sinna would later be King of Mide, High King and self-styled King of All Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ The death of Flann, together with Donnchad mac Follamain is reported by the Annals of Ulster in 845 [AU 845.7]. Donnchad appears to have been a first cousin once-removed of Flann and Máel Sechnaill. 
2 CONT ^ Notices at AU 839.6 and AU AU 841.2. 
2 CONT ^ Byrne, p.225; Herbert, pp. 62-63. 
2 CONT ^ Reported by the Annals of Ulster [AU 845.6]. 
2 CONT ^ AU 845.8. 
2 CONT ^ Byrne, p. 263: Cináed was king of Brega, of the southern Uí Néill sept of the Síl nÁedo Sláine. See also Dan M. Wiley. "Máelsechnaill 7 na Danair". The Cycles of the Kings. http://www.hastings.edu/academic/english/Kings/M
2 CONC aelsechnaill_ocus_na_Danair.htm. Retrieved on 2007-01-01. . 
2 CONT ^ Byrne, pp. 263-265; Herbert, pp. 63-65. 
2 CONT ^ Byrne, pp 263-265; Herbert, pp. 63-65. 
2 CONT ^ Byrne, pp 265-266; Herbert, pp 64-65. 
2 CONT ^ AU 862.5, the date corresponding with 30 November 862. 
2 CONT ^ Fragmentary Annals, FA ?293. 
2 CONT ^ Byrne, pp 266-270. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT A Popular History of Ireland: From the earliest period to Emancipation of the catholics by Thomas D'Arcy McGee 
2 CONT Byrne, Francis John, Irish Kings and High-Kings. Batsford, London, 1973. ISBN 0-7134-5882-8 
2 CONT Herbert, Máire, "Ri Éirenn, Ri Alban: kingship and identity in the ninth and tenth centuries" in Simon Taylor (ed.), Kings, clerics and chronicles in Scotland 500-1297. Fourt Courts, Dublin, 2000. ISBN 1-85182-516-9 
2 CONT Ó Cróinín, Dáibhí, Early Medieval Ireland: 400-1200. Longman, London, 1995. ISBN 0-582-01565-0  
