1 OCCU King, King of Ireland (465-480)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Muiredach mac Éogain (c. 434 – 507) was an early medieval warlord, sovereign King of Ailech, and a prominent chieftain of the Northern Uí Néill. As the son and successor of Eógan mac Néill, he successfully consolid
2 CONC ated his family’s newly conquered territories in western Ulster. Muiredach ruled from the massive stone fortress of the Grianan of Ailech, maintaining his dynasty's iron grip on the Inishowen peninsula and anchoring the political 
2 CONC alliances established during his father's reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muiredach's rule was defined by highly strategic matrimonial alliances that bridged both political power and spiritual authority. His most famous union was with Erca (ingen Loairn), the daughter of Loarn mac Eirc, a powerful king 
2 CONC of Dalriada (the Gaelic maritime kingdom spanning northeastern Ulster and western Scotland). This marriage forged a potent cross-channel alliance. In later generations, Erca was heavily mythologized in early Irish literature as a 
2 CONC figure of immense fertility and power. Additionally, Muiredach maintained a second strategic union with Mell ingen Caechar, ensuring deep tribal alliances within the heavily armed territories of northern Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT While his father, Eógan, was a conqueror who carved out land with the sword, Muiredach’s primary role was consolidation. He organized the conquered lands of Inishowen, ensuring that the Cenél nÉogain (the descendants of Eógan) bec
2 CONC ame deeply entrenched and economically secure. He guarded the maritime trade and raiding lanes through Loch Swilly and Loch Foyle, ensuring that the northernmost territories of Ireland remained a dominant launching pad for campaig
2 CONC ns across the Irish Sea. Under his stewardship, the family transitioned from newly arrived invaders to the undisputed, legitimate rulers of western Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muiredach died around the year 480 of natural causes, a rarity for chieftains of this brutal, transitional era. His passing triggered immediate, violent rivalries among his numerous and ambitious sons. He was buried near the ances
2 CONC tral royal grounds of his father, leaving behind a heavily fortified sub-kingdom that was primed to expand its borders southward toward the sacred Overkingship of Tara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prolific Legacy and Descendants. Muiredach's lineage yielded some of the most dominant figures of early Christian Ireland. His sons divided the expanding territories of the Cenél nÉogain:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Muirchertach mac Ercae (d. 534) – His eldest and most famous son, who took his mother's name to form a distinct royal branch and claimed the High Kingship of Ireland.
2 CONT #Moen mac Muiredaig – Founder of the Cenél Moen branch.
2 CONT #Feradach mac Muiredach – Founder of the Cenél Feradaig branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the traditional genealogical records of Clan MacNeil, Muiredach mac Éogain is recognized as the 3rd Ancestral Chief. He represents the critical third link in the patrilineal chain, holding the royal lineage secure at the Gr
2 CONC ianan of Ailech before subsequent generations pushed further into the historical and legendary history of the clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
2 CONT 4. Jorge O'Neill, styled Count of Tyrone & Clannaboy (1848-1925).
2 CONT 5. Jorge Torlades O'Neill (1817-1890).
2 CONT 6. Jose-Maria O'Neill (b. 1788).
2 CONT 7. Carlos O'Neill (1760-1835).
2 CONT 8. Shane or Joao O'Neill, immigrated to Portugal (fl. 1740-1750).
2 CONT 9. Constantine O'Neill in Dublin.
2 CONT 10. Felim O'Neill (d. 1709).
2 CONT 11. Capt. Ever O'Neill (fl. ca. 1670).
2 CONT 12. Feidhlimidh Dubh O Neill (fl. 1640-1650).
2 CONT 13. Conn Buidhe O Neill (d. ca. 1630).
2 CONT 14. Domnall O Neill (d. bef. 1567).
2 CONT 15. Muircheartach Duileanach O Neill, the Mac Ui Neill Bhuidhe Iochtar
2 CONT (d. aft. 1556).
2 CONT 16. Niall Mor O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r. 1482-1512).
2 CONT 17. Conn O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r. 1468-1482).
2 CONT 18. Aodh Buidhe O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r. 1425-1444).
