1 OCCU King of Ireland, 5th Ancestral Chief of Clan MacNeil
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Domnall Ilchealgach mac Muirchertaig (495 – 566), often known simply as Domnall of the Many Deceits, was a formidable early medieval warlord, sovereign King of Ailech, and Joint High King of Ireland. As a prominent
2 CONC  member of the Cenél nÉogain branch of the Northern Uí Néill, his reign was defined by aggressive multi-provincial alliances and brutal civil warfare designed to maintain his family's absolute supremacy over the northern half of t
2 CONC he island.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall's rise to supreme power was marked by a rare and highly strategic co-ruling partnership. Following the death of High King Diarmait mac Cerbaill, Domnall ascended to the High Kingship of Ireland in 565, sharing the sovereig
2 CONC n throne at the Hill of Tara equally with his brother, Forggus mac Muirchertaig. This dual-leadership system allowed the Cenél nÉogain to split their forces, securing the northern borders from their fortress at Ailech while simult
2 CONC aneously enforcing tribute across the midlands and the borders of Leinster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall's career was forged in relentless tribal warfare. His most famous military triumph occurred in 543 (or 547, depending on regional chronicles) at the bloody Battle of Sligo. In a massive, coordinated strike alongside his br
2 CONC other Forggus and allied Connacht kings, Domnall’s warriors completely routed the forces of the rival Northern Uí Néill branch, slaying King Éogan Bél. This victory cleared the path for Domnall to exact heavy collateral, establish
2 CONC ing total geopolitical dominance over the northern coastlines. In 561, he joined another massive coalition at the Battle of Cúl Dreimne (the Battle of the Book), successfully turning back the forces of High King Diarmait.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall's reign was abruptly cut short in 566 by the Plague of Justinian (recorded as the Blefed or Mortalitas Magna in early Irish annals). This catastrophic global pandemic traveled along maritime trade routes and devastated ear
2 CONC ly medieval populations. The virus decimated the upper echelons of Irish leadership, claiming Domnall just one year after he secured the absolute Overkingship of Tara. The virulence of this specific outbreak explains why his broth
2 CONC er and co-ruler, Forggus, also died suddenly in the exact same year without sustaining a single battlefield injury. Domnall was buried near Loch Foyle, leaving behind a heavily fortified network of territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall's marriage to Duinseach ingen Duach (daughter of the King of Connacht) cemented critical western alliances. His prolific lineage guaranteed the survival of his specific royal line:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Áed Uaridnach (Áed of the Shivering Sickness, d. 612) – His primary son, who later claimed the High Kingship of Ireland.
2 CONT #Eochaid mac Domnaill (d. 572) – Co-ruled as King of Ailech alongside his uncle Báetán.
2 CONT #Colgu mac Domnaill (d. 580) – A prominent warrior chieftain of the northern dynastic lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the traditional genealogical lineages of Clan MacNeil, Domnall Ilchealgach is strictly cataloged as the 5th Ancestral Chief. He represents a vital chronological link in the royal patrilineal chain, holding the ancient Irish
2 CONC  lineage secure at the peak of Uí Néill power before the ancestral lines eventually transitioned across the Irish Sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
