1 NOTE
2 CONT Macbeth of Scotland
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mac Bethad mac Findláich, known in English as Macbeth c. 1005 - August15, 1057 was the Àrd Rìgh or High King of Scotland from August 14, 1040to August 15, 1057. Mac Bethad in Middle Irish literally means "Son ofLife," which transl
2 CONC ates figuratively as "an Anointed One" (compare macbáis ("son of death" or "damned one"), used as a term for fíanna warriors).
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth was the son of Findláech, who found himself on the wrong side ofthe regal dispute with their cousins. Findláech had married Dabda(Anglicised 'Dovda'), a daughter of Malcolm II and sister of Bethoc(mother of the future Dunc
2 CONC an I). This alliance with the House of Athollestranged him from his own line, the House of Moray, and his own brothereventually ordered Findlàech's death by the hands of his nephews, GillaCoemgáin and Malcolm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm died in 1029 and was succeeded by Gilla Coemgáin, who died in1031 or 1032, in a raid mounted against him by High King Malcolm II.Macbeth then succeeded his cousin as Mormaer of Moray, and married GillaCoemgáin's widow, Pri
2 CONC ncess Gruoch — a granddaughter of Kenneth IIIthrough his son, Prince Bòidhe; thus becoming step-father to her son byGillecomgain, Lulach.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Anglo-Saxon chronicle, Macbeth was one of three Scottishkings who submitted to Canute the Great in 1031. 'King' in this context,as it pertains to Macbeth, refers to his rulership of the province ofMoray.
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time, Scotland was still a tribal and quasi-federal "HighKingdom," like Ireland. There was one High King of Scots and Picts, butthere were many regional kings and underkings — the term mormaer beingmore commonly used in Sc
2 CONC otland (whereas in Ireland one reads of the HighKing of Tara, the King of Munster, etc.).
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some disagreement about the system of royal succession that wasin use at the time:
2 CONT
2 CONT One possibility is the system of tanistry, where the Tainiste or heir waschosen from among the male members of the ruling house. This wascertainly in use in Ireland at the time, and many scholars believe it wasalso the custom in S
2 CONC cotland. There is substantial evidence for tanistryfrom several sources, even up to relatively modern times.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tanistry tends to result in the crown's regularly moving from one branchof the royal line to another. Macbeth's naming his son Malcolm as hisheir in Shakespeare's play may represent a vestigial understanding of thecustom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Others argue that Scotland used an old Irish/Pictish matrilineal systemof kingship at this time, and for nearly another two centuries after.Under this proposed system, the grand chief of Scots and Picts was alwaysa king, but he wa
2 CONC s eligible to be king because his mother was a rankingPrincess Royal. She, and the woman who became Queen, were representativesof the Goddess of Sovereignty, whom the king symbolically married uponhis accession. Kings were thus su
2 CONC cceeded by their mother's other sons,then their sisters' sons, then their maternal aunts' sons. This resultedin the crown traveling from brother to brother, uncle to nephew, andcousin to cousin - a system that became ripe for corr
2 CONC uption by ambitiousmen, as the sacred role of womanhood diminished. There is little directevidence for this system in Macbeth's time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth's own grandfather, Malcolm II, was one such ambitious man. Hewished the crown to be settled upon his grandson Duncan, son of hisdaughter, Bethoc, who had married Crinan, the Mormaer ("provincial king")and Abbot of Dunkeld.
2 CONC  This may have been because Bethoc was his favouritedaughter, or because political alliance with Crinan was important, butmore probable is because Bethoc was the elder daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime before his death, Malcolm II proceeded to eliminate the heirs tothe throne who were ahead of Duncan in the succession. of these, onlyLulach survived, then a very young child and rumoured (successfully) tobe half-witted. H
2 CONC aving the protection of Macbeth did not hurt, and beingstep-father and husband of Gruoch bolstered Macbeth's own position underthe old system. When Malcolm II died on November 25, 1034, Duncaninherited the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT To challenge Duncan's position, Macbeth formed an alliance with his andDuncan's cousin Thorfinn, Earl of Orkney, son of Malcolm II's youngestdaughter. They engaged Duncan in battle, who died fighting them nearElgin on August 14, 1
2 CONC 040.
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth was a strong king and ruled over a kingdom stable enough for himto be able to leave for several months on a pilgrimage to Rome. Heinstituted a new form of law and order in Scotland. His reign was notedas a time of prosperi
2 CONC ty. However, in 1054, Malcolm Canmore, Duncan's son,began his campaign for the throne with the assistance of Siward, Earl ofNorthumbria, by capturing the south of Scotland. Macbeth was killed byMalcolm's forces at a battle near Lu
2 CONC mphanan and the throne passed toMacbeth's stepson, Lulach, in August, 1057.
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth is buried on Iona, Scotland's sacred isle and the traditionalburial place of High Kings of Scots and Picts at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some claim the context of the 13th century Chronicle of Huntingdon, whichnames Macbeth a nepos (meaning "grandson" as well as "nephew") of KingMalcolm, refers to Malcolm III Canmore, rather than Malcolm II — but thisis impossible,
2 CONC  since it was Malcolm III Canmore, son of Duncan I, whosucceeded Macbeth - after defeating him in battle in 1057 and killing himeither then or shortly thereafter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth in fiction
2 CONT William Shakespeare's play Macbeth is loosely based upon the history inRaphael Holinshed's Chronicles of England, Scotland, and Ireland.Unfortunately its account of Macbeth was based on Hector Boece's 1527Scotorum Historiae which 
2 CONC flattered the antecedents of Boece's patron,king James IV of Scotland. This greatly maligned the real Macbeth, andhis Queen Gruoch (a princess royal reduced to the status of a mere"Lady"). But the principal objective of Shakespear
2 CONC e's play is politicalpatronage, not history. James VI & I, whose Stewart/Stuart line descendedfrom Malcolm III Canmore, was newly on the throne of England. The Stewartsuccession and James VI & I himself are obliquely refered to in
2 CONC  the play.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy Dunnett's novel King Hereafter (1982) retells the life of thehistorical Macbeth, but with a singular twist: Thorfinn of Orkney is notjust an ally and a relative, but is actually the same person as Macbethhimself. Dorothy D
2 CONC unnett explains here that her research indicated thatthis identification was historically plausible, but that she chose towrite the novel rather than spend more time attempting to establish orrefute the idea in a scholarly way.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Cargill Thompson's play Macbeth Speaks 1997, a reworking of hisearlier Macbeth Speaks, is a monologue delivered by Macbeth. It containsseveral novel historical ideas:
2 CONT
2 CONT that Macbeth came to the throne in 1034 rather than six years later, andDuncan was a rebel against him;
2 CONT that his wife Gruoch was the Macduff i.e. the royal heir
2 CONT that the purpose of his trip to Rome in 1050 was to petition Pope Leo IXto tell his bishops to leave the Celtic church alone.
1 NOTE Regerade 1040-1057
1 NOTE King MacBeth of Scotland
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=e58ef8ed-3614-484a-b84d-8968dd5af2ec&tid=7053141&pid=-1146507812
1 NOTE He was a commander for King Duncan I, and a chieftain of the Moray 
2 CONT district. After murdering Duncan in 1040, Macbeth claimed the kingdom and 
2 CONT ruled for 17 prosperous years. In 1057 he was killed in battle by Malcolm 
2 CONT Canmore (1031?-93), a son of Duncan I who was later, as Malcolm III, king 
2 CONT of Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth, a tragedy in five acts written by Shakespeare, is based on the 
2 CONT career of King Macbeth. Possibly performed as early as 1606, the play was 
2 CONT first printed in the edition of Shakespeare's works that was published in 
2 CONT 1623 and is known as the First Folio. The principal source used by the 
2 CONT author for this work was Chronicles of England, Scotland, and Ireland 
2 CONT (1577) by the English chronicler Raphael Holinshed. The tragedy is a 
2 CONT penetrating, concentrated, and harrowing study of ambition. In the 
2 CONT characters of Macbeth and his wife, Lady Macbeth, the play provides two 
2 CONT strong roles long regarded as attractive vehicles for the leading actors 
2 CONT of the world. Shakespeare's tragedy also provided the basis for the 
2 CONT libretto of the opera Macbeth (1847) by the Italian composer Giuseppe 
2 CONT Verdi.
1 NOTE "MORAY"; GENERAL OF DUNCAN; KING 1040-1057; KILLED IN BATTLE
1 NOTE Regerade 1040-1057
1 NOTE King of Scots from 1040, the legend of whose life was the basis of Shakespeare's Macbeth. He was probably a [great (kn)] grandson of King Kenneth II (ruled 971-995), and he married Gruoch, a descendant of King Kenneth III (ruled 9
2 CONC 97-1005). About 1031 Macbeth succeeded his father, Findlaech (Sinel in Shakespeare), as mormaer, or chief, in the province of Moray, in northern Scotland. Macbeth established himself on the throne after killing his cousin King Dun
2 CONC can I in battle near Elgin--not, as in Shakespeare, by murdering Duncan in bed--on Aug. 14, 1040. Both Duncan and Macbeth derived their rights to the crown through their mothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth's victory in 1045 over a rebel army, near Dunkeld (in modern Tayside region) may account for the later references (in Shakespeare and others) to Birnam Wood, for the village of Birnam is near Dunkeld. In 1046 Siward, Earl 
2 CONC of Northumbria, unsuccessfully attempted to dethrone Macbeth in favour of Malcolm (afterward King Malcolm III Canmore), eldest son of Duncan I. By 1050 Macbeth felt secure enough to leave Scotland for a pilgrimage to Rome. But in 
2 CONC 1054 he was apparently forced by Siward to yield part of southern Scotland to Malcolm. Three years later Macbeth was killed in battle by Malcolm, with assistance from the English.
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth was buried on the island of Iona, regarded as the resting place of lawful kings but not of usurpers. His followers installed his stepson, Lulach, as king; when Lulach was killed on March 17, 1058, Malcolm III was left supr
2 CONC eme in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Acceded: 1040. King of Scotland.
1 NOTE He gained the title of Mormaer of Moray between 1029 and 1032. He succeeded to the title of King MacBeth of Scotland on 14/8/1040. He fought in the battle of Lumphanan on 15/8/1057. He succeeded Duncan I, who he killed in battle a
2 CONC t Pitgaveny, near Elgin. MacBeth himself had no rightful claim to the throne, but his wife Gruoch was a grandaughter of Kenneth II, who was murdered by Malcolm II, Duncan's grandfather. This killing re-opened the bloody feud that 
2 CONC ravaged the MacAlpin dynasty 40 years earlier. Duncan is thought to have provoked macBeth by interfering in the affairs of Moray. He died of wounds in a skirmish with his rival, Malcolm succeeded by Lulach, the so of his wife by h
2 CONC er first marriage.
