1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Máel Mórda mac Murchada
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Mórda mac Murchada (died 23 April 1014) was King of Leinster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Murchad mac Brain and brother of Gormflaith, he belonged to the Uí Fáeláin sept of the Uí Dúnlainge, whose lands lay around Naas on the middle reaches of the River Liffey, in modern County Kildare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Máel Mórda is best known as the enemy of Brian Bóruma; both men died in the bloody battle of Clontarf, fought on Good Friday in 1014. Máel Mórda's son Bran was king of Leinster from 1016 until he was deposed in 1018. Thereafter, p
2 CONC erhaps as a result of the heavy losses at Clontarf, the kingship of the Uí Dúnlainge was held by the Uí Muiredaig sept.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%A1el_M%C3%B3rda_mac_Murchada
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000079500369807?from_flash=1&through=6000000079500369807&fsession_id=1529777196241 MURCHAD] (-972).  King of Leinster 965.  The Annals of the Four Masters record that “Murchadh son of Finn k
2 CONC ing of Leinster” led an army “into Osraighe” in 965[613].  The Annals of Inisfallen record the death in 972 of "Murchad son of Finn king of Laigin”[614].  m ---.  The name of Murchad´s wife is not known.  Murchad & his wife had th
2 CONC ree children:
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>a)         '''[https://www.geni.com/people/Mael-M%C3%B3rda-King-of-Leinster/6000000079501380104?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529777196241&through=6000000079501380104  MAELMORDA] (-killed in battle Clontarf 23 Apr 1014).  King of 
2 CONC Leinster.  The Annals of Ulster record that "Brian son of Ceinnéitig son of Lorcán king of Ireland and Mael Sechnaill son of Domnall king of Temair" led an army to “Áth Cliath” in 1014, adding that “Mael Mórda son of Murchad king 
2 CONC of Laigin” was killed in the battle[615].  The Annals of the Four Masters record that “Murchadh son of Finn king of Leinster” was killed in 1013 in the battle [of Clontarf][616].  m ---.  The name of Mail-mordha´s wife is not know
2 CONC n.  Mail-mordha & his wife had two children''': 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>i)          CEARBALL (-killed 1017).  The Annals of Tigernach record that “Cearball mac Mail-mordha crownprince of Leinster” was killed in 1017[617]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>ii)         BRAEN (-Köln 1052).  King of Leinster.  The Annals of Ulster record that "Braen son of Mael Mórda king of Laigin” was blinded in 1018 “in Áth Cliath by Sitriuc son of Amlaib”[618]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>b)         GORMLAITH (-1030).  The Annals of Tigernach record the death in 1030 of “Gormlaith, daughter of Murchad son of Fland” mother of “Sitric son of Olaf king of the Foreigners and of Donnchad son of Brian king of Munster
2 CONC ”[619].  Her third marriage is confirmed by the Annals of the Four Masters which record the death in 1030 of “Gormlaith daughter of Murchadh son of Finn, mother of the king of the foreigners Sitric, Donnchadh son of Brian king of 
2 CONC Munster, and Conchobhar son of Maeleachlainn king of Teamhair”[620].  It is assumed that Maelsechnaill separated from Gormlaith as he is recorded with another wife before Gormlaith died.  m firstly as his third wife, OLAF Sihtrics
2 CONC son King of Dublin, son of SIHTRIC King of York & his first wife --- (-981).  m secondly (after 981, [separated]) [as his second wife,] BRIAN Boroma, son of CEINNÉITIG & his wife --- (941-killed in battle Clontarf 23 Apr 1014).  m
2 CONC  thirdly ([separated]) as his [third] wife, MAELSECHNAILL King of Ireland, son of DOMNALL & his wife --- ([949/50]-Cró Inis of Lough Ennell 2 Sep 1022). 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>c)         BRAEN (-killed in battle Teamhair [978/80]).  The Annals of the Four Masters record the death in 973 of “Ceallach son of Domhnall, lord of Ui-Faelain” killed by “Broen son of Murchadh”[621].  The Annals of the Four 
2 CONC Masters record that “Braen son of Murchadh royal heir of Leinster” was killed in 978 in “the battle of Teamhair”[622], the battle being dated between 978 and 980 in different sources. 
2 CONT *[http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/IRELAND.htm Charles Cawley's Medieval Lands: IRELAND] 
