1 NAME Muirchertach mac Muiredaig - High King of Ireland
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 466
2 ADDR
3 CITY Letterkenny
3 CTRY Ireland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 NOV 534
1 OCCU King of Ailech
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Muirchertach mac Muiredaig (c. 466 – 534), universally recognized in historical lore as Muirchertach mac Ercae, was a dominant early medieval warlord, sovereign King of Ailech, and widely regarded as the first Chri
2 CONC stian High King of Ireland. A figure of towering martial power, he elevated the Cenél nÉogain branch of the Northern Uí Néill into the supreme force of the Irish midlands and north, permanently altering the island's political traj
2 CONC ectory through relentless warfare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muirchertach is uniquely distinct in early Irish genealogy for carrying his mother’s name as his primary cognomen (mac Ercae). His mother was Erca ingen Loarn, a royal princess of Scottish Dalriada. Taking a matronymic title was a
2 CONC  powerful political statement, directly advertising his maternal royal bloodlines across the Irish Sea to secure regional alliances. Within early Irish records, his identity frequently merges with other regional warlords of the er
2 CONC a, demonstrating his pervasive presence across multiple battle fronts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muirchertach’s reign was an unending series of violent campaigns designed to extort submission from rival provinces. In 482, he achieved massive renown by joining forces with Lugaid mac Lóegairi to crush and kill the rival king Ai
2 CONC lill Molt at the bloody Battle of Ochae in the midlands. Throughout the 480s and 490s, Muirchertach launched repeated, aggressive offenses southward into Leinster, winning major territorial victories at the Battles of Grainert, Ce
2 CONC ll Losnaid, and Inne Mór. By securing the submission of the Airgialla and local midland tribes, he successfully claimed the ultimate seat of sovereign authority at the historic Hill of Tara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muirchertach’s death in 534 is one of the most famous and highly stylized accounts in all of Celtic mythology, recorded in the historical saga Aided Muirchertaig meic Erca (The Slaying of Muirchertach son of Erca). Having abandone
2 CONC d his queen for a mysterious, otherworldly fairy woman named Sín, the king fell under a pagan curse. Sín used illusions to make the king believe he was under siege by enemies. Floundering in confusion, Muirchertach suffered a clas
2 CONC sic Indo-European "Threefold Death": he was simultaneously severely wounded by an attacker, trapped in his burning royal house at Cleitech above the River Boyne, and ultimately drowned while attempting to hide inside a massive vat
2 CONC  of wine on the hilltop.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prolific Legacy and Descendants. Muirchertach's direct descendants formed the elite Cenél maic Ercae line, which held undisputed dominance over the northern half of Ireland for generations. His primary sons continued the dynasty's
2 CONC  expanding regional control:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Domnall Ilchealgach (Domnall of the Many Deceits, d. 566) – Succeeded to the High Kingship.
2 CONT #Forggus mac Muirchertaig (d. 566) – Co-ruled alongside his brother as High King.
2 CONT #Báetán mac Muirchertaig (d. 572) – King of Ailech and High King.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the traditional patrilineal lineages of Clan MacNeil, Muirchertach mac Ercae is strictly cataloged as the 4th Ancestral Chief. He represents the high-water mark of the clan's royal Irish ancestry, serving as the sovereign k
2 CONC ingly link before the ancestral lines began their historic geographic shift toward the Hebrides of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
