2 CONC meic-Cin%C3%A1eda-Princess-of-Scots/6000000001061869741?through=6000000001744982887 Donada] of Scotland ([1005]-killed in battle Lumphanan 15 Aug 1057, bur Isle of Iona).  He succeeded in 1040 as MACBETH King of Scotland.  Gruoch 
2 CONC & her [first husband] had [one child]: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>i)          [https://www.geni.com/people/Lulach-mac-Gille-Coemg%C3%A1in-King-of-Scots/6000000000307274746?through=6000000004533031237 '''LULACH'''] ([1032]-killed in battle Essie, Strathbogie 17 Mar 1058, bur Isle of Iona).  Th
2 CONC e 12th century Cronica Regum Scottorum lists "…Macheth filius Findleg xvii, Lulac nepos filii Boide iv mensibus et dimidium…" as kings[228].  The Continuation of the Synchronisms of Flann Mainistreach records Lulach as son of Macb
2 CONC eth[229].  The Chronicle of John of Fordun records that "on the death of Machabeus, some of his kinsfolk" installed "his cousin Lulath…the Simple" as king at Scone but that King Malcolm killed him 3 Apr 1057, adding that he was bu
2 CONC ried "in the island of Iona"[230].  He succeeded [his stepfather] in 1057 as LULACH "the Simple" King of Scotland, crowned Aug 1057 at Scone Abbey, Perthshire.  The Chronicon of Marianus Scottus records that "Lulag" was killed "[1
2 CONC 058]…in Martio"[231].  The Annals of Ulster record that "Lulach son of Gilla Comgain, over-king of Scotland was killed in battle by Mael Coluim son of Donnchad" in 1058[232], although curiously this entry precedes the record of th
2 CONC e death of King Macbeth in the same year.  The Annals of Tigernach record that “Lulach rí Alban” was killed by “Mael-Coluimb, son of Donnchad” in 1058[233], also preceding the record in the same source of the death of King Macbeth
2 CONC .  The Chronicle of the Scots and Picts dated 1177 records that "Lulach fatuus" reigned 4 months, was killed "in Esseg in Strathbolgin" and was buried "in Iona insula"[234].] 
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#_Toc209085741
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach appears to have been a weak king, as his nicknames suggest. He does, however, have the distinction of being the first king of Scotland of whom there are coronation details available. He was crowned in August 1057 at Scone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach was the son of Gruoch of Scotland, from her first marriage to Gille Coemgáin, Mormaer of Moray, and thus the stepson of Macbeth (Mac Bethad mac Findlaích). Following the death in battle of Macbeth in 1057, the king's follow
2 CONC ers placed Lulach in the throne. Lulach ruled only for a few months before being assassinated and succeeded by Malcolm III (Máel Coluim mac Donnchada).
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach's son Máel Snechtai was Mormaer of Moray, while Óengus of Moray was the son of Lulach's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is believed to be buried on Saint Columba's Holy Island of Iona in or around the monastery. The exact position of his grave is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT A fictional version of Lulach appeared in the animated television series Gargoyles under the name Luach [sic].
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach 'the Fool' of Scotland, King of Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #102886, b. circa 1030, d. 17 March 1058
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach 'the Fool' of Scotland, King of Scotland|b. c 1030\nd. 17 Mar 1058|p10289.htm#i102886|Gillacomgan Macrory, Earl of Moray|d. 1032|p10289.htm#i102884|Gruoch (?)|b. c 1015|p10529.htm#i105284|||||||Beoedhe of Scotland|d. b 1033
2 CONC |p10290.htm#i102895||||
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=29 Oct 2002
2 CONT
2 CONT      Lulach 'the Fool' of Scotland, King of Scotland was born circa 1030. He was the son of Gillacomgan Macrory, Earl of Moray and Gruoch (?). He married Finnghuala (?), daughter of Sinill, Mormaer of Angus.1 He died on 17 March 1
2 CONC 058 at Essie, Strathbogie, Scotland, a blow by King Malcolm III of Scotland.1 He was buried at Isle of Iona, Argyllshire, Scotland.1
2 CONT
2 CONT      Lulach 'the Fool' of Scotland, King of Scotland was crowned King of Scotland on 15 August 1057 at Scone Abbey, Scone, Perthshire, Scotland. He succeeded to the title of King Lulach of Scotland on 15 August 1057.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Lulach 'the Fool' of Scotland, King of Scotland and Finnghuala (?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Malsnechtai of Scotland, Mormaer of Moray d. 10851 
2 CONT
2 CONT unknown daughter of Scotland+ b. b 10581 
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 183. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach mac Gille Coemgáin (Modern Gaelic: Lughlagh mac Gille Chomghain,[1] known in English simply as Lulach, and nicknamed Tairbith, "the Unfortunate"[2] and Fatuus, "the Simple-minded" or "the Foolish"[3] (died 17 March 1058), w
2 CONC as King of Scots between 15 August 1057 and 17 March 1058.
2 CONT
2 CONT He appears to have been a weak king, as his nicknames suggest. He does, however, have the distinction of being the first king of Scotland of whom there are coronation details available. He was crowned in August 1057 at Scone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach was the son of Gruoch of Scotland, from her first marriage to Gille Coemgáin, Mormaer of Moray, and thus the stepson of Macbeth (Mac Bethad mac Findlaích). Following the death in battle of Macbeth in 1057, the king's follow
2 CONC ers placed Lulach in the throne. Lulach ruled only for a few months before being assassinated and succeeded by Malcolm III (Máel Coluim mac Donnchada).
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach's son Máel Snechtai was Mormaer of Moray, while Óengus of Moray was the son of Lulach's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is believed to be buried on Saint Columba's Holy Island of Iona in or around the monastery. The exact position of his grave is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach de Escocia
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT
2 CONT Escudo de armas de la Casa Real de EscociaLulach (Lulach mac Gille Coemgáin) (1030 - 17 de marzo de 1058), fue rey de los escoceses del 15 de agosto de 1057 al 17 de marzo de 1058.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parece haber sido un rey débil, y de hecho era conocido como Lulach el Simple o Lulach el Bufón. Tiene, sin embargo, la distinción de ser el primer rey de Escocia del que se conservan datos de su coronación. Fue coronado en agosto
2 CONC  de 1057 en Scone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach era hijo de Gruoch de Escocia (Lady Macbeth), y de su primer matrimonio con Gille Coemgáin, Mormaer de Moray, y por tanto hijastro de Mac Bethad. Tras la muerte en batalla de este último en 1057, los seguidores del rey situ
2 CONC aron a Lulach en el trono, pese a la gran resistencia de la facción de Máel Coluim III. Lulach reinó tan sólo por unos meses, y fue finalmente asesinado y sucedido por Máel Coluim el 17 de marzo de 1058. Así acabó el periodo puram
2 CONC ente céltico de la historia de Escocia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach estaba casado con Finnghuala de Angus. Su hijo Máel Snechtai era Mormaer de Moray, mientras que Óengus de Moray era nieto de Lulach por parte de su hija.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se presupone que sus restos yacen en la isla sagrada de Saint Columba de Iona en, o alrededor del, monasterio. El lugar preciso de su tumba se desconoce.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach was the son of Gillacomgain, Mormaer of Moray and Gruoch, who was the grand-daughter of Kenneth III. He was born around 1029-32.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lulach's mother, Gruoch, had taken Macbeth as her second husband, making Lulach his step-son. On the death of Macbeth he succeeded to the throne. His is the first coronation of a King of Scotland for which recorded details survive
2 CONC . He was crowned on the ancient Coronation Stone at Scone Abbey on 15th August, 1057. Known as Lulach 'the Simple' or 'the Fool', there is evidence to believe that Lulach was mentally retarded or somewhat backward. Lulach was kill
2 CONC ed in battle by his successor, Malcolm III 'Canmore', at Essie in Strathbogie on 17th March, 1058.
2 CONT
2 CONT The line of Lulach continued, his daughter had a son, Angus, who inherited the title of Mormaer of Moray and made an unsuccessful attempt to claim the throne in the reign of David I. Which ended in his defeat and death in 1130.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Crowned August 1057 at Scone. Assassinated after a few months and succeeded by MaelColuin III
2 CONT
2 CONT Believed to be buried on St Columbas's Holy Island of Iona with rest of Gaelic kings
2 CONT
2 CONT ------http://www.biographicon.com/view/rlig4
2 CONT -------
