1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Muirchertach mac Néill (c. 890 – 943), widely celebrated in history as Muirchertach of the Leather Cloaks (Muirchertach na Cochall Craicinn), was an exceptionally powerful 10th-century King of Ailech and the design
2 CONC ated heir (Rígdamna) to the High Kingship of Ireland. He earned his famous nickname from the heavy sheepskin leather cloaks he commissioned for his soldiers to protect them during freezing winter campaigns.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Niall Glúndub mac Áedo, the 170th High King of Ireland (d. 919).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Gormlaith ingen Flann Sinna, an iconic Irish queen and poet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Flann ingen Donnchaid, a princess of Mide and daughter of High King Donnchad Donn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muirchertach rose to prominence after his father was killed by the Vikings in 919. Assuming military leadership of the Northern Uí Néill, he became one of the most relentless and successful anti-Viking commanders of his era, repea
2 CONC tedly defeating the Norse forces of Dublin, Newry, and the Hebrides.
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest military achievement occurred in the winter of 941, when he launched a legendary grand circuit of Ireland. Leading a synchronized march around the island, he took royal hostages from every provincial kingdom—including
2 CONC  the King of Dublin and the King of Munster—to completely secure his future succession to the High Kingship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before he could officially claim the High Throne, Muirchertach was killed in a fierce vanguard battle against the forces of Blácaire mac Gofrith, King of Dublin, at Ardee on 26 February 943. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His death notice in the Annals of Ulster famously laments him as "the Hector of the western world."
2 CONT
2 CONT He left behind several children, 
2 CONT
2 CONT #Domnall ua Néill (c. 915 – 980) – Highly capable military reformer and 173rd High King of Ireland.
2 CONT #Flaithbertach (d. 949) 
2 CONT #Murchadh
2 CONT #Flann
2 CONT #Dúnflaith ingen Muirchertaig (d. 999)
2 CONT
2 CONT #(Melkorka Mýrkjartansdóttir is classified as an alleged or semi-legendary daughter from the Laxdæla saga.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
2 CONT the O'Neills of Clannaboy (b. 1939).
2 CONT 2. Jorge O'Neill, the O'Neill of Clannaboy (b. 1908).
2 CONT 3. Hugo Joseph Jorge Ever O'Neill (1874-1940).
2 CONT 4. Jorge O'Neill, styled Count of Tyrone & Clannaboy (1848-1925).
2 CONT 5. Jorge Torlades O'Neill (1817-1890).
2 CONT 6. Jose-Maria O'Neill (b. 1788).
2 CONT 7. Carlos O'Neill (1760-1835).
2 CONT 8. Shane or Joao O'Neill, immigrated to Portugal (fl. 1740-1750).
2 CONT 9. Constantine O'Neill in Dublin.
2 CONT 10. Felim O'Neill (d. 1709).
2 CONT 11. Capt. Ever O'Neill (fl. ca. 1670).
2 CONT 12. Feidhlimidh Dubh O Neill (fl. 1640-1650).
2 CONT 13. Conn Buidhe O Neill (d. ca. 1630).
2 CONT 14. Domnall O Neill (d. bef. 1567).
2 CONT 15. Muircheartach Duileanach O Neill, the Mac Ui Neill Bhuidhe Iochtar
2 CONT (d. aft. 1556).
2 CONT 16. Niall Mor O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r. 1482-1512).
2 CONT 17. Conn O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r. 1468-1482).
2 CONT 18. Aodh Buidhe O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r. 1425-1444).
2 CONT 19. Brian Ballach O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r. 1395-1425).
2 CONT 20. Muircheartach Ceannfhada O Neill, Lord of Clann Aodha Buidhe (r.
2 CONT 1369-1395).
2 CONT 21. Enri O Neill, King of Tir Eogain (d. 1347).
2 CONT 22. Brian O Neill, King of Tir Eogain (r. 1291-1295).
2 CONT 23. Aed Buide O Neill, King of Tir Eogain (r. 1260-1261; 1263-1283).
2 CONT 24. Domnall Oc O Neill, King of Tir Eogain (r. 1230-1241).
2 CONT 25. Aed Meith O Neill, King of Tir Eogain (r. 1196-1230).
2 CONT 26. Aed In Macaem Toinlesc O Neill, King of Tir Eogain (r. 1166-1177).
2 CONT 27. Muircheartach Muighe Line O Neill, styled King of Tir Eogain (d.
2 CONT 1160).
2 CONT 28. Tadhg Glinne O Neill, styled King of Tir Eogain.
2 CONT 29. Conchobar na Fiodhbhuidhi O Neill, styled King of Tir Eogain.
2 CONT 30. Flaithbertach Locha Feadhaidh O Neill, styled King of Tir Eogain.
2 CONT 31. Domnall an tOgdhamh, styled King of Tir Eogain.
2 CONT 32. Aed O Neill, King of Ailech (d. 1033).
2 CONT 33. Flaithbertach Ua Neill, King of Ailech (r. 1004-1030; 1032-1036).
2 CONT 34. Muirchertach Ua Neill, King of Mide? (d. 977).
2 CONT 35. Domnall ua Neill, High-King of Ireland (r. 956-980).
2 CONT 36. Muirchertach mac Neill, King of Ailech (r. 938-943).
2 CONT 37. Niall Glundub, High-King of Ireland (r. 916-919).
2 CONT 38. Aed Findliath, High-King of Ireland (r. 862-879).
2 CONT 39. Niall Caille, High-King of Ireland (r. 833-846).
2 CONT 40. Aed Oirdnide, High-King of Ireland (r. 797-819).
2 CONT 41. Niall Frossach, High-King of Ireland (d. 778).
2 CONT 42. Fergal mac Maele Duin, High-King of Ireland (r. 710-722).
2 CONT 43. Mael Duin (d. 681).
2 CONT 44. Mael Fithrich (d. 630).
2 CONT 45. Aed Uaridnach, High-King of Ireland (r. 604-612).
2 CONT 46. Domnall, High-King of Ireland (r. 565-566).
2 CONT 47. Muirchertach Mac Ercae, High-King of Ireland (r. 507-534/36).
2 CONT 48. Muiredach.
2 CONT 49. Eogan.
2 CONT 50. Niall Noigiallach, High-King of Ireland (r. ca. 445-453?).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official traditions of Clan MacNeil, Muirchertach is recognized as the 15th Ancestral Chief. While his historical title was King of Ailech rather than a Scottish clan chief, he represents a crucial link in the tradition
2 CONC al lineage connecting the clan to the royal Uí Néill dynasty of Ireland.
2 CONT
