1 NOTE Kenneth MacAlpin
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT (Redirected from Kenneth I of Scotland)
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT Kenneth MacAlpin
2 CONT (Cináed mac Ailpín)
2 CONT King of the Picts
2 CONT Image:History-kenneth.jpg
2 CONT Reign 	843–858
2 CONT Died 	13 February 858
2 CONT Place of death 	Cinnbelachoir
2 CONT Buried 	Iona
2 CONT Predecessor 	See text
2 CONT Successor 	Donald I (Domnall mac Ailpín)
2 CONT Offspring 	Constantine I (Causantín mac Cináeda)
2 CONT Áed
2 CONT Máel Muire ingen Cináeda
2 CONT perhaps others
2 CONT Royal House 	Alpin
2 CONT Father 	Alpín mac Echdach
2 CONT
2 CONT Cináed mac Ailpín (Modern Gaelic: Coinneach mac Ailpein)[1], commonly Anglicised as Kenneth MacAlpin and known in most modern regnal lists as Kenneth I (died 13 February 858) was king of the Picts and, according to national myth, 
2 CONC first king of Scots, earning him the posthumous nickname of An Ferbasach, "The Conqueror".[2] Kenneth's undisputed legacy was to produce a dynasty of rulers who claimed descent from him. Even though he cannot be regarded as the fa
2 CONC ther of Scotland, he was the founder of the dynasty which ruled that country for much of the medieval period.
1 NOTE Kenneth I of Scotland
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=image&guid=1be74788-3fb8-4ed4-ba2a-05c9d7706f47&tid=7053141&pid=-1146462007
1 NOTE 1  UID 0BA30D0F1821AA4CA0B3E7C41FAB19CBA52E
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Founder of the Kingdom of Scotland
1 NOTE King Kenneth I
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=d0a3ba25-81a3-4821-be38-e2c76250d3d6&tid=7053141&pid=-1145799933
1 NOTE Source: THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry.  Chart: page 45.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 44:
2 CONT
2 CONT 14.    Kenneth I MacAlpin (Cinaed the Hardy), d. 858; came to the throne of Dalriada of the Scots 843 when the land was still made up of small kingdoms whose people were Scots, Picts, Angles and Britons.  He united Dalriada and Pi
2 CONC ctland 844 in a partnership never broken thereafter.  He himself was of Scottish-Pictish descent.  The new union was well timed, as the Norse incursions were becoming a serious threat.  Kenneth invaded Lothian of the Angles six ti
2 CONC mes, burning Dunbar and taking possessions of Melrose.  He is said to have called a Parliament.  About 850 he brought relics of St. Columba from Iona to Dunkeld, which was made the head church of his kingdom.  He d. of a fistula a
2 CONC t Forteviot, near Perth.  His brother ruled as Domnall I until 862.
2 CONT
2 CONT <I<<Dunkeld, Pertshire, is one of the most historic places in Scotland.  At an early date it emerged as an important center of the Celtic Church.  About 815, Constantine, King of the Picts, rebuilt the abbey for the Celtic monks o
2 CONC f Iona who had been displaced by the Norsemen.  Although its dignity as headquarters of the Scottish Church passed to St. Andrews, Dunkeld continued to be a major religious center.>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amie, Adam and FAL
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 145 line (170-13):
2 CONT
2 CONT 13.    Cinaed.  This is the famous Kenneth Mac Alpin, King of the Picts and Scots, 843-d. 858.  (For more details on generations 1-13, see also H. Pirie-Gordon "Succession of the Kingdom of Strathclyde" The Armorial vol. I pp. 35-
2 CONC 40, 79-87, 143-148, 192-196; vol. II 9-14,92-102, with cited authorities.  This reference also provides the descent to Kenneth MacAlpin of the lines of the Kings of Strathclyde and of the Picts.)
1 NOTE From Genealogical Library book "House of Adam".
1 NOTE Sucedeu ao pai em 841. Em 843 adquiriu ou conquistou a Pictavia. Em 843 os Pictos tinham unido suas terras com as de Dal Riata e aceitaram em 846 como seu rei KENNETH MAC ALPIN, rei dos escoceses. Uma das razões seria que Kenneth 
2 CONC mac Alpin descendia da linha real picta por sua mãe. Como os pictos eram uma sociedade matriarcal, mac Alpin tinha provavelmente direito de unir os dois reinos Mas lamentavelmente, poucas gerações mais tarde, a lingua e cultura si
2 CONC ngular dos Pictos se perdeu.
1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT See Europäisch Stammtafeln Bund II tafel 67.
