1 NOTE Constantine I of Scotland
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT Constantine II
2 CONT (Causantín mac Cináeda)
2 CONT King of the Picts
2 CONT
2 CONT 18th century depiction of Constantine, son of Kenneth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín), called "Constantinus II" (in reference to Constantine son of Fergus, King of the Picts, as Constantinus I). The depiction is highly anachronistic.
2 CONT Reign 	862–877
2 CONT Died 	877
2 CONT Place of death 	Inverdovat?
2 CONT Buried 	Iona
2 CONT Predecessor 	Donald I (Domnall mac Ailpín)
2 CONT Successor 	Áed (Áed mac Cináeda)
2 CONT Offspring 	Donald II (Domnall mac Causantín)
2 CONT Royal House 	Alpin
2 CONT Father 	Kenneth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín)
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine, son of Cináed (Mediaeval Gaelic: Causantín mac Cináeda; Modern Gaelic: Còiseam mac Choinnich), known in most modern regnal lists as Constantine I[1], nicknamed An Finn-Shoichleach, "The Wine-Bountiful"[2] (d.877) was 
2 CONC a son of Kennneth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín). Although tradition makes Constantine and his father King of Scots, it is clear from the entries in the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba and the Annals of Ulster, that he was, like his 
2 CONC father, king of the Picts. He became king in 862 on the death of his uncle Donald MacAlpin (Domnall mac Ailpín).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 866, the Chronicle states that Pictland — the Annals of Ulster say Fortriu — was ravaged by Vikings led by Amlaíb Conung (Olaf) and Auisle (Ásl or Auðgísl). The Chronicle claims that Amlaíb was killed by Constantine that year, 
2 CONC but this is either incorrectly dated, or a different Amlaíb is intended as the Irish annals make it clear that Amlaíb Conung was alive long after 866. A date of 874 has been proposed for this event.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 870, Amlaíb Conung and Ímar captured Alt Clut, chief place of the kingdom of Strathclyde. The king, Artgal, was among the many captives. The Annals of Ulster say that Artgal was killed "at the instigation of Causantín mac Cináe
2 CONC da" (Constantine son of Kenneth) in 872. Artgal's son Run was married to a sister of Constantine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 875, the Chronicle and the Annals of Ulster again report a Viking army in Pictland. A battle, fought near Dollar, was a heavy defeat for the Picts; the Annals of Ulster say that "a great slaughter of the Picts resulted". Althou
2 CONC gh there is agreement that Constantine was killed fighting Vikings in 877, it is not clear where this happened. Some believe he was beheaded on a Fife beach, following a battle at Fife Ness, near Crail. William Forbes Skene read t
2 CONC he Chronicle as placing Constantine's death at Inverdovat (by Newport-on-Tay), which appears to match the Prophecy of Berchán. The account in the Chronicle of Melrose names the place as the "Black Cave" and John of Fordun calls it
2 CONC  the "Black Den". Constantine was buried on Iona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine's son Donald II and his descendants represented the main line of the kings of Alba and later Scotland.
