1 NOTE From Genealogical Library book "House of Adam".
1 NOTE Konstantin (Causantín mac Cináeda) (født 836, død 877) var konge av Skottland fra 863 til sin død. Han var sønn av kong Kenneth I, og etterfulgte sin onkel Donald I på tronen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gjennom det meste av sin tid som konge kjempet han mot vikinger eller prøvde å ekspandere riket sørover. I 864 angrep Olav Kvite av Dublin, men han ble raskt ble raskt slått av Konstantin. Det var så fred med de nordiske kongen
2 CONC e en tid, inntil Torstein den Røde ledet nye angrep. Disse ble også raskt slått tilbake.
2 CONT
2 CONT I 872 fikk han myrdet Arthgal av Strathclyde, som var hans svoger, og tok dermed kontroll over sørlige Skottland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Selv om han normalt utmerket seg som en god leder i strid, benyttet Konstantin seg også av bestikkelser til rivaler for å bevare freden. Dette var en politikk som senere skulle bli brukt i Skottland av en engelsk konge, Ethelred
2 CONC  den rådville, i år 1000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Til slutt ble vikingene hans bane. En gruppe fra Dublin som utførte plyndringstokter i Skottland slo seg til i Fife, og Konstantin møtte dem for å drive dem ut. I 877 falt han i kamp mot vikinger ved ?Den svarte hulen? (Inverdova
2 CONC t) i Forgan, Fife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han ble etterfulgt av sin bror Aedh, og hans sønn Donald ble konge etter Eochaids og Girics samkongedømme.
1 NOTE He died in action against the Danes. 
1 NOTE OR "CAUSANTIN"; KING OF SCOTS 863-877; KILLED IN BATTLE WITH THE DANES/NORSE
1 NOTE King of the Picts & Scots and all Alba, with his royal city at Scone
2 CONT Succeeded his Uncle Donald in 863 and reigned until 877 when he was fell in battle with the Danes. {Burke�s Peerage} [GADD.GED]
1 NOTE King of Scotland or Alba, the united kingdom of the Picts and Scots, who succeeded his uncle Donald I. Constantine's reign was occupied with conflicts with the Norsemen. Olaf the White, the Danish king of Dublin, laid waste the co
2 CONC untry of the Picts and Britons year after year; in the south the Danish leader Halfdan devastated Northumberland and Galloway. Constantine was slain at a battle at Inverdovat in Fife, at the hands of another band of northern marau
2 CONC ders. His heir was his brother Aed, who was killed by the Scots after a year and was succeeded by a nephew, Eochaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cause of his death was killed in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Acceded: King of Scotland or Alba, 862.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Burke's Peerage 99th ed (GS #942 D22bup prefix pp. 285-86)
2 CONT     2. A Vindication of MacBeth (GS #929.2706 M288c p. 14)
2 CONT     3. Dict. of Nat'l Biog. (GS #920.042 D561v vol 12 pp. 46-47)
2 CONT     4. Also searched without possitive results: Encyclopedia Britannica, Scots Peerage, The Complete Peerage, Royal Dau of Engl, and Queens of Scotland and English Princes.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Burke's Peerage 99th ed (GS #942 D22bup prefix pp. 285-86)
2 CONT     2. A Vindication of MacBeth (GS #929.2706 M288c p. 14)
2 CONT     3. Dict. of Nat'l Biog. (GS #920.042 D561v vol 12 pp. 46-47)
2 CONT     4. Also searched without possitive results: Encyclopedia Britannica, Scots Peerage, The Complete Peerage, Royal Dau of Engl, and Queens of Scotland and English Princes.
1 NOTE He succeeded his uncle, Domnall in 862, establishing a pattern of alternating succession whereby the kingship passed not from father to son but between brother and brother or from uncle to nephew or cousin to cousin.  Castantin fa
2 CONC ced threats on several fronts from a variety of Scandanavian invaders. A treaty with Olafr of Norway resulted in the marriage of his sister to him. He also counselled his brother-in-law to kill the captive King Arthgal of Strathcl
2 CONC yde.  After Olafr's death, raids intensified, and in 877 Castantin was killed in battle with Norwegians. His younger brother, Áed, took the throne.
1 NOTE He succeeded his uncle, Domnall in 862, establishing a pattern of alternating succession whereby the kingship passed not from father to son but between brother and brother or from uncle to nephew or cousin to cousin.  Castantin fa
2 CONC ced threats on several fronts from a variety of Scandanavian invaders. A treaty with Olafr of Norway resulted in the marriage of his sister to him. He also counselled his brother-in-law to kill the captive King Arthgal of Strathcl
2 CONC yde.  After Olafr's death, raids intensified, and in 877 Castantin was killed in battle with Norwegians. His younger brother, Áed, took the throne.
