2 CONT  BRAN .  m ---.  The name of Bran´s wife is not known.  Bran & his wife had one child:   a)         MAELMUAD (-killed Belach Lechta 978).  King of Caisel.  The Annals of Tigernach record that “Mathgamain son of Cenn-étig king of M
2 CONC unster” was killed in [974/75] by “Mael-Muad son of Bran king of the Húi Echach (Iveagh)” after being “treacherously delivered up by Donnában son of Cathal king of the Húi Fidgenti”[943].  The Annals of the Four Masters call him “
2 CONC Maelmhuaidh son of Bran, lord of Desmond” when recording the same incident[944].  The Annals of Inisfallen record that "Brian son of Cennétig” defeated and killed “Mael Muad son of Bran king of Caisel” at “the battle of Belach Lec
2 CONC hta” in 978[945].  The Annals of the Four Masters record that “Maelmhuaidh lord of Desmond” was killed in 976 at “the battle of Bealach-Leachta [with] Brian son of Cenneidigh”[946].  m ---.  The name of Mael Muad´s wife is not kno
2 CONC wn.  Mael Muad & his wife had four children:   i)          CIAN (-killed in battle [1013/14]).  The Annals of Inisfallen record the death in 1014 of "Cian son of Mael Muad and his brother”[947].  The Annals of the Four Masters rec
2 CONC ord that “Cian, Cathal and Roghallach, three sons of Maelmhuaidh” were killed in 1013 in the battle between the “Ui-Eathach themselves, i.e. between Cian son of Maelmhuaidh and Domhnall son of Dubh-da-bhoireann”[948].   ii)       
2 CONC   CATHAL (-killed in battle [1013/14]).  The Annals of Inisfallen record the death in 1014 of "Cian son of Mael Muad and his brother”[949].  The Annals of the Four Masters record that “Cian, Cathal and Roghallach, three sons of Ma
2 CONC elmhuaidh” were killed in 1013 in the battle between the “Ui-Eathach themselves, i.e. between Cian son of Maelmhuaidh and Domhnall son of Dubh-da-bhoireann”[950].   iii)        ROGHALLACH (-killed in battle [1013/14]).  The Annals
2 CONC  of the Four Masters record that “Cian, Cathal and Roghallach, three sons of Maelmhuaidh” were killed in 1013 in the battle between the “Ui-Eathach themselves, i.e. between Cian son of Maelmhuaidh and Domhnall son of Dubh-da-bhoir
2 CONC eann”[951].   iv)       son .  The Annals of Inisfallen record the death in 1015 of "Domnall son of Dub dá Bairenn in a battle against the son of Bran”[952].  
