1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall ua Néill (925 – 980), historically recorded as Domnall Arda Macha (Domnall of Armagh), was a powerful early medieval monarch, King of Ailech, and High King of Ireland. Representing the Northern Uí Néill dynasty, he was the
2 CONC  very first ruler to formally introduce and popularize the hereditary surname Ó Néill (meaning "grandson of Niall"), adopting it in direct honor of his iconic grandfather, Niall Glúndub. A warrior of exceptional tactical foresight
2 CONC , Domnall's long reign permanently altered Irish warfare through the introduction of heavy, mobile maritime fleets. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To combat the heavily fortified strongholds of midland rivals and Viking longphorts, Domnall constructed a massive network of portable river boats. His warriors dragged these heavy transport fleets overland from the northern coast
2 CONC lines, launching them directly into the waters of Lough Neagh, the River Shannon, and Lough Erne. This gave his northern forces unparalleled regional mobility, allowing him to bypass treacherous mountain passes, strike deep into t
2 CONC he heart of Connacht, and extract heavy cattle-tribute from territories previously considered unreachable.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hegemony and the Midland Campaigns. Domnall’s career was defined by an unyielding, bitter rivalry with the Southern Uí Néill king, Domnall mac Murchada. Upon ascending to the High Kingship of Ireland in 956 following the death of 
2 CONC Congalach Cnogba, Domnall aggressively pushed his boundaries southward. He launched a massive, multi-year punitive campaign against the Kingdom of Meath, carving heavy trenches across the midlands and burning ancient monastic cent
2 CONC ers used as economic hubs by his rivals. Although his southern offensives met fierce resistance and temporary tactical setbacks, he successfully asserted his sovereign authority at the Hill of Tara, forcing regional rulers into di
2 CONC rect submission.
2 CONT
2 CONT After an exhausting twenty-four-year reign defined by constant border conflicts, Domnall chose a peaceful, deeply religious path at the end of his life. In 978, suffering from advanced age and the physical toll of decades in the s
2 CONC addle, he formally abdicated his royal authority. He entered the sacred monastic precinct of Armagh as a religious penitent, spending his final years in quiet contemplation. Domnall passed away peacefully in his monastic cell in 9
2 CONC 80 and was buried with full sovereign honors within the holy grounds of Armagh, earning him his posthumous historical name, Domnall Arda Macha.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall's marriage to Mór ingen Congalaig (a prominent midland princess) secured crucial regional ties, ensuring the survival of the newly minted Ó Néill line:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Muiredach mac Domnaill Ó Néill (d. 980) – His primary son, who ruled as King of Ailech before being slain in battle.
2 CONT #Muirchertach Ó Néill (d. 977) – A prominent warrior prince who was killed during the intense border conflicts against the Kingdom of Meath.
2 CONT #Áed Ó Néill (d. 1004) – King of Ailech, who fiercely maintained the family's northern borders.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the ancient patrilineal lineages preserved by Clan MacNeil of Scotland, Domnall ua Néill is formally recognized as the 10th Ancestral Chief. He stands as a towering genealogical landmark in the clan's lineage—the absolute f
2 CONC irst individual to bear the ancestral "O'Neill" moniker, cementing the family name that his descendants carried across the sea to the Western Isles.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
