2 CONT * Name: '''King Donald II of Scotland'''
2 CONT * Father: King Constantine I
2 CONT * Mother: unknown
2 CONT * Relation to Elizabeth II: 31st great-grandfather
2 CONT * House of: MacAlpin
2 CONT * Ascended to the throne: 889
2 CONT * Married: unknown
2 CONT * Children: Malcolm I
2 CONT * Died: 900, killed at Dunnottar, Aberdeen
2 CONT * Buried at: Isle of Iona
2 CONT * Succeeded by: his cousin Constantine
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Donald (Domnall mac Causantín)''' was the son of Constantine. He succeeded Eochaid and Giric and annexed Srathclyde, establishling himself as King of the Scots. He had some success against the Vikings in the West, but the North
2 CONC  was dominated by Viking Sigurd the Mighty based in Orkney. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Donald was killed in 900, possibly murdered, at Donnattar near Stonehaven, Aberdeenshire. He was buried on Iona
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Donald II, King of Alba 
2 CONT
2 CONT      Donald II, King of Alba was the son of Constantine I, King of Alba. He died in 900 at Dunfother, Scotland, killed in action.1 He was buried at Isle of Iona, Argyllshire, Scotland.1
2 CONT      He was also known as Domnall mac Causantín.2 He succeeded to the title of  King Donald II of Alba in 889.1 
2 CONT      Succeeded the joint rule of Giric and Eochaid. Succeeded by Constantine II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Donald II, King of Alba 1.Malcolm I, King of Alba+ b. c 897, d. 954
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 169. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families. 2.[S130] Wikipedia, online http;//www.wikipedia.org. H
2 CONC ereinafter cited as Wikipedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10290.htm#i102891
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of Scotland 899/00. Dun-fother. Killed in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes  ◦Other parishes in Scotland may boast themselves the birthplace of greater men than can Fordoun, but no district in Scotland has seen the death of so many Sovereigns.
2 CONT  Donald, King of Alban (889 - 900) was killed in a battle between the Danes and the Scots.
2 CONT  "Over Fotherdun upon the brink of the waves he lies,
2 CONT  In the east, in his broad, gory bed"
2 CONT  says St Berchan.
2 CONT  Dr Skene identifies this with the parish of Fordoun, although referring to Dun Fother or Dunnottar as the locality. The Kingdom of Alban extended from the Forth to the Spey.
2 CONT  (http://www.auchenblae.net/strathfinella.htm)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources  1.[S327] Lakey - Genealogy, Gilbert Marlow Lakey, (http://members.cox.net/benchrest/Genealogy.html)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.[S370] Kings & Queens, Neil Grant, (pub 2003 by HarperCollinsPublishers Hammersmith London W6 8JB), p8 (Reliability: 3)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.[S410] Pedigrees of the Scottish Clans, John D McLaughlin, (http://members.aol.com/lochlan4/pedigree.htm), Genelaig Albanensium - (Pedigree of the Kings of Scotland) (Reliability: 3)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Domnall mac Causantín,  anglicized as Donald II, was King of the Picts or King of Scotland (Alba) in the late 9th century. He was the son of Constantine I (Causantín mac Cináeda). Donald is given the epithet Dásachtach, "the Madma
2 CONC n", by the Prophecy of Berchán.
2 CONT
2 CONT The change from king of the Picts to king of Alba is seen as indicating a step towards the kingdom of the Scots, but historians, while divided as to when this change should be placed, do not generally attribute it to Donald in vie
2 CONC w of his epithet. The consensus view is that the key changes occurred in the reign of Constantine II (Causantín mac Áeda), but the reign of Giric has also been proposed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba has Donald succeeded by his cousin Constantine II. Donald's son Malcolm (Máel Coluim mac Domnall) was later king as Malcolm I. The Prophecy of Berchán appears to suggest that another king reigned
2 CONC  for a short while between Donald II and Constantine II, saying "half a day will he take sovereignty". Possible confirmation of this exists in the Chronicon Scotorum, where the death of "Ead, king of the Picts" in battle against t
2 CONC he Uí Ímair is reported in 904. This, however, is thought to be an error, referring perhaps to Ædwulf, the ruler of Bernicia, whose death is reported in 913 by the other Irish annals.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_II_of_Scotland
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Name: King Donald II of Scotland Father: King Constantine I Mother: unknown Relation to Elizabeth II: 31st great-grandfather House of: MacAlpin Ascended to the throne: 889 Married: unknown Children: Malcolm I Died: 900, killed at 
2 CONC Dunnottar, Aberdeen Buried at: Isle of Iona Succeeded by: his cousin Constantine Donald (Domnall mac Causantín) was the son of Constantine. He succeeded Eochaid and Giric and annexed Srathclyde, establishling himself as King of th
2 CONC e Scots. He had some success against the Vikings in the West, but the North was dominated by Viking Sigurd the Mighty based in Orkney.
2 CONT
2 CONT Donald was killed in 900, possibly murdered, at Donnattar near Stonehaven, Aberdeenshire. He was buried on Iona -------------------- Donald II, King of Alba
2 CONT
2 CONT Donald II, King of Alba was the son of Constantine I, King of Alba. He died in 900 at Dunfother, Scotland, killed in action.1 He was buried at Isle of Iona, Argyllshire, Scotland.1 He was also known as Domnall mac Causantín.2 He s
2 CONC ucceeded to the title of King Donald II of Alba in 889.1 Succeeded the joint rule of Giric and Eochaid. Succeeded by Constantine II. Child of Donald II, King of Alba 1.Malcolm I, King of Alba+ b. c 897, d. 954
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 169. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families. 2.[S130] Wikipedia, online http;//www.wikipedia.org. H
2 CONC ereinafter cited as Wikipedia.
2 CONT
