1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demm%C3%A1n_mac_Cairell
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT Demmán mac Cairill (died 572) was a king of the Dál Fiatach, and over-king of Ulaid in Ireland. He was the son of Cairell mac Muiredaig Muinderg (died 532), a previous Ulaid king and brother of Báetán mac Cairill (died 581).[1] He
2 CONC  ruled the Dál Fiatach and Ulaid from 557 to 572.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT According to the genealogies, he was fostered by a certain Domangart mac Predae, possibly Domangart Réti, King of Dál Riata.[3] He acquired the kingship of Ulaid by defeating and slaying Fergnae mac Oengusso Ibdaig at the Battle o
2 CONC f Druim Cleithe (Kilclief, modern County Down, Northern Ireland) and had the support of the Uí Echach Arda.[4] He himself was slain by the shepherds of Boirenn.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Garbae ingine Néilléne of the Cenél nEógain. He had five sons including Fiachnae mac Demmáin (died 627), a later king of Ulaid. His descendants the Clan Demmáin went on to monopolize the kingship of the Dál Fiata
2 CONC ch. His fort, Ráth Deamáin, stood just west of Crossgar in what is now the townland of Rademan in the parish of Kilmore, County Down, Northern Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT  Byrne, Table 6; Charles-Edwards, appendix XXI; Mac Niocaill, pg.74
2 CONT  the Book of Leinster gives him a reign of 11 years and Laud Synchronisms 14 years
