1 NOTE Grand Roi d'Irlande, roi de Leinster <li>RBS : He led the great army of Leath-chuinn, with by Murchadh, and Toirdhealbhach Ua Briain, King of Munster, into Connaught; and Aedh Ua Conchobhair set an ambuscade for them, so that Ua C
2 CONC onchobhair, lord of Ciarraighe-Luachra, and many persons along with him, were killed, in 1067. King of Leinster in Ireland. 177th Monarch of Ireland before 1072. Annals of the Four Masters 20 February 1072: "Diarmaid, son of Mael-
2 CONC na-mbo, King of Leinster, of the foreigners of Ath-cliath, and of Leath-Mogha-Nuadhat, was slain and beheaded in the battle of Odhbha, on Tuesday, the seventh of the Ides of February, the battle having been gained over him by Conc
2 CONC hobhar O'Maeleachlainn, King of Meath. / Diarmait, mac Maoil na m-Bó, rí Laighen, Gall Atha Cliath, & Leithe Mogha Nuadhat cosnamhthaigh Laighen frí Leith Cuinn, do mharbhadh, & do dhichenttadh h-i c-cath Odhbha dia Mairt an uii I
2 CONC d Febhru, iar maidhm in chatha fair lá Concobhar Ua Maoileachlainn, .i. ri Midhe." ( (an unknown value)). He died on 20 February 1072 in the Battle of Odhbha. He was killed in battle, and beheaded, by Conchobhar O'Maeleachlainn, K
2 CONC ing of Meath.  "Diarmaid, son of Mael-na-mbo, King of Leinster, of the foreigners of Ath-cliath, and of Leath-Mogha-Nuadhat, was slain and beheaded in the battle of Odhbha, on Tuesday, the seventh of the Ides of February, the batt
2 CONC le having been gained over him by. Annals of the Four Masters an unknown date : "Of the death of Diarmaid was said:  Two, seven times ten above one thousand, From the birth of Christ is reckoned, To this year, in which Diarmaid, F
2 CONC irst man in Leinster, fell. Diarmaid, of the ruddy-coloured aspect, A king who maintained the standard of war, Whose death brought scarcity of peace, The loss of the heroes of Ladhrann, with their ships. Comely youths were cut dow
2 CONC n there, Together with the head of Claire and Cualann. It caused in the breeze a noise not pleasant, The loss of the King of Riada of great valour. Until at Muillenn-Chul was slain A brave chieftain of a strong fortress, Until the
2 CONC  furious fire-brand fell by treachery, They found no hero who dared with him contend. It is a red wound through my firm heart; For the host from Caindruim it was not just To destroy our noble chief they had no right, It has quench
2 CONC ed their spirit greatly, Diarmaid of the laughing teeth under violent sorrow; There is not on account of his death banquet or feast; There will not be peace, there will not be armistice." ( (an unknown value)).
