2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Heimskringla, the saga of St Olav:
2 CONT
2 CONT It is related that in the days of Harald Harfager, the king of Norway, the islands of Orkney, which before had been only a resort for vikings, were settled . The first earl in the Orkney Islands was called Sigurd, who was a son of
2 CONC  Eystein Glumra, and brother of Ragnvald earl of More. After Sigurd his son Guthorm was earl for one year. After him Torf-Einar, a son of Ragnvald, took the earldom, and was long earl, and was a man of great power. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Torf-Einar, his sons Arnkel, Erlend, and Thorfin Hausakljufer (1) ruled over these lands. In their days came Eirik Blood-axe from Norway, and subdued these earls. Arnkel and Erlend fell in a war expedition; but Thorfin ruled
2 CONC  the country long, and became an old man. His sons were Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot, and Skule. Their mother was Grelad, a daughter of Earl Dungad of Caithness. Her mother was Groa, a daughter of Thorstein Raud. In the latter da
2 CONC ys of Earl Thorfin came Eirik Blood-axe's sons, who had fled from Earl Hakon out of Norway, and committed great excesses in Orkney. Earl Thorfin died on a bed of sickness, and his sons after him ruled over the country, and there a
2 CONC re many stories concerning them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hlodver lived the longest of them, and ruled alone over this country. His son was Sigurd the Thick, who took the earldom after him, and became a powerful man and a great warrior. In his days came Olaf Trygvason from his viking exp
2 CONC edition in the western ocean, with his troops, landed in Orkney and took Earl Sigurd prisoner in South Ronaldsha, where he lay with one ship. King Olaf allowed the earl to ransom his life by letting himself be baptized, adopting t
2 CONC he true faith, becoming his man, and introducing Christianity into all the Orkney Islands. As a hostage, King Olaf took his son, who was called Hunde or Whelp. Then Olaf went to Norway, and became king; and Hunde was several years
2 CONC  with King Olaf in Norway, and died there. After his death Earl Sigurd showed no obedience or fealty to King Olaf. He married a daughter of the Scottish king Malcolm, and their son was called Thorfin. Earl Sigurd had, besides, old
2 CONC er sons; namely, Sumarlide, Bruse, and Einar Rangmund. Four or five years after Olaf Tryggvason's fall Earl Sigurd went to Ireland, leaving his eldest sons to rule the country, and sending Thorfin to his mother's father, the Scott
2 CONC ish king. On this expedition Earl Sigurd fell in Brian's battle (l). When the news was received in Orkney, the brothers Sumarlide, Bruse, and Einar were chosen earls, and the country was divided into three parts among them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thorfin Sigurdson was five years old when Earl Sigurd fell. When the Scottish king heard of the earl's death he gave his relation Thorfin Caithness and Sutherland, with the title of earl, and appointed good men to rule the land fo
2 CONC r him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl Thorfin was ripe in all ways as soon as he was grown up: he was stout and strong, but ugly; and as soon as he was a grown man it was easy to see that he was a severe and cruel but a very clever man. So says Arnor, the earls' 
2 CONC skald: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Under the rim of heaven no other,
2 CONT
2 CONT So young in years as Einar's brother,
2 CONT
2 CONT In battle had a braver hand,
2 CONT
2 CONT Or stouter, to defend the land."
2 CONT
2 CONT ENDNOTES: (1) Hausakljufer -- the splitter of skulls. -- L. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Brian's battle is supposed to have taken place on the 23rd April 1014, at Clontart, near Dublin; and is known in Irish history as the battle of Clontarf, and was one of the bloodiest of the age. It was fought between a viking 
2 CONC called Sigtryg and Brian king of Munster, who gained the victory, but lost his life. -- L. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Earl of Orkney
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kilde: Fossmo/Berglund - slekta.  Birger Nytrøen 1995
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thorfinn was the youngest son of Torf-Einar. He may have ruled jointly with his brothers and they were all subject to Erik Bloodaxe of Denmark and later Eric's mother, Gunnhildr. Thorfinn's wife Gredlog was a daughter of Duncan (D
2 CONC undag), Earl of Caithness and Groa Torsteinsdatter, the daughter of Thorstein the Red, King of Caithness. He had five sons and two daughters.
2 CONT --------------------
