1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Alhfrith or Ealhfrith (c. 630 – c. 664) was King of Deira under his father Oswiu, King of Bernicia''', from 655 until sometime after 664. Appointed by Oswiu as a subordinate ruler, Alhfrith apparently clashed wi
2 CONC th his father over religious policy, which came to a head at the Synod of Whitby in 664. After this, Alhfrith disappears from the historical record.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alhfrith was the oldest son of Oswiu, who became King of Bernicia in 642. His mother was Oswiu's first wife, Rieinmelth, granddaughter of king Rhun of Rheged; the marriage also produced a daughter, Alhflaed. Both children were lik
2 CONC ely born in the 630s.[1] In the early 650s, when Alhfrith was a young man, Oswiu had him married to Cyneburh, daughter of Oswiu's great rival Penda of Mercia. Shortly after, Alhflaed married Penda's son Peada. Alhflaed, a devout C
2 CONC hristian, urged Peada to convert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations between Oswiu and Penda remained contentious, and Penda invaded Bernicia with a large army in 655.[4] One of Penda's allies was Œthelwald, king of Deira and Oswiu's nephew; Oswiu considered Deira part of his realm and Œt
2 CONC helwald his sub-king, but resistance to his rule continued throughout his reign.[5] Alhfrith served in his father's significantly smaller army as they pursued Penda. Their forces caught Penda's at the Battle of the Winwaed; Œthelw
2 CONC ald and others withdrew their troops at a critical moment, which contributed to Oswiu winning a decisive victory in which Penda was killed.[5][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The victory enabled Oswiu to re-establish his control over Deira. He evidently removed Œthelwald and installed Alhfrith as under-king (a largely autonomous ruler) owing homage to him.[5][6] However, Deiran resistance to Oswiu's ru
2 CONC le continued under Alhfrith, who might have tried to use it to assert his independence.[5] Alhfrith initially followed his father in adhering to Celtic Christianity, which practised certain customs at odds with those endorsed by t
2 CONC he Bishop of Canterbury and Continental Europe. However, he soon began challenging his father's policies of preferring the Celtic practices. Around 658, Alhfrith's ally Cenwalh introduced him to Wilfrid, a Northumbrian churchman w
2 CONC ho had studied in Europe and strongly advocated the Roman customs. Subsequently, he began openly challenging the Celtic customs and his father's policies.[7] In 664, Oswiu convened the Synod of Whitby to determine which form of Ch
2 CONC ristianity Northumbria would follow; Alhfrith served as a key proponent of Wilfrid and the Roman system, which ultimately prevailed.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alhfrith requested that Wilfrid be made bishop for Deira, most likely with a see at York. Apparently with Oswiu's consent, Wilfrid travelled to Gaul to be consecrated, as he did not consider any bishops in Great Britain or Ireland
2 CONC  to be validly consecrated; he did not return until around 666. In the mid-660s, Alhfrith requested to go on pilgrimage to Rome with Benedict Biscop, which Oswiu forbade. Alhfrith subsequently vanishes from the historical record. 
2 CONC In the Ecclesiastical History of the English People, Bede mentions that Alhfrith "attacked" his father. David Kirby suggests that Alhfrith may have participated and died in a rebellion against Oswiu, perhaps inspired by their cont
2 CONC inuing religious differences, Alhfrith's anxieties about the rise of his half-brother Ecgfrith, or Oswiu's military aspirations to the north that shifted focus away from Deira in the south.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to later tradition, Oswiu replaced Alhfrith as King of Deira with his brother Ecgfrith, who subsequently became king of Bernicia and installed another brother, Ælfwine, in Deira.[9] It is not clear if Alhfrith had childr
2 CONC en; it is possible that the later Northumbrian king Osric was his son, though he might instead have been the son of Aldfrith.[10]
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alhfrith
