1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''WILLIAM FitzDuncan ([1091/94]-[1153/54])'''
2 CONT s/o  DUNCAN II King of Scotland ([1060/65] ) & Ethelreda of Northumberland
2 CONT x NN (mistress?)
2 CONT *GOSPATRICK
2 CONT *DONALD MacWilliam (-killed in battle Mamgarvey [Mngarnia] Moor, Speyside 31 Jul 1187)
2 CONT xx ([1138])  ALICE de Rumilly, d/o WILLIAM FitzRanulf "Meschin" &  Cecily de Rumilly Lady of Skipton
2 CONT *WILLIAM "the Boy of Egremont" (-drowned Bolton Wharf >1155)
2 CONT *CECILY ([1138/43?]-<1190) Lady of Skipton x GUILLAUME Comte d'Aumâle Lord of Holderness
2 CONT * AMABEL (-<1201) Lady of Egremont and Copeland x  (<1162) REYNOLD de Lucy
2 CONT *ALICE de Rumilly (<[1153/54]]-[6 Mar or 18 Mar] [1212/1215], bur [Gysburne/Gisborough Priory]) x GILBERT Pipard Sheriff of Gloucester and Hereford xx  (<8 Dec 1195) ROBERT de Courtenay Lord of Sutton, Berkshire, Sheriff of Cumber
2 CONC land
2 CONT xxx Mistress
2 CONT *WILLIAM 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#WilliamFitzDuncandied11531154
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT 1.         DUNCAN ([1060/65]-murdered Monthechim/Mondynes, Kincardineshire 12 Nov 1094, bur Dunfermline Abbey, Fife).  William of Malmesbury names Duncan as illegitimate son of King Malcolm, when recording that he was knighted by 
2 CONC William II King of England[329].  There is no indication of the identity of Duncan's mother, as explained above.  His birth date is estimated on the assumption that he was a child when given as a hostage in 1072, which precludes h
2 CONC is being the son of Queen Margaret.  It is possible that he was illegitimate, although there is no indication that he was thereby excluded from succession to the throne.  "Dunecanus fili regii Malcolum constans hereditarie rex Sco
2 CONC tie" donated property to the monks of St Cuthbert for the souls of his father, "fratri mei, uxore mea et infans mei" (all unnamed), by charter dated 1093, witnessed by "Eadgari, [Etheread], Aceard, Ulf, Malcolub[330], Hormer, Hemi
2 CONC ng, Ælfric, Teodbold, Earnulf"[331].  The copy in Early Scottish Charters lists the witnesses in a different order, and adds "Grentonis…Vinget"[332].  He was given as a hostage to William I King of England at Abernethy in 1072 to 
2 CONC guarantee his father's good behaviour[333].  The Annals of Ulster record that the "French went into Scotland and brought away the son of the king of Scotland as hostage" in 1072[334], which presumably refers to Duncan as any of hi
2 CONC s half-brothers (if then born) would have been infants at the time.  He was kept in Normandy.  Florence of Worcester records that Robert III "Curthose" Duke of Normandy released "Ulfam Haroldi quondam regis Anglorum filium, Dunech
2 CONC aldumque regis Scottorum Malcolmi filium" from custody after his father's death in Sep 1087, knighted them and allowed them to leave Normandy[335].  He joined William II King of England and remained at his court in England[336].  
2 CONC Florence of Worcester records that Duncan served in the army of King William II, who supported his bid to depose his uncle, and to whom Duncan swore fealty before leaving for Scotland[337].  He deposed his uncle in 1094 and procla
2 CONC imed himself DUNCAN II King of Scotland[338].  Florence of Worcester records that "Dufenaldum regis Malcolmi fratrem" was elected king after his brother's death but that "filius regis Malcolmi Dunechain" expelled "patruum suum Duf
2 CONC enaldum"[339].  The Annals of Inisfallen record that "Domnall son of Donnchadh” killed “Donnchadh son of Mael Coluim king of Alba” in 1094 and “took the kingship of Alba”[340].  The Annals of Ulster record that "Donnchad son of Ma
2 CONC el Coluim, king of Scotland, was treacherously killed by his own brothers Domnall and Edmond" in 1094[341].  William of Malmesbury records that King Duncan was "murdered by the wickedness of his uncle Donald"[342].  Florence of Wo
2 CONC rcester records that "Scotti regem…Dunechan" was killed in [1094][343].  The Chronicle of the Picts and Scots dated 1251 records that "Donechat mac Malcolm" was killed "a Malpeder Mackcolm comite de Merns in Monacheden" through th
2 CONC e treachery of "Donald mac Donehat"[344].  The Chronicle of John of Fordun records that "Duncan, King Malcolm’s illegitimate son" was "slain at Monthechin by the Earl of Mernys…Malpetri, in Scottish, Malpedir, through the wiles of
2 CONC  his uncle Donald" as was buried "in the island of Iona"[345].  m ([1090]) ETHELREDA of Northumberland, daughter of GOSPATRICK Earl of Northumberland & his wife --- (bur Dunfermline Abbey, Fife).  The Cronicon Cumbriæ records that
2 CONC  “Waldevus filius comitis Cospatricii” enfeoffed “Waldeve filio Gileminii” with property and “Ethreda sorore sua”[346].  The Cronicon Cumbriæ records that “Ethreda sorore Waldevi patris sui” married “Doncani comes de Murrayse” and
2 CONC  that their son “Willielmus” succeeded her nephew “Alanus filius Waldevi”[347].  It is assumed that Duncan was Ethelreda’s first husband and Waltheof her second husband.  She married secondly Waltheof.  King Duncan II & his wife h
2 CONC ad one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         '''WILLIAM FitzDuncan ([1091/94]-[1153/54]).  His parentage is confirmed by the Chronicle of John of Fordun (Continuator - Annals) which records the rebellion of his son "Macwilliam whose real name was Donald Bane…son o
2 CONC f William son of Duncan the bastard" against King William[348].  That William was his father's only child is shown by King Duncan's charter dated to 1093 referring to "infans mei".  As the actual date of the charter is more likely
2 CONC  to be 1094, this leaves little time for the birth of any more children before the king's murder.  "…Willelmo nepote comitis…" witnessed the charter dated to [1120] under which "David comes filius Malcolmi Regis Scottorum" founded
2 CONC  the abbey of Selkirk[349].  Lord of Skipton and Craven, by right of his [second] wife.''' 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#WilliamFitzDuncandied11531154
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT C.      DESCENDANTS of WILLIAM FitzDuncan
2 CONT
2 CONT '''WILLIAM FitzDuncan, son of DUNCAN II King of Scotland & his wife Ethelreda of Northumberland ([1091/94]-[1153/54]).  His parentage is confirmed by the Chronicle of John of Fordun (Continuator - Annals) which records the rebelli
2 CONC on of his son "Macwilliam whose real name was Donald Bane…son of William son of Duncan the bastard" against King William[680].  That William was his father's only child is shown by King Duncan's charter dated to 1093 referring to 
2 CONC "infans mei".  As the actual date of the charter is more likely to be 1094, this leaves little time for the birth of any more children before the king's murder.  "…Willelmo nepote comitis…" witnessed the charter dated to [1120] un
2 CONC der which "David comes filius Malcolmi Regis Scottorum" founded the abbey of Selkirk[681].  "David comes filii Malcolmi regis Scotorum" founded the monastery of Kelso by charter dated to [1119/24] witnessed by "Matilda comitissa, 
2 CONC Henrico filio comitis…Willo nepote comitis…"[682].  ["Alexander nepos regis Alexandri…" subscribed the possibly spurious charter dated to [1120] of "Alexander…Rex Scottorum…Sibilla regina Scottorum…"[683].  No other reference to A
2 CONC lexander nephew of King Alexander has been found.  Duncan suggests the entry is an error for William and that his position in the witness list indicates that he may then have been intended by the king as his successor[684].  Howev
2 CONC er, the fact that Alexander also witnessed the [1114/15] Scone charter (see above) indicates that he was a separate person.]  William acquired rights in Allerdale, Cumberland, held by his maternal uncle Waltheof[685].  A charter o
2 CONC f King Henry II records donations to York St Mary, including the donation of rights relating to “ecclesiam S. Begæ…et terram de Hothneth” by “Willielmus filius Duncani"[686].  "…Willelmus nepos ipsius principis…" witnessed inquisi
2 CONC tions by "David…Cumbrensis regionis princeps", dated 1124, concerning land owned by the church of Glasgow[687].  "…Willelmo filio Duncani…" witnessed a charter dated to [1128] by which "David…Rex Scottorum" made grants to the chur
2 CONC ch of St John in the castle of Roxburgh[688].  The Cronicon Cumbriæ records that “Ethreda sorore Waldevi patris sui” married “Doncani comes de Murrayse” and that their son “Willielmus” succeeded his cousin “Alanus filius Waldevi”[
2 CONC 689].  "Willelmo nepote Regis…" witnessed a charter dated to [1135] by which "David Rex Scottorum" granted Swinton to "Arnulfo…mee militi"[690].  Lord of Skipton and Craven de iure uxoris.  “Willielmus filius Dunecani nepos regis 
2 CONC Scotiæ…et Aeliz de Rumeili uxor mea” confirmed donations of property to Bolton Priory by undated charter[691].  "Willelmo filio Duncani, Gospatrico filio eius…" witnessed a charter dated to before 1138 under which "Gospatricus com
2 CONC es frater Dolfini" made grants to the monks of St Cuthbert[692].  He fought at the battle of the Standard in Aug 1138[693].  "…Willo nepote regis…" witnessed the charter dated 29 Apr 1141 under which David King of Scotland donated
2 CONC  "terram de Eldune…Dernewic" to Melrose abbey[694].''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT [m firstly ---.  The name of William’s supposed first wife or mistress is not known.  It is clear that William FitzDuncan’s sons Gospatrick and Donald could not have been born from his known marriage.  It is not known whether they
2 CONC  were sons of an earlier otherwise unrecorded marriage or were illegitimate.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT m [secondly] ([1138]) as her first husband, ALICE de Rumilly, daughter of WILLIAM FitzRanulf "Meschin" & his wife Cecily de Rumilly Lady of Skipton.  A manuscript genealogy of William de Forz Comte d’Aumâle names “Aliciam de Rumel
2 CONC eya” as daughter of “Willielmus de Mechines primus hæres de Sciptun in Craven”, adding that she married “Willielmo filio Duncan” and was buried “apud Fontes”[695].  A different version of her parentage is provided by the Cronicon 
2 CONC Cumbriæ which records that “Willielmus”, son of “Doncani comes de Murrayse”, married “Aliciam filiam Roberti de Romeney, domini de Skipton in Craven” and his wife “filiam Willielmi de Meschinis domini de Coupland”[696].  Lady of S
2 CONC kipton: Clay says that Alice “succeeded her brother Ranulf in her father’s lands in Copeland, and her mother in the honour of Skipton”[697].  “Willielmus filius Dunecani nepos regis Scotiæ…et Aeliz de Rumeili uxor mea” confirmed d
2 CONC onations of property to Bolton Priory by undated charter[698].  “Aaliz de Rumelli” donated property to Pontefract Priory, with the consent of “Willielmi filii mei”, for the soul of “domini mei Willielmi filii Dunecani”, by undated
2 CONC  charter[699].  “Adeliz de Rumelio”, with the consent of “Willelmi filii et heredis mei et filiarum mearum”, donated “manerium de Boelton...” to Embsay priory by charter dated to [1155], witnessed by “Willelmo filio meo de Egremun
2 CONC t...”[700].  She married secondly (before 1156[701]) Alexander FitzGerold.  Dugdale summarises donations to Southwark priory, including the donation of “cheese at Badleking in the manor of Kingston Lisle in Berkshire” made by "Ale
2 CONC xander Fitzgerald" and confirmed by "Alice de Rumeley, wife of Alexander"[702].  “Alexander filius Geroldi” confirmed the donation of “terre et dimidiam in Kylneseia...” made by “Willelmus filius Dunecani et Aaliz de Rumeli” to Fo
2 CONC untains abbey by charter dated to [1155/64], witnessed by “...Willelmus de Egrem[unt]...”[703]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (1): ---.  The name of William's mistress is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William FitzDuncan & his [first wife or mistress] had two [maybe illegitimate] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         GOSPATRICK .  "Willelmo filio Duncani, Gospatrico filio eius…" witnessed a charter dated to before 1138 under which "Gospatricus comes frater Dolfini" made grants to the monks of St Cuthbert[704]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         DONALD MacWilliam (-killed in battle Mamgarvey [Mngarnia] Moor, Speyside 31 Jul 1187).  The Chronicle of Melrose records that "Macwilliam" rebelled against William "the Lion" King of Scotland, but was killed by Scottish
2 CONC  forces on Mount Mamgarvey, near Moray[705].  John of Fordun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records the rebellion of "Mackwillam…vero nomine Donaldum Ban…filium…Willelmi filii Duncani Bastardi, qui fuit filius major Malcolmi regis
2 CONC  Scotiæ viri S. Margaretæ" who captured "duo castella…Dunschath et Edirdovar" but was killed "in mora…Macgarvy prope Moraviam…pridie Kal Aug"[706].  m ---.  The name of Donald's wife is not known.  Donald & his wife had four child
2 CONC ren: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         GODFREY MacWilliam (-beheaded Kincardine 1211).  The Chronicle of Melrose records that "Guthred son of Macwilliam" rebelled against William "the Lion" King of Scotland in 1211[707].  The Extracta ex Cronicis Scocie reco
2 CONC rds that "Gothredus Makwilliam" rebelled against King William, was captured by "Willelmum Cummyne comitem Buchquanie" and beheaded "apud Kincardin"[708]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         DONALD MacWilliam (-killed in battle Morayshire 15 Jun 1215).  The Chronicle of Melrose records that "Donald Ban the son of Macwilliam" invaded Moray in 1215 with Kenneth MacAht and "the son of a certain king of Ireland
2 CONC  at the head of a large troop of wicked ones", but he was beheaded by Ferquhard MacTaggart (later created Earl of Ross) who presented his head to Alexander II King of Scotland 15 Jun 1215[709]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         daughter .  m --- MacEwen, son of ---.  --- MacEwen & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          GILLESPIE MacEwen (-killed 1229).  John of Fordun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records the rebellion in 1222 of "quidam iniqui de genere MacWilliam…Gillascop et filii eius, et Rodericus in extremis Scotiæ finibus"[71
2 CONC 0].  The Chronicle of Lanercost records the rebellion in 1230 of "quidam iniqui de genere Mach William…et filius eius, et quidam Rotherike"[711].  John of Fordun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records that "Gillescop…et duo filii 
2 CONC eius" were killed in 1229 and their heads sent to the king[712].  m ---.  The name of Gillespie's wife is not known.  Gillespie & his wife had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT (a)       2 sons (-killed 1229).  John of Fordun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records that "Gillescop…et duo filii eius" were killed in 1229 and their heads sent to the king[713]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (b)       daughter (-killed Forfar [1229/30]).  The Chronicle of Lanercost records the rebellion in 1230 of "quidam iniqui de genere Mach William…et filius eius, et quidam Rotherike" and the murder of "Mac William filia, adhuc rec
2 CONC ens de matris utero edita" at Forfar[714]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT d)         GOSPATRICK MacWilliam (-before 1208).  Lord of Airton in Yorkshire.  His descendants are extant[715]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William FitzDuncan & his second wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         WILLIAM "the Boy of Egremont" (-drowned Bolton Wharf after 1155).  The Cronicon Cumbriæ names “Willielmum puerum de Egremund” as the son “Willielmus”, son of “Doncani comes de Murrayse”, and his wife Alice, adding that 
2 CONC he died young[716].  “Aaliz de Rumelli” donated property to Pontefract Priory, with the consent of “Willielmi filii mei”, for the soul of “domini mei Willielmi filii Dunecani”, by undated charter[717].  Lord of Egremont.  “Adeliz 
2 CONC de Rumelio”, with the consent of “Willelmi filii et heredis mei et filiarum mearum”, donated “manerium de Boelton...” to Embsay priory by charter dated to [1155], witnessed by “Willelmo filio meo de Egremunt...”[718].  “Alexander 
2 CONC filius Geroldi” confirmed the donation of “terre et dimidiam in Kylneseia...” made by “Willelmus filius Dunecani et Aaliz de Rumeli” to Fountains abbey by charter dated to [1155/64], witnessed by “...Willelmus de Egrem[unt]...”[71
2 CONC 9].  Clay says that “There is no reason...to doubt the truth of the legend that the boy of Egremont was drowned in the Wharfe at the Strid at Bolton - a legend...enshrined in The Force of Prayer and The White Doe of Rylstone by Wo
2 CONC rdsworth...”[720].  A document dated [15 Nov] 1223 records a claim relating to "the moiety of Alicia de Rumelly’s land in Cumberland”, stating that “the ‘Boy of Egremont’ had three sisters...[between whom his] land was partitioned
2 CONC ...as he died without heir of his body”[721]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         CECILY ([1138/43?]-before 1190).  The Cronicon Cumbriæ names “prima…Cecilia…secunda Amabilla…tertia Alicia” as the three daughters of “Willielmus”, son of “Doncani comes de Murrayse”, and his wife Alice, adding that Cec
2 CONC ily received the honor of Skipton and married “Willielmo le Grossus comiti Albemarliæ”[722].  Her birth date is estimated on the basis of her parents’ marriage date and the supposition that she was her parents’ oldest daughter.  C
2 CONC o-heiress of her brother.  Her marriage date is estimated from her younger sister Amabel marrying “before 1162”, on the assumption that the older sister would have married first.  Lady of Skipton.  "Willelmus comes Albemarlie" con
2 CONC firmed donations to St Bees, for the souls of "…antecessorum uxoris mee Cecilie", by undated charter[723].  "Cecilia comitissa Albamarlie" donated land "inter Esc et Duden…Kirkesantan et Haverigg…et Thueites" to St Bees, and confi
2 CONC rmed the donation of "ecclesia de Gosford" made by "W[illelmi] fratris mei", by undated charter[724].  "Cecilia comitissa Albemarlie" confirmed land "de Hensin“ham et de Waltona" which she held “quando dominus meus, Willelmus come
2 CONC s Albemarlie fuit vivus et mortuus” to St Bees by undated charter [presumably dated to after 20 Aug 1179], witnessed by "Galfrido filio comitis Albamarlie..."[725].  Clay says that “Cecily de Rumilly was probably dead at Michaelma
2 CONC s 1190, when William de Forz, her son-in-law, owed scutage for the honour of Skipton”[726].  m [as his second wife,] GUILLAUME Comte d'Aumâle Lord of Holderness, son of ETIENNE de Troyes Comte d'Aumâle [Blois] & his [first] wife H
2 CONC awise de Mortimer ([before 1105?]-20 Aug 1179, bur Abbey of Thornton, Lincolnshire). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         AMABEL (-before 1201).  The Cronicon Cumbriæ names “prima…Cecilia…secunda Amabilla…tertia Alicia” as the three daughters of “Willielmus”, son of “Doncani comes de Murrayse”, and his wife Alice, adding that Amabel receiv
2 CONC ed the honor of Egremont and married “Reginaldo de Lucy”, by whom he fathered “Amabillam et Aliciam, et successit Amabillæ Lambertus de Multon”, the latter being succeeded by “Thomas de Multon de Egremond”[727].  Co-heiress of her
2 CONC  brother.  Lady of Egremont and Copeland.  The Testa de Nevill includes a writ of King John dated 1212 which records that "Robertus de Stutevill" held "villam de Thorpennoi de domino rege" in Cumberland which was "de baronia Alici
2 CONC e de Rumill" and that "Reginaldus de Lucy" had held the land "cum sorore predicte Alicie" and refused homage to "Philippi de Valoines, antecessoris predicti Roberti et uxoris sue"[728].  m (before 1162) REYNOLD de Lucy, [729][rela
2 CONC tive of RICHARD de Lucy, Justiciar of England] (-[1199/1200]).  Keeper of Nottingham castle when it was burnt by Earl Ferrers in the rebellion of 1174[730]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         ALICE de Rumilly (before [1153/54][731]-[6 Mar or 18 Mar] [1212/1215], bur [Gysburne/Gisborough Priory]).  The Cronicon Cumbriæ names “prima…Cecilia…secunda Amabilla…tertia Alicia” as the three daughters of “Willielmus”
2 CONC , son of “Doncani comes de Murrayse”, and his wife Alice, adding that Alice received “Aspatrike, et baronia de Allerdale et libertate de Cokermouth” and married “Gilberto Pipard” and secondly “Roberto de Courtenay”, but died child
2 CONC less and was succeeded by “Thomas de Lucy, cui successit Thomas filius eius, cui successit Antonius frater eius”[732].  Co-heiress of her brother.  Lady of Allerdale and Cockermouth.  "Ric de Luci fil Reginaldi de Luci" paid a fin
2 CONC e for "terra sua de Copland et in Cautebige" relating to a claim against "B. com Albemarl et uxore sua et versus Rob de Curtenay et Alic uxorem suam", dated 1200[733].  “Ælicia de Rumely, filia Willielmi filii Duncani” confirmed d
2 CONC onations of property to Gysburne/Gisborough Priory by “antecessorum meorum…Waldevi filii Cospatrici comitis, et Alani filii Waldevi”, by undated charter[734].  “Alicia de Rumelli filia Willielmi filii Dunecani” donated property to
2 CONC  Fountains Abbey by undated charter[735].  The Feet of Fines records the judgment dated 8 Dec 1195 in a claim by "Walterus Pipard" against "Rob de Curtenai…loco Alic de Rumilie ux sue" concerning land "in Croumse"[736].  "Robertus
2 CONC  de Curtenei" donated revenue from "molendino…de Kokermuth" to St Bees, with the advice of "uxoris mee Aaliz de Rumeleie", to St Bees by undated charter, witnessed by "…Willelmo de Curtenei…"[737].  “Alicia de Rumely, filia Willie
2 CONC lmi filii Duncani” donated property to Gysburne/Gisborough Priory, for the souls of “maritorum meorum Gilberti Pypard et Roberti de Curtenay”, by undated charter[738].  The Red Book of the Exchequer records "Aliz de Romely" holdin
2 CONC g property in Cumberland in [1210/12][739].  The Testa de Nevill includes a writ of King John dated 1212 which records that "Alicia de Rumilly" held "terram suam in Alredal de domino rege" in Cumberland which King Henry I had gran
2 CONC ted to "Waldevo filio Gospatric, antecessoris predicte Alicie"[740].  The obituary of Gysburne/Gisborough priory records the death “II Non Mar” of "Aliciæ de Rumley"[741].  m firstly GILBERT Pipard Sheriff of Gloucester and Herefo
2 CONC rd, son of --- (-[Sep 1191/Sep 1192]).  m secondly (before 8 Dec 1195) as his second wife, ROBERT de Courtenay Lord of Sutton, Berkshire, Sheriff of Cumberland, son of RENAUD Seigneur de Courtenay & his second wife Hawise d’Avranc
2 CONC hes (-[1207/09]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT William FitzDuncan had one illegitimate son by Mistress (1): 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.          WILLIAM .  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.   m (1138) ---. 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#WilliamFitzDuncandied11531154
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William had numerous offspring both in and out of wedlock - it is likely that Fonia of Moray and Bishop Wimund were among them.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_fitz_Duncan
2 CONT
2 CONT William fitz Duncan (a modern anglicisation of the Old French Guillaume fils de Duncan and the Middle Irish Uilleam mac Donnchada) was a Scottish prince, a territorial magnate in northern Scotland and northern England, a general a
2 CONC nd the legitimate son of king Donnchad II of Scotland by Athelreda of Dunbar.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1094, his father Donnchad II was killed by Mormaer Máel Petair of Mearns, supporting the claims of King Domnall III Bán. It is probable that William left Scotland with his mother in that year, to the safety of Allerdale in Cumb
2 CONC erland. A decade or so later, he ventured to the court of his half-uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the reign of his half-uncle Alexander I, it is highly likely that William was regarded as a viable tánaiste (i.e. "designated heir"), but Henry I of England supported David. When David succeeded, William, as the legitimate k
2 CONC ing under the rules of primogeniture was certainly bought off by David, probably being made tánaiste. William repeatedly leads the lists of witnesses appearing in Scottish royal charters in the reigns of Alexander I and David I.
2 CONT
2 CONT A 13th century northern English source claims that William was Mormaer of Moray. As this source had no reason to make this up, it is highly likely that William was made the ruler of Moray after the defeat of King Óengus of Moray i
2 CONC n 1130. It is feasible that this grant had something to do with the coming of age of David's son, Prince Henry. As well as being the ruler of Moray, William controlled the English lands of Allerdale, Skipton and Craven, making him
2 CONC  one of the greatest barons of northern England.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was a great warrior. He frequently led Scottish armies. In the campaign of 1138, he led an army of Gaels that defeated a Norman English army at the Battle of Clitheroe, raising the hopes for the success of the royal army, 
2 CONC hopes which failed to materialize at the Battle of the Standard.
2 CONT
2 CONT William had several marriages. His first marriage was over, presumably through death, by the year 1137, when he married Alice de Rumilly, daughter of William Meschin. By the latter, he had a son, also called William (William of Eg
2 CONC remont or William the Atheling), who died in 1160, and a daughter Cicely, Lady of Skipton, who married William le Gros, 1st Earl of Albemarle. He also had a large number of bastards, probably including Wimund of the Isles. It is n
2 CONC ow presumed that Domnall mac Uilleim, and the Meic Uilleim clan that repeatedly rebelled against later Scottish kings in their quest to gain the Scottish throne, were legitimate descendants of a marriage to a daughter of Óengus of
2 CONC  Moray.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1147, whereupon Moray fell back into the hands of David.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William fitz Duncan var en skotsk prins, en legitim sønn av kong Donnchad II (Duncan II av Skottland) med Octreda. Han var en hærfører og en territorial magnat i nordlige Skottland og nordlige England. Navnet som han er best kjent
2 CONC  under er en moderne anglofisering av det gammelfranske Guillaume fils de Duncan eller middelirske Uilleam mac Donnchada.
2 CONT
2 CONT I 1094 ble hans far drept av mormaer Máel Petair av Mearns som støtte til kravet til tronen av kong Domnall III Bán (Donald III av Skottland). Sannsynligvis forlot William Skottland det samme året sammen med sin mor og fant tryggh
2 CONC et i Allerdale i Cumberland. Et tiår senere var han ved det skotske hoffet til sin onkel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under regimet til hans onkel Alexander I av Skottland er meget sannsynlig at William ble sett på som «viable tanaiste» (= utsett arvtager), men kong Henrik I av England støttet David I av Skottland. Da David etterfulgte som konge 
2 CONC ble William som den legitime konge ved regelen om førstefødselsrett høyst sannsynligvis «kjøpt» av David, sannsynligvis igjen gjort til tanaiste. William ledet gjentatte ganger listene over vitner som opptrer i de skotske, kongeli
2 CONC ge charter under regimet til Alexander I og David I.
2 CONT
2 CONT En kilde fra 1200-tallets nordlige England påstår at William var mormaer av Moray. Ettersom den kilden ikke har noen grunn til å lyve er det sannsynlig at William ble gjort til hersker av Moray etter at kong Óengus av Moray ble be
2 CONC seiret i 1130. Det er mulig at denne tildelingen hadde noe å gjøre med Davids sønn, prins Henry av Skottland, 3. jarl av Huntingdon. Samtidig som William var hersker av Moray kontrollerte han engelsk land i Allerdale, Skipton og C
2 CONC raven, noe som gjorde ham til en av de mest betydningsfulle baronene i det nordlige England.
2 CONT
2 CONT William var en stor kriger. Han ledet ofte skotske hærstyrker. Under kampanjen i 1138 ledet han en hærstyrke av gælere som beseiret en engelsk hær i slaget ved Clitheroe, noe som høynet håpet om suksess for den kongelige hæren, et
2 CONC  håp som ble knust i Fanenes slag.
2 CONT
2 CONT William var gift flere ganger. Hans første ekteskap ble avsluttet, sannsynligvis ved hans hustrus død, i 1137 da han giftet seg med Alice de Rumilly. Med den sistnevnte hadde han en sønn som også ble kalt William. Han hadde også e
2 CONC n rekke barn utenfor ekteskap, sannsynligvis Wimund, biskop over de ytre øyene, og meget sannsynlig også Domnall «MacWilliam». MacWilliam-klanen gjorde ofte opprør mot de senere skotske kongene i håp om overta den skotske tronen.
2 CONT
2 CONT William døde i 1147, og da gikk Moray tilbake til tronen.
