2 CONT ==[http://oaks.nvg.org/orkneyingsoga.html From the Orkney saga (translated into Norwegian)]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Det er fortalt at Orknøyene ble bygd i de dager da Harald Hårfagre var konge i Norge; før hadde det vært vikingbøle der. Den første jarlen på Orknøyene het Sigurd; han var sønn til Øystein Glumra, og bror til Ragnvald Mørejarl. E
2 CONC tter Sigurd var Guttorm, sønn hans, der en vinter. Etter ham fikk Torv-Einar jarledømmet, sønn til Ragnvald Mørejarl; han var jarl lenge, og var en mektig mann. Halvdan Hålegg, sønn til Harald Hårfagre, gikk mot Torv-Einar og drei
2 CONC v ham bort fra Orknøyene. Einar kom tilbake og drepte Halvdan på Rinansøy. Etter dette kom kong Harald til Orknøyene med en hær. Da rømte Einar opp i Skottland. Kong Harald lot orknøyingene sverge at han skulle få all odelen. Så b
2 CONC le kongen og jarlen forlikte; jarlen ble hans mann og fikk landet i len av kongen, men han skulle ikke svare noen skatt av det, for det lå så utsatt for herjinger. Jarlen bøtte seksti mark gull til kongen. Så herjet kong Harald i 
2 CONC Skottland, slik det heter i Glymdråpa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Etter Torv-Einar rådde sønnene hans for øyene, det var Arnkjell, Erlend og Torfinn Hausakljuv. I deres tid kom Eirik Blodøks fra Norge, og så stod jarlene under ham. Arnkjell og Erlend falt på hærferd, men Torfinn styrte landet og
2 CONC  ble en gammel mann. Hans sønner var Arnfinn, Håvard, Lodve, Ljot og Skule; mora deres var Grelod, som var datter til Dungad jarl på Katanes; hennes mor var Groa, datter til Torstein Raud. I Torfinn jarls siste tid kom Blodøks-søn
2 CONC nene fra Norge, de hadde rømt unna for Håkon jarl. De herjet fælt på Orknøyene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Torfinn jarl døde av sjukdom. Sønnene hans rådde over øyene etter ham, og det går lange frasagn om dem. Lodve levde lengst av dem, og da rådde han alene for øyene; sønn hans het Sigurd Digre, han ble jarl etter ham. Det var en mek
2 CONC tig mann, og en svær hærmann. I hans dager kom Olav Tryggvason fra vesterviking med hæren sin, han la til i Orknøyene og tok Sigurd jarl til fange på Ragnvaldsøy da han lå der med bare ett skip. Kong Olav tilbød jarlen å løse live
2 CONC t om han tok imot dåpen og den rette tro og ble hans mann og bød kristendom på alle Orknøyene. Kong Olav tok sønn hans til gissel, han het Hunde eller Valp. Derfra drog kong Olav til Norge, og der ble han konge. Hunde var hos kong
2 CONC  Olav noen år, så døde han, og siden viste ikke Sigurd jarl noen lydighet mot kong Olav. Han giftet seg med datter til Melkolm skottekonge; sønn deres het Torfinn; det var noen eldre sønner av Sigurd også: Sumarlide, Bruse og Eina
2 CONC r Vrangmunn." fra Heimskringla, Snorre
2 CONT
2 CONT =Heimskringla, the saga of St Olav, by Snorri Sturlusson=
2 CONT
2 CONT It is related that in the days of Harald Harfager, the king of Norway, the islands of Orkney, which before had been only a resort for vikings, were settled . The first earl in the Orkney Islands was called Sigurd, who was a son of
2 CONC  Eystein Glumra, and brother of Ragnvald earl of More. After Sigurd his son Guthorm was earl for one year. After him Torf-Einar, a son of Ragnvald, took the earldom, and was long earl, and was a man of great power. Halfdan Haleg, 
2 CONC a son of Harald Harfager, assaulted Torf- Einar, and drove him from the Orkney Islands; but Einar came back and killed Halfdan in the island Ronaldsha. Thereafter King Harald came with an army to the Orkney Islands. Einar fled to 
2 CONC Scotland, and King Harald made the people of the Orkney Islands give up their udal properties, and hold them under oath from him. Thereafter the king and earl were reconciled, so that the earl became the king's man, and took the c
2 CONC ountry as a fief from him; but that it should pay no scat or feu-duty, as it was at that time much plundered by vikings. The earl paid the king sixty marks of gold; and then King Harald went to plunder in Scotland, as related in t
2 CONC he "Glym Drapa". After Torf-Einar, his sons Arnkel, Erlend, and Thorfin Hausakljufer (1) ruled over these lands. In their days came Eirik Blood-axe from Norway, and subdued these earls. Arnkel and Erlend fell in a war expedition; 
2 CONC but Thorfin ruled the country long, and became an old man. His sons were Arnfin, Havard, Hlodver, Liot, and Skule. Their mother was Grelad, a daughter of Earl Dungad of Caithness. Her mother was Groa, a daughter of Thorstein Raud.
2 CONC  In the latter days of Earl Thorfin came Eirik Blood-axe's sons, who had fled from Earl Hakon out of Norway, and committed great excesses in Orkney. Earl Thorfin died on a bed of sickness, and his sons after him ruled over the cou
2 CONC ntry, and there are many stories concerning them. Hlodver lived the longest of them, and ruled alone over this country. His son was Sigurd the Thick, who took the earldom after him, and became a powerful man and a great warrior. I
2 CONC n his days came Olaf Trygvason from his viking expedition in the western ocean, with his troops, landed in Orkney and took Earl Sigurd prisoner in South Ronaldsha, where he lay with one ship. King Olaf allowed the earl to ransom h
2 CONC is life by letting himself be baptized, adopting the true faith, becoming his man, and introducing Christianity into all the Orkney Islands. As a hostage, King Olaf took his son, who was called Hunde or Whelp. Then Olaf went to No
2 CONC rway, and became king; and Hunde was several years with King Olaf in Norway, and died there. After his death Earl Sigurd showed no obedience or fealty to King Olaf. He married a daughter of the Scottish king Malcolm, and their son
2 CONC  was called Thorfin. Earl Sigurd had, besides, older sons; namely, Sumarlide, Bruse, and Einar Rangmund. Four or five years after Olaf Tryrgvason's fall Earl Sigurd went to Ireland, leaving his eldest sons to rule the country, and
2 CONC  sending Thorfin to his mother's father, the Scottish king. On this expedition Earl Sigurd fell in Brian's battle (l). When the news was received in Orkney, the brothers Sumarlide, Bruse, and Einar were chosen earls, and the count
2 CONC ry was divided into three parts among them. Thorfin Sigurdson was five years old when Earl Sigurd fell. When the Scottish king heard of the earl's death he gave his relation Thorfin Caithness and Sutherland, with the title of earl
2 CONC , and appointed good men to rule the land for him. Earl Thorfin was ripe in all ways as soon as he was grown up: he was stout and strong, but ugly; and as soon as he was a grown man it was easy to see that he was a severe and crue
2 CONC l but a very clever man. So says Arnor, the earls' skald: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Under the rim of heaven no other,
2 CONT
2 CONT So young in years as Einar's brother,
2 CONT
2 CONT In battle had a braver hand,
2 CONT
2 CONT Or stouter, to defend the land."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ENDNOTES:''' (1) Hausakljufer -- the splitter of skulls. -- L. (2) Brian's battle is supposed to have taken place on the 23rd April 1014, at Clontart, near Dublin; and is known in Irish
2 CONT
2 CONT history as the battle of Clontarf, and was one of the bloodiest of the age. It was fought between a viking called Sigtryg and Brian king of Munster, who gained the victory, but lost his life. -- L.
2 CONT
2 CONT Årsaken til hans død var Battle of Clontarf Dublin Ireland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sigurd the Stout=
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Sigurd Hlodvirsson)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd Hlodvisson (circa 960–23 April 1014), popularly known as Sigurd the Stout, was Earl of Orkney. The main source for his life is the Orkneyinga Saga, written some two centuries after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Orkneyinga Saga reports that Sigurd was the son of Hlodvir, one of the five sons of Thorfinn Skull-Splitter, and Eithne, said to be a daughter of Kjarvalr, King of Ireland—Cerball mac Dúnlainge, King of Osraige, who died in 88
2 CONC 8. Hlodvir died in his bed and was succeeded as Earl by Sigurd.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd's uncle Ljot had been killed in war against the Scots, and Sigurd soon faced trouble from his southern neighbours. An "Earl Finnleik" led an army against him which outnumbered Sigurd's men by seven to one. The Saga famously
2 CONC  records Sigurd's mother's reply when he went to her for advice:
2 CONT
2 CONT I would long have fostered thee in my wool-basket, if I had known that thou wouldst live for ever, and fortune decides as to a man's life, and not circumstances. It is better to die with honour than to live with dishonour. Receive
2 CONC  a standard which I have made with my whole knowledge, and I expect it will be victorious to him before whom it is carried, but the bane of him who bears it.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Raven banner worked as just Sigurd's mother said: he was victorious but the standard-bearer was killed.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 13th century Njal's Saga, Gormflaith prompted her son, Sigtrygg Silkbeard, into getting Sigurd to fight against her former husband, Brian Ború:
2 CONT
2 CONT "...she sent him to Earl Sigurd to ask for support... Sigtrygg sailed back to Ireland and told his mother that the jarl had joined him."
2 CONT
2 CONT The 12th century Irish source, the Cogadh Gaedhil re Gallaibh, records the events of the Battle of Clontarf in 1014. The "foreigners and Leinstermen" were led by Brodir of the Isle of Man and Sigurd, and the battle lasted all day.
2 CONC  Though Brian was killed in the battle, the Irishmen ultimately drove back their enemies into the sea, and Sigurd himself was killed. Sigurd left four sons: Brusi, Sumarlidi, Einar and Thorfinn, each of whom would also bear the ti
2 CONC tle Earl of Orkney.
2 CONT
