1 NOTE meurt près de Dublin en Irelande à la bataille de Clontarf
1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fell at the Battle of Clontarf in 1014, and is mentioned in the Irishannals for that year.  (The Irish annals refer to Sigurd's father asbeing named "Lodar", which is in agreement with the sagas.)
1 NOTE     * OCCUPATION: Earl of Orkneys. King of Man
2 CONT     * DEATH: 1014, ('Digre') Clontarf, Irland 
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://www.geocities.com/marthajanehick/b155.htm#P6297; NST 274XXXI
1 NOTE 1  UID 947E161BCD6D814A8FC397C819BF47C0D852
2 CONT
2 CONT 1  UID E4E6B054D3BF984BA69D655C79DBCA91F689
1 NOTE d. 1014, #107665
2 CONT Last Edited=8 Jul 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd II Digri, Jarl of Orkney and Caithness married Donalda of Alba, daughter of Kenneth II of Alba, King of Scotland, between 1005 and 1008.1 He died in 1014, killed.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sigurd II Digri, Jarl of Orkney and Caithness and Donalda of Alba
2 CONT Thorfinn Jamoforloney, Jarl of Orkney and Caithness+ b. c 1009, d. bt 1056 - 1065
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 175. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 NOTE Also Known As: The Stout
1 NOTE Was the Earl of Orkney, Known as Sigurd the Stout. Fought under the Ravens banner in the Battle of Clontarf  on Good Friday April 23rd 1014. Long back story about this battle and how he died.
1 NOTE d. 1014, #107665
2 CONT Last Edited=8 Jul 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd II Digri, Jarl of Orkney and Caithness married Donalda of Alba, daughter of Kenneth II of Alba, King of Scotland, between 1005 and 1008.1 He died in 1014, killed.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Sigurd II Digri, Jarl of Orkney and Caithness and Donalda of Alba
2 CONT Thorfinn Jamoforloney, Jarl of Orkney and Caithness+ b. c 1009, d. bt 1056 - 1065
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 175. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 NOTE KNOWN AS "THE STOUT""THE CORPULENT"; EARL OF ORKNEY
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT Earl of Orkney Islands
1 NOTE Sigurd Hlodvisson (circa 960-23 April 1014), popularly known as Sigurd the Stout, was Earl of Orkney. The main source for his life is the Orkneyinga Saga, written some two centuries after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Orkneyinga Saga reports that Sigurd was the son of Hlodvir, one of the five sons of Thorfinn Skull-Splitter, and Eithne, said to be a daughter of Kjarvalr, King of Ireland-Cerball mac Dúnlainge, King of Osraige, who died in 8
2 CONC 88. Hlodvir died in his bed and was succeeded as Earl by Sigurd.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd's uncle Ljot had been killed in war against the Scots, and Sigurd soon faced trouble from his southern neighbours. An "Earl Finnleik" led an army against him which outnumbered Sigurd's men by seven to one. The Saga famously
2 CONC  records Sigurd's mother's reply when he went to her for advice:
2 CONT
2 CONT I would long have fostered thee in my wool-basket, if I had known that thou wouldst live for ever, and fortune decides as to a man's life, and not circumstances. It is better to die with honour than to live with dishonour. Receive
2 CONC  a standard which I have made with my whole knowledge, and I expect it will be victorious to him before whom it is carried, but the bane of him who bears it.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Raven banner worked as just Sigurd's mother said: he was victorious but the standard-bearer was killed.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 13th century Njal's Saga, Gormflaith prompted her son, Sigtrygg Silkbeard, into getting Sigurd to fight against her former husband, Brian Ború:
2 CONT
2 CONT "...she sent him to Earl Sigurd to ask for support... Sigtrygg sailed back to Ireland and told his mother that the jarl had joined him." 
2 CONT The 12th century Irish source, the Cogadh Gaedhil re Gallaibh, records the events of the Battle of Clontarf in 1014. The "foreigners and Leinstermen" were led by Brodir of the Isle of Man and Sigurd, and the battle lasted all day.
2 CONC  Though Brian was killed in the battle, the Irishmen ultimately drove back their enemies into the sea, and Sigurd himself was killed. Sigurd left four sons: Brusi, Sumarlidi, Einar and Thorfinn, each of whom would also bear the ti
2 CONC tle Earl of Orkney.
1 NOTE d. 1014, #107665
2 CONT Last Edited=8 Jul 2005
