2 CONT Blácaire mac Gofrith (died 948), in Old Norse Blákári Guðrøðsson, was King of Dublin. Son of Gofraid ua Ímair, he was a great-grandson of Ímar, ancestor of the Uí Ímair kindred which dominated the Scandinavianised and Norse-Gael r
2 CONC egions of the British Isles in the tenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/IRELAND.htm#_Toc389126206
2 CONT
2 CONT Guthfrith & his wife had four children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         BLACAIR (-killed in battle 948).  King of Dublin: the Annals of the Four Masters record in 938 that “Blacaire son of Godfrey came to Ath-cliath”[1234].  The Annals of the Four Masters record the death 26 Mar 941 of “Mui
2 CONC rchertach of the Leather Cloaks, son of Niall Glundubh, lord of Aileach…slain at Ath-Fhirdiath” by “Blacaire son of Godfrey lord of the foreigners”[1235].  The Annals of the Four Masters record that “Blacaire one of the chiefs of 
2 CONC the foreigners was expelled from Dublin” in 943 and “Amhlaeibh remained after him there”[1236].  The Annals of Ulster record that "Blacair gave up Áth Cliath” in 945 and that “Amlaíb succeeded him”[1237].  The Annals of Ulster rec
2 CONC ord that "Blacair son of Gothfrith king of the foreigners” was killed in 948 by “Congalach son of Mael Mithig”[1238].
