1 OCCU King of Aileach and Prince of Ulster and Tyrone
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT Flaithbertach Ua Néill (c. 975 – 1036), widely known historically as Flaithbertach an Trostáin ("Flaithbertach of the Pilgrim's Staff"), was a powerful 11th-century King of Ailech in north-west Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Muirchertach Ua Néill (Muirchertach Midech), an eligible heir to the High Kingship of Ireland who was killed in battle in 977. (Note: He is frequently confused in unverified genealogies with his grandfather, Muir
2 CONC chertach "of the Leather Cloaks" who died in 943).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Cres Cumal of the Uí Maine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Bébinn, a daughter of the legendary High King of Ireland, Brian Boru.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flaithbertach first appears in the Irish annals leading military campaigns in 1004. He ruled the kingdom of Ailech through a volatile era of conflict and shift in Irish power.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1030, Flaithbertach abdicated his throne to undertake a religious pilgrimage to Rome. It was this journey that earned him his famous moniker, an Trostáin (of the Pilgrim's staff). While he was away or in retirement, leadership 
2 CONC passed to his son and heir, Aodh Athlamhan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the tragic and untimely death of his son Aodh in late 1033, the aging Flaithbertach was forced to return from his retirement. He resumed the active kingship of the Northern Uí Néill to steady the realm for his remaining 
2 CONC years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He passed away in 1036 in Ulster, with the Annals recording that he died "after a good life and penance".
2 CONT
2 CONT He had three primary, historically documented sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Aodh Athlamhan (d. 1033) – King of Ailech and father of Aodh Aonrachan (Anrothan), who carried the line into Scotland.
2 CONT #Domnall (d. 1027)
2 CONT #Muiredach (d. 1039)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Within the official traditions of Clan MacNeil, Flaithbertach is recognized as an essential ancestral patriarch. While his historical title was King of Ailech rather than a Scottish clan chief, he represents a crucial link in the 
2 CONC traditional lineage connecting the clan to the royal Uí Néill dynasty of Ireland. Making Flaithbertach 18th ancestral Chief of Clan MacNeil. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Hugo O'Neill, heir apparent (in 1970 anyway) to the Chieftainry of
