2 CONT Halga, Helgi, Helghe or Helgo was a legendary Danish king living in the early 6th century[1]. His name would in his own language (Proto-Norse) have been *Hailaga[2] (dedicated to the gods).
2 CONT
2 CONT Scholars generally agree that he appears in both Anglo-Saxon (Beowulf) and Scandinavian tradition (Norse sagas and Danish chronicles)[3]. In both traditions, he was a Scylding, the son of Healfdene and the brother of Hroðgar. In B
2 CONC eowulf, his relationship to Hroðulf is not explained, but if he was not his father, as in the Scandinavian tradition, he was at least his uncle. Both traditions also mention his family's feud with Froda and Ingeld.
2 CONT
2 CONT Whereas, not much is said about Halga in Anglo-Saxon sources, much more is said in Scandinavian ones, all of them containing a version of the story of his incestuous relationship with his own daughter Yrsa. This liaison resulted i
2 CONC n Halga's son Hroðulf.
2 CONT
