2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian  ferch Gruffydd (Gwenllian, daughter of Gruffydd) (b.c. 1097, died 1136) was Princess-consort of Deheubarth in Wales, and the daughter of Gruffydd ap Cynan (1055-1137), prince of Gwynedd, and a member of the princely Abe
2 CONC rffraw family of Gwynedd. Gwenllain was the sister of prince Owain I of Gwynedd (died 1170). Gwenllian's "patriotic revolt" and subsequent death in battle at Kidwelly Castle contributed to the Great Revolt of 1136.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are several notable artistic depictions of Gwenllian. However, she is often confused with Gwenllian ferch Llywelyn, who lived two centuries later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian was the youngest daughter of Gwynedd's prince Gruffydd ap Cynan and his wife Angharad. She was born in 1097 on Ynys Môn at the family seat at Aberffraw, and was the youngest of eight children; four older sisters, Mared, 
2 CONC Rhiannell, Susanna, and Annest, and three older brothers, Cadwallon, Owain and Cadwaladr. Gwenllian grew to be strikingly beautiful, and after Gruffydd ap Rhys, the prince of Deheubarth, ventured to Gwynedd around 1113 to meet wit
2 CONC h her father, she and Deheubarth's prince became romantically involved and eloped.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian joined her husband at his family seat of Dinefwr in Deheubarth. However, Deheubarth was struggeling against the Norman invasion in south Wales, with Norman, English, and Flemish colonists in footholds through-out the cou
2 CONC ntry. While the conflict between the Normans and the Welsh continued, the princely family were often displaced, with Gwenllian joining her husband in mountainous and forested strongholds.[1] From here, she and Gruffydd ap Rhys led
2 CONC  retalitory strikes against Norman-held positions in Deheubarth.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]The Great Revolt 1136
2 CONT
2 CONT See also Wales and the Normans: 1067–1283
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1136 an opportunity arose for the Welsh to recover lands lost to the Marcher lords when Stephen de Blois displaced his cousin Empress Matilda from succeeding her father to the English throne the prior year, sparking the Anarchy
2 CONC  in England.[2][3] The usurption and conflict it caused eroded central authority in England.[2] The revolt began in south Wales, as Hywel ap Maredudd, lord of Brycheiniog (Brecknockshire), gathered his men and marched to the Gower
2 CONC , defeating the Norman and English colonists there. [2][1] Inspired by Hywel of Brycheiniog's success, Gruffydd ap Rhys hastened to meet with Gruffydd I of Gwynedd, his father-in-law, to enlist his aid in the revolt.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT While her husband was in Gwynedd seeking an alliance with her father against the Normans, Maurice of London and other Normans led raids against Deheubarth's Welsh, and Gwenllian was compelled to raise an army for their defense.[2]
2 CONC  In a battle fought near Kidwelly Castle, Gwenllian's army was routed, and she was captured and beheaded by the Normans.[2] In the battle her son Morgan was also slain and another Maelgwen captured and executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though defeated, her 'patriotic revolt' inspired others in south Wales to rise.[2] The Welsh of Gwent, led by Iowerth ab Owain (grandson of Caradog ap Gruffydd, Gwent's Welsh ruler displaced by the Norman invasions), ambushed and 
2 CONC slew Richard Fitz Gilbert, the Norman lord who controlled Ceredigion.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT When word reached Gwynedd of Gwenllain's death and the revolt in Gwent, Gwenllian's brothers Owain and Cadwaladr invaded Norman controlled Ceredigon, taking Llanfihangel, Aberystwyth, and Llanbadarn.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Gwenllian's legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian's actions can be compared to another Celtic leader: Boadicea or Buddug. This is the only known example of a medieval age woman leading a Welsh army into battle. The field where the battle is believed to have taken place,
2 CONC  close to Kidwelly Castle and north of the town, is known as Maes Gwenllian (Welsh for Field of Gwenllian) . A spring in the field is also named after her, supposedly welling up on the spot where she was beheaded.
2 CONT
2 CONT For centuries after her death, Welshmen cried-out Revenge for Gwenllian when engaging in battle.[1] Additionally, Gwenllian and her husband harassed Norman, English, and Flemish colonists in Deheubarth, taking goods and money and 
2 CONC redistributed them among the Deheubarth Welsh who were themselves dispossessed by those colonizers, as a pair of Robin Hoods of Wales, wrote historin and author Philip Warner[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian's youngest son went on to become a notable leader, The Lord Rhys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gryffith ap Rhys ap Tewdwr, the rightful Prince of South Wales, openly defied the King and continually created pandemonium and general mayhem in the Carmarthen - Llandovery - Swansea district. Gryffith's wife, Gwenllian, daughter 
2 CONC of Griffith ap Cynan, Prince of North Wales, was, in her own right as formidable a foe as her husband, and in the year 1130, while her husband was fundraising in the North, she, at the head of an army, marched on Kidwelly; taking 
2 CONC on the King's forces led by Maurice de Londres, Lord of the Manor of Kidwelly.
2 CONT
2 CONT The King's army took up their position on the slope of a hill about a mile and a half north of Kidwelly, on the west bank of the Gwendraeth Fach River, a district still known to this day as Kingswood. Opposite, on the other side o
2 CONC f the river, the actual site of the engagement is now the location of a farm Maes Gwenllian (Gwenllian's Field). On this site, both Gwenllian and her son Morgan, were put to death by the victorious de Londres, and it is said that 
2 CONC Gwenllian's severed head never left the field!
2 CONT
2 CONT GWENLLIAN (d. 1136), daughter of Gruffudd ap Cynan (q.v.), by Angharad, daughter of Owen ap Edwin. She m. Gruffydd ap Rhys (q.v.) shortly after 1116, the most famous of her sons being the ‘lord’ Rhys ap Gruffydd (q.v.). At the ope
2 CONC ning of the great Welsh uprising in 1136, she led an attack on the Norman fortress of Kidwelly, in her husband's absence, and was killed fighting outside the town, at a spot still known as Maes Gwenllian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hist. W.
2 CONT
2 CONT Author:
2 CONT
2 CONT Professor Thomas Jones Pierce, M.A., F.S.A., (1905-1964), Aberystwyth
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Excellent discussions about Gwenllian, the Welsh herione: http://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/wales/posts/Gwenllian-Welsh-heroine http://cadw.wales.gov.uk/docs/cadw/publications/Gwenllian_EN.pdf
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gwenllian_ferch_Gruffydd#Gwenllian.27s_legacy
2 CONT
