1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Birth: circa 565 
2 CONT ** Danmark  
2 CONT *Death: 603 (38) 
2 CONT **,Uppsala, Sweden   Immediate Family: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Daughter of Helgi* "Acutus" "Hvasse" Halfdansson, of Denmark and Olaf the Mighty, a queen of Saxland
2 CONT *Wife of Adils* "Athils" Ottarsson, king in Uppsala and Helgi* "Acutus" "Hvasse" Halfdansson, of Denmark    (the Swedish king Eadgils)
2 CONT *Mother of Eystein* / Øystein /Östen Vendel The Crow of Adilsson, king in Uppsala, Sweden, Rolv Helgeson Krake, King, Queen Skuld, Skuld Adilsdatter of Sweden and Gautrek Gautsson
2 CONT *Sister of Alf* Olafsson av Olafsson, Gyrithe Alfsdatter, Rolf Kraki and Skulda
2 CONT *Half sister of Alf* Olafsson av Olafsson
2 CONT
2 CONT Yrsa, Yrse, Yrs or Urse (6th century) was a tragic heroine of Scandinavian legend.
2 CONT
2 CONT She appears in several versions relating to her husband, the Swedish king Eadgils, and/or to her father and rapist/lover/husband Halga (the younger brother of king Hroðgar who received Beowulf) and their son Hroðulf. The consensus
2 CONC  view is that the people surrounding Yrsa are the same people as those found in Beowulf, and the common claim in Beowulf studies that Hroðulf probably was the son of Halga is taken from the Yrsa tradition. Several translators (e.g
2 CONC . Burton Raffel) and scholars have emended her name from a corrupt line (62) in the manuscript of Beowulf, although this is guesswork. In the Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson describes her personality as follows (Samuel Laing's tra
2 CONC nslation):
2 CONT
2 CONT “	Yrsa was not one of the slave girls, and it was soon observed that she was intelligent, spoke well, and in all respects was well behaved. All people thought well of her, and particularly the king; and at last it came to this tha
2 CONC t the king celebrated his wedding with her, and Yrsa became queen of Sweden, and was considered an excellent woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicon Lethrense (and the included Annales Lundenses) tells that one day, the Danish king Helghe arrived in Halland/Lolland[3] and slept with Thore, the daughter of one of Ro's farmers. This resulted in Yrse. Much later, he
2 CONC  met Yrse, and without knowing that she was his daughter, he made her pregnant with Rolf Krage. Lastly, he found out that Yrse was his own daughter, went east and killed himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ro is the same personage as Hroðgar, who received Beowulf at Heorot. His co-king Helghe is the same as Hroðgar's brother Halga, and Rolf Krage is the same personage as Hroðgar's nephew Hroðulf. However, in Beowulf, it is never exp
2 CONC lained in what way they were uncle and nephew.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gesta Danorum (book 2) reports that Helgo was the brother of the Danish king Ro, but whereas Ro was king of the Danish lands, Helgo had inherited the sea. One day during his sea roving, arrived at Thurø, where he found and rap
2 CONC ed the young girl Thora, who became pregnant with Urse. When Helgo after many years returned to Thurø, Thora avenged her lost virginity by sending Urse to Helgo who, unknowingly raped his own daughter. This resulted in Roluo Kraki
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT During a Swedish invasion, Ro was killed by Hothbrodd, the king of Sweden. Helgo avenged his brother's death and made the Swedes pay tribute. However, he then chose to commit suicide due to his shame for his incestuous relationshi
2 CONC p with Urse, and their son Roluo Kraki succeeded him. Athislus, the new king of Sweden thought that the tribute to the Daner might be smaller if he married the Danish king's mother and so took Urse for a queen. However, after some
2 CONC  time, Urse was so upset with the Swedish king's greediness that she thought out a ruse to run away from the king and at the same time liberate him of his wealth. She encited Athislus to rebell against Roluo, and arranged so that 
2 CONC Roluo would be invited and promised a wealth in gifts.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the banquet had lasted for three days, Urse and Roluo escaped from Uppsala, early in the morning in carriages where they had put all the Swedish king's treasure. In order to lessen their burden, and to occupy any pursuing war
2 CONC riors they spread gold in their path, although there was a rumour that she only spread gilded copper. When Athislus, who was pursuing the escapers saw that a precious ring was lying on the ground, he bent down to pick it up. Roluo
2 CONC  was pleased to see the king of Sweden bent down, and escaped in the ships with his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT This account is more elaborate than that of Chronicon Lethrense and Annales Lundenses. Helgo is the same personage as Helghe/Halga. His brother the Danish king Ro is the same as Hroðgar, Roluo is the same as Hroðulf/Rolf Krage, an
2 CONC d the Swedish king Athislus is the same as Eadgils, the Swedish king of Beowulf. Yrse is here called Urse, and the story of her son fleeing the Swedish king with all his treasure is also found in the following accounts. It is note
2 CONC worthy that all the Danish sources, Chronicon Lethrense, Annales Lundenses and Gesta Danorum differ on where Halga found Yrsa, but make her Danish. The Icelandic sources that follow make her a Saxon, on the other hand, and not Dan
2 CONC ish.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the version from Hrolf Krakis saga, which gives her mother's name as Olaf, see Helgi's record.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yrsa
2 CONT
