2 TITL Heimskringla: Harald Hárfagri's saga, Snorri Sturlusson
1 NOTE From Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagre's saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "22. KING HARALD'S VOYAGE TO THE WEST.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald heard that the vikings, who were in the West sea in winter, plundered far and wide in the middle part of Norway; and therefore every summer he made an expedition to search the isles and out-skerries (1) on the coast. W
2 CONC heresoever the vikings heard of him they all took to flight, and most of them out into the open ocean. At last the king grew weary of this work, and therefore one summer he sailed with his fleet right out into the West sea. First 
2 CONC he came to Hjaltland (Shetland), and he slew all the vikings who could not save themselves by flight. Then King Harald sailed southwards, to the Orkney Islands, and cleared them all of vikings. Thereafter he proceeded to the Sudre
2 CONC ys (Hebrides), plundered there, and slew many vikings who formerly had had men-at-arms under them. Many a battle was fought, and King Harald was always victorious. He then plundered far and wide in Scotland itself, and had a battl
2 CONC e there. When he was come westward as far as the Isle of Man, the report of his exploits on the land had gone before him; for all the inhabitants had fled over to Scotland, and the island was left entirely bare both of people and 
2 CONC goods, so that King Harald and his men made no booty when they landed. So says Hornklofe: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "The wise, the noble king, great
2 CONT Whose hand so freely scatters gold,
2 CONT Led many a northern shield to war
2 CONT Against the town upon the shore.
2 CONT The wolves soon gathered on the sand
2 CONT Of that sea-shore; for Harald's hand
2 CONT The Scottish army drove away,
2 CONT And on the coast left wolves a prey."
2 CONT
2 CONT In this war fell Ivar, a son of Ragnvald, Earl of More; and King Harald gave Ragnvald, as a compensation for the loss, the Orkney and Shetland isles, when he sailed from the West; but Ragnvald immediately gave both these countries
2 CONC  to his brother Sigurd, who remained behind them; and King Harald, before sailing eastward, gave Sigurd the earldom of them. ..."
