1 OCCU King of Dublin
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ívarr Gudrødsson''', Viking king of Dublin and perhaps also of York. He sacked Dumbarton in 870.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father: King Godhfraidh (Gudrød)
2 CONT * Mother: Unknown
2 CONT * Children:
2 CONT ** Baird (died 881)
2 CONT ** Sigfrith (died 888)
2 CONT ** Sigtrygg (died 895)
2 CONT ** Olaf (died 896)
2 CONT **unnamed son
2 CONT **other unnamed children (unknown number of individuals of unknown gender)
2 CONT
2 CONT == Details ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar Gudrødsson, birth date and place are not known; died 873 or 874 in Dublin, Ireland. Mentioned 853-873 as King of Dublin.
2 CONT Ímar's death notice in the Annals of Ulster is as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Imhar rex Nordmannorum totius Hibernię Brittanie uitam finiuit'' > Ímar king of the Norwegian Vikings of the whole of Ireland and Britain ended his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar said to have been the brother of Olaf Hvite, and according to a contemporary Irish source, he was the son of King Godhfraidh (Gudrød), about whom nothing further is known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==the identification of Ímar and Ingvar/Ivar==
2 CONT There is a commonly held opinion that Ímar of Dublin and [http://www.geni.com/people/Ivar-The-Boneless/6000000002043186264 Ivar "the Boneless" Ragnarsson] are identical, but it is equally frequently been rejected or treated as a m
2 CONC ere possibility. On Geni it has been accepted that we do not know and separate profiles are maintained.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Read more:===
2 CONT *http://www.snl.no/.nbl_biografi/Ivar_Gudr%C3%B8dsson/utdypning
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%8Dmar
2 CONT *D. Ó Corrain: Ireland before the Normans, Dublin 1972
2 CONT *A. P. Smyth: Scandinavian York and Dublin. The history and archaeology of two related viking kingdoms, bd. 1–2, Dublin 1975–79
2 CONT *http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/IRELAND.htm#_Toc389126206
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Hyfaidd ap Bleddri versus Sons of Rhodri Mawr; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id266.html. (Steven Ferry, May 5, 2021.)
