1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (Check dates born/deceased)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1090 he along with his elder brother Robert built a castle at Pembroke in West Wales. In 1093 king William II of England rewarded his efforts with the formal lordship of Pembroke; some historians say that he was in fact cre
2 CONC ated Earl of Pembroke. In any case the lordship was smaller than the later Pembrokeshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT His holdings were greatly expanded in 1096 when Rufus gave him the lordship of Holderness, which in addition to that part of Yorkshire included land in Lincolnshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Earl. He was the Earl of Pembroke, before 1100.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf of Montgomery (c. 1068–1118/1122) was an Anglo-Norman aristocrat, who played a role in the history of England, Wales, and Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the youngest son of Roger de Montgomerie, 1st Earl of Shrewsbury and Mabel of Bellême.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Early Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1090 he along with his elder brother Robert built a castle at Pembroke in West Wales. In 1093 king William II of England rewarded his efforts with the formal lordship of Pembroke; some historians say that he was in fact cre
2 CONC ated Earl of Pembroke. In any case the lordship was smaller than the later Pembrokeshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT His holdings were greatly expanded in 1096 when Rufus gave him the lordship of Holderness, which in addition to that part of Yorkshire included land in Lincolnshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Rebellion & Banishment
2 CONT
2 CONT It is likely that Arnulf had been designated heir of his brother Hugh of Montgomery, 2nd Earl of Shrewsbury, but after Hugh's death in 1098 Arnulf was outmaneouvered by the eldest brother Robert, who became the 3rd earl of Shrewsb
2 CONC ury. This caused some rift between the brothers but nevertheless Robert participated in their rebellion of 1102 against Henry I of England which caused the loss of all their English and Welsh lands, and their banishment from the k
2 CONC ingdom of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf turned his attention to Ireland, where not long before he had married Lafrocoth, daughter of the Irish king Muircheartach Ua Briain in about the year 1100, and certainly before 1102, when he is mentioned by Muirchertach as 
2 CONC his son-in-law in a letter to the Archbishop of Canterbury Anselm of Bec . Muirchertach provided support for Arnulf's rebellion, and as a result King Henry I of England placed a trade embargo on Ireland. It is suggested by Orderic
2 CONC  Vitalis that Arnulf went to Ireland after the rebellion failed and served for Muirchertach Ua Briain, although the Irish Annals make no mention of this.
2 CONT
2 CONT In later years he was in the entourage of count Fulk V of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter G. Ashworth: Wikipedia encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 1066 - approx.1122
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Roger de Montgomery, Viscount of Hiemos and Mabel de Belleme. Roger was a kinsman of William the Conquerer, but did not participate in the invasion. He was made Earl of Shrewsbury, and was one of the richest Tenants in Chie
2 CONC f in England. He also built the original fort at Pembroke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnulf was Earl of Pembroke, and Marcher Lord of Wales. Seated at the original Norman fortification at Pembroke, later Pembroke Castle. Revolted against King of England with his brother. Married a daughter of the King of Munster. 
2 CONC Gerald de Windsor was his Steward. He spent most of his time in England, later in Normandy and Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnulf_de_Montgomery#undefined
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Arnulf de Montgomery (c. 1066–1118×22), also known as Arnulf of Montgomery, was an Anglo-Norman magnate. A younger son of a leading magnate in Normandy and England, Arnulf played an active part in the Anglo-Norman invasion of sout
2 CONC hwestern Wales in the late eleventh century. Following his successes against the Welsh, Arnulf established himself at Pembroke, built an earth and timber castle, and was likely rewarded with the title Earl of Pembroke. At the turn
2 CONC  of the twelfth century Arnulf reached his height, with his lordship including much of the former Welsh kingdom of Deheubarth and lands in Yorkshire. A short time later, he joined his elder brother's rebellion against the king of 
2 CONC England, and married a daughter of the king of Munster in an effort to gain military support against his English sovereign. Following the ultimate collapse of the rebellion, Arnulf and his brothers were outlawed and banished from 
2 CONC the realm, and Arnulf appears to have spent much of the next twenty-odd years in a peripatetic life in Ireland and Normandy.
