2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/34/48/9a/08/53444868be2492ab/img_7833_original.jpg?hash=356f69fe30a257602b9e803141de13d5e1a6c65dd82e42056d0e10d2e88867a4.1794643199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Coat of Arms for Arnulph de Montgomery, Earl of Pembroke (b. 1066)
2 NOTE Arms Attributed to Arnulph de Montgomery, Earl of Pembroke (b. 1066):
3 CONT
3 CONT The coat of arms herein displayed—Gules, a lion rampant Or—is a retroactively attributed device aligned with the early heraldic tradition of the Montgomery family. While Arnulph himself preceded the codification of armorial bearin
3 CONC gs, this design reflects the arms later borne by his descendants and kin, particularly during the rise of heraldic usage in the 12th and 13th centuries.
3 CONT
3 CONT The lion rampant, rendered in gold upon a red field, symbolizes martial prowess and feudal authority—virtues strongly associated with the Norman aristocracy of Arnulph’s standing. It is emblematic of personal valor rather than sov
3 CONC ereign dominion.
3 CONT
3 CONT The previously displayed arms featuring two lions passant guardant are more accurately identified with the Duchy of Normandy and, subsequently, the Angevin monarchs of England. As such, their application to Arnulph de Montgomery w
3 CONC ould be anachronistic and improperly confer ducal or royal connotations upon a vassal earl.
3 CONT
3 CONT This correction restores heraldic fidelity, anchoring the arms in the appropriate familial and historical context.
