1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Donal "the Hero" was one of two known sons of Aodh "Athlamh" O'Neill. He was a Prince of Tyrone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnhall became the progenitor of the O'Neill's of Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Domhnall "an t-Ogdhamh" (Donald the Young Ox), sometimes called "Donal the Hero", was a Prince of Tyrone,  was the son of Aodh Athlamh, the King of Aileach.In the traditional pedigree of Clan MacNeil, Aodh Athlamh is counted as th
2 CONC e 19th lineal descendant from Niall of the Nine Hostages—making Domnall his brother to the branch that went to Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall remained in Ulster, fought for the northern throne until his death in 1057, becaming the direct ancestor of the O'Neill Kings of Tyrone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anradhán settled in Argyll, Scotland, married into the local Norse-Gaelic nobility, and became the traditional progenitor of the MacNeils of Barra, as well as the MacSweens, Lamonts, and MacLachlans.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was styled a King of Ulster (with opposition), though in effect, he was a pretender to the northern throne for five years until he died in 1057, killed by Mac Lochlainin, the effective king.   
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the historical narrative and medieval Irish texts like the Leabhar Chlainne Suibhne:The Brotherly Dispute: Aodh Athlamh had two main sons: Domnall and Anradhán (also written as Aodh Aonrachan).The Division of 1033: In
2 CONC  1033, a dispute or agreement took place regarding the leadership. Anradhán chose to leave Ireland and sailed to Scotland, leaving Domnall behind.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
