2 CONT Boete, Boite, Beoedhe, Bode  Father of Gille and Gruoch who married Macbeth
2 CONT
2 CONT Etymology of his name is obscure; it may be cognate with Irish "Buadach" (victorious; cf. Boudica), or "Buite" (a saint's name), or "Beoaodh" (living fire); or he may have been directly named for the philosopher Boethius (c. 480–5
2 CONC 24 AD), who had a high reputation throughout the Middle Ages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death date of "1058" is ''not'' confirmed, and he may ''not'' have survived the purges of Malcolm II. If he did, he had some flaw (perhaps as minor as a port-wine birthmark of the face) that disqualified him (but not his descendan
2 CONC ts) from the kingship.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#_Toc209085741
2 CONT Boite mac Cináeda
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Bodhe)
2 CONT
2 CONT Boite mac Cináeda ("Boite son of Kenneth") (also, Bodhe, Boedhe, etc) (985-1058) was a Scottish prince, son of either King Kenneth II (Cináed mac Maíl Choluim) or King Kenneth III (Cináed mac Duib).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the father of Gruoch of Scotland and friend to Findláech of Moray, Macbeth's father. He arranged the marriage of Macbeth and Gruoch in 1032, which permitted Macbeth to assume the throne of Scotland in 1040. Later he was beh
2 CONC ind the short-lived ascension of Lulach, his grandson, to the throne in 1057.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Alternative accounts have it that Malcolm II ''did'' include him in a purge of all possible rivals to his grandson Duncan - which would give Gruoch an even stronger motive for revenge.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Kenneth III of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether Boite mac Cináeda was a son of this Kenneth [III], or of Kenneth II, is uncertain, although most propose this Kenneth. A son, or grandson of Boite, was reported to be killed by Malcolm II in 1032 in the Annals of Ulster.[6
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that descent from Kenneth II would give Gruoch, and therefore Macbeth, a *far* stronger claim to the throne. On the other hand, that would make Macbeth and Gruoch first cousins once removed, and they would have had to get a d
2 CONC ispensation from the Pope for that (which, admittedly, was sometimes granted after-the-fact).
