1 NAME Cacht ingen /Ragnaill/
2 GIVN Cacht ingen
2 SURN Ragnaill
2 _MARNM MacBriain
1 SEX F
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1052
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Cacht, daughter of Ragnall, d. 1052 [AT] or 1054 [CS], wife of Donnchad (son of Brian Boru), whom she married in 1032 [AI]. It is apparently unknown whether or not she was the mother of any of Donnchad's children. 
2 CONC So the mother of Darbforgaill aka Devorgilla may be Gormflaith ingen Murchada, the other wife of Donnchad.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Too Many Ragnalls"
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the common problems in genealogy and history is that there may be two or more sources, each of which mentions an individual of the same name, and one must then decide whether or not these sources pertain to the same individ
2 CONC ual. This appendix is a minor revision of a posting which originally appeared under the same title as a separate posting to soc.genealogy.medieval. As can be easily guessed from the title of the appendix, this appendix involves se
2 CONC veral individuals named Ragnall. We start off with four men of the name who are fairly easy to distinguish in the records. The problems come with the later generations, when we have individuals who are referred to as children or g
2 CONC randchildren of Ragnall, without explicitly stating which one. The purpose of this appendix will be to set out the basic evidence, and no attempt will be made here to definatively sort out which Ragnall was the father (or grandfat
2 CONC her) of which child. It should be noted that there were also other earlier and later Vikings named Ragnall who are not listed, as we are only concerned with listing those Ragnalls who might make chronologically possible parents (o
2 CONC r grandparents) of the individuals having unknown ancestry.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ragnalls in question are (numbered in order of their first appearance in the records):
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) Ragnall, son of Amlaib Cuaran (see Table 1). This Ragnall was apparently the recognized heir of Amlaib (d. 981) to the kingship of Dublin, but was killed in the Battle of Tara in 980 [AU, AT, AI, CS]. While he seems a bit earl
2 CONC y to be the father of most of the candidates below, such a relationship is not impossible.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Ragnall, a prince of the dynasty of Waterford who was killed by Leinstermen in either 994 [AU] or 995 [AI and AT]. According to AU, Ragnall was the son of Imar (d. 1000) king of Waterford, whereas AI states that Ragnall was th
2 CONC e grandson of Imar, and AT does not give Ragnall's parentage. AU is probably correct on this point. [King Imar of Waterford, whose parentage is apparently unknown, also temporarily ruled Dublin in the 990's, in a back-and-forth co
2 CONC ntest with Sitric "Silkbeard", son of Amlaib Cuaran.]
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) Ragnall, king of the Isles [i.e., the Hebrides], d. 1005 [AU], son of Goffraid mac Arailt (d. 989), king of the Isles (see Table 2).
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) Ragnall, king of Waterford, d. 1035 [AU], grandson of Imar of Waterford. AU has a duplicate entry placing his death in 1031, and AT apparently also places his death in 1031 [the years in AT are not labelled], but that is clear
2 CONC ly an error, since the following entry of ATin the same year is the death of the famous Cnut, who certainly died in 1035. Probably some entries from 1035 were accidently placed in 1031 in one of the intermediate stages of compilat
2 CONC ion. AU calls Ragnall the grandson of Imar, and AT calls him the son of "Radnall", son of Imar. Todd, in his edition of "Coghad Gaedhel re Gallaibh", states that AT[1031] calls Ragnall the son of Radnall, "daughter" of Imar (and R
2 CONC adnall is a known feminine name). Thus, either Whitley Stokes's edition of AT is in error, or Todd has misquoted AT here (Todd wrote earlier than the Stokes edition of AT.) I leave it open whether Imar was Ragnall's paternal or ma
2 CONC ternal grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT The known individuals who were children (or grandchildren) of men named Ragnall (not counting the possible #4 above) were:
2 CONT
2 CONT (a) NN, unnamed son of Ragnall, son of Imar, who was killed in 1015 [CS]. He was evidently the son of Ragnall #2.
2 CONT
2 CONT (b) Cacht, daughter of Ragnall, d. 1052 [AT] or 1054 [CS], wife of Donnchad (son of Brian Boru), whom she married in 1032 [AI]. It is apparently unknown whether or not she was the mother of any of Donnchad's children.
2 CONT
2 CONT (c) Echmarcach, king of Dublin (1036-1038 and 1046-1052), son of Ragnall. He was expelled from the kingship of Dublin in 1052, and went "over the sea". The contemporary Marianus Scotus records his death in 1065, as "rex innarenn",
2 CONC  generally interpreted that he was king of the Rhinns in Galloway. See Table 3 above.
2 CONT
2 CONT (d) NN, unnamed son of Ragnall, apparently king of the Isle of Man in the year 1061, when Murchad, son of Diarmait mac Mael na mBo invaded Man, took tribute, and defeated Ragnall's son [AT]. Often regarded as the same person as Ec
2 CONC hmarcach.
2 CONT
2 CONT (e) NN, unnamed son of Ragnall, king of the Foreigners, d. 1064 [AI]. Probably the same as Echmarcach, as the discrepancy is only one year.
2 CONT
2 CONT (f) Gofraid, king of Dublin, d. 1075 [AU, AT, AI]. AI calls him grandson of Ragnall, and AU apparently calls him son of an unidentified Amlaib [but there is confusion at this point in the different manuscripts of AU]. Thus, he wou
2 CONC ld appear to be son of Amlaib, son of Ragnall. The Sitric son of Amlaib who was killed on the Isle of Man in 1073 [AU] is a possible brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT (g) Two or more grandsons of Ragnall, who led a fleet to the Isle of Man in 1087, and were killed there [AU].
2 CONT
2 CONT For a recent discussion of some of these individuals, see Duffy (1992), which assigns some of these children to the Waterford branch, but without discussing the other possible candidates. With regard to the parentage of Echmarcach
2 CONC , Hudson (1992) is more cautious, also mentioning the king of the Isles who died in 1005 as a possibility.
