1 OCCU Queen-consort of Leinster, Queen Consort of Leinster
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===MOR O'TOOLE QUEEN-CONSORT OF LEINSTER IRELAND===
2 CONT
2 CONT Mor was born in Castledermot, Kildare, Ireland in about 1114, the daughter of Muitchertach O'Toole (O'Tuathail), King of the Ui Muirdeaigh, and Cacht Inion Loigsig O'Morda. Her paternal grandparents were Gilla Comgaill O'Toole and
2 CONC  Sadb Mael Morda O'Domnail. Her maternal grandparents were Loigsig O'Morda, King of Loigsig and Gormlaith Inion Finn O'Caellaide (O'Kelly).
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Mor's four half-brothers was St. Laurence O'Toole, Archbishop of Dublin who was canonised in 1225 by Pope Honorius III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime about 1140 in Lough Carmen, County Wexford, Mor married as his first wife, King Dermot MacMurrough of Leinster, making her Queen-consort of Leinster. His second wife was Sadhbh Ni Fhaolain. In 1152, he abducted the wife o
2 CONC f Tiernan O'Rourke, King of Breifny. She was Derbhforghaill Ni Mhaol Seachlainn, who is often referred to as King Dermot's third wife, however, she was in point of fact, his mistress, by whom he had several children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together Dermot and Mor had about three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Conchobhar MacMurrough (died 1167)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Aoife of Leinster (1145- 1188), married Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, known in history as Strongbow, by whom she had two children, including Isabel de Clare, 4th Countess of Pembroke, who became the heiress to her 
2 CONC father's titles and estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Orlachan of Leinster, married Donough More, King of Thomond, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1167, Mor's son Conchobhar was killed by Rory O'Connor, High King of Ireland, after having been taken hostage while Dermot waged war against Rory with the aim of overthrowing him in order to take his place as the High King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Mor died in 1191, three years after her eldest daughter, Aoife. Her husband King Dermot had died on 1 May 1171 in Ferns, shortly after the Cambro-Norman invasion of Ireland led by their son-in-law, Strongbow, and which Dermo
2 CONC t had himself instigated and abetted.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In Celtic, Mór Uí Thuathail.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mor_O%27Toole
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mor_O%27Toole
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Mór Uí Thuathail is often anglicized as "More O'Toole." She was one of the two simultaneous wives of Diarmuid MacMurrough, King of Leinster, our ancestor.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Mór Ní Thuathail (c. 1114–1191) was a Queen-consort of Leinster as the principal first wife of King Diarmait Mac Murchada. Under Brehon Law, Irish men were allowed more than one wife. King Dermot's second wife was Sadhbh Ní Fhaola
2 CONC in.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mór was the mother of Aoife of Leinster, the wife of Richard de Clare, Earl of Pembroke, known to history as Strongbow.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Mór was born in Castledermot, Kildare, Ireland in about 1114, the daughter of Muirchertach Ua Tuathail, King of the Uí Muirdeaigh, and Cacht Ní Morda.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Gilla Comgaill Ua Toole and Sadbh Ní Domnail and her maternal grandparents were Loigsig Ua Morda, King of Laois and Gormlaith Ní Caellaide.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Mor's four half-brothers was St. Lorcán Ua Tuathail, Archbishop of Dublin, who was canonised in 1225 by Pope Honorius III.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and issue'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime about 1140 in Loch Garman, County Wexford, Mór was married to King Diarmait Mac Murchada of Leinster as his principal first wife, making her Queen-consort of Leinster. His second wife was Sadhbh Ní Fhaolain. Under Brehon 
2 CONC Law, Irish men were permitted more than one wife. In 1152, he abducted Derbforgaill Ní Mhaol Seachlainn, the wife of the King of Breifne, Tighearnán Ua Ruairc (Irish: Tighearnán Ua Ruairc).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Together Dermot and Mór had about three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Conchobhar Mac Murchada (died 1167)
2 CONT *Aoife MacMurrough (1145–1188), married 29 August 1170, Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, known to history as Strongbow, by whom she had two children, including Isabel de Clare, 4th Countess of Pembroke, who became the heire
2 CONC ss to her father's titles and estates.
2 CONT *Órlaith of Leinster,[1] married Domnall Mór Ua Briain, King of Thomond, by whom she had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1167, Mór's son Conchobhar was killed by Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair, High King of Ireland, after having been taken hostage while Diarmait waged war against Ruaidrí with the aim of overthrowing him in order to take his place as the H
2 CONC igh King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Mór died in 1191, three years after her eldest daughter, Aoife. Her husband predeceased her on 1 May 1171 in Ferns, shortly after the Cambro-Norman invasion of Ireland led by their son-in-law, Strongbow.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/no-name/6000000079563782104 DIARMAIT] (-Fermanagh 1 May 1171).  King of Leinster.  The Annales Cambriæ record that "Diermit rex filius Murcath" was expelled from Ireland by his people and went to the E
2 CONC nglish king, recording in later passages that he returned to Ireland and built "castellum Carrec iuxta Wisefordiam" in 1170, and died in 1172[693].  "Diarmicius Rex Lageniensium" donated "terram…Balidubgaill" to All Saints Dublin 
2 CONC by undated charter witnessed by "…Enna filio meo…"[694].  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that Henry II King of England arrived in Ireland in 1171, went to Dublin where he received “the kingship of Leinster and of th
2 CONC e men of Meath, Brefne, Oriel and Ulster”[695].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records the death "apud Fernas circa kal Mai" of "Dermitius Murchardi filius" [in 1171][696].  m [https://www.geni.com/people/no-name/6000000079565452902 ''
2 CONC 'MOR'''], daughter of ---].  The primary source which confirms her marriage has not yet been identified.   Mistress: (1152) [https://www.geni.com/people/Derbforgaill/6000000079565324093?through=6000000079565128944 DERBFORGAILL], w
2 CONC ife of [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000079565128944?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529949407223&through=6000000079565128944 TIERNAN O'Rourke] King of Breifny, daughter of [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/60000000795653260
2 CONC 87?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529949407223&through=6000000079565326087 MURCHAD O'Malaghlin] King of Meath & his wife ---.  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that "Dermitius…Murchardi filius Langeniensium princeps" eloped with "Orori
2 CONC cio…Medensium rege…uxor ipsius Omachlachelini filia" [in 1152][697].  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that “Diarmait Mac Murchada king of Leinster forcibly carried off out of Meath the wife of Húa Ruairc…Derb-forgail
2 CONC l daughter of Murchad with her wealth” in 1152, adding in a later passage that she “came again to Húa Ruairc by flight from Leinster” in 1153[698].  Diarmait & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>i)          [https://www.geni.com/people/Eva-of-Leinster-Countess-Of-Stirgoil/6000000002253465789?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529951541967&through=6000000002043192150  AOIFE] [Eva] (-after 1189).  The Annales Cambriæ record th
2 CONC at "Ricardus comes de Striguil" invaded Ireland in 1171 and married "filiam Diermit regis"[699].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records the marriage of "filia…Dermitii, Eva" and Earl Richard[700].  She was styled Countess of Ireland in
2 CONC  1185, but Countess of Strigoil in 1186[701].  m (Waterford [26 Aug 1171]) [https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-de-Clare-2nd-Earl-of-Pembroke/6000000001446542148?through=6000000002253465789 RICHARD de Clare "Strongbow"] Earl of Pe
2 CONC mbroke, son of GILBERT de Clare "Strongbow" Earl of Pembroke & his wife Isabel de Beaumont ([1130]-Dublin 20 Apr 1176, bur Dublin, Holy Trinity).  In [1168] he promised Dermot King of Leinster to help him recover his kingdom in re
2 CONC turn for the hand of his daughter and eventual succession to the crown.  He landed near Waterford 23 Aug 1170, and marched to Dublin.  He acceded to the demand of King Henry II to surrender his Irish conquests to him and was grant
2 CONC ed Leinster in fee[702]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>ii)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Urlachan-MacMurrough/6000000023118216712?through=6000000002253465789 URLACHAN] .  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.   m [https:
2 CONC //www.geni.com/people/Domnall-M%C3%B3r-%C3%93-Briain-R%C3%AD-Tuadhmhumhain-1168-1194/6000000023173413930?through=6000000023118216712  DONOUGH] [Donald] More King of Thomond, son of TADHG King of Thomond & his wife --- (-1194).
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>Dermot had three illegitimate children by unknown mistresses: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>iii)        [https://www.geni.com/people/Enna-mac-Murchada/6000000079566379873?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529952139398&through=6000000079566379873 ENNA] (-1168 or after).  "Diarmicius Rex Lageniensium" donated "terram…Balidub
2 CONC gaill" to All Saints Dublin by undated charter witnessed by "…Enna filio meo…"[703].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that "Ossiriæ princeps Duvenaldus" [Dounchad [Donough] Lord of Ossory] blinded "Dermitio…filium eiusdem primoge
2 CONC nitum"[704].  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that “Enna Mac Murchada crownprince of Leinster” was blinded in 1168 by “Donnchad Mac Gilla Pátraic king of Ossory”[705]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>iv)        [https://www.geni.com/people/Conchobar-Mac-Murchadha/6000000002187764124 CONNOR] (-1170).  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that "Dermitio" gave "filium suum Chuchurum" as hostage to Roderic of Connaught [in 1169]
2 CONC [706].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that Roderic killed Dermot's son [in 1170][707]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>v)         [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000079566357909?through=6000000079566357909&fsession_id=1529952139398&from_flash=1  DOMNALL Caemanach] (-killed 1175).  The Annals of Ulster record the death in 1175 of "Dom
2 CONC nall Caemanach son of Diarmaid Mac Murchadha king of Leinster…[slain]”[708]. 
