2 CONC aw also recognised a transfer of "swordland" following a conquest. Aoife conducted battles on behalf of her husband and is sometimes known as Red Eva (Irish: Aoife Rua).
2 CONT
2 CONT She had two sons and a daughter with her husband Richard de Clare, and via their daughter, Isabel de Clare, within a few generations their descendants included much of the nobility of Europe including all the monarchs of Scotland 
2 CONC since Robert I (1274-1329) and all those of England, Great Britain and the United Kingdom since Henry IV (1367-1413); and, apart from Anne of Cleves, all the queen consorts of Henry VIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: O Croinin, Daibhi (1995) Early Medieval Ireland 400-1200 London: Longman Press; p. 281 Salmonson, Jessica Amanda.(1991) The Encyclopedia of Amazons. Paragon House. Page 160. ISBN 1-55778-420-5 Weis, Frederick Lewis Ancest
2 CONC ral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700, Lines: 66-26, 175-7, 261-30
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=gonefishin&id=I098327] 2. [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aoife_MacMurrough] c.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=mjr6387&id=I56374 -------------------- Aoif
2 CONC e MacMurrough, She also known as Aoife of Leinster. She was the daughter of Dermot MacMurrough King of Leinster, and his wife Mor O'Toole. On 29 August 1170, following the Norman invasion of Ireland that her father had requested, 
2 CONC she married Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, better known as Strongbow, the leader of the Norman invasion force, in Christchurch Cathedral, Waterford.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had been promised to Strongbow by her father who had visited England to ask for an invasion army. He was not allowed to give his daughter away, as under Early Irish Law Aoife had the choice of whom she married, but she had to 
2 CONC agree to an arranged marriage . Under Anglo-Norman law, this gave Strongbow succession rights to the Kingdom of Leinster. Under Irish Brehon law, the marriage gave her a life interest only, after which any land would normally reve
2 CONC rt to male cousins; but Brehon law also recognised a transfer of "swordland" following a conquest. Aoife conducted battles on behalf of her husband and is sometimes known as Red Eva (Irish: Aoife Rua). She had two sons with her hu
2 CONC sband Richard de Clare the first son she named after her late father, Dermott MacMurrough, King of Leinster. and a daughter Isabel who married William Marshal 1st Earl of Pembroke A life-size statue of her sits at Carrickfergus Ca
2 CONC stle, with a plaque describing her as "thinking of home." <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Aoife MacMurrough (c.1145–1188, Irish: Aoife Ní Diarmait), also known by later historians as Eva of Leinster, was the daughter of Derm
2 CONC ot MacMurrough (c.1110-1171) (Irish: Diarmait MacMurchada), King of Leinster, and his wife Mor O'Toole (c.1114-1191).
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and rights
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 29 August 1170, following the Norman invasion of Ireland that her father had requested, she married Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, better known as Strongbow, the leader of the Norman invasion force, in Reginald's T
2 CONC ower in Waterford. She had been promised to Strongbow by her father who had visited England to ask for an invasion army. He was not allowed to give his daughter away, as under Early Irish Law Aoife had the choice of whom she marri
2 CONC ed, but she had to agree to an arranged marriage, that is, to select from a list of suitable suitors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Anglo-Norman law, this gave Strongbow succession rights to the Kingdom of Leinster. Under Irish Brehon law, the marriage gave her a life interest only, after which any land would normally revert to male cousins; but Brehon l
2 CONC aw also recognised a transfer of "swordland" following a conquest. Aoife conducted battles on behalf of her husband and is sometimes known as Red Eva (Irish: Aoife Rua).
2 CONT
2 CONT She had two sons and a daughter with her husband Richard de Clare, and via their daughter, Isabel de Clare, within a few generations their descendants included much of the nobility of Europe including all the monarchs of Scotland 
2 CONC since Robert I (1274-1329) and all those of England, Great Britain and the United Kingdom since Henry IV (1367-1413); and, apart from Anne of Cleves, all the queen consorts of Henry VIII.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Donnchad-mac-Murchada-King-of-Leinster-and-Dublin/6000000002043258537 DONCHAD], son of MURCHAD King of Leinster & his wife --- (-1115).  King of Leinster.  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record
2 CONC  that “the son of Murchad, son of Diarmait, son of Máel na mbó” won a battle in 1114[689].  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that “Donnchad son of Murchad king of Leinster” was killed in battle by “Domnall son of Murc
2 CONC hertach Húa Bríáin” in 1115[690]. m ---.  The name of Donchad´s wife is not known. Donchad & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>1.         [https://www.geni.com/people/Enna-King-of-Leinster/6000000010646172298 ENNA] (-1126).  King of Leinster.  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that “Toirdelbach son of Ruaidri king of Ireland” gave “the kin
2 CONC gship of the Foreigners to the king of Leinster” in 1125[691].  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that “Enna son of Donnchad son of Murchad king of Leinster” died in 1126, after which “Toirdelbach Húa Conchobair king o
2 CONC f Ireland” was made king of Leinster, unsuccessfully challenged by “Cormac Mac Carthaig king of Desmond”[692].  m ---.  The name of Enna´s wife is not known.  Enna & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/no-name/6000000079563782104 DIARMAIT] (-Fermanagh 1 May 1171).  King of Leinster.  The Annales Cambriæ record that "Diermit rex filius Murcath" was expelled from Ireland by his people a
2 CONC nd went to the English king, recording in later passages that he returned to Ireland and built "castellum Carrec iuxta Wisefordiam" in 1170, and died in 1172[693].  "Diarmicius Rex Lageniensium" donated "terram…Balidubgaill" to Al
2 CONC l Saints Dublin by undated charter witnessed by "…Enna filio meo…"[694].  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that Henry II King of England arrived in Ireland in 1171, went to Dublin where he received “the kingship of Le
2 CONC inster and of the men of Meath, Brefne, Oriel and Ulster”[695].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records the death "apud Fernas circa kal Mai" of "Dermitius Murchardi filius" [in 1171][696].  m [https://www.geni.com/people/no-name/600000
2 CONC 0079565452902 MOR], daughter of ---].  The primary source which confirms her marriage has not yet been identified.   Mistress: (1152) DERBFORGAILL, wife of [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000079565128944?from_flash=1&fsess
2 CONC ion_id=1529949407223&through=6000000079565128944 TIERNAN O'Rourke] King of Breifny, daughter of [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000079565326087?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529949407223&through=6000000079565326087 MURCHAD O'M
2 CONC alaghlin] King of Meath & his wife ---.  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that "Dermitius…Murchardi filius Langeniensium princeps" eloped with "Ororicio…Medensium rege…uxor ipsius Omachlachelini filia" [in 1152][697].  The Annals 
2 CONC of Tigernach (Continuation) record that “Diarmait Mac Murchada king of Leinster forcibly carried off out of Meath the wife of Húa Ruairc…Derb-forgaill daughter of Murchad with her wealth” in 1152, adding in a later passage that sh
2 CONC e “came again to Húa Ruairc by flight from Leinster” in 1153[698].  Diarmait & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>i)          ['''https://www.geni.com/people/Eva-of-Leinster-Countess-Of-Stirgoil/6000000002253465789?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529951541967&through=6000000002043192150  AOIFE] [Eva] (-after 1189).  The Annales Cambriæ record
2 CONC  that "Ricardus comes de Striguil" invaded Ireland in 1171 and married "filiam Diermit regis"[699].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records the marriage of "filia…Dermitii, Eva" and Earl Richard[700].  She was styled Countess of Ireland
2 CONC  in 1185, but Countess of Strigoil in 1186[701].  m (Waterford [26 Aug 1171]) [https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-de-Clare-2nd-Earl-of-Pembroke/6000000001446542148?through=6000000002253465789 RICHARD de Clare "Strongbow"] Earl of
2 CONC  Pembroke, son of GILBERT de Clare "Strongbow" Earl of Pembroke & his wife Isabel de Beaumont ([1130]-Dublin 20 Apr 1176, bur Dublin, Holy Trinity).  In [1168] he promised Dermot King of Leinster to help him recover his kingdom in
2 CONC  return for the hand of his daughter and eventual succession to the crown.  He landed near Waterford 23 Aug 1170, and marched to Dublin.  He acceded to the demand of King Henry II to surrender his Irish conquests to him and was gr
2 CONC anted Leinster in fee[702].''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>ii)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Urlachan-MacMurrough/6000000023118216712?through=6000000002253465789 URLACHAN] .  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.   m [https:
2 CONC //www.geni.com/people/Domnall-M%C3%B3r-%C3%93-Briain-R%C3%AD-Tuadhmhumhain-1168-1194/6000000023173413930?through=6000000023118216712  DONOUGH] [Donald] More King of Thomond, son of TADHG King of Thomond & his wife --- (-1194).
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>Dermot had three illegitimate children by unknown mistresses: 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>iii)        [https://www.geni.com/people/Enna-mac-Murchada/6000000079566379873?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1529952139398&through=6000000079566379873 ENNA] (-1168 or after).  "Diarmicius Rex Lageniensium" donated "terram…Balidub
2 CONC gaill" to All Saints Dublin by undated charter witnessed by "…Enna filio meo…"[703].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that "Ossiriæ princeps Duvenaldus" [Dounchad [Donough] Lord of Ossory] blinded "Dermitio…filium eiusdem primoge
2 CONC nitum"[704].  The Annals of Tigernach (Continuation) record that “Enna Mac Murchada crownprince of Leinster” was blinded in 1168 by “Donnchad Mac Gilla Pátraic king of Ossory”[705]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>iv)        [https://www.geni.com/people/Conchobar-Mac-Murchadha/6000000002187764124 CONNOR] (-1170).  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that "Dermitio" gave "filium suum Chuchurum" as hostage to Roderic of Connaught [in 1169]
2 CONC [706].  The Expugnatio Hibernica records that Roderic killed Dermot's son [in 1170][707]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>v)         [https://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000079566357909?through=6000000079566357909&fsession_id=1529952139398&from_flash=1  DOMNALL Caemanach] (-killed 1175).  The Annals of Ulster record the death in 1175 of "Dom
2 CONC nall Caemanach son of Diarmaid Mac Murchadha king of Leinster…[slain]”[708]. 
