2 CONT The Great Revolt 1136 See also: Wales and the Normans: 1067–1283
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1136 an opportunity arose for the Welsh to recover lands lost to the Marcher Lords when Stephen de Blois displaced his cousin, Empress Matilda, from succeeding her father to the English throne the year prior, sparking the Anarc
2 CONC hy in England.[4][5] The usurpation and conflict it caused eroded central authority in England.[4] The revolt began in South Wales, as Hywel ap Maredudd, Lord of Brycheiniog (Brecknockshire), gathered his men and marched to the Go
2 CONC wer, defeating the Norman and English colonists there at the Battle of Llwchwr.[3][4] Inspired by Hywel of Brycheiniog's success, Gruffydd ap Rhys hastened to meet with Gruffudd ap Cynan of Gwynedd, his father-in-law, to enlist hi
2 CONC s aid in the revolt.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT While her husband was in Gwynedd seeking an alliance with her father against the Normans, Maurice of London and other Normans led raids against Deheubarth's Welsh and Gwenllian was compelled to raise an army for their defence.[4][
2 CONC 6][7] In a battle fought near Kidwelly Castle, Gwenllian's army was routed, she was captured in battle and beheaded by the Normans.[4] In the battle her son Morgan was also slain and another son, Maelgwyn captured and executed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though defeated, her patriotic revolt inspired others in South Wales to rise.[4] The Welsh of Gwent, led by Iowerth ab Owain (grandson of Caradog ap Gruffydd, Gwent's Welsh ruler displaced by the Norman invasions), ambushed and sl
2 CONC ew Richard Fitz Gilbert de Clare, the Norman lord who controlled Ceredigion.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT When word reached Gwynedd of Gwenllain's death and the revolt in Gwent, Gwenllian's brothers Owain and Cadwaladr invaded Norman controlled Ceredigion, taking Llanfihangel, Aberystwyth, and Llanbadarn.[4]
