1 NOTE From "The Ynglinga Saga":
2 CONT
2 CONT "43. DEATH OF THE KINGS GRANMAR AND HJORVARD.
2 CONT ... It was a common saying that King Ingjald had killed twelve kings, and deceived them all under pretence of peace; therefore he was called Ingjald the Evil-adviser. He was king over the greater part of Sweden. He married his dau
2 CONC ghter Aasa to Gudrod king of Scania; and she was like her father in disposition. Aasa brought it about that Gudrod killed his brother Halfdan, father of Ivar Vidfavne; and also she brought about the death of her husband Gudrod, an
2 CONC d then fled to her father; and she thus got the name also of Aasa the Evil-adviser."
2 CONT
2 CONT "44. OF INGJALD'S DEATH.
2 CONT Ivar Vidfavne came to Scania after the fall of his uncle Gudrod, and collected an army in all haste, and moved with it into Sweden. Aasa had gone to her father before. King Ingjald was at a feast in Raening, when he heard that Kin
2 CONC g Ivar's army was in the neighbourhood. Ingjald thought he had not strength to go into battle against Ivar, and he saw well that if he betook himself to flight his enemies would swarm around him from all corners. He and Aasa took 
2 CONC a resolution which has become celebrated. They drank until all their people were dead drunk, and then put fire to the hall; and it was consumed, with all who were in it, including themselves, King Ingjald, and Aasa. ..."
1 NOTE Reine de Scanie/Scania par son époux. cf: Dynastie des Skjoldungiens. ?
2 CONT
2 CONT She killed her spouse King Gudrod.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was like her father in disposition. Aasa brought it about that Gudrod killed his brother Halfdan, father of Ivar Vidfavne; and also she brought about the death of her husband Gudrod, and then fled to her father; and she thus g
2 CONC ot the name also of Aasa the Evil-adviser.
