1 NOTE
2 CONT from &quot;Our Folk&quot; by Albert D Hart, Jr.
1 NOTE Konge i Viken.
2 CONT 221809484. Kong Olav "GEirstadalv" HARALDSSON i Viken(13078) died in 934.(13079) Han ble allerede i 934 drept i Tunsberg av sin bror Erik Blodøks. He was a Konge in Viken. (13080) I ca 912 gav Harald Hårfagre alle sine sønner kon
2 CONC genavn. Olav fikk Vingulmarkog Raumarike. Egter haralds død i 933, tok vikværingene ham til overkonge. He was married.
1 NOTE  roi de Vige
1 NOTE     * OCCUPATION: Konge i Viken
2 CONT     * BIRTH: ABT 0895, (?)
2 CONT     * DEATH: 0934 
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT King in Viken
2 CONT Fikk Vingulmark and Raumarike. Ble tatt til overkonge in Viken after
2 CONT Harald died, men slain by Eirik Blod”ks.
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT Bu709 http://home.sol.no/~torsolhe/valjerd.htm
2 CONT http://nermo.org/slekt/d0004/g0000016.html#I1855
1 NOTE Olav Haraldsson (født ca 890, død 934) var sønn av Harald Hårfagre. Han ble gjort til småkonge av Vingulmark av sin far, og arvet senere Vestfold etter at broren hans, Bjørn Farmann, ble drept av deres halvbror Eirik Blodøks. Ei
2 CONC rik var kong Haralds favorittsønn, utpekte etterfølger og ikke populær blant sine halvbrødre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Etter sin fars død utropte Olav seg til konge av Østlandet, og allierte seg med halvbroren Sigrød Haraldsson mot Eirik. De tre kongene møttes i kamp utenfor Tønsberg. Olav og Sigrød tapte og ble begge drept der. Olavs sønn, Tryggv
2 CONC e Olavsson, ble senere småkonge av Ranrike og Vingulmark. Tryggve ble far til Olav Tryggvason som ble konge av Norge i 995.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hentet fra ±http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olav_Haraldsson_Geirstadalf»
1 NOTE Konge i Viken
1 NOTE 221827276. Grev Udo II BEBHARDSON i Wetterau(13263) was born about 895.(13264) He died on 12 Dec 949.(13265) He was a Greve in Wetterau. (13266) Konradiner He was married to N HEIBERTSDTR av Vermandois in 918
1 NOTE "DIGERBEIN"; KING OF RANRIKE AND VINGULMARK
1 NOTE Snorre, 68.  Drept av sin bror Eirik Blodøks.
1 NOTE Olav "Geirstad-Alv" was king in Viken.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagre's saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "21. HARALD'S MARRIAGE AND HIS CHILDREN.
2 CONT When King Harald had now become sole king over all Norway, he remembered what that proud girl had said to him; so he sent men to her, and had her brought to him, and took her to his bed. And these were their children: Alof -- she 
2 CONC was the eldest; then was their son Hrorek; then Sigtryg, Frode, and Thorgils. King Harald had many wives and many children. Among them he had one wife, who was called Ragnhild the Mighty, a daughter of King Eirik, from Jutland; an
2 CONC d by her he had a son, Eirik Blood-axe. He was also married to Svanhild, a daughter of Earl Eystein; and their sons were Olaf Geirstadaalf, Bjorn and Ragnar Rykkil. Lastly, King Harald married Ashild, a daughter of Hring Dagson, u
2 CONC p in Ringerike; and their children were, Dag, Hring, Gudrod Skiria, and Ingigerd. It is told that King Harald put away nine wives when he married Ragnhild the Mighty. ...".
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre beskriver også tidligere en Olav Geirstad-Alv som var bror til Halvdan Svarte. Ca. 912 ga Harald Hårfagre alle sine sønner kongenavn. Olav fikk Vingulmark og Raumarike. Etter Haralds død i 933 tok vikværingene ham til overk
2 CONC onge. Olav was already killed in 934 in Tunsberg by his brother Erik Blodøks.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hårfagre's saga:
2 CONT
2 CONT "44. EIRIK BROUGHT TO THE SOVEREIGNTY.
2 CONT When King Harald was eighty years of age (A.D. 930) he became very heavy, and unable to travel through the country, or do the business of a king. Then he brought his son Eirik to his high-seat, and gave him the power and command o
2 CONC ver the whole land. Now when King Harald's other sons heard this, King Halfdan the Black also took a king's high-seat, and took all Throndhjem land, with the consent of all the people, under his rule as upper king. After the death
2 CONC  of Bjorn the Chapman, his brother Olaf took the command over Vestfold, and took Bjorn's son, Gudrod, as his foster-child. Olaf's son was called Trygve; and the two foster-brothers were about the same age, and were hopeful and cle
2 CONC ver. Trygve, especially, was remarkable as a stout and strong man. Now when the people of Viken heard that those of Hordaland had taken Eirik as upper king, they did the same, and made Olaf the upper king in Viken, which kingdom h
2 CONC e retained. Eirik did not like this at all. Two years after this, Halfdan the Black died suddenly at a feast in Throndhjem and the general report was that Gunhild had bribed a witch to give him a death-drink. Thereafter the Thrond
2 CONC hjem people took Sigrod to be their king."
2 CONT
2 CONT "45. KING HARALD'S DEATH.
2 CONT King Harald lived three years after he gave Eirik the supreme authority over his kingdom, and lived mostly on his great farms which he possessed, some in Rogaland, and some in Hordaland. ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "46. THE DEATH OF OLAF AND OF SIGROD.
2 CONT King Eirik took all the revenues (A.D. 934), which the king had in the middle of the country, the next winter after King Harald's decease. But Olaf took all the revenues eastward in Viken, and their brother Sigrod all that of the 
2 CONC Throndhjem country. Eirik was very ill pleased with this; and the report went that he would attempt with force to get the sole sovereignty over the country, in the same way as his father had given it to him. Now when Olaf and Sigr
2 CONC od heard this, messengers passed between them; and after appointing a meeting place, Sigrod went eastward in spring to Viken, and he and his brother Olaf met at Tunsberg, and remained there a while. The same spring (A.D. 934), Kin
2 CONC g Eirik levied a great force, and ships and steered towards Viken. He got such a strong steady gale that he sailed night and day, and came faster than the news of him. When he came to Tunsberg, Olaf and Sigrod, with their forces, 
2 CONC went out of the town a little eastward to a ridge, where they drew up their men in battle order; but as Eirik had many more men he won the battle. Both brothers, Olaf and Sigrod, fell there; and both their grave-mounds are upon th
2 CONC e ridge where they fell. Then King Eirik went through Viken, and subdued it, and remained far into summer. Gudrod and Trygve fled to the Uplands. Eirik was a stout handsome man, strong, and very manly, -- a great and fortunate man
2 CONC  of war; but bad-minded, gruff, unfriendly, and silent. Gunhild, his wife, was the most beautiful of women, -- clever, with much knowledge, and lively; but a very false person, and very cruel in disposition. The children of King E
2 CONC irik and Gunhild were, Gamle, the oldest; then Guthorm, Harald, Ragnfrod, Ragnhild, Erling, Gudrod, and Sigurd Sleva. All were handsome, and of manly appearance."
1 NOTE ble gjort til småkonge av Vingulmark av sin far, og arvet senere Vestfold etter at broren hans, Bjørn Farmann, ble drept av deres halvbror Eirik Blodøks. Eirik var kong Haralds favorittsønn, utpekte etterfølger og ikke populær bla
2 CONC nt sine halvbrødre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Etter hans fars død utropte Olav seg til konge av Østlandet, og allierte seg med halvbroren Sigrød Haraldsson mot Eirik. De tre kongene møttes i kamp utenfor Tønsberg. Olav og Sigrød tapte og ble begge drept der. Olavs sønn, Trygg
2 CONC ve Olavsson, ble senere småkonge av Ranrike og Vingulmark. Tryggve ble far til Olav Tryggvason som ble konge av Norge i 995.
