2 CONT biographical and/or anecdotal:
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth siezed the throne of Scotland in 1040 after defeating and killing *Duncan I near Elgin.
2 CONT
2 CONT He based his claim to the crown on his wife's royal descent (Duncan's former wife Sibyl).
2 CONT
2 CONT Malcolm III, (son of Duncan I), and Earl Siward of Northumberland defeated Macbeth at
2 CONT
2 CONT Dunsinane in 1054, but they did not dethrone him. Three years later, Malcolm III killed
2 CONT
2 CONT Macbeth at Lumphanan. Macbeth's stepson Lulach reigned for a few months,
2 CONT
2 CONT and then Malcolm III succeeded him as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Shakespeare based his play, Macbeth, one of his greatest tragedies,
2 CONT
2 CONT upon a distorted version of these events which he found in
2 CONT
2 CONT Raphael Holinshed's 'Chronicle of Scottish History.'
2 CONT
2 CONT The only kernel of historical truth in the play is Duncan's death at the hand of Macbeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT From this fact, Shakespeare drew his portrait of ambition leading to a violent and tragic end.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT House of Dunkeld, 1034-1286
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan succeeded to the throne as the maternal grandson of Malcolm II. After an unsuccessful reign, Duncan was killed in battle by Macbeth, who had a long and relatively successful reign. In a series of battles between 1057 and 10
2 CONC 58, Duncan's son Malcolm III defeated and killed Macbeth and Macbeth's stepson and heir Lulach, and claimed the throne. The dynastic feuds did not end there: on Malcolm's death in battle, his brother Donald Ban claimed the throne,
2 CONC  expelling Malcolm's sons from Scotland; a civil war in the family ensued, with Donald Ban and Malcolm's son Edmund opposed by Malcolm's English-backed sons, led first by Duncan II and then by Edgar. Edgar triumphed, sending his u
2 CONC ncle and brother to monasteries. After the reign of David I, the Scottish throne was passed according to rules of primogeniture, moving from father to son, or where not possible, brother to brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://images.google.com/imgres?imgurl=http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Domnall_D%C3%A1sachtach.jpg/70px-Domnall_D%C3%A1sachtach.jpg&imgrefurl=http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_monarchs_of_Scotland&usg=_
2 CONC _HWHPBS0PjJqtG6GXeq-qb6W2vSo=&h=94&w=70&sz=3&hl=en&start=14&tbnid=HP2XBCdwLKSBLM:&tbnh=80&tbnw=60&prev=/images%3Fq%3Ddonald%2BII%2Bthe%2Bmadman%2Bking%2Bof%2Bthe%2Bpicts%26gbv%3D2%26hl%3Den%26sa%3DG
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.clan-duncan.co.uk/duncan1.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan was the son of Malcolm II's eldest daughter Bethoc and her husband Crinan, Lay Abbot of Dunkeld. He was about 33 when he succeeded his grandfather. Married to a cousin of Siward, Earl of Northumberland
2 CONT
2 CONT William Shakespeare based his play, Macbeth, one of his greatest tragedies, upon a distorted version of these events which he found in Raphael Holinshed's 'Chronicle of Scottish History.' The only kernel of historical truth in the
2 CONC  play is Duncan's death at the hand of Macbeth. From this fact, Shakespeare drew his portrait of ambition leading to a violent and tragic end.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps05/ps05_443.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan was the grandson of King Malcolm II (ruled 1005-34), who irregularly made him ruler of Strathclyde when that region was absorbed into the Scottish kingdom (probably shortly before 1034). Malcolm violated the established sys
2 CONC tem of succession whereby the kingship alternated between two branches of the royal family. Upon Malcolm's death, Duncan succeeded peacefully, but he soon faced the rivalry of Macbeth, Mormaor (subking) of Moray, who probably had 
2 CONC a better claim to the throne. Duncan besieged Durham unsuccessfully in 1039 and in the following year was murdered by Macbeth. Duncan's elder son later killed Macbeth and ruled as King Malcolm III Canmore (1058-93).
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan married a daughter of Siward, Danish Earl of Northumbria (a Viking) and his first wife Elfleda of Northumbria; Siward, d. 1055, is son of Earl Berne of the Royal House of Denmark. Duncan succeeded his maternal grandfather, 
2 CONC Malcolm II, in 1034 ("the first example of inheritance of the Scottish throne in the direct line"); previously he was King of Cumbria (ruling from Strathclyde){-Encycl.Brit.,1956,7:736,20:146}. He was slain by his own general, Mac
2 CONC beth. His male line ruled Scotland until the death of Alexander III in 1286. Duncan is the first king of the House of Atholl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Killed by MacBeth, his successor. He was slain by his cousin Macbeth, whose wife Gruoch was a claiment
2 CONT
2 CONT to the throne. His life was forfeited according to the old Gaelic system whereby a king expected to be slain by his rightful successor, unless he could manage to kill him first, a relic from Pagan times...that was written about by
2 CONC  William Shakespeare in "MacBeth".
2 CONT
2 CONT References: [AR7],[Paget1],[RFC],[Weis1],[BurkeP]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Married to the daughter or sister of Siward, Earl of Northumberland. Killed by MacBeth, his successor. King of Strathclyde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes: This is the King Duncan who was murdered by Macbeth in the fictional play Macbeth by William Shakespeare. Apparently, Shakespeare used as his source the writings of Raphael Holinshed, author of 'Chronicle of Scottish Histor
2 CONC y', but the only historical fact to survive in Shakespear's rendition is Duncan's death at the hand of Macbeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan I (1001?-40), king of Scotland (1034-40), grandson of King Malcolm II Mackenneth, whom he succeeded. Before his accession to the Scottish throne he was ruler of the kingdom of Strathclyde. Macbeth, who ruled the neighboring
2 CONC  kingdom of Moray and served Duncan as a general, killed him and became king of Scotland. Shakespeare's tragedy Macbeth is based on the struggle between the two kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Duncan I," Microsoft(R) Encarta(R) 98 Encyclopedia. (c) 1993-1997 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.
2 CONT
2 CONT History: Duncan I (1001?-1040), king of Scotland (1034-1040), grandson of King Malcolm II Mackenneth, whom he succeeded. Before his accession to the Scottish throne he was ruler of the kingdom of Strathclyde. Macbeth, who ruled th
2 CONC e neighboring kingdom of Moray and served Duncan as a general, killed him and became king of Scotland. Shakespeare's tragedy Macbeth is based on the struggle between the two kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT History: Microsoft® Encarta® Encyclopedia 2002. © 1993-2001 Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Donnchad mac Crínáin (en inglés, Duncan) (fallecido el 15 de agosto de 1040) fue rey de Alba. Era hijo de Crínán, de la abad hereditaria de Dunkeld, y de Bethóc, hija del rey Máel Coluim mac Cináeda.
2 CONT
2 CONT A diferencia del "Rey Duncan" de la obra de William Shakespeare, Macbeth, el Donnchad histórico, y por tanto real, parece haber sido un hombre joven. Sucedió a su abuelo Máel Coluim como rey, tras la muerte de este último el 25 de
2 CONC  noviembre de 1034, sin una aparente oposición. Debió ser reconocido como el sucesor legítimo de Máel Coluim o tanista, debido a que la sucesión no no fue inmemorable.[1] Algunos relatos más tempranos, siguiendo a John de Fordun, 
2 CONC hacen suponer que Donnchad había sido rey de Strathclyde en tiempos de su abuelo, gobernando el primer reino de Strathclyde como un apanage. Los historiadores modernos, sin embargo, no dan crédito a esta idea.[2
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan I av Skottland (gælisk Donnchad mac Crínáin) 15. august 1001 – 15. august 1040, var skottenes konge, sønn av Crínán av Dunkeld, lekmannsabbed av klosteret i Dukeld og prinsesse Bethoc av Skottland. Han ble konge i Skottland
2 CONC  ved å etterfølge sin farfar Malcolm II av Skottland i 1034 etter tidligere å ha styrt som rex Cumbrorum (småkonge) over kongedømmet Strathclyde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans tronebesittelse sies å ha vært det første eksempel i Skottland hvor tronen ble arvet i direkte linje, i motsetning til det gamle gæliske system, tanisteri, hvor kongen valgte sine etterfølger uten hensyn til familiebånd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan var også kjent som Duncan den nådige, men det er tittel som ikke var dekkende i alle sammenhenger. Hans følelseskalde holdning for politiske spørsmål gjorde ham upopulær blant folk og hos adelen. Duncan var ingen sterkt her
2 CONC sker, og han er hovedsakelig kjent i dag via sin forbindelse med kong Macbeth som ble gjort udødelig av William Shakespeare, skjønt for de gale årsaker. Striden mellom disse angikk nok hvem som skulle kunne kreve den skotske trone
2 CONC n. Detaljene er derimot svært uklare, og det eneste fakta som kan bli slått fast med sikkerhet er at
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan ble drept i et slag av Macbeth. Det skjedde i nærheten av Elgin, Moray den 15. august 1040.
2 CONT
2 CONT Året før, i 1039, marsjerte Duncan sørover med hæren for beleire Durham i Northumbria, men ble beseiret med store tap. Han forsøkte også ta kontroll over Moray, men ble beseiret to ganger av Torfinn den mektige, sønn av jarlen av 
2 CONC Orknøyene, før han ble drept i et slag mot Macbeth og senere begravet på Iona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Detaljer om hans øvrige liv blir stadig diskutert blant historikerne. Den skotske kongeliste, Scottish Regnal List I, kaller hans kone for Suthen, mens John av Fordun nevner henne som en slektning av den danske «Sigurd Bjørnsson» 
2 CONC eller «Sigurd Danske», forvansket til Siward av Northumbria, jarl av Northumbria, og i Storbritannias offisielle kongeliste slår fast at hun var Siwards kusine.
2 CONT
2 CONT To av Ducans sønner, Malcolm III Canmore og Donald Bane ble begge konge av Skottland. En annen sønn, Máel Muire, ble far til Matad, mormaer av Atholl, og dennes sønn, Harald Maddadsson, halvt norsk på morsiden, styrte senere Orknø
2 CONC yene som norrøn jarl.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan I 'the Gracious', King of Scotland was born circa 1001.2 He was the son of Crinan, Mormaer of Atholl and Bethoc of Scotland. He married Sybilla (?), daughter of Siward Digera, Earl of Northumberland and Elfleda (?), circa 1
2 CONC 030. He died on 14 August 1040 at Pitgaveny, Elgin, Morayshire, Scotland, killed by a blow from Macbeth.4 He was buried at Isle of Iona, Argyllshire, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duncan I 'the Gracious', King of Scotland gained the title of King Duncan of Strathclyde in 1018.3,5 He succeeded to the title of King Duncan I of Scotland on 25 November 1034. Children of Duncan I 'the Gracious', King of Scotland
2 CONC  and Sybilla (?)
