1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT = Hakon "The Imperious" Palson, Jarl of Orkney=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Orkneyöarna ligger ca 30 km norr om skotska fastlandet och är brittisk ögrupp och skotskt förvaltningsdistrikt. Av ett 70-tal öar är ca 20 bebodda. Ögruppen har 19500 invånare och huvudort är Kirkwall. Öarna har varit bebodda se
2 CONC dan forntiden och erövrades på 800-talet av norska vikingar, som där upprättade ett välde vilket med vissa avbrott varade till 1472, då öarna tillföll Skottland. Befolkningen talade norska till på 1600-talet. (Bra Böcker)
2 CONT
2 CONT Paulsson, Hakon, Earl of Orkney. Acceded: 1105. Died: 1126.
2 CONT * His father was Thorfinnson, Paul I of Orkney, Earl of Orkney.
2 CONT  *Mother: Hakondottir, Daughter.
2 CONT *Child 1: Hakonsson, Paul the silent, Earl of Orkney. Associated with Helga.
2 CONT *Child 2: Hakonsson, Harald Smooth-spoken, Earl of Orkney
2 CONT *Child 3: Hakonsdottir, Ingibiorg.
2 CONT *Child 4: Hakonsdottir, Margaret, Countess of Orkney.
2 CONT
2 CONT  (Directory of Royal Genealogical Data, Hull, England)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hakon murdered his cousin Magnus (the Saint and Earl); repented; went on pilgrimage. After his return honoured the Saint, about 1110-1120. (Frances Coakley)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.espell.se/saga/p0cc05950.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Haakon Paulsson was a Norwegian Jarl (1105–1123) and jointly ruled the Earldom of Orkney together with his cousin Magnus Erlendsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father was Paul Thorfinnsson, the son of Thorfinn Sigurdsson and Ingibiorg Finnsdottir. His father and his uncle, Erlend Thorfinnsson, had ruled together as Earls of Orkney. King Magnus III of Norway took possession of the Ork
2 CONC ney islands in 1098, deposing both Erlend and Paul. Haakon Paulsson was chosen to become regent on behalf of the Norwegian prince, the future King Sigurd I of Norway, who made Haakon an earl in 1105.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Orkneyinga Saga, his cousin Magnus Erlendsson was initially rejected by the Norwegians rulers because of his religious convictions. Magnus was obliged to take refuge in Scotland, but returned to Orkney in 1105 and
2 CONC  disputed the succession of Haakon. Having failed to reach an agreement, Magnus sought help from King Eystein II of Norway who granted him the joint earldom of Orkney.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus and Haakon ruled jointly from 1105 until 1114. Their followers then had fallen out and the two sides met at the Thing assembly on the Orkney mainland, ready to do battle. Peace was negotiated and the Earls arranged to meet 
2 CONC each other on the island of Egilsay, each bringing only two ships. Magnus arrived with his two ships, but Haakon turned up with eight ships. Magnus took refuge in the island's church overnight, but the following day he was capture
2 CONC d and offered to go into exile or prison. An assembly of chieftains insisted that one earl must die. Haakon killed Magnus on the island of Egilsay in April 1116. This led to the "martyrdom" of Magnus and the construction of St Mag
2 CONC nus Cathedral in Kirkwall
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         HAKON Paulsson (before [1070]-Orkney [1126]).  Orkneyinga Saga names “Hakon” as son of “Earl Paul Thorfinnson” and his wife[966].  It is extremely unlikely that Hakon was born before [1070], given the estimated birth da
2 CONC te of his granddaughter Margaret (see below).  Snorre names "Hakon, a son of Earl Paul" when recording that "a year or two after King Magnus's fall…[he] came from Orkney" and was given the earldom of Orkney by Kings Eystein, Sigur
2 CONC d and Olav, before sailing back to Orkney[967].  Orkneyinga Saga records that Hakon left Orkney for Norway where he visited “King Olaf the Peaceful…towards the end of Olaf’s reign” (King Olaf’s death is dated to 1093) before saili
2 CONC ng east to Sweden and “King Ingi Steinkelsson”[968].  He was appointed Joint Lieutenant for Sigurd Magnusson King of Orkney and the Isles, when the latter returned to Norway in 1103 on his accession as king.  He was installed in [
2 CONC Spring 1105] as Jarl of Orkney by King Sigurd.  He ruled as Joint Jarl from Spring 1108, when his cousin Magnus Erlendsson was installed as Jarl of ½ Orkney.  He later quarrelled with his cousin, took him prisoner on Egilsay and h
2 CONC ad him killed.  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earl Hakon Paulsson died in his bed in Orkney”[969].  m ---.  The name of Hakon’s wife is not known.  Mistress (1): HELGA, daughter of MODDAN of Dalr in Caithness & his wife ---.  Orkn
2 CONC eyinga Saga names “Helga” daughter of “a farmer…Moddan…living in the Dales of Caithness” as “Earl Hakon’s mistress”[970].  Her stepson banished her and her sister Frakok to the latter's estates in Helmsdale, Sutherland after the d
2 CONC eath of her son [1131].   Hakon & his wife had one child: a)         PAUL [II] Hakonsson "the Silent" (-after 1137).  . Hakon had three illegitimate children by Mistress (1): b)         HARALD [I] "Slettmali/Smooth-spoken" (before
2 CONC  [1090]-[1131]).   c)          INGIBJÖRG .  Orkneyinga Saga names “Harald…Smooth-Tongue and two daughters…Ingibjorg…Margaret” as the children of Earl Hakon and his mistress Helga, adding that Ingibjörg married “Olaf Tit-Bit King o
2 CONC f the Hebrides”[975].  m [as his second wife], OLAV Bitling King of  d)         MARGARET (before [1115]-).  
2 CONT
