1 NOTE {geni:about_me} GENI NOTE:
2 CONT
2 CONT Very easily confused with Gudrød "Veidekonge"/"Hunterking", son of Halvdan Eysteinsson "The meek"/"Milde", who was the grandson of this guy's brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gudrød Halvdansson
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=gb&emne=asatru&person=Gudr%F8d%20Halvdansson&list=&vis=
2 CONT
2 CONT FATHER:Halvdan Kvitbein (Olavsson)
2 CONT
2 CONT MOTHER: Åsa Øysteinsdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT BROTHER: Øystein Halvdansson (Eysteinn Hálfdansson)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Snorre's "Ynglingesoga", paragraph 44:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Halvdan Kvitbein var en mektig konge; han var gift med Åsa, datter til opplandskongen Øystein Hardråde, som rådde på Hedmark. Halvdan og Åsa hadde to sønner, Øystein og Gudrød."
2 CONT
2 CONT The easily confused Gudrød, son of Halfdan son of Øystein, is in paragraph 48.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources that confuse the two ==
2 CONT A classical example of confusing the two Gudrøds:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ancestral Roots..." (Baltimore, 1992) 243a-15: "Gudrod, 'the Magnificent,' also called 'the Hunting-King', s. Halfdan 'White-Leg' and Asa ..., King of Vermaland, Vestfold, and Vingulmark, murdered 810-827 at instigation of (2) wi
2 CONC fe Asa in revenge for forcibly abducting her and killing ca. 800 her father and brother; m. (1) Alfhilde, dau. Afrim, ruler of Vingulmark; m. (2) Asa, dau. Harald, 'Red-Beard', King of Agdir. Asa believed bur. in Oseberg ship, ric
2 CONC hest Scandinavian archeological find." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruled in Norway and Denmark. Probably "Godfrey the Proud" of the Franks who opposed the Emperor Charlemagne.
