0 @N6000000002188056166@ NOTE Findláech of Moray
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Findláech of Moray, or Findláech mac Ruaidrí, was the King or Mormaer of Moray, ruling from some point before 1014 until his death in 1020.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Annals of Ulster and in the Book of Leinster, Findláech is called rí Alban, which meant "King of Scotland" in the Gaelic language. As far as we know from other sources, the only rí Alban of the time was Máel Coluim mac Ciná
1 CONC eda, i.e. Máel Coluim II, so this title can only mean that Findláech, as ruler of Moray, was understood by many to have been the High-King of all northern Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Findláech's main claim to fame these days is as the father of Mac Bethad, made famous by William Shakespeare's play Macbeth. Indeed, the Irish historian known in Latin as Marianus Scotus calls Macbethad simply MacFindlaeg
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Historians are fairly certain that Findláech was ruling before 1014 because the Orkneyinga Saga records a that before the Battle of Clontarf, Jarl Siguðr of Orkney fought a battle with the Scots, who were led by a Jarl Finnlekr (i
1 CONC .e. Findláech the Mormaer). An Irish princess called Eithne made a banner for Siguðr, which had on it a raven. The saga records that Siguðr later brought the banner to Clontarf, where he was killed. If we believe this, then Findlá
1 CONC ech would be ruler quite a bit before 1014.
1 CONT
1 CONT His death date, as mentioned above, derives from the Annals of Ulster, which notes s.a. 1020 Finnloech m. Ruaidhri, ri Alban, a suis occisus est, that is, that Findláech was killed by his own people. No reason for this is given, b
1 CONC ut the logical thing is to conclude that his successor, his nephew Máel Coluim mac Máil Brigti, had something to do with it. Indeed, the Annals of Tigernach tell us that the sons of Máel Brigte were responsible; the only sons we k
1 CONC now of are Máel Coluim and Gille Coemgáin, both of whom evidently benefited from the killing, as both succeeded to the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History: AD 500-1286, 2 Vols, (Edinburgh, 1922) 
1 CONT Hudson, Benjamin T., Kings of Celtic Scotland, (Westport, 1994) 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT ? Mormaer of Moray
1 CONT before 1014-1020 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Máel Coluim mac Máil Brigti 
0 @N6000000003615291776@ NOTE Brderbund WFT Vol. 1, Ed. 1, Tree #4408, Date of Import: 2 Sep 1998 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Vol. 1 FINLEY FINDINGS
1 CONT
1 CONT Introduction
1 CONT
1 CONT This issue is a compilation of the data contained in Volume 1 of FINLEY FINDINGS INTERNATIONAL, from July 1987 to May 1988. While it omits the membership lists found in the individual issues, the historical information remains int
1 CONC act, with some additional information included which was not available at the time the individual issues were written. A complete bibliography is included at the end of this complete edition.
1 CONT
1 CONT  The main goal of this organization continues to be the updating of genealogies done several years ago, in order to keep our family history current. In doing research, we will fill in the gaps which may have existed for years -- o
1 CONC nly because we did not know someone else had the information we needed. Important sources of information will be presented, as well as where these sources may be found. An important step will be to attempt to authenticate all sour
1 CONC ces, as some errors have been found in past genealogies.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Queries are accepted from both subscribers and non-subscribers at no charge. All possible efforts will be made to answer such queries, but it must be recognized that all answers are based on past data and may be subject to variou
1 CONC s interpretations. In order for our organization to be totally successful, every subscriber is strongly encouraged to submit family group sheets and any information which they have available, with the sources given for each.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Five main genealogies should be credited for the enormous information which they contain. Although each has been found to contain certain errors, they provide a good starting place for the FINLEY researcher. In fact, each one has
1 CONC  been built off the work of the others, diverging into separate lines. It is the divergence of these lines that form the incredible wealth of world history that should make each one of us proud to have had FINLEY forefathers.
1 CONT
1 CONT  The genealogies to which I refer are: The Clan FINLEY, by Maj. Albert Finley FRANCE (unpublished, but written 1941-42, with additions by Lillian Hicks FRANDSEN, February 1962); The Clan FINLEY, by Rear Adm. Herald Franklin STOUT 
1 CONC (Dover, OH: The Eagle Press, First Edition, Limited, 1940; Vol. 1, Second Edition, 1957; Vol. 2, Second Edition, 1961); TORRENCE and Allied Families, by Robert McIlvaine TORRENCE; and unpublished genealogies by the Carrie Alexande
1 CONC r WOOD, formerly of Dallas Co, TX (written 1938-41), and Rex Bird FINLEY, currently of Wichita, KS (written in 1963).
1 CONT
1 CONT  All of these genealogies have been inputted into my computer (minus historical notes), with corrections as submitted by subscribers. This information will be made available to FINLEY FINDINGS INTERNATIONAL subscribers only, with 
1 CONC a complete set of floppy disks available for purchase for the price of $100. There are over 36,000 names and almost 14,000 marriages recorded, which are directly linked. The disks are written to complement the Personal Ancestral F
1 CONC ile program, which must first be purchased from the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints, as the disks are written in the GEDCOM format.
1 CONT
1 CONT The enormous contributions of material made by subscribers are greatly appreciated. This is your organization, which I believe to be the only currently active organization in the world totally dedicated to researching the FINLEY s
1 CONC urname and all of its derivatives, from the beginning of time to the present.
1 CONT
1 CONT  One of our subscribers pointed out that in the work of genealogy, we are actually writing history. As such, the information contained in FINLEY FINDINGS INTERNATIONAL is only as good as its sources. Where conflicts are found, I w
1 CONC ill attempt to point them out. It must be admitted that not all information will be accurate, but the best attempt to prove the facts will be made.
1 CONT
1 CONT  All suggestions for improvement of this product will be gladly accepted. If you enjoy this newsletter, please feel free to share it with relatives and friends. I only ask that each subscriber encourage others to join our organiza
1 CONC tion by paying $25 dues in the U.S., $35 U.S. funds in all other countries. I feel I have made membership well worth this small price.
1 CONT
1 CONT  As a special bonus, I am now accepting lifetime memberships for $250.
1 CONT Lifetime members will never have to pay dues again and will receive every issue of FINLEY FINDINGS INTERNATIONAL. In addition, lifetime members will receive a complete set of the computer disks mentioned above.
1 CONT
1 CONT Foreword From The Clan Finley By Maj. Albert Finley France, April 1942
1 CONT
1 CONT In my self-appointed research, I have found the work of a genealogist to be a complicated and perplexing one. Pursuing as he must among the musty records of the past, he finds his efforts hedged about with difficulties from the st
1 CONC art and it is only by the most persevering efforts that anything like accuracy can be obtained.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After charting the immediate members of my family, there developed an interest in the earlier and still earlier generations. Inquiries were made; letters written; records searched; new facts found; different names added, each of 
1 CONC which connected itself with others and each marriage opened new vistas of opportunity for research and so the story grew.
1 CONT
1 CONT No effort has been spared to insure accuracy in this outline of facts. Where errors are found, the compiler begs indulgence and bespeaks correction thereof.
1 CONT
1 CONT  It cannot be denied that to our forefathers we owe much of our prosperity and happiness we now enjoy and every descendant must feel a strong desire to become intimately acquainted with those gallant adventurous spirits: OUR ANCES
1 CONC TORS.
1 CONT
1 CONT  There remains now the pleasant duty of thanking those who have aided in this work. Without cooperation and help, it could not have been completed. My obligations are due to Mr. Edward C. FINLEY of Los Angeles, California and Prof
1 CONC essor John FINLEY of the University of Glasgow, Scotland and the Scottish Record Society of Edinburgh.
1 CONT
1 CONT  I must express my gratitude for their interest, perseverence, care and the most satisfactory results of their examination of the archives and manuscripts of Scotland, England and Ireland. Behind each one of us is an influence str
1 CONC onger than Patriotism, stronger than duty. It is the stuff of which Patriotism and Honor and Duty are made. It is the influence of our Mothers -- these pages are set apart to honor them.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finley Coat of Arms
1 CONT
1 CONT  FRANCE describes the FINLEY coat of arms as: "Argent on a chevron, azure, between three roses gules, two swords point downward conjoined at the pumil," with the crest: "A dexter hand holding a sword," and the motto: "Fortis in Ar
1 CONC duis -- Brave in difficulties." STOUT adds, "The ancient crest was a boar passant and motto: 'Cor Nobyle Cor Immobyle: A noble heart is an immovable heart.'
1 CONT
1 CONT  In the letterhead above, the crest with the FINDLAY spelling was adapted from one found in "Genealogies of Pennsylvania Families," from The Pennsylvania Genealogical Magazine, Vol. I (Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co. Inc., 
1 CONC 1982). It is described as, "Armorial Bearings displayed by Descendants of Samuel FINDLAY of Mercersburg, PA. Blazon: Shield -- Argent, on a chevron azure between two roses in chief and one in base all gules, two swords pointed dow
1 CONC nwards and conjoined at the pommels of the field, hilted and pommelled or. Crest -- A boar passant proper. Motto -- Fortis in arduis."
1 CONT
1 CONT  The crest with the FINLAY spelling was taken from Armorial Families, Vol. I, by Arthur Charles FOX-DAVIES. It is described as follows, using the alternative spelling of FINLAY: "FINLAY (L.O., 1000). Argent, on a chevron between t
1 CONC wo roses in chief gules, and in base a salmon on its back proper, with a signet ring in its mouth or, two swords chevronwise points downward of the first, hilted and pommelled gold, all within a bordure of the second. Mantling gul
1 CONC es and argent. Crest -- On a wreath of the colours, a dexter hand couped at the wrist, holding a sword in bend all proper. Motto -- 'I'll be wary.' Livery -- Blue, red facings, silver buttons. Eld. son of Ian Archibald FINLAY of C
1 CONC astle Toward, Major N. Irish Horse, formerly Lieut. R. Scots Greys, served S. Africa 1899-1901, and Great War 1914-19, b. 1878; d. 1925; m. 1904, Elizabeth MURIEL, only d. of 1st Baron Glenarthur: -- Alastair John Kirkman FINLAY, 
1 CONC Gentleman, Royal Scots Greys (Supp. Res.), b. 1908. Seat -- Temple Hall, Coldingham, Berkwickshire."
1 CONT
1 CONT  The design with the FINLEY spelling was sent to me by a subscriber, Mary HEPLE of Sacramento, CA.
1 CONT
1 CONT A Finley By Any Other Name
1 CONT
1 CONT  There are several ways a person can change his or her name, whether through marriage, court procedures, illiteracy or by recording his or her name incorrectly in official records made by court clerks. For example, my grandmother 
1 CONC was born as Elizabeth Esther FASSETT. When her original birth certificate was destroyed, a duplicate one was issued, but her name was recorded as Esther Elizabeth FASSETT. To this date, she is known as Esther and not Elizabeth.
1 CONT
1 CONT  The late Dr. James Wallace FINDLEY told how his version of our surname began to be spelled with a "D." He said that several years ago, a Scottish resident noticed his name and told him that the "D" signified on which side of the 
1 CONC River Dee in Scotland that particular branch of the family lived.
1 CONT
1 CONT  WOOD states the following, "The change of the surname FINLAY to FINLEY is found in various documents relating to this family under date of 1461 to 1501; appears under the form of FYNLAY, FENLAY, and under date of 1506, John FINLE
1 CONC Y, partitioner of Cupar Angus, son of John FYNLAY, is mentioned as partitioner of County Lanark." (WOOD uses the spelling of FINLEY from that point on. STOUT uses various spellings for the same names that WOOD lists as FINLEY. Sin
1 CONC ce there are many who retain some of the older spellings, in future documents to be presented, I will use STOUT's spelling from the 1500s to the present unless noted otherwise.)
1 CONT
1 CONT  In The Surnames of Scotland, by George F. BLACK (The New York Public Library, 1974), the following appears: "FINLAY, FINLEY, FINDLAY, Gaelic FIONNLAGH, less correctly FIONNLADH and FIONNLA. In the Gaelic genealogical manuscript o
1 CONC f 1467, the name occurs in the genitive as FINLAEIC, and in the Duan Albanach, an old poetical chronicle of the kings of Dalriada (c. 1070, though in text of later date) as FIONNLAOICH.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "In the Book of Leinster, the name of MACBETH's father is spelled FINDLAECH (1070), and in Marianus Scotus (c. 1080, but text of later date), FINNLOECH.
1 CONT In the Norse chronicle known as the Flateyjarbok, his name is spelled FINNLEIKR, the name being understood by the Norsemen as meaning in their language 
1 CONT FINN's game or sport.' The name has been also explained as 
1 CONT fair hero,' from FIONN, 
1 CONT white,' or 
1 CONT fair,' and LAOCH, 
1 CONT hero,' which comes from the Old Irish LAECH (which is borrowed from Latin, LAICUS). "The name has also been explained as a substitution for, or popular rendering of, FIONNLUGH, earlier FIND-LUG, meaning 
1 CONT Fair one of (the god) LUG.'
1 CONT
1 CONT  "FINLAY is a not uncommon name in early Scots records. FYNLAYUS, clericus, witnessed a charter by Alexander filius senescallus, 1246 (RMP, p. 89).
1 CONT FYNLAI, 
1 CONT sutor,' was prepositus (provost) of Stirling, 1327 (ER, I, p. 67). FYNDLAW, Thane of Glentilt, leased the lands of Enirvak in 1480 (Cupar-Angus, I, p. 231). Andrew FYNDELAI, chaplain of Brechin in 1526, appears again in 1537 as An
1 CONC drew FINLAIUS, presbyter of St. Andrew's diocese (REB, II, p. 180, 192).
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Robert FINLAW in Leith accepts the king's coronation, 1567 (RPC, I, p. 563). John PHINLAW was retoured heir of James PHINLAW, portioner of Balcristie, his brother-german, in 1629, and Arthur FINLAU was retoured heir of Andrew FY
1 CONC NLAW, portioner of Balcrystie in same year (Retours, Fife, p. 419, 424).
1 CONT
1 CONT "John FINDLO, burgess of Montrose, d 1639 (Jervise, II, p. 223). Andrew FINLAW is located in Langton, 1734 (Kirkcudbright). In the Lewis, the fairies are, for some unknown reason, called in Gaelic, MUINNTIR FHIONLAIDH, 
1 CONT FINLAY's people' (Rev. Malcolm MACPHAIL, Folklore, XI, p. 442). FINDLAW, 1599; FINDLOW, 1702; FYNDELAY, 1526; FYNDLAE, 1594; FYNDLLA, 1597."
1 CONT
1 CONT  Also in BLACK are these derivatives of FINLEY: "FINLAYSON, FINLAISON. English forms of Gaelic MAC FHIONNLAIGH, 
1 CONT son of FINLAY,' q.v. Brice FYNLAWESONE of Netbolge, Stirlingshire, rendered homage in 1296 (BAIN, II, p. 205). Duncanus filius FINDLAI (the Latin form of the name) was one of an inquest held at Banff in 1342 (REA, I, p. 73), and K
1 CONC essog FINDLAUSON performed like duty at Dunipace in 1426 (CANBUS, p. 87). Andrew FYNDLAI (again, Latin form) was chaplain in Brechin, 1450 (REB, I, p. 138). John FINLAWSONE witnessed an Atholl charter in 1455 (Athole, p. 708); Mic
1 CONC hal FYNLOSON was tenant of half of Cragneuydy in 1478 (Cupar-Angus, I, p. 227); Ade FINDELASONE, tenant of the Bishop of Aberdeen in Petglass, 1511; Alexander FINDELASONE in Inuerquhat in the same year; and John FINLASON in Balfad
1 CONC dy (REA, I, p. 369, 374).
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Thom FINDLAISONE was presbyter in Dunkeld, 1528 (REB, II, p. 182), and Robert FYNDLASOUN leased the Cruikit aiker called Sanct-augus-tines (St. Augustine's) land in 1585 (SCON, p. 229). FINDLEYSONE, 1647; FINLASONE, 1561; FINLAS
1 CONC OUN, 1620; FINLAWSOUNE, 1598; FINLISON, 1678; FONLOSOUN, 1585; FINLOSOUNE, 1502; FINLSOUN, 1512; FYNDELOSOUN, 1529; FYNDLOSONE, 1506."
1 CONT
1 CONT History of The Clan Finley
1 CONT Part 1: In The Beginning
1 CONT
1 CONT In her unpublished genealogy, written between 1938 and 1941, the late Carrie Alexander WOOD gives quite a detailed sketch of the earliest FINLEY family history. Dating from Adam and Eve, she lists among her sources The Chart of De
1 CONC scent of House of FINLEY, from Manuscript Pedigrees at the Society of Genealogists, London, derived from Annals of Four Masters; CRONNELLY's Irish Families; and KEATING's History of Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Somewhat less detailed, but which would tend to confirm most of WOOD's lineage chart, is a chart entitled, House of FINLEY Traced Back To ADAM and EVE, submitted by Ruth MOORE, with information taken from the Holy Bible and the P
1 CONC edigree Chart of Queen ELIZABETH and the Royal Family of Great Britain.
1 CONT
1 CONT  This chart can be found on a microfilm entitled, FINLEY Family, which may be obtained from the LDS Church Library in Salt Lake City. The microfilm also contains the entire text of Albert Finley FRANCE's The Clan FINLEY, as compil
1 CONC ed by Lillian Hicks FRANDSEN, with additional information added by FRANDSEN. The microfilm may also be leased through Mormon stake libraries by requesting MISC Film Area, #0560192, Item 12 (which means you will have to search thro
1 CONC ugh unrelated items on the microfilm until you come to the 12th item, which is the FINLEY section).
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Bible, ADAM (meaning "red earth") was the first man and was created on the sixth day. He died when he was 930 years old. EVE was the first "woman," so called because she was taken out of "man," created from ADAM's
1 CONC  rib.
1 CONT  Tempted by the serpent to eat the forbidden fruit, she and ADAM were cast out of the Garden of Eden.
1 CONT
1 CONT  CAIN was the first child born and later he became a farmer. When God rejected CAIN's first offering in favor of one presented by his brother, ABEL, who was a shepherd, CAIN committed the first murder in history and slew his broth
1 CONC er, later moving to the Land of Nod, east of Eden. The Bible identifies one more son of ADAM and EVE by name, SETH, from whom our second generation begins.
1 CONT SETH was born when his father was 130 years old and SETH died at age 912.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Generations 3-9 are as follows: ENOS, b when father was 105, d at 905; CAINAN, b when father was 90, d at 910; MAHALALEEL, b when father was 70, d at 895; JARED, b when father was 65, d at 962; ENOCH, b when father was 162, said 
1 CONC to have walked with God for 300 years and when he was 365, disappeared, for God took him; METHUSELAH, b when his father was 65, d at 969, said to have been the oldest person who has ever lived; LAMECH, b when his father was 187, d
1 CONC  at 777.
1 CONT
1 CONT  NOAH was born when his father, LAMECH, was 182. He built the Ark and was 600 when it was loaded. When the 40 days and 40 nights of rain began, he was 600 years, 2 months and 17 days old. After the flood, he became a farmer and di
1 CONC ed at 950. His children identified in the Bible were SHEM, HAM and JAPHETH. It is at this point that WOOD and MOORE's genealogies differ.
1 CONT
1 CONT WOOD's lineage chart comes through JAPHETH, whose children were GOMER, MAGOG, MADAI, JAVAN, TUBAL, MESHECH and TIRAS. The Bible lists GOMER's children as ASHKENAZ, RIPHATH and TOGARMAH, but WOOD adds BAOTH. She then gives the foll
1 CONC owing outline of the Milesians:
1 CONT
1 CONT  "After the flood, the population of the world assembled to raise the Tower of Babel in 2247 B.C. Of these, the Scythians descended from GOMER settled in the north of Egypt. GOMER's grandson, FENIUS, or PHOENIUSA, son of BAOTH, wa
1 CONC s King of the Scythians and was a prince who applied himself to the study of letters. He left his kingdom, having placed his son, NIUL, on the throne as Regent.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "During his absence, he proceeded with 72 learned men to the plains of Shenaar and founded a school of languages. While FENIUS presided over his school, his second son, NEULL, was born and on the death of FENIUS, the elder son, N
1 CONC IUL, succeeded to the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "NEULL was left with no riches other than his learning. Therefore, the King of Egypt induced him to go thither to instruct the Egyptians, and was so well satisfied with him, that he gave his daughter, SCOTA, as NEULL's wife. The 
1 CONC Godelians, as the people were now called (from GODELAS, son of NEULL), proceeded to a country which the Irish annals named "Gothland," or the country of the Goths, where they remained 150 years before proceeding to Spain."
1 CONT
1 CONT  From NEULL, WOOD's lineage gives generations 16-33 as follows: GAODHAL, a quo the Clann, na GAODHAIL or the Gaels; ASRUTH; SRUTH; HEBER SCUTT (SCOTT); BEOUMAN; OGHAMAN; TAIT; AGNAN; LAMHFIONN; HEBER GLUNFIONN; AGNAN FIONN; FEBRIC
1 CONC  GLAS; NENUALL; NUADHAD; ALLADH; ARCADH; DEAGH; and BRATH.
1 CONT
1 CONT  MOORE, as quoted by FRANDSEN, includes the following statement at the end of her chart:
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Queen VICTORIA, by whose command the original compilation of the British Royal Family's Genealogical Chart was made, once expressed the hope that she would be the monarch who would hand over the sceptre when the King of Kings re
1 CONC turns. The original chart is in the Library of Windsor Castle. The original copy is in the British Museum."
1 CONT
1 CONT  The chart lists the following generations: ADAM and EVE; SETH; ENOS; CAINAN; MAHALEEL; JARED; ENOCH; METHUSELAH; LAMECH; NOAH; SHEM (note the deviation from WOOD's chart); ARPHAXAD; SALAH; EBER; PELEG; REU; SERUG; NAHOR; TERAH; A
1 CONC BRAM (ABRAHAM); ISSAC; JACOB; JUDAH.
1 CONT
1 CONT  At this point, branches are given from twin sons of JUDAH, ZARAH and PHAREZ. ZARAH's line includes: ETHAN; MAHOL; CALCOL; GADHOL (GAODHAL); EASRU (ASRUTH); SCRU (SRUTH); HEBER SCOT; ROAMHAIN (BEOUMAN); AYBAIMHAIN (OGHAMAN); TAIT;
1 CONC  AGHENOIN (AGNAN); LAMH FIONN; HEBER (HEBER GLUNFIONN); AAHNOIN (AGNAN FIONN); EOABHLA GLAS (FEBRIC GLAS); NEIN NUAIL (NENUALL); ALLOID (ALLADH); EARCHADA (ARCADH); DEAGFATHA (DEAGH); BRATHA (BRATH); BREOGAN (BREOGHAN); BILLE (BIL
1 CONC E); GALLAM; and EOCHAIDH.
1 CONT
1 CONT  PHAREZ's line is as follows: EZROM; ARAM; AMINADAB; NASHON; SALMON; BOAZ (m RUTH); OBED; JESSE; DAVID; SOLOMON; REHOBOAM; ABIJAM; ASA; JEHOSOPHAT; JEHORAM; AHAZIAH; JOASH; AMAZIAH; UZZIAH (or AZARIAH); JOTHAM; AHAZ; HEZEKIAH; MAN
1 CONC ASSEH; AMON; JOSIAH; ZEDEKIAH (name changed from MATTANIAH); and TAMAR (TEA TEPHI), who MOORE says m EOCHAIDH (EOCHAIDH BUADHACH; generation 58 on WOOD).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT This is where the lines of ZARAH and PHAREZ are said to join together again as EOCHAIDH is said to be ZARAH's descendant and TAMAR is PHAREZ's descendant. Both WOOD and MOORE list UGAINE MOR as the son of EOCHAIDH.
1 CONT
1 CONT  While there seems to be some agreement between the two charts as to generations 1-10 and 16-35, there are major discrepancies in the rest of the lineage. MOORE's lineage chart basically follows the lineage shown in the Bible, as 
1 CONC far as PHAREZ's branch is concerned. In fact, in FRANDSEN's narrative, she says the British Royal Family points to their descent through this line as proof of their claim to the throne, through the Davidic Covenant, which states t
1 CONC hat David's descendants shall forever possess the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT WOOD's commentary, on the other hand, continues the lineage through the Milesians, as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT "One of the most distinguished princes in the direct line from NEULL was the grandson of DEAGH, BREOGHAN, who defeated the Spaniards in many battles and built a city named Bregantea (afterwards known as Brogansa).
1 CONT
1 CONT "His grandson, MILESIUS, son of BILE, collected his kinsmen and returned to Scythea and became Prime Minister. His popularity in the sequel excited the king's jealousy and he only escaped death by invading the palace with his fait
1 CONC hful Godelians and slaughtering the king.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Disgusted with the ingratitude of the Scythians, they returned to their fleet and proceeded to the shores of Egypt. Here, MILESIUS soon gained the affection of PHARAOH, who gave MILESIUS his daughter in marriage, who, like the w
1 CONC ife of NEULL, bore the name of SCOTA (It is this SCOTA for whom Scotland is named).
1 CONT
1 CONT  "MILESIUS, after remaining seven years in Egypt, again put to sea and landed at length in northern Spain on the coast of the Bay of Biscay. When the age of the world was 3501 B.C., MILESIUS, a mighty warrior of renown, saw a gree
1 CONC n island from the top of a tall tower in Spain and chose the most stalwart from among his splendid band of 32 sons to conquer the distant land. They made the dangerous journey in 30 ships, vanquished the race then in possession an
1 CONC d seized upon the country which was divided between HEBER and HEREMON, sons of MILESIUS.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Irish genealogy begins with this legendary settlement and the four great race stems, to which the leading families of Ireland converge, are HEBER, ITH, IR and HEREMON. It is from HEREMON that the FINLEYs claim ancestry, but it i
1 CONC s not until the reign of FEREDACH the Just, 102nd Ard-Rich, or High Monarch of Ireland, that authentic history begins.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "The old and historic name is pure Milesian in origin through the House of HEREMON. Its foundation is co-equal with that of the HyNialls Sept of Ulster, the chief families of which for nearly 600 years ruled as Ard-Rich of Erin (
1 CONC Ireland).
1 CONT
1 CONT  "From HEREMON were descended the kings, nobility and gentry of the kingdoms of Connaught, Dalriada, Idenster, Meath, Orgiall and Ossary; of Scotland since the fifth century; of Ulster since the fourth century; and of England from
1 CONC  the reign of King HENRY II, down to the present time.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "He and his brother, HEBER, began to reign in 1699 B.C. and were, jointly, the first monarchs of Ireland. HEBER was slain in 1698 B.C. HEREMON reigned 14 years and died in 1683 B.C."
1 CONT
1 CONT  I would like to add one final note on the line of HEREMON. The time span between MILESIUS' vision and the beginning of the reign of HEREMON and HEBER should be questioned. FRANCE, in his The Clan FINLEY, states that MILESIUS' vis
1 CONC ion came in 2934 B.C., not in 3501 B.C. as WOOD and STOUT have claimed. In either case, there is a gap of over 1,000 years between his vision and the time that HEREMON and HEBER began their reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Also, consider this: MOORE's chart shows the possibility of a gap between BILLE (BILE on WOOD's chart) and GALLAM (who is not shown on WOOD's chart). GALLAM is said by MOORE to be the son of BILLE, with EOCHAIDH shown as the next
1 CONC  generation after GALLAM, but with an arrow bringing EOCHAIDH down to the generation level of TAMAR.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Also confusing is that TAMAR is shown as the daughter of ZEDEKIAH, who, according to a biblical chart that I have, reigned from 598 to 587 B.C. Both WOOD and MOORE show UGAINE MOR (or HUGONY) the Great as the son of EOCHAIDH, but
1 CONC  WOOD states UGAINE was slain in 593 B.C., which would put it during the reign of his grandfather.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another discrepancy is found in the fact that MOORE lists ANGUS the Prolific as son of UGAINE the Great. As seen earlier, WOOD shows UGAINE MOR as generation 59, while AONGUS TUIRMEACH-TEAMRACH, the Prolific, is in generation 66.
1 CONT
1 CONT  So, it can be seen that both charts have some historical corroboration. It is entirely possible that each chart is accurate, with certain exceptions, and chose to go down separate branches, converging at some points and going on 
1 CONC separate, but parallel, tracks at other junctions. If any of our members are going to the British Isles, perhaps clues may be found in the original documents to determine the authenticated line. As for now, I will accept WOOD's li
1 CONC neage chart as the proven line until other evidence surfaces.
1 CONT Part 2: Our Royal Ancestry
1 CONT Of Ireland and Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT This section begins with generation 79, as shown in the chart in the membership directory, with the following commentary from WOOD:
1 CONT
1 CONT  In the years A.D. 14 to 36, Feredach FION-FEACHTNACH, the True, Just and Sincere, ruled as king, and on his death, was succeeded by his son, Fiacha FIONN OLA (Fiacha of the White Oxen), who had married EITHNE, the daughter of IMG
1 CONC HEAL, King of the Picts. Fiacha lost his throne to ELIM, King of Ulster, who had wrestled the Crown of Ireland from its rightful owners. Young TUATHAL, son of Fiacha, who was looked upon as the next heir, was carried back to Albai
1 CONC n (Scotland), where he received the protection of the King of the Picts, his maternal grandfather.
1 CONT
1 CONT Encouraged by the representation of the Milesians, who had become weary of the reign of ELIM, in A.D. 76, TUATHAL returned to Ireland with a small army raised in Scotland and landed at Jorrus Domhrionn, where he joined his Irish a
1 CONC dherents, who had already risen in arms and were plundering the possessions of their enemies.
1 CONT
1 CONT  The young prince lost no time in marching to Tara, where he found the principal men of the Milesian race assembled to welcome him and he was proclaimed King, under the title of Tuathal TEACHTMAR, the Acceptable, or Legitimate. He
1 CONC  died A.D. 106 and had married BAIN, daughter of Sgaile BALBH, King of Finland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Three kings now reigned and slew each other in succession and then came Fedhlimidh (Felim) RACHTMAR, the Legislator, son of Tuathal, and under whom the laws of Ireland were again revised and reformed. He was one of the few Irish k
1 CONC ings who was permitted to die in peace, as he died of thirst. He ruled from A.D. 110 to 119 and had married UGHNA, daughter of the King of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Then came CONN of the Hundred Battles, son of FEDHLIMIDH, a prince whose long reign was devoted, as his distinctive title imparts, to a series of conflicts. He had married LANDABAND, daughter of CRIMTHAN, King of Leister, Ireland
1 CONC . In A.
1 CONT  D. 173, he fell by the hands of assassins. However, from the family of this hero descended the race of chieftains, who, under the title of Dalriadic Kings, supplied Albain, the modern Scotland, with the first Scottish rulers.
1 CONT
1 CONT  CONN was succeeded on the throne by his son-in-law, CONARY II, a descendant of Cairbre FIONN, or CONARY the Great, King of Munster, also of the House of HEREMON, and therefore, of the Milesian race. Cairbre RIADA, son of CONARY I
1 CONC I by SARAD (SOADIA), daughter of CONN of the Hundred Battles, in the third century had established the first Irish settlement at Argyleshire, Scotland.
1 CONT He became so important as to found a dynasty. The people governed by his descendants were called Dalriadians and their territory formed the northern part of the present County Antrim, Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT  The event of high political importance took place in the fifth century and this was the establishment under the sons of ERC of the Scotch-Irish monarchy in Albain. The colony planted there by Cairbre RIADA, though constantly fed 
1 CONC with supplies from the parent stock, the Dalriadians of Antrim, had run frequent risks of expiration from the superior power of their neighbors, the Picts.
1 CONT
1 CONT  When, therefore, a certain youth called LORNE, noble and with unbounded power, begotten of a race of kings, heard this, that a nameless tribe of his own country was wandering through the vast solitude of Albain, living in misery 
1 CONC among the Picts without a ruler, he was stimulated by these exhortations and by ambition of reigning, and aided by the all-powerful influence of the Hy-Niall family, he proceeded to Albain and there constituted himself the first k
1 CONC ing.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Three brothers who were paramount chiefs in the territory known as the Kingdom of Dalriada, Ireland, LORNE, ANGUS and FERGUS, with a strong army of followers, crossed into Scotland. They were sons of ERC, who was the son of EOCHA
1 CONC IDH the Valiant, who in turn was the son of Colla UAIS, King of Tara, who was the son of Cairbre RIADA.
1 CONT
1 CONT  On leaving Ireland, LORNE, the eldest brother, occupied the territory in western Scotland which still bears his name. He may be counted as the first king, as his reign began in A.D. 470, though his brother, ANGUS, possessed the i
1 CONC slands of Islay, Jura and Iona, and other relatives of LORNE were more or less endured.
1 CONT
1 CONT  It was the youngest son, Fergus MC ERC, who succeeded LORNE as king. Though, as we have seen, the eldest brother, LORNE, ruled before him, Fergus holds a more conspicuous position as father of the dynasty, since it was his descen
1 CONC dants and not those of LORNE who afterwards ruled in Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In A.D. 501, Fergus was crowned king and he consolidated the three territories into one kingdom which he named "Dalriada," after his native territory in Ireland. The Lia Fail, or "Stone of Destiny," used in the election of Irish 
1 CONC monarchs, was brought from Tara in order that Fergus might be crowned king upon it, and was never taken back to Ireland. Eventually, it was brought to England by EDWARD I in 1300 and deposited in Westminster Abbey. In A.D. 506, Fe
1 CONC rgus, at a late period of his life, decided to revisit his native country. On the way over, his ship was wrecked and he drowned. His body drifted ashore on the strand by the rock on which the Castle of Carrickfergus (Rock of Fergu
1 CONC s) now stands.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Fergus was succeeded by his son, DOMANGART, who died A.D. 508, and who was succeeded, in turn, by his son, CONGALLUS I (COMGALL), who reigned from A.D. 508 to 537. CONGALLUS was succeeded by his brother, GABHRAN, who reigned A.D.
1 CONC  537 to 560, and who had married LLEIAN, daughter of Brychan BRECHENA, King of Brecknoch, South Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After the Dalriadic Scots had firmly settled in Scotland, their possessions seem to have been divided among four tribes. These were the Cinal Lorne, descended from LORNE; Cinal Gabhran and Cinal Comgall, descended respectively fr
1 CONC om the two sons of DOMANGART; and the Cinal Angus, descended from ANGUS. Cinal Lorne occupied that part of Argyleshire now known as Lorne; Cinal Gabhran had the district of Argyll proper and Kintyre; Cinal Comgall had the territor
1 CONC y of Comgall, now known as Cowal; and Cinal Angus had for its share the islands of Islay, Jura and Iona.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In accordance with the Laws of Tanistry, GABHRAN was succeeded by his nephew, CONALL, son of CONGALLUS I, and who, as King of Dalriada, ruled over the united land of Gabhran and Comgall. In A.D. 574, CONALL died (STOUT names him 
1 CONC as CONGALLUS II and lists reign of A.D. 560 to 570) and in the same year, his hosts, led by DUNCAN, his son, were destroyed at the Battle of Delgin against the Picts in Kintyre. It is said the king perished there as well.
1 CONT
1 CONT  This opened the throne to Aedhan MC GABHRAN, son of GABHRAN. St. COLUMBA ordained Aedhan as king at Iona, which was the first consecration of a Christian king in Scotland. This evolution of political events in Britain, Albain and
1 CONC  Erin (Ireland) had opened up a brilliant future for the proper man, Aedhan, as the man of destiny.
1 CONT
1 CONT  His lineage was right royal, as on his father's side he had the blood of the Nealls in his veins, and on his mother's side that of King COYL, through LLEIAN, daughter of King BRYCHAN, who gave his name to Brecknoch, South Wales. 
1 CONC Aedhan was connected with one of the three holy families of Britain and also with many powerful reigning families among the Cymry and Gall, who had married the men of the north, Goglia, as the Cymreck bards styled the chieftains w
1 CONC ho afterwards became the allies of Aedhan when he combined the Dalriadians and Brychans against the Picts of the north.
1 CONT
1 CONT  (Charles HANNA, in The Scotch-Irish Families of America, Vol. I, p. 203, states the following: "Aedan ascended the throne of Dalriada in 574, or perhaps it would be more correct to say he became chief of the Dalriad tribe. In 603
1 CONC , he led a numerous force -- recruited largely from the Britons of Strathclyde -- against AETHELFRID, the Anglican King of Bernicia.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Meeting him in Liddesdale, near the frontier line of the kingdoms of Bernicia and Strathclyde [in the present Roxburghshire], a decisive battle was fought at Degsastan, which resulted in the utter defeat and rout of Aedan's army
1 CONC , and the extension of the western boundary of the Anglican kingdom to the River Esk. The annalist, TIGHERNAC, records Aedan's death in 606, at the age of 74.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "He was succeeded by his son, Eocha BUIDHE, who resigned the throne to his son, Conadh CERR. In the year 629, the latter was slain in the Battle of Fedhaeoin, fought in Ireland between the Irish Dalriads and the Irish Picts, or C
1 CONC ruithne. Both parties to this contest received auxiliaries from Scotland. Eocha BUIDHE appears also in this battle, on the side of the Picts, and opposed to his son, CONADH, the leader of the Dalriad Scots. Mr. SKENE infers from t
1 CONC his, and other confirmatory circumstances, that Eocha, at this time having withdrawn from Dalriada, must have been ruler of the Galloway Picts. He died later in the same year.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "Domnall BRECC, or BREAC, brother to Conadh CERR, succeeded to the throne of Dalriada on the death of the latter. In 634, he fought the Northumbrians at Calathros (now Callender, in Stirlingshire), and was defeated. Three years l
1 CONC ater, he was again defeated with great loss in the Battle of Mag Rath in Ireland, whither he had gone as an ally of the Cruithne, or Irish Picts, in their contest with Domnall MAC AED, King of the Irish Dalriads.
1 CONT  "In 638, TIGHERNAC records another battle and defeat, being that of Glinnemairison, or Glenmureson, which name has been identified with that of the present Mureston Water, south of the River Almond, in the parishes of Mid and Wes
1 CONC t Calder (Edinburghshire). As the siege of Etin (Edinburgh) is mentioned in the same reference, and as this was the second defeat which the Dalriad king had suffered at the hands of the Angles within the space of four years in con
1 CONC tiguous territory, it is to be supposed that these battles may have resulted from the efforts of Domnall BRECC to dispossess the Angles of that portion of their dominions in or near which the battles were fought.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "The Battle of Degsastan, near the Esk, in 603, and these fights on both sides of the Avon in 634 and 638, would seem to fix these streams as at that time marking the extremities of the frontier line between Northumbria and Strat
1 CONC hclyde.
1 CONT
1 CONT  "While the Britons were naturally allied with the Scots in these wars against the common enemy of both, it appears that the circumstances of their union were not otherwise sufficiently favorable to insure more than the temporary 
1 CONC ascendancy of the Dalriad chief as their leader at this time. It is possible he may have taken the opportunity of his leadership as an occasion for seeking permanent rule. But if this were so, he could not have met with much encou
1 CONC ragement from the Britons, for in the year 642, TIGHERNAC tells us he was slain at Strathcawin (or Strath Carron) by OAN, King of the Britons.")
1 CONT  The race of MC GABHRANs experienced repeated reverses. With a feud between the two kindred races of LORNE and FERGUS, the Crown of Dalriada passed for a time to the Clan of LORNE, although it returned to the Clan of GABHRAN upon 
1 CONC the death in A.D. 676 of Mailduin, line of FERGUS.
1 CONT
1 CONT  By a revolution, the sceptre was transferred to Fearchar FADA the Long, the great-great grandson of LORNE, and who reigned from A.D. 677 to 697. From his line, some of the most powerful thanes and clans of Scotland trace their de
1 CONC scent, with the most famous being MACBETH, who, on his mother's side, is traced back to Aedhan MC GABHRAN.
1 CONT
1 CONT  During the whole of this state of affairs, in A.D. 736, a series of conflicts between the Scots and Picts commenced that ended after a long struggle with alternate success in placing a Scotch-Irish prince on the throne of the Pic
1 CONC tish kings. With the expectation of softening by a family alliance the mutual hostility of the two kingdoms, a marriage was contracted between EOCHAID, or AYCHA II, and a Pictish princess, URQUSIA, daughter of URQUIS, King of the 
1 CONC Picts. EOCHAID was the son of Aodh FIONN, who ruled from A.D. 748 to 778, and ETHELBAYA, daughter of CATHNEA, King of West Saxon.
1 CONT
1 CONT  However, this marriage led to disputes over succession of the Pictish Crown and in the struggle that followed, ALPIN, son of AYCHA II, who ruled over Scotland Picts from A.D. 833 to 836, was slain in a battle with the Picts on 20
1 CONC  July 836. The whole matter was finally decided by the victory of ALPIN's son, Kenneth MC ALPIN, who urged his rights of inheritance by defeating WRAD, the last of the Pictish monarchs, who died at Fortivet in A.D. 842, in defense
1 CONC  of his kingdom. Eight years later, Kenneth MC ALPIN succeeded to the throne, thus uniting Scotland into one kingdom. He had married CINAITH, daughter of ANICUM of the Isles. He was succeeded by his brother, Donald, who died in A.
1 CONC D. 863.
