0 @N6000000001579451877@ NOTE Macbeth was another grandson of Malcolm II and had as good a claim to the throne as Duncan. Shakespeare, using poetic license, has distorted the historical facts, which facts show that Macbeth ruled Scotland for 17 years quite suc
1 CONC cessfully. He was married to Kenneth III's granddaughter Gruoch, who had a son, Lulach, by a previous marriage, which strengthened his claim to the throne. In 1045 Macbeth defeated and killed Duncan's father Crinan at Dunkeld. Hhi
1 CONC s reign was peaceful for the most part and he was generous to the Church. He was defeated in 1054 by Malcolm Canmore at Scone and killed by Malcolm Canmore in 1057
1 CONT
1 CONT Lulach (the Fool) (1057-58)
1 CONT Lulach the Fool ruled next. Remember, he is Macbeth's step-son. (FYI, his father's name was Gillacomgan.) After a few months of rule he was killed by Malcolm Canmore, who also killed Macbeth (see above).
0 @N6000000001744969875@ NOTE NOTES: Reign: 1040-1057; Macbeth was slain by Malcolm III, Duncan I's, son. In Shakespeare's play, Macbeth was urged by Gruoch (his wife) to seize the Kingdom.
0 @N6000000003300459755@ NOTE MACBETH (r. 1040-57)
1 CONT Macbeth's father was Finlay, Mormaer of Moray, and his mother may have be en Donada, second daughter of Malcolm II. (A Mormaer was literally a Hig h Steward of one of the ancient Celtic provinces of Scotland, but in Lati n documen
1 CONC ts the word is usually translated as 'Comes', which means Earl. ) Shakespeare's famous tragedy Macbeth is based upon his life, but is no t historically accurate. In the play, Macbeth and his wife murder the age d King Duncan when 
1 CONC he comes to visit them in their castle. In reality, Ma cbeth killed Duncan, who was about 39, in battle, and made himself king i nstead. Macbeth's marriage to Kenneth III's granddaughter Gruoch strength ened his claim to the thron
1 CONC e. In 1045, Macbeth defeated and killed Dunca n I's father Crinan at Dunkeld.
1 CONT His reign was for the most part peaceful, and he was known for his genero sity to the Church. He made a pilgrimage to Rome in 1050, 'scattering mon ey like seed'. Seven years later, on 15 August 1057, he was killed at th e Battle 
1 CONC of Lumphanan in Aberdeenshire by Duncan's eldest son, Malcolm Ca nmore. Macbeth was the last Scottish king to be buried on Iona.
1 CONT Macbeth (1005- 1057)
1 CONT Most people are aware that the Macbeth of Shakespeare's play was very di fferent from the real man, although some of the events in the play were b ased on real life incidents.
1 CONT Since Scotland was just coming out of the "Dark Ages" and the region in w hich Macbeth lived was in the far north, in Morayshire, his origins are o bscure. His name "mac-Bethad" means "Son of Life" and his mother was Dona da, a da
1 CONC ughter of Kenneth II or III or possibly Malcolm II. His father wa s Finlay McRory, Mormaer (Lord) of Moray. Macbeth married, Gruoch, a gran d-daughter of Kenneth II, king of Alba. She already had a son, Lulach, b y a first marriag
1 CONC e (to another Mormaer of Moray).
1 CONT As in Shakespeare, Macbeth was the commander-in-chief of King Duncan's ar my and he did kill Duncan in 1040 and succeeded him to the throne. Duncan 's son, the future King Malcolm III (called "caen more" or Canmore - larg e head) 
1 CONC fled to England.
1 CONT Unlike in Shakespeare, however, Macbeth was a powerful and successful mon arch who ruled from 1040 to 1057. He was confident enough of his positio n to go on a pilgrimage to Rome in 1050 and is said to have been so wealt hy that h
1 CONC e "scattered alms like seed corn".
1 CONT Macbeth was defeated by Malcolm Canmore, helped by an English army, at Du nsinane in 1054. But Macbeth continued to rule for another three years . A second invasion in 1057 saw his defeat and death on August 15 at Lump hanan, near
1 CONC  Aberdeen. He was killed by Malcolm and his English allies le d by Earl Siward of Northumbria. Like many of his royal predecessors, Mac beth was buried on Iona.
1 CONT Lulach, stepson of Macbeth, nicknamed "The Fool", initially became king o n his stepfather's death. He was the first monarch known to have been cro wned at Scone but he was defeated and killed by Malcolm Canmore at Essi e in Strat
1 CONC hbogie less than a year later.
0 @N6000000002188056155@ NOTE Macbeth of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Mac Bethad mac Findláich, known in English as Macbeth c. 1005 – August 15, 1057 was King of Scots (or of Alba) from 1040 until his death. He is best known as the subject of William Shakespeare's tragedy Macbeth and the many works 
1 CONC it has inspired, although the play itself is of limited historical accuracy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Origins and family 
1 CONT 2 Mormaer and dux 
1 CONT 3 High-King of Alba 
1 CONT 4 Life to legend 
1 CONT 5 Notes 
1 CONT 6 References 
1 CONT 6.1 Primary sources 
1 CONT 6.2 Secondary sources 
1 CONT 6.3 Further reading 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Origins and family
1 CONT Main article: Mormaer of Moray
1 CONT Mac Bethad was the son of Findláech mac Ruaidrí, mormaer of Moray. His mother is sometimes supposed to have been a daughter of Máel Coluim mac Cináeda. This may be derived from Andrew of Wyntoun's Orygynale Cronykil of Scotland wh
1 CONC ich makes Mac Bethad's mother a grand-daughter, rather than a daughter, of Máel Coluim. [1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Mac Bethad's paternal ancestry can be traced in the Irish genealogies contained in the Rawlinson B.502 manuscript:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mac Bethad son of Findláech son of Ruadrí son of Domnall son of Morggán son of Cathamal son of Ruadrí son of Ailgelach son of Ferchar son of Fergus son of Nechtan son of Colmán son of Báetán son of Eochaid son of Muiredach son of 
1 CONC Loarn son of Ercc son of Eochaid Muinremuir.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT This should be compared with the ancestry claimed for Máel Coluim mac Cináeda which traces back to Loarn's brother Fergus Mór.[3] Several of Mac Bethad's ancestors can tentatively be identified: Ailgelach son of Ferchar as Ainbcel
1 CONC lach mac Ferchair and Ferchar son of Fergus (correctly, son of Feredach son of Fergus) as Ferchar Fota, while Muiredach son of Loarn mac Eirc, his son Eochaid and Eochaid's son Báetán are given in the Senchus fer n-Alban. So, whil
1 CONC e the descendants of Cináed mac Ailpín saw themselves as coming off the Cenél nGabráin of Dál Riata, the northern kings of Moray traced their origins back to the rival Cenél Loairn.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT Mac Bethad's father Findláech was killed c. 1020 - one obit calls him king of Alba - most probably by his successor, his brother Máel Brigte's son Máel Coluim.[5] Máel Coluim died in 1029, the circumstances are unknown, but violen
1 CONC ce is not suggested; he is called king of Alba by the Annals of Tigernach.[6] However, king of Alba is by no means the most impressive title used by the Irish annals. Many deaths reported in Irish annals in the 11th century are of
1 CONC  rulers called Ard Rí Alban - High-King of Scotland. It is not entirely certain whether Máel Coluim was followed by his brother Gille Coemgáin or by Macbeth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gille Coemgáin's death in 1032 was not reported by Tigernach, but the Annals of Ulster record:
1 CONT
1 CONT Gille Coemgáin son of Máel Brigte, mormaer of Moray, was burned together with fifty people.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT Some have supposed that Mac Bethad was the perpetrator.[8] Others have noted the lack of information in the Annals, and the subsequent killings at the behest of Máel Coluim mac Cináeda to suggest other answers.[9] Gille Coemgáin h
1 CONC ad been married to Gruoch, daughter of Boite mac Cináeda, with whom he had a son, the future king Lulach.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not clear whether Gruoch's father was a son of Cináed mac Duib (d. 1005) or of Cináed mac Maíl Coluim (d. 997), either is possible chronologically.[10] After Gille Coemgáin's death, Mac Bethad married his widow and took Lula
1 CONC ch as his step-son. Gruoch's brother, or nephew, his name is not recorded, was killed in 1033 by Máel Coluim mac Cináeda.[11]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Mormaer and dux
1 CONT When Canute the Great came north in 1031 to accept the submission of Máel Coluim mac Cináeda, Mac Bethad too submitted to him:
1 CONT
1 CONT ... Malcolm, king of the Scots, submitted to him, and became his man, with two other kings, Mac Bethad and Iehmarc ...[12]
1 CONT
1 CONT Some have seen this as a sign of Mac Bethad's power, others have seen his presence, together with Iehmarc, who may be Echmarcach mac Ragnaill, as proof that Máel Coluim mac Cináeda was overlord of Moray and of the Kingdom of the I
1 CONC sles.[13] Whatever the true state of affairs in the early 1030s, and it seems more probable that Mac Bethad was subject to the king of Alba, Máel Coluim died at Glamis, on 25 November 1034. The Prophecy of Berchan is apparently al
1 CONC one in near contemporary sources in reporting a violent death, calling it a kinslaying.[14] Tigernan's chronicle says only:
1 CONT
1 CONT Máel Coluim son of Cináed, king of Alba, the honour of western Europe, died.[15]
1 CONT
1 CONT Máel Coluim's grandson, Donnchad mac Crínáin, was acclaimed as king of Alba on 30 November 1034, apparently without opposition. Donnchad appears to have been tánaise ríg, the king in waiting, so that far from being an abandonment 
1 CONC of tanistry, his kingship was a vindication of the practice. Previous successions had involved strife between various rígdomna - men of royal blood.[16] Far from being the aged King Duncan of Shakespeare's play, the real Donnchad 
1 CONC was a young man in 1034, and even at his death in 1040 his youthfulness is remarked upon.[17]
1 CONT
1 CONT Perhaps due to his youth, Donnchad's early reign was unremarkable. His later reign, in line with his description as "the man of many sorrows" in the Prophecy of Berchán, was not successful. In 1039, Strathclyde was attacked by the
1 CONC  Northumbrians, and a retaliatory raid led by Donnchad against Durham in 1040 turned into a disaster. Later in 1040, Donnchad led an army into Moray, where he was killed by Mac Bethad on 15 August, at Pitgaveny near Elgin.[18]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT High-King of Alba
1 CONT On Donnchad's death, Mac Bethad became king. No resistance is known at this time, but it would be entirely normal if his reign were not universally accepted. In 1045, Donnchad's father Crínán was killed in a battle between two Sco
1 CONC ts armies.[19]
1 CONT
1 CONT John of Fordun wrote that Donnchad's wife fled Scotland, taking her children, including the future kings Máel Coluim III and Domnall III with her. Based on the author's beliefs as to whom Donnchad married, various places of exile,
1 CONC  Northumbria and Orkney among them, have been proposed. However, the simplest solution is that offered long ago by E. William Robertson: the safest place for Donnchad's widow and her children would be with her or Donnchad's kin an
1 CONC d supporters in Atholl.[20]
1 CONT
1 CONT After the defeat of Crínán, Mac Bethad was evidently unchallenged. Marianus Scotus tells how the king made a pilgrimage to Rome in 1050, where, Marianus says, he gave money to the poor as if it were seed.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1052, he found himself involved indirectly in the strife in the Kingdom of England between Godwin, Earl of Wessex and Edward the Confessor when he received a number of Norman exiles from England in his court, perhaps becoming t
1 CONC he first king of Scots to introduce feudalism to Scotland. In 1054, Edward's Earl of Northumbria, Siward, led a very large invasion of Scotland. The campaign led to a bloody battle in which the Annals of Ulster report 3000 Scots a
1 CONC nd 1500 English dead, which can be taken as meaning very many on both sides, and one of Siward's sons and a son-in-law were among the dead. The result of the invasion was that Máel Coluim - not Máel Coluim (III) mac Donnchada - "s
1 CONC on of the king of the Cumbrians" was restored to his throne, i.e. as ruler of kingdom of Strathclyde.[21] It may be that events of 1054 are responsible for the idea, which appears in Shakespeare's play, that Máel Coluim III was pu
1 CONC t in power by the English.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mac Bethad certainly survived the English invasion, for he was defeated and mortally wounded by Máel Coluim mac Donnchada in battle at Lumphanan, on the north side of the Mounth in 1057, dying at Scone, sixty miles to the south, s
1 CONC ome days later.[22] Mac Bethad's stepson Lulach mac Gille Coemgáin was installed as king soon after.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unlike later writers, no near contemporary source remarks on Mac Bethad as a tyrant. The Duan Albanach, which survives in a form dating to the reign of Máel Coluim (III) mac Donnchada calls him "Mac Bethad the renowned". The Proph
1 CONC ecy of Berchán, a verse history which purports to be a prophecy, describes him as "the generous king of Fortriu", and says:
1 CONT
1 CONT The red, tall, golden-haired one, he will be pleasant to me among them; Scotland will be brimful west and east during the reign of the furious red one.[23]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life to legend
1 CONT Main article: Macbeth
1 CONT Mac Bethad's life, like that of Donnchad, had progressed far towards legend by the end of the 14th century, when John of Fordun and Andrew of Wyntoun wrote their histories. Hector Boece, Walter Bower and George Buchanan all contri
1 CONC buted to the legend.
1 CONT
1 CONT The influence of William Shakespeare's Macbeth towers over mere histories, and has made the name of Macbeth infamous. Even his wife has gained some fame along the way, lending her Shakespeare-given title to a short story by Nikola
1 CONC i Leskov and the opera by Dmitri Shostakovich entitled Lady Macbeth of Mtsensk. The historical content of Shakespeare's play, unlikely to have greatly concerned him, is drawn from Holinshed's Chronicles of England, Scotland, and I
1 CONC reland, which draws on Hector Boece's 1527 Scotorum Historiae which flattered the antecedents of Boece's patron, king James V of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT In modern times, Dorothy Dunnett's novel King Hereafter aims to portray a historical Macbeth, but proposes that Mac Bethad and his rival and sometime ally Thorfinn of Orkney are one and the same (Thorfinn is his birth name and Mac
1 CONC beth is his baptismal name). John Cargill Thompson's play Macbeth Speaks 1997, a reworking of his earlier Macbeth Speaks, is a monologue delivered by the historical Macbeth, aware of what Shakespeare and posterity have done to him
1 CONC . Macbeth is also a recurring character in animated television series Gargoyles in which his reign is portrayed sympathetically, and his success is a result of an alliance with the titular creatures.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT ^ Hudson, Propehcy of Berchán, pp. 224–225 discusses the question and the reliability of Wyntoun's chronicle. 
1 CONT ^ Rawlinson B. 502 ¶1698 Genelach Ríg n-Alban. 
1 CONT ^ Rawlinson B. 502 ¶1696 Genelach Ríg n-Alban. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, p. 32; Sellar, "Moray". 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Tigernach 1020.8; Annals of Ulster 1020.6. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Tigernach 1029.5; Annals of Ulster 1029.7. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Ulster 1032.2. 
1 CONT ^ Sellar, "Moray". 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, p. 32. 
1 CONT ^ See Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, p. 345; Lynch, Oxford Companion, p. 680; Wolfe, "Macbeth". 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Ulster 1033.7. The victim is reported as M. m. Boite m. Cináedha, which is variously read as "the son of the son of Boite ..." or as "M. son of Boite ...". 
1 CONT ^ Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Ms. E, 1031. 
1 CONT ^ Compare Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, pp. 29–30 with Hudson, Prophecy of Berchán, pp. 222–223. 
1 CONT ^ Hudson, Prophecy of Berchán, p. 223; Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, p. 33. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Tigernach 1034.1 
1 CONT ^ Donnchad as tánaise ríg, the chosen heir, see Duncan, The Kingship of the Scots, pp. 33–34; Hudson, Prophecy of Berchán,pp. 223–224, where it is accepted that Donnchad was king of Strathclyde. For tanistry, etc., in Ireland, see
1 CONC  Ó Cróinín, Early Medieval Ireland, 63–71. Byrne, Irish Kings and High-Kings, pp. 35–39, offers a different perspective. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Tigernach 1040.1. 
1 CONT ^ Hudson, Prophecy of Berchán, p.223–224; Duncan, The Kingship of the Scots, pp.33–34. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Tigernach 1045.10; Annals of Ulster 1045.6. 
1 CONT ^ Robertson, Scotland under her Early Kings, p. 122. Hudson, Prophecy of Berchán, p. 224, refers to Earl Siward as Máel Coluim mac Donnchada's "patron"; Duncan, The Kingship of the Scots, pp. 40–42 favours Orkney; Woolf offers no 
1 CONC opinion. Northumbria is evidently a misapprehension, further than that cannot be said with certainty. 
1 CONT ^ Florence of Worcester, 1052; Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Ms. D, 1054; Annals of Ulster 1054.6; and discussed by Duncan, The Kingship of the Scots, pp. 38–41. 
1 CONT ^ The exact dates are uncertain, Woolf gives 15 August, Hudson 14 August and Duncan, following John of Fordun, gives 5 December; Annals of Tigernach 1058.5; Annals of Ulster 1058.6. 
1 CONT ^ Hudson, Prophecy of Berchán, p. 91, stanzas 193 and 194. 
