1 CONC had at least three wives, the mother of his son, Donnchad, being Gormflaith of Naas, dau. of Murchard, King of Leinster (d. 972).  Gormflaith was the wid. of Anlaf (Olaf), King of Dublin (d. 981), and had been the wife of Mael-Sec
1 CONC hnaill, King of Ireland.  She d. 1030.  (Brian's ancestry table depends solely on tradition and is not accepted as proven).
0 @N6000000003949839466@ NOTE The O'Briens, one of the few native Irish families with a peerage, descend in the male line from Brian Boroimhe, Prince of Thomond (North Munster or the north part of southwest Ireland, roughly commensurate with an area centered o
1 CONC n modern Co. Clare) and Chief of the Dalgais, who became High King of Ireland 1002 and was killed at the victory over the Danes of Clontarf 23 April 1014. [Burke's' Peerage, p. 1495The O'Briens, one of the few native Irish familie
1 CONC s with a peerage, descend in the male line from Brian Boroimhe, Prince of Thomond (North Munster or the north part of southwest Ireland, roughly commensurate with an area centered on modern Co. Clare) and Chief of the Dalgais, who
1 CONC  became High King of Ireland 1002 and was killed at the victory over the Danes of Clontarf 23 April 1014. [Burke's' Peerage, p. 1495]
1 CONT As many of you realize, the Battle of Clontarf was one of those great changing points in the history of the world. To recap, Brian Boru, Emperor of the Irish was defending the island against a rebellion that was supported by a Vik
1 CONC ing invasion. Mael Morda, King of Leinster, was rebelling against Brian's rule. He was supported by Sigtrygg
1 CONT Silkbeard, the Danish king of Dublin, who was married to Brian's daughter. Brian was married to Gormlaith, mother of Sigtrygg, sister to King Mael Morda of Leinster and former spouse of none other than Malachi! Malachi, of course,
1 CONC  is the infamous king before Brian, whose actions led to the battle's result. Various disaffected and rebellious Irish clans quickly threw in their lot with the Dublin Vikings. The rebellion was supported by the invading forces of
1 CONC  Sigurd, the Viking earl of the Orkney Isles (who was half-Irish), another renowned Viking warrior of the age - Brodir of the Isle of Man. Both men had been
1 CONT promised the hand of Gormlaith. Sigurd not only desired Gormlaith, but he also wanted to sit in Brian's seat as high king, like his countrymen Svein and Cnut had done in England. Brodir was promised the Irish throne as well as a b
1 CONC ride in return for military assistance.
1 CONT Their forces, over 2,000 strong came to support the invasion.
1 CONT Brian, informed of the mustering of Vikings and rebellious Irish clans in Dublin, called in his own allies - his old enemy Malachi and the clans of Meath, the clans of Connacht, Munster and his own Dal Caissans, as well as a thous
1 CONC and foreign (Viking) mercenaries. Perhaps because of old quarrels, the Irishmen of Meath, commanded by ex-high king Malachi, drew off from the rest of the army and refused to take
1 CONT part in the battle plans.
1 CONT The allied-Viking army formed up into five battle divisions on the field; a sixth remained in Dublin, where Sigtrygg also stayed with 1,000 of his Dublin Vikings manning the walls, effectively guaranteeing himself a ringside seat 
1 CONC without fear of personal harm.
1 CONT Sigtrygg's son commanded the extreme left division of the allied-Viking army as leader of all those Dublin warriors who desired to fight in the open that day, perhaps another 1,000 men.  Next in line stood a strong, 3,000-man forc
1 CONC e of Irish rebels  from Leinster in two divisions, commanded by Brian's rebellious brother-in-law, Mael Morda.  Armed and armoured the same as the Irish clans opposite them, these fighters from Leinster were the weakest link in th
1 CONC e allied-Viking line.  Sigurd's Orkney Vikings manned the center - 1,000
1 CONT well-armoured, axe-wielding veterans of many wars.  And finally, on the right were stationed Brodir's Vikings, 1,000 or more them, eager to come to grips with the supposedly inferior foe.  Brodir's men anchored their flank on the 
1 CONC banks of Dublin Bay, where the longboats lay beached
1 CONT Opposite the Viking allies, Brian's allied-Irish forces also formed into regional divisions.  On the Irish right, 1,000 foreign mercenaries and Manx Vikings assembled opposite the Dublin Vikings.  Next to them, 1,500 clansmen of C
1 CONC onnacht were gathered under their kings, while more than 2,000 Munster warriors under Brian's son Murchad continued the front, flanked by 1,400 Dal Caissans on the extreme left - they were led by Murchad's 15-year-old son,
1 CONT Tordhelbach, and Brian's brother, Cuduiligh.
1 CONT The battle raged and raged. The Irish forces managed to push the Vikings back and back, after a long deadly struggle. Many brave warriors died that day.    Somewhere in the battle, either the Norwegian Prince Anwud or Eric of Denm
1 CONC ark fought his way through to the exhausted Murchad, son of Brian Boru, who still managed to throw the attacking Viking to the ground and stab him before he, too, was killed. It was shortly after this, than the turning point of th
1 CONC e battle came.
1 CONT Many of the Vikings had been fought off, there was some slight retreats of them. The Irish were suffering as well.  The diffident Malachi had held his own Irishmen in place all day, watching their fellow countrymen bleed and die o
1 CONC n the fields before them.  But now
1 CONT that the Viking enemy appeared to be in retreat, he ordered his Meath warriors to join the battle. They turned upon Brian's army attacking from the flanks. The retreating Vikingfs saw this, and rallied.  Fighting back in a pincer 
1 CONC movement, they turned the tide of the
1 CONT battle. The Vikinbfs snached victory fom the jaws of defeat. The worst was yet to come.
1 CONT It seems Brodir, hiding in the woods, saw Brian just outside, lightly guarded while most of his men were off in furious pursuit of the fugitives.  The Vikings fell upon the few Irish retainers, decapitating Brian with one blow.  T
1 CONC hen they retreated, Brodir yelling, "Now let man tell man that Brodir felled Brian," and were quickly subdued and taken prisoner by the enraged Irish. He was ecxecuted shortly thereafter in a futile gesture by the Irish.
1 CONT Losses to both sides were incredibly high.  Out of a total of between 7,000 and 8,000 combatants, the estimated allied loss was more than 5,000, including almost all the leaders.  Irish losses were least 3,600, and perhaps as high
1 CONC  as 5,000, including their king, Brian Boru.  It became one of the bloodiest battles in Irish history.  The annals tell of blood dripping from trees in the forest where the most savage axe-fighting took place. The only leaders lef
1 CONC t were Sigurd,  Sigtrygg,who had stayed in Dublin, and Malachi.
