0 @N6000000002188035644@ NOTE Olaf Cuaran
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Olaf Cuaran (d. 981), was also known as Olaf Sihtricson. In Irish he is known as Amlaíb Cuaran. His nickname, cuaran, means "sandals".
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigtrygg Caech, Olaf's father, ruled Deira, a part of Northumbria, until his death in 927. Upon Sigtrygg's death, Athelstan annexed Deira and Olaf fled to Scotland. Until 937, he spent time in Scotland and Ireland and participated
1 CONC  in the Battle of Brunanburh as part of the defeated alliance.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among his wives was Gormflaith, daughter of Murchad mac Finn, King of Leinster, and future wife of Brian Boru. She was mother of his successor, Sigtrygg Silkbeard.
1 CONT
1 CONT Athelstan died in 939 and Olaf Guthfrithson, king of Dublin at the time, invaded Northumbria and compelled Edmund, Athelstan's successor, to surrender Deira. When Olaf Guthfrithson died in 942, Olaf Cuaran succeeded him, finally r
1 CONC ecovering the throne of his father in addition to the throne of Dublin. Two years later, however, Edmund expelled him from Northumbria and Olaf was left with only Dublin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Olaf managed to regain his lands in Northumbria in 949, only to be expelled again in 952, this time by Erik Bloodaxe.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 980, Olaf suffered a massive defeat at the Battle of Tara, which destroyed the power of the Danes in Ireland. He went to Iona and probably died there in 981. He was succeeded by his son Glun Iarainn ("Iron Knee"), son of his wi
1 CONC fe Donnflaith.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Olaf III Guthfrithson King of Dublin Succeeded by:
1 CONT Glun Iarainn 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Ketil Ken Nygaard's Genealogy, http://nygaard.howards.net/files/2507.htm
0 @N6000000003949663434@ NOTE King of the Danish kingdoms of Northumbria and of Dublin. He was the son of Sihtric, king of Deira, and was related to the English  king Aethelstan. When Sihtric died about 927 Aethelstan annexed Deira,  and Olaf took refuge in Sc
1 CONC otland and in Ireland until 937, when he was  one of the leaders of the formidable league of princes that was destroyed  by Aethelstan at the famous Battle of Brunanburh. Again he sought a home  among his kinsfolk in Ireland, but 
1 CONC just after Aethelstan's death in 940  he or Olaf Guthfrithson was recalled to England by the Northumbrians.  Both crossed over, and in 941 the new English king, Edmund, gave up Deira  to the former.
1 CONT The peace between the English and Danes did not, however, last long.  Wulfstan, archbishop of York, sided with Olaf; but in 944 Olaf was driven  from Northumbria by Edmund, and crossing to Ireland, Olaf ruled over the  Danish king
1 CONC dom of Dublin. From 949 to 952 he was again king of  Northumbria, until he was expelled once more (this time by Erik  Bloodaxe), and he passed the remainder of his active life in warfare in  Ireland. But in 980 his dominion was sh
1 CONC attered by the defeat of the Danes  at the Battle of Tara.  He went to Iona, where he died probably in 981, although one dubious account says he was in Dublin in 994.  Ruled York #NC13
