0 @N6000000001503816598@ NOTE Cináed mac Ailpín (after 800–13 February 858) (Anglicised Kenneth MacAlpin) was king of the Picts and, according to national myth, first king of Scots. Cináed's undisputed legacy was to produce a dynasty of rulers who claimed desc
1 CONC ent from him. If he cannot be regarded as the father of Scotland, he was the founder of the dynasty which ruled that country for much of the medieval period.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 King of Scots?
1 CONT     * 2 Background
1 CONT     * 3 Reign
1 CONT     * 4 Notes
1 CONT     * 5 References
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT     * 7 Further reading
1 CONT     * 8 See also
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] King of Scots?
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Origins of the Kingdom of Alba
1 CONT
1 CONT The Cináed of myth, conqueror of the Picts and founder of the kingdom of Alba, was born in the centuries after the real Cináed died. In the reign of Cináed mac Máil Coluim, when the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba was compiled, the
1 CONC  annalist wrote:
1 CONT
1 CONT     So Kinadius son of Alpinus, first of the Scots, ruled this Pictland prosperously for 16 years. Pictland was named after the Picts, whom, as we have said, Kinadius destroyed. ... Two years before he came to Pictland, he had rec
1 CONC eived the kingdom of Dál Riata.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 15th century Andrew of Wyntoun's Orygynale Cronykil of Scotland, a history in verse, added little to the account in the Chronicle:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Quhen Alpyne this kyng was dede, He left a sowne wes cal'd Kyned,
1 CONT     Dowchty man he wes and stout, All the Peychtis he put out.
1 CONT     Gret bataylis than dyd he, To pwt in freedom his cuntre!
1 CONT
1 CONT When humanist scholar George Buchanan wrote his history Rerum Scoticarum Historia in the 1570s, a great deal of lurid detail had been added to the story. Buchanan included an account of how Cináed's father had been murdered by the
1 CONC  Picts, and a detailed, and entirely unsupported, account of how Cináed avenged him and conquered the Picts. Buchanan was not as credulous as many, and he did not include the tale of MacAlpin's Treason, a story from Giraldus Cambr
1 CONC ensis, who reused a tale of Saxon treachery at a feast in Geoffrey of Monmouth's inventive Historia Regum Britanniae.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later 19th century historians such as William Forbes Skene brought new standards of accuracy to early Scottish history, while Celticists such as Whitley Stokes and Kuno Meyer cast a critical eye over Welsh and Irish sources. As a 
1 CONC result, much of the misleading and vivid detail was removed from the scholarly series of events, even if it remained in the popular accounts. Rather than a conquest of the Picts, instead the idea of Pictish matrilineal succession,
1 CONC  mentioned by Bede and apparently the only way to make sense of the list of Kings of the Picts found in the Pictish Chronicle, advanced the idea that Cináed was a Gael, and a king of Dál Riata, who had inherited the throne of Pict
1 CONC land through a Pictish mother. Other Gaels, such as Caustantín and Óengus, the sons of Fergus, were identified among the Pictish king lists, as were Angles such as Talorcen son of Eanfrith, and Britons such as Bridei son of Beli.[
1 CONC 1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Modern historians would reject parts of the Cináed produced by Skene and subsequent historians, while accepting others. Medievalist Alex Woolf, interviewed by The Scotsman in 2004, is quoted as saying:
1 CONT
1 CONT     The myth of Kenneth conquering the Picts - it’s about 1210, 1220 that that’s first talked about. There’s actually no hint at all that he was a Scot. ... If you look at contemporary sources there are four other Pictish kings af
1 CONC ter him. So he’s the fifth last of the Pictish kings rather than the first Scottish king."[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Many other historians could be quoted in terms similar to Woolf.[3]
1 CONT [edit] Background
1 CONT
1 CONT Cináed's origins are uncertain, as are his ties, if any, to previous kings of the Picts or Dál Riata. Among the genealogies contained in the Middle Irish Rawlinson B.502 manuscript, dating from around 1130, is the supposed descent
1 CONC  of Máel Coluim mac Cináeda. Medieval genealogies are unreliable sources, but some historians accept Cináed's descent from the Cenél nGabrain of Dál Riata. The manuscript provides the following ancestry for Cináed:
1 CONT
1 CONT     ... Cináed mac Ailpín son of Eochaid son of Áed Find son of Domangart son of Domnall Brecc son of Eochaid Buide son of Áedán son of Gabrán son of Domangart son of Fergus Mór ...[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT Leaving aside the shadowy kings before Áedán son of Gabrán, the genealogy is certainly flawed insofar as Áed Find, who died c. 778, could not reasonably be the son of Domangart, who was killed c. 673. The conventional account woul
1 CONC d insert two generations between Áed Find and Domangart: Eochaid mac Echdach, father of Áed Find, who died c. 733, and his father Eochaid.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although later traditions provided details of his reign and death, Cináed's father Alpín is not listed as among the kings in the Duan Albanach, which provides the following sequence of kings leading up to Cináed:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Naoi m-bliadhna Cusaintin chain,   The nine years of Causantín the fair;,  
1 CONT     a naoi Aongusa ar Albain,   The nine of Aongus over Alba;  
1 CONT     cethre bliadhna Aodha áin,   The four years of Aodh the nobl
1 CONT     is a tri déug Eoghanáin.   And the thirteen of Eoghanán.  
1 CONT     Tríocha bliadhain Cionaoith chruaidh,   The thirty years of Cionaoth the hardy,  
1 CONT
1 CONT It is supposed that these kings are the Caustantín son of Fergus and his brother Óengus, who have already been mentioned, Óengus's son Eóganán, as well as the obscure Áed mac Boanta, but this sequence is considered doubtful if the
1 CONC  list is intended to represent kings of Dál Riata, as it should if Cináed were king there.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT The idea that Cináed was a Gael is not entirely rejected, but modern historiography distinguishes between Cináed as a Gael by culture, and perhaps in ancestry, and Cináed as a king of Gaelic Dál Riata. Cináed could well have been 
1 CONC the first sort of Gael. Kings of the Picts before him, from Bridei son of Der-Ilei, his brother Nechtan as well as Óengus son of Fergus and his presumed descendants were all at least partly Gaelicised.[6] The idea that the Gaelic 
1 CONC names of Pictish kings in Irish annals represented translations of Pictish ones was challenged by the discovery of the inscription Custantin filius Fircus(sa), the latinised name of the Pictish king Caustantín son of Fergus, on th
1 CONC e Dupplin Cross.[7] Other evidence, such as that furnished by place-names, suggests the spread of Gaelic culture through Pictland in the centuries before Cináed. For example, Atholl, a name used in the Annals of Ulster for the yea
1 CONC r 739, has been thought to be "New Ireland".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Compared with the many questions on his origins, Cináed's ascent to power and subsequent reign can be dealt with simply. Cináed's rise can be placed in the context of the recent end of the previous dynasty, which had dominated For
1 CONC triu for two or four generations. This followed the death of king Eógan son of Óengus of Fortriu, his brother Bran, Áed mac Boanta "and others almost innumerable" in battle against the Vikings in 839. The resulting succession cris
1 CONC is seems, if the Pictish Chronicle king-lists have any validity, to have resulted in at least four would-be kings warring for supreme power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cináed's reign is dated from 843, it was probably not until 848 that he defeated the last of his rivals for power. The Pictish Chronicle claims that he was king in Dál Riata for two years before becoming Pictish king in 843, but t
1 CONC his is not generally accepted. In 849, Cináed had relics of Columba, which may have included the Monymusk Reliquary, transferred from Iona to Dunkeld. Other that these bare facts, the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba reports that he
1 CONC  invaded Saxonia six times, captured Melrose and burnt Dunbar, and also that Vikings laid waste to Pictland, reaching far into the interior.[8] The Annals of the Four Masters, not generally a good source on Scottish matters, do ma
1 CONC ke mention of Cináed, although what should be made of the report is unclear:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Gofraid mac Fergusa, chief of Airgíalla, went to Alba, to strengthen the Dal Riata, at the request of Cináed mac Ailpín.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT Cináed died from a tumour on 13 February, 858 at the palace of Cinnbelachoir, perhaps near Scone. The annals report the death as that of the "king of the Picts", not the "king of Alba". The title "king of Alba" is not used until t
1 CONC he time of Cináed's grandsons, Domnall and Causantín. The Fragmentary Annals of Ireland quote a verse lamenting Cináed's death:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Because Cináed with many troops lives no longer
1 CONT     there is weeping in every house;
1 CONT     there is no king of his worth under heaven
1 CONT     as far as the borders of Rome.[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT Cináed left at least two sons, Causantín and Áed, who were later kings, and and at least two daughters. One daughter married Run, king of Strathclyde, Eochaid being the result of this marriage. Cináed's daughter Máel Muire married
1 CONC  two important Irish kings of the Uí Néill. Her first husband was Áed Finnliath of the Cenél nEógain. Niall Glúndub, ancestor of the O'Neill, was the son of this marriage. Her second husband was Flann Sinna of Clann Cholmáin. As t
1 CONC he wife and mother of kings, when Máel Muire died in 913, her death was reported by the Annals of Ulster, an unusual thing for the misogynistic chronicles of the age.
0 @N6000000001579451852@ NOTE In the early middle ages, Scotland consisted of four separate kingdoms:
1 CONT Dalriada inhabitated by Scots,
1 CONT Strathclyde inhabited by Britons,
1 CONT The Kingdom of the Picts,
1 CONT Northumbria inhabited by Angles.
1 CONT Scottish and Pictish families began intermarrying in the 8th century, and their kingdoms were often ruled by the same king. The monarchy of Scotland evolved from this union, known as the Kingdom of Alba. By the late 9th century, t
1 CONC he Kingdom of Alba began absorbing the kingdoms of the Britons and Angles. Thus, through intermarriage and conquest, the Scottish Kings of Dalriada emerged as the overall Kings of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Scots of Dalriada claimed a legendary antiquity beginning with Gaythelos, son of a King of Greece who went to Egypt during the time of Moses where he married the eponymous Scoti, daughter of the Pharaoh. Gaythelos, Scoti, and 
1 CONC their family emigrated to Spain and eventually several groups of their descendants emigrated to Ireland; the final group under Simon Brek, whose grandson led a colony from Ireland to northern Britain and named it "Scotia". In the 
1 CONC year 330 BC, these Scots elected as their king Fergus, son of Ferehard; and they remained in Scotland until 360 AD when they were driven back to Ireland by the Picts and Britons. In the 5th century, they returned to Scotia under t
1 CONC he leadership of Fergus, son of Erc. Or so the story goes.
1 CONT History knows nothing of the Scots earlier than about 500 AD, but at this point, the name of Fergus MorMacErc (Fergus, son of Erc) emerges from the mists of legend as the King of Scots in Dalriada.
1 CONT
1 CONT It should be noted that in early mediæval Scotland, it was the eldest and/or ablest male of the royal house, and not the heir of line, that inherited the throne. This meant that any energetic male connected with the royal line cou
1 CONC ld assert a claim to the throne. Thus, Kenneth (I) MacAlpin (838 - 858) was followed as king by his brother Donald (I) (858 - 862). Kenneth's son Constantine (I) did not become king until 862.
0 @N6000000002319315489@ NOTE by the year 843, thanks to the   determined efforts of Kenneth
1 CONT MacAlpin, King of the Scots of Dalriada, who claimed the throne of the
1 CONT Picts after he had defeated them in battle. He  created his capital at
1 CONT Forteviot, in Pictish territory; moved his religious center  to
1 CONT Dunkeld, on the River Tay, in present-day Perthshire, where he
1 CONT transferred   the remains of St. Columba from Iona.
1 CONT
1 CONT    According to the Huntingdon Chronicle, MacAlpin "was the first of
1 CONT the Scots to
1 CONT   obtain the monarchy of the whole of Albania, which is now called
1 CONT Scotia."
1 CONT  From that time on, the Picts, the tattooed or painted people, have
1 CONT remained a
1 CONT  shadowy, poorly documented race. It is a pity that no Pictish
1 CONT literature has
1 CONT   survived. All we have are the sculptured stones with their remarkable
1 CONT designs
1 CONT  incised that show warriors, huntsmen and churchmen.
1 CONT
1 CONT  At roughly the same time that the people of Wales were separated from
1 CONT the            invading Saxons by the artificial boundary of Offa's
1 CONT Dyke, MacAlpin was
1 CONT  creating a kingdom of Scotland. His successes in part were due to the
1 CONT threat
1 CONT  coming from the raids of the Vikings, many of whom became settlers. The
1 CONT  seizure of control over all Norway in 872 by Harold Fairhair caused
1 CONT many of              previously independent Jarls to look for new lands
1 CONT to establish themselves.
1 CONT  One result of the coming of the Norsemen and Danes with their command
1 CONT of
1 CONT the sea was that Scotland became surrounded and isolated. The old link
1 CONT with
1 CONT  Ireland was broken; the country was now cut off from southern England
1 CONT and
1 CONT  the Continent. Thus, the kingdom of Alba established by MacAlpin was
1 CONT thrown
1 CONT   in upon itself and united against a common foe.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Acceded: 839
1 CONT Died: 859, Forteviot, Perthshire
1 CONT Interred: Isle of Iona, Scotland
1 CONT
1 CONT      Burke calls him Kenneth II. Kings of Picts & Alba. King of
1 CONT Galloway.
1 CONT      See EuropSisch Stammtafeln Bund II tafel 67.
