0 @N6000000000269768469@ NOTE Kenneth I, called MacAlpin (flourished 832-60), traditionally, the founder and first king of Scotland. About 834 he succeeded his father, Alpin (reigned about 832-34), as king of the Gaelic Scots in Galloway. In a series of battle
1 CONC s (841-46) he conquered the Pictish Kingdom and, uniting it with his own, called his expanded domains Scotland. The kingdom is sometimes called Scone, after Kenneth's capital. In later years, the king led six invasions of Lothian,
1 CONC  southern Scotland, then part of Saxon Northumbria.
0 @N6000000003232718098@ NOTE [Sargent.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine was a warring King. During his reign he spent most of his days fighting off the Vikings or trying to expand his kingdom into the south. In 872 his assassination of 'Run' King of Straclyde, and his brother-in-law, meant
1 CONC  that the southern regions of what is now Scotland, became apart of his own ALBA.  864 saw the rampage of the Norsemen led by 'Olaf the White' from Dublin. Swiftly defeated by Constantine the Norsemen relaxed their threats on him 
1 CONC until 'Þorsten the Red' led them, but he too was defeated successfully by King Constantine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although usually confident in battle, Constantine often resorted to tactics of bribes and payoffs to his rivals in order to keep the peace. This form of peace keeping was later employed by the English Royals, namely King Æþelræd t
1 CONC he Unready in the year 1000. In the end though, Constantine was finally revenged upon by the Norsemen, and a raiding party known as the 'Black Strangers' from Dublin made a base for themselves in Fife where they launched their att
1 CONC acks. It was during one of these attacks that Constantine met his match.
1 CONT
1 CONT King of Scots, 862, died 877, slain in battle by the Norse.
0 @N6000000001353843050@ NOTE Constantine I (862-877)
1 CONT Constantine succeeded the reign of his uncle Donald I, as ruler of the united Picts and Scots(862-877) His reign was occupied with many conflicts with the Norsemen. Olaf the White, the Danish king of Dublin, laid waste the country
1 CONC  of the Picts and Britons year after year. In the south the Danish leader Halfdan devastated Northumberland and Galloway. Constantine was slain at a battle at Inverdovat in Fife, at the hands of another band of northern marauders.
1 CONC  His heir was his brother Aed, who was killed by the Scots after a year and was succeeded by a nephew, Eochaid.
