0 @N6000000002187590801@ NOTE Constantine I of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine I
1 CONT (Causantín mac Cináeda) 
1 CONT King of the Picts 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign 862–877 
1 CONT Died 877 
1 CONT  Inverdovat? 
1 CONT Buried Iona 
1 CONT Predecessor Donald I (Domnall mac Ailpín) 
1 CONT Successor Áed (Áed mac Cináeda) 
1 CONT Issue Donald II (Domnall mac Causantín) 
1 CONT Royal House Alpin 
1 CONT Father Kenneth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín)
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine, son of Cináed (Mediaeval Gaelic: Causantín mac Cináeda; Modern Gaelic: Còiseam mac Choinnich), known in most modern regnal lists as Constantine I[1], nicknamed An Finn-Shoichleach, "The Wine-Bountiful"[2] (d.877) was 
1 CONC a son of Kennneth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín). Although tradition makes Constantine and his father King of Scots, it is clear from the entries in the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba and the Annals of Ulster, that he was, like his 
1 CONC father, king of the Picts. He became king in 862 on the death of his uncle Donald MacAlpin (Domnall mac Ailpín).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 866, the Chronicle states that Pictland — the Annals of Ulster say Fortriu — was ravaged by Vikings led by Amlaíb Conung (Olaf) and Auisle (Ásl or Auðgísl). The Chronicle claims that Amlaíb was killed by Constantine that year, 
1 CONC but this is either incorrectly dated, or a different Amlaíb is intended as the Irish annals make it clear that Amlaíb Conung was alive long after 866. A date of 874 has been proposed for this event.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 870, Amlaíb Conung and Ímar captured Alt Clut, chief place of the kingdom of Strathclyde. The king, Artgal, was among the many captives. The Annals of Ulster say that Artgal was killed "at the instigation of Causantín mac Cináe
1 CONC da" (Constantine son of Kenneth) in 872. Artgal's son Run was married to a sister of Constantine.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 875, the Chronicle and the Annals of Ulster again report a Viking army in Pictland. A battle, fought near Dollar, was a heavy defeat for the Picts; the Annals of Ulster say that "a great slaughter of the Picts resulted". Althou
1 CONC gh there is agreement that Constantine was killed fighting Vikings in 877, it is not clear where this happened. William Forbes Skene read the Chronicle as placing Constantine's death at Inverdovat (by Newport-on-Tay), which appear
1 CONC s to match the Prophecy of Berchán. The account in the Chronicle of Melrose names the place as the "Black Cave" and John of Fordun calls it the "Black Den". Constantine was buried on Iona.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constantine's son Donald II and his descendants represented the main line of the kings of Alba and later Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Notes 
1 CONT 2 References 
1 CONT 3 External links 
1 CONT 4 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Until the Victorian era, Caustantín of the Picts was listed as "Constantine I of Scotland", and this Constantine as "Constantine II". Since then, revised historical opinion has led to this Constantine being retitled as "Constant
1 CONC ine I". 
1 CONT ^ Skene, Chronicles, p. 85. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History A.D 500–1286, volume 1. Reprinted with corrections. Paul Watkins, Stamford, 1990. ISBN 1-871615-03-8 
1 CONT A.A.M. Duncan,The Kingship of the Scots 842–1292: Succession and Independence. Edinburgh: Edinburgh University Press, 2002. ISBN 0-7486-1626-8 
1 CONT Smyth, Alfred P., Warlords and Holy Men: Scotland AD 80–1000. Edinburgh UP, Edinburgh, 1984. ISBN 0-7486-0100-7 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba (CKA) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Kingdom of Alba 
1 CONT Origins of the Kingdom of Alba 
1 CONT Constantine I of Scotland
1 CONT House of Alpin
1 CONT Died: 877 
1 CONT Regnal titles 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Donald (Domnall) I King of the Picts
1 CONT (traditionally King of Scots)
1 CONT 862–877 Succeeded by
1 CONT Áed 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT King of Scots 863 - 877. Killed in battle against the forces of Halfdan, ruler of Danish York. Source: RoyaList King of the Picts & Scots. King of Alba. *Weir* says he was killed in a battle against the Danes at Inverdorat, the Bl
1 CONC ack Cove, Angus. Source: Brian Tompsett 
0 @N6000000003300525601@ NOTE Constantine I, son of Kenneth mac Alpin, succeeded Kenneth's brother Dona ld on the throne of Scotland. He reigned till ca 877. According to the an nals put together by Donald mac Firbis, a Viking attack on Fortriu in 84 6 or 866 
1 CONC in which new tactics were adopted, taking hostages as pledge fo r ransom. Constantine's kingdom was invaded three or four times, probabl y from the west and the "gentile" armies extracted tribute during protrac ted stays and event
1 CONC ually Constantine gave battle and was killed. Dumbarto n was besieged and taken in 870 and it seems likely that for a time thi s was the Vikings headquarters while they mulcted the king of Strathclyde , whom Constantine caused to 
1 CONC be killed in 872.
1 CONT Constantine I was succeeded by his brother Aed, son of Kenneth, who was k illed in Strathallan, after a year as king, by his cousin, Giric son of D onald who seems to have ruled concurrently with Eochaid, son of a daughte r of Ken
1 CONC neth mac Alpin, during the period of 878-889.12
1 CONT CONSTANTINE I (r. 862-77)
1 CONT Constantine was the son of Kenneth I. The Norse invaded his kingdom sever al times, and he was killed in battle against them at Forgan, Fife in 877.
