0 @N6000000002188056098@ NOTE Máel Snechtai of Moray
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Máel Snechtai of Moray, or Máel Snechtai mac Lulaich, was the ruler of Moray, and, as his name suggests, the son of Lulach, King of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is called on his death notice in the Annals of Ulster, "Máel Snechtai m. Lulaigh ri Muireb" (="Máel Snechtai, Lulach's son, King of Moray"), which is a significant terminological development, since previously the titles for the
1 CONC  ruler of Moray had either been "King of Scotland" or "Mormaer." The title is repeated for his successor, Óengus (if indeed the latter were his successor).
1 CONT
1 CONT Perhaps then the events of Máel Snechtai's caused some kind of identity disassociation between the Men of Moray and the Men of Scotland. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, s.a. 1078, reports that "In this year Máel Coluim seized the mothe
1 CONC r of Máel Snechtai ...and all his treasures, and his cattle; and he himself escaped [only] with difficulty." Sadly, the ... represents a lacuna in the text one line long; 1 but it is clear that Máel Snechtai was defeated by King M
1 CONC áel Coluim III in some kind of conflict. Grant's suggestion 2 that Máel Snechtai subsequently retired to a monastery is based only on textual innuendo, and is made unlikely by the fact that he was called "King of Moray" on his dea
1 CONC th. Máel Snechtai, as the son of King Lulach, undoubtedly perceived himself as the rightful king of Scotland, and the already noted conflict with Máel Coluim III strongly suggests that he pursued his claim.
1 CONT
1 CONT His death date is based on the year given by the Annals of Ulster, s.a. 1085.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT 1. A.O. Anderson, Scottish Annals, p. 100) 
1 CONT 2. "The Province of Ross," p. 103 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History: AD 500-1286, 2 Vols, (Edinburgh, 1922) 
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Scottish Annals from English Chroniclers: AD 500-1286, (London, 1908), republished, Marjorie Anderson (ed.) (Stamford, 1991) 
1 CONT Grant, Alexander, "The Province of Ross and the Kingdom of Alba" in E.J. Cowan and R.Andrew McDonald (eds.) Alba: Celtic Scotland in the Medieval Era, (Edinburgh, 2000) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Anglo-Saxon Chronicle 
1 CONT Annals of Ulster 
1 CONT Annals of Tigernach 
1 CONT Book of Deer 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Lulach mac Gillai Coemgáin Mormaer of Moray
1 CONT 1058-1085 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Óengus 
