0 @N6000000003949883529@ NOTE Since contemporary Irish annal entries clearly refer to Alpin's sons Kenneth and Donald as being sons of Alpin, his existence is quite secure.  While the scanty sources that survive represent him as having been killed while fighti
1 CONC ng in Galloway, it is certainly not true that he "is usually presumed to be a sub king of Galloway."  The available sources are far too slim to make such a rash statement, and many historians sensibly avoid jumping to conclusions 
1 CONC like that.  If you want information on the early Scottish kings, you should go to   Marjorie O. Anderson's "Kings & Queens of early Scotland" 
