0 @N6000000002187590857@ NOTE Donald I of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald I
1 CONT (Domnall mac Ailpín ) 
1 CONT King of the Picts 
1 CONT Reign 858–862 
1 CONT Died 13 April 862 
1 CONT  Cinnbelachoir?, Rathinveralmond? 
1 CONT Buried Iona 
1 CONT Predecessor Kenneth MacAlpin 
1 CONT Successor Constantine I (Causantín mac Cináeda) 
1 CONT Issue Giric? 
1 CONT Royal House House of Alpin 
1 CONT Father Alpín mac Echdach 
1 CONT
1 CONT Domnall mac Ailpín (Modern Gaelic: Dòmhnall mac Ailpein), [1] anglicised as Donald MacAlpin, and known in most modern regnal lists as Donald I (died 13 April 862), was king of the Picts from 858 to 862. He followed his brother Ken
1 CONC neth MacAlpin (Cináed mac Ailpín) to the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba says that Donald reigned for four years, matching the notices in the Annals of Ulster of his brother's death in February 858 and his own in April 862.[2] The Chronicle notes:
1 CONT
1 CONT “ In his time the Gaels with their king made the rights and laws of the kingdom, [that are called the laws] of Áed, Eochaid's son in Forteviot.[3] ” 
1 CONT
1 CONT The laws of Áed Find are entirely lost, but it has been assumed that, like the laws attributed to Giric and Constantine II (Causantín mac Áeda), these related to the church and in particular to granting the privileges and immuniti
1 CONC es common elsewhere.[4] The significance of Forteviot as the site of this law-making, along with Kenneth's death there and Constantine's later gathering at nearby Scone, may point to this as being the heartland of the sons of Alpí
1 CONC n's support.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Chronicle of Melrose says of Donald, "in war he was a vigorous soldier ... he is said to have been assassinated at Scone."[5] No other source reports Domnall's death by violence.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prophecy of Berchán may refer to Donald in stanzas 123–124:
1 CONT
1 CONT “ Evil will be Scotland's lot because of [the death of Kenneth MacAlpin]; long will it be until his like will come. Long until the king takes [sovereignty], the wanton son of the foreign wife. He will be three years in the kingdom
1 CONC  and three months (although thou countest them). His tombstone will be above Loch Awe. He dies of disease.[6] ” 
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Donald is generally been supposed to have been childless, it has been suggested that Giric was a son of Donald, reading his patronym as mac Domnaill rather than the commonly supposed mac Dúngail.[7] This, however, is not 
1 CONC widely accepted.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald died, either at the palace of Cinnbelachoir (location unknown), or at Rathinveralmond (also unknown, and may be the same place, presumed to be near the junction of the Almond and the Tay, near Scone).[9] He was buried on Io
1 CONC na.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Notes 
1 CONT 2 References 
1 CONT 3 External links 
1 CONT 4 See also 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Domnall mac Ailpín is the Mediaeval Gaelic form. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Ulster, s.a. 858 & 862. 
1 CONT ^ Anderson, ESSH, p. 291, citing Skene. 
1 CONT ^ Smyth, p. 188. 
1 CONT ^ Anderson, ESSH, p. 291. 
1 CONT ^ Anderson, ESSH, p. 292, citing Skene. 
1 CONT ^ Smyth, p. 187. 
1 CONT ^ Compare Duncan, p. 11ff. 
1 CONT ^ Anderson, ESSH, p. 291; Duncan, pp. 10–11. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History A.D 500–1286, volume 1. Reprinted with corrections. Paul Watkins, Stamford, 1990. ISBN 1-871615-03-8 
1 CONT A.A.M. Duncan,The Kingship of the Scots 842–1292: Succession and Independence. Edinburgh: Edinburgh University Press, 2002. ISBN 0-7486-1626-8 
1 CONT Smyth, Alfred P., Warlords and Holy Men: Scotland AD 80–1000. Edinburgh UP, Edinburgh, 1984. ISBN 0-7486-0100-7 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
