0 @N6000000002187590780@ NOTE Donald II of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Donald II
1 CONT (Domnall mac Causantín) 
1 CONT King of the Picts
1 CONT or King of Alba 
1 CONT
1 CONT 18th century drawing — There is absolutely no evidence that suggests he actually looked like this 
1 CONT Reign 889–900 
1 CONT Died 900 
1 CONT  Forres or Dunnottar 
1 CONT Buried Iona 
1 CONT Predecessor Giric (Giric mac Dúngail) 
1 CONT Successor Constantine II (Causantín mac Áeda) 
1 CONT Issue Malcolm I (Máel Coluim mac Domnall) 
1 CONT Royal House Alpin 
1 CONT Father Constantine I (Causantín mac Cináeda) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Domnall mac Causantín (Modern Gaelic: Dòmhnall mac Chòiseim), [1], anglicised as Donald II (d.900) was King of the Picts or King of Scotland (Alba) in the late 9th century. He was the son of Constantine I (Causantín mac Cináeda). 
1 CONC Donald is given the epithet Dásachtach, "the Madman", by the Prophecy of Berchán.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 See also 
1 CONT 3 Notes 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Donald became king on the death or deposition of Giric (Giric mac Dúngail), the date of which is not certainly known but usually placed in 889. The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba reports:
1 CONT
1 CONT “ Doniualdus son of Constantini held the kingdom for 11 years [889–900]. The Northmen wasted Pictland at this time. In his reign a battle occurred between Danes and Scots at Innisibsolian where the Scots had victory. He was killed
1 CONC  at Opidum Fother [modern Dunnottar] by the Gentiles.[3] ” 
1 CONT
1 CONT It has been suggested that the attack on Dunnottar, rather than being a small raid by a handful of pirates, may be associated with the ravaging of Scotland attributed to Harald Fairhair in the Heimskringla.[4] The Prophecy of Berc
1 CONC hán places Donald's death at Dunnottar, but appears to attribute it to Gaels rather than Norsemen; other sources report he died at Forres.[5] Donald's death is dated to 900 by the Annals of Ulster and the Chronicon Scotorum, where
1 CONC  he is called king of Alba, rather that king of the Picts. He was buried on Iona.
1 CONT
1 CONT The change from king of the Picts to king of Alba is seen as indicating a step towards the kingdom of the Scots, but historians, while divided as to when this change should be placed, do not generally attribute it to Donald in vie
1 CONC w of his epithet.[6] The consensus view is that the key changes occurred in the reign of Constantine II (Causantín mac Áeda),[7] but the reign of Giric has also been proposed.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba has Donald succeeded by his cousin Constantine II. Donald's son Malcolm (Máel Coluim mac Domnall) was later king as Malcolm I. The Prophecy of Berchán appears to suggest that another king reigned
1 CONC  for a short while between Donald II and Constantine II, saying "half a day will he take sovereignty". Possible confirmation of this exists in the Chronicon Scotorum, where the death of "Ead, king of the Picts" in battle against t
1 CONC he Uí Ímair is reported in 904. This, however, is thought to be an error, referring perhaps to Ædwulf , the ruler of Bernicia, whose death is reported in 913 by the other Irish annals.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Kingdom of Alba 
1 CONT Origins of the Kingdom of Alba 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Domnall mac Causantín is the Mediaeval Gaelic form. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, p. 358; Kelly, Early Irish Law, pp. 92–93 & 308: "The dásachtach is the person with manic symptoms who is liable to behave in a violent and destructive manner." The dásachtach is not responsible for his actions. The same w
1 CONC ord is used of enraged cattle. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, pp. 395–397. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, p 396, note 1 & p. 392, quoting St Olaf's Saga, c. 96. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, pp. 395–398. 
1 CONT ^ Smyth, pp. 217–218, disagrees. 
1 CONT ^ Thus Broun and Woolf, among others. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp.14–15. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, p. 304, note 8; however, the Annals of Ulster, s.a. 904, report the death of Ímar ua Ímair (Ivar grandson of Ivar) in Fortriu in 904, making it possible that Ead (Áed ?) was a king, if not the High King. 
1 CONT
