0 @N6000000002187764299@ NOTE Diarmait mac Mail na mBo
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign: 1042– 1072 
1 CONT Predecessor: Murchad mac Dunlaing 
1 CONT Successor: Dommall mac Murchad 
1 CONT Date of Birth: c. 1000 
1 CONT Place of Birth: Leinster,
1 CONT Ireland 
1 CONT Wives:   
1 CONT Buried: Glendalough? 
1 CONT Date of Death: 1072 
1 CONT Parents: Donnchad Mail na mBo mac Domnall Cloen 
1 CONT
1 CONT Diarmait mac Mail na mBo (died 1072) was king of Leinster and a contender for the title of High King of Ireland. He was one of the most important and significant Kings in Ireland in the pre-Norman era.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Leinster, the Norse and the Dal gCais 
1 CONT 2 Capture of Wexford 
1 CONT 3 Niall mac Eochada 
1 CONT 4 The Man Who Would Be High King 
1 CONT 5 The Sons of Harald II 
1 CONT 6 Death of Murchad and Diarmait 
1 CONT 7 Diarmait's Death in the Annals of the Four Masters 
1 CONT 8 Source 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Leinster, the Norse and the Dal gCais
1 CONT His dynasty, the Ui Cheinnselaigh, were based around Ferns in the south-east of Wicklow but had sunk into obscurity for several centuries.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Capture of Wexford
1 CONT It was their control of the Norse town of Wexford that ensured Ui Cheinnselaigh's rise to power and fame under Diarmait.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Niall mac Eochada
1 CONT He made an alliance with Niall mac Eochada, king of Ulaid, which helped to put pressure both from the north and south on the kingdoms of Mide, Brega and Dublin - ruled by the High King.
1 CONT
1 CONT This alliance paid off handsomly as the High King, who up till then had Dublin as a subject kingdom, was unable to prevent it slipping into the hands of Diarmait.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT The Man Who Would Be High King
1 CONT In time he was able to claim the title "King of Leinster" and install his son, Murchad, as king of Dublin. Thus ruler of two of the most powerful and wealthy towns on the island, he was able to make a bid for the High-Kingship. It
1 CONC  was during a battle against the king of Mide, Conchobar Ua Maelsechalinn, that he was killed in 1072.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT The Sons of Harald II
1 CONT The surviving sons of King Harald II of England escaped to Leinster after the Battle of Hastings in 1066 where they were hosted by Diarmait. In 1068 and 1069 Diarmait lent them the fleet of Dublin for their attempted invasions of 
1 CONC England.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Death of Murchad and Diarmait
1 CONT He is also famous as as the ancestor of Diarmait MacMurrough.silly billy
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Diarmait's Death in the Annals of the Four Masters
1 CONT "Diarmaid, son of Mael-na-mbo, King of Leinster, of the foreigners of Ath-cliath, and of Leath-Mogha-Nuadhat, was slain and beheaded in the battle of Odhbha, on Tuesday, the seventh of the Ides of February, the battle having been 
1 CONC gained over him by Conchobhar O'Maeleachlainn, King of Meath. There were also slain many hundreds of the foreigners and Leinstermen, along with Diarmaid, in that battle. In it was killed Gillaphadraig O'Fearghaile, lord of the For
1 CONC tuatha, &c. Of the death of Diarmaid was said:"
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Two, seven times ten above one thousand, 
1 CONT From the birth of Christ is reckoned, 
1 CONT To this year, in which Diarmaid, 
1 CONT First man in Leinster, fell. 
1 CONT Diarmaid, of the ruddy-coloured aspect, 
1 CONT A king who maintained the standard of war, 
1 CONT Whose death brought scarcity of peace, 
1 CONT The loss of the heroes of Ladhrann, with their ships. 
1 CONT Comely youths were cut down there, 
1 CONT Together with the head of Claire and Cualann. 
1 CONT It caused in the breeze a noise not pleasant, 
1 CONT The loss of the King of Riada of great valour. 
1 CONT Until at Muillenn-Chul was slain 
1 CONT A brave chieftain of a strong fortress, 
1 CONT Until the furious fire-brand fell by treachery, 
1 CONT They found no hero who dared with him contend. 
1 CONT It is a red wound through my firm heart; 
1 CONT For the host from Caindruim it was not just 
1 CONT To destroy our noble chief they had no right, 
1 CONT It has quenched their spirit greatly, 
1 CONT Diarmaid of the laughing teeth under violent sorrow; 
1 CONT There is not on account of his death banquet or feast; 
1 CONT There will not be peace, there will not be armistice. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Source
1 CONT "Annals of the Four Masters; http://www.ucc.ie/celt/published/T100005B/index.html 
1 CONT "Irish Kings and High Kings", Francis John Bryne, Dublin 1973. 
