0 @N6000000003051310324@ NOTE Jarl Ragnvald then gave the Earldom to his next [illegitimate] son, Einar I (called Turf-Einar because he introduced the use of turf as fuel among his islanders), and gave him one longship and manned it for his use. Einar went to 
1 CONC Shetland to gather a force, and thence south to the Orkneys, which had been occupied by the Danish pirates, Thori Tree-beard and Kalf Skurfa, both of whom he killed circa 894. Halfdan Haaleg, King Harald Haarfagri?s son, unexpecte
1 CONC dly raided Orkney after the burning of Jarl Ragnvald circa 894, and Turf-Einar fled, but returned, surprised Halfdan, defeated him, and next day captured him and cut a "spread-eagle" or "erne" upon his back in order to avenge Jarl
1 CONC  Ragnvald. King Harald Haarfagri came with a fleet to the Orkneys circa 895, and collected a fine of 60 marks of gold from Jarl Turf-Einar for the slaughter of his son Halfdan Haaleg. The name of Jarl Turf-Einar?s wife and the dat
1 CONC e of his death are not recorded; but he "ruled over the Orkneys for a long time and died of sickness." He had 3 sons: Arnkel, Erlend I and Thorfinn. [Complete Peerage, X:Appendix A:4-5]
0 @N6000000003949530977@ NOTE Jarl Ragnvald then gave the Earldom to his next [illegitimate] son, Einar I (called Turf-Einar because he introduced the use of turf as fuel among his islanders), and gave him one longship and manned it for his use. Einar went to 
1 CONC Shetland to gather a force, and thence south to the Orkneys, which had been occupied by the Danish pirates, Thori Tree-beard and Kalf Skurfa, both of whom he killed circa 894. Halfdan Haaleg, King Harald Haarfagri’s son, unexpecte
1 CONC dly raided Orkney after the burning of Jarl Ragnvald circa 894, and Turf-Einar fled, but returned, surprised Halfdan, defeated him, and next day captured him and cut a "spread-eagle" or "erne" upon his back in order to avenge Jarl
1 CONC  Ragnvald. King Harald Haarfagri came with a fleet to the Orkneys circa 895, and collected a fine of 60 marks of gold from Jarl Turf-Einar for the slaughter of his son Halfdan Haaleg. The name of Jarl Turf-Einar’s wife and the dat
1 CONC e of his death are not recorded; but he "ruled over the Orkneys for a long time and died of sickness." He had 3 sons: Arnkel, Erlend I and Thorfinn. [Complete Peerage, X:Appendix A:4-5]
1 CONT The following was copied from Martha Jane Hicks web information. 
