0 @N6000000003635701733@ NOTE [5117] EDWARD3.DOC
0 @N6000000003949959531@ NOTE  Duff was the son of King Malcolm I, and succeeded to the throne on the death (or possibly the abdication) of King Indulf, in 962.
1 CONT The abiding problem faced by Duff during his reign was the challenge of Culen, King Indulf's son. Culen was not prepared to accept the succession arrived at by the law of tanistry and felt that as Indulf's son, the crown should ha
1 CONC ve come directly to him. Following the battle, Duff fell seriously ill and the affairs of state fell into disrepair and rebellions started in a number of areas.  But he recovered, and sought to regain his authority with campaigns 
1 CONC in Moray and Ross. Leaders of the rebellions there were brought to Forres for execution. Those executed included kinsmen of the governor of Forres Castle. In revenge, the governor arranged for Duff's guards to be drugged and for D
1 CONC uff to be kidnapped and then murdered. The murderers diverted a stream from its course under a bridge at Kinloss and buried his body under the stream bed and under the bridge, before allowing the stream to resume its normal course
1 CONC .  Duff's body was recovered from its hiding place and reburied in the graveyard at Saint Oran's Chapel on the Isle of Iona. Duff was succeeded by Culen, who most Scots at the time believed to have been behind Duff's death: and wh
1 CONC o may indeed have been involved. 
0 @N6000000001579451935@ NOTE (Medical):Culen then ruled as King of Alba 966 - 971. Culen in turn was killed by Ryderch, King of Strathclyde, whose daughter he had kidnapped.
