0 @N6000000002187590703@ NOTE Kenneth II of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth II
1 CONT (Cináed mac Maíl Coluim) 
1 CONT King of Alba 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign 971–995 
1 CONT Born before 954 
1 CONT Died 995 
1 CONT  Fettercairn ? 
1 CONT Predecessor Cuilén (Cuilén mac Iduilb) 
1 CONT Successor Constantine III (Causantín mac Cuilén) 
1 CONT Issue Malcolm II (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda)
1 CONT Boite ?
1 CONT Dúngal ? 
1 CONT Royal House Alpin 
1 CONT Father Malcolm I (Máel Coluim mac Domnaill) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Cináed mac Maíl Coluim, (Modern Gaelic: Coinneach mac Mhaoil Chaluim)[1] anglicised as Kenneth II, and nicknamed An Fionnghalach, "The Fratricide" [2] (before 954 – 995) was King of Scotland (Alba). The son of Malcolm I (Máel Colu
1 CONC im mac Domnaill), he succeeded King Cuilén (Cuilén mac Iduilb) on the latter's death at the hands of Amdarch of Strathclyde in 971.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba was compiled in Kenneth's reign, but many of the place names mentioned are entirely corrupt, if not fictitious.[3] Whatever the reality, the Chronicle states that "[h]e immediately plundered [Str
1 CONC athclyde] in part. Kenneth's infantry were slain with very great slaughter in Moin Uacoruar." The Chronicle further states that Kenneth plundered Northumbria three times, first as far as Stainmore, then to Cluiam and lastly to the
1 CONC  River Dee by Chester. These raids may belong to around 980, when the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records attacks on Cheshire.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 973, the Chronicle of Melrose reports that Kenneth, with Máel Coluim I (Máel Coluim mac Domnaill), the King of Strathclyde, "Maccus, king of very many islands" (i.e. Magnus Haraldsson (Maccus mac Arailt), King of Mann and the I
1 CONC sles) and other kings, Welsh and Norse, came to Chester to acknowledge the overlordship of the English king Edgar the Peaceable.[5] It may be that Edgar here regulated the frontier between the southern lands of the kingdom of Alba
1 CONC  and the northern lands of his English kingdom. Cumbria was English, the western frontier lay on the Solway. In the east, the frontier lay somewhere in later Lothian, south of Edinburgh.[6]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Annals of Tigernach, in an aside, name three of the Mormaers of Alba in Kenneth's reign in entry in 976: Cellach mac Fíndgaine, Cellach mac Baireda and Donnchad mac Morgaínd. The third of these, if not an error for Domnall mac
1 CONC  Morgaínd, is very likely a brother of Domnall, and thus the Mormaer of Moray. The Mormaerdoms or kingdoms ruled by the two Cellachs cannot be identified.
1 CONT
1 CONT The feud which had persisted since the death of King Indulf (Idulb mac Causantín) between his descendants and Kenneth's family persisted. In 977 the Annals of Ulster report that "Amlaíb mac Iduilb [Amlaíb, son of Indulf], King of 
1 CONC Scotland, was killed by Cináed mac Domnaill." The Annals of Tigernach give the correct name of Amlaíb's killer: Cináed mac Maíl Coluim, or Kenneth II. Thus, even if only for a short time, Kenneth had been overthrown by the brother
1 CONC  of the previous king.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT Adam of Bremen tells that Sweyn Forkbeard found exile in Scotland at this time, but whether this was with Kenneth, or one of the other kings in Scotland, is unknown. Also at this time, Njal's Saga, the Orkneyinga Saga and other so
1 CONC urces recount wars between "the Scots" and the Northmen, but these are more probably wars between Sigurd Hlodvisson, Earl of Orkney, and the Mormaers, or Kings, of Moray.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Chronicle says that Kenneth founded a great monastery at Brechin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth was killed in 995, the Annals of Ulster say "by deceit" and the Annals of Tigernach say "by his subjects". Some later sources, such as the Chronicle of Melrose, John of Fordun and Andrew of Wyntoun provide more details, ac
1 CONC curately or not. The simplest account is that he was killed by his own men in Fettercairn, through the treachery of Finnguala (also called Fimberhele), daughter of Cuncar, Mormaer of Angus, in revenge for the killing of her only s
1 CONC on.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Prophecy of Berchán adds little to our knowledge, except that it names Kenneth "the kinslayer", and states he died in Strathmore.[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth's son Malcolm II (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda) was later king of Alba. Kenneth may have had a second son, named either Dúngal or Gille Coemgáin.[11] Sources differ as to whether Boite mac Cináeda should be counted a son of Ken
1 CONC neth II or of Kenneth III (Cináed mac Duib).[12]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Cináed mac Maíl Coluim is the Mediaeval Gaelic form. 
1 CONT ^ Skene, Chronicles, p. 96. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, p. 21. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, p. 512; Duncan, p.25. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, pp. 478–479; SAEC, pp. 75–78. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp.24–25. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp. 21–22; ESSH, p. 484. 
1 CONT ^ See ESSH, pp. 483–484 & 495–502. 
1 CONT ^ The name of Cuncar's daughter is given as Fenella, Finele or Sibill in later sources. John of Fordun credits Constantine III (Causantín mac Cuilén) and Kenneth III (Cináed mac Duib) with the planning, claiming that Kenneth II pl
1 CONC anned to change the laws of succession. See ESSH, pp. 512–515. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, p. 516. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of the Four Masters, s.a. 998: "Dúngal Cináed's son, was killed by Gille Coemgáin, Cináed's son." It is not clear if the Cináeds (Kenneths) referred to are Cináed mac Maíl Coluim (Kenneth II) or his nephew and namesake Ci
1 CONC náed mac Duib (Kenneth III). Smyth, pp. 221–222, makes Dúngal following ESSH p. 580. 
1 CONT ^ Compare Duncan, p.345 and Lynch (ed), Genealogies, at about p. 680. See also ESSH, p. 580. 
1 CONT
