0 @N6000000001353915923@ NOTE Erik I, byname ERIK BLOODAX, Norwegian EIRIK BLODØKS (d. 954, Stainmore, Eng.), king of Norway (c. 930-935) and later king of Northumberland (948, 952-954). On the death of his father, Harald I Fairhair, first king of united Norwa
1 CONC y, Erik attempted to make himself sole king of Norway, defeating and slaying two of his brothers to whom vassal kingdoms had been assigned by their father; but his tyranny fostered the reaction that had set in against the strong r
1 CONC ule of Harald. Another son,Haakon, who had been brought up in England, was invited to Norway by dissident nobles and succeeded in ejecting Erik.
1 CONT Much later Erik turned up in Northumbria, once a Viking stronghold but at this time under English overlordship; there he established himself as king in 948 but was driven out the same year. In 952 he returned, only to be expelled 
1 CONC again in 954, when King Eadred of England took the Northumbrian kingdom into his own hands. Erik was slain the same year at Stainmore. With his expulsion, the line of Norse kings in York ended. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, ER
1 CONC IK I]
