0 @N6000000002187590756@ NOTE Kenneth III of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth III
1 CONT (Cináed mac Duib) 
1 CONT King of Alba 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign 997–1005 
1 CONT Born before 967 
1 CONT Died 25 March 1005 
1 CONT  Monzievaird 
1 CONT Buried Iona 
1 CONT Predecessor Constantine III (Causantín mac Cuiléin) 
1 CONT Successor Malcolm II (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda) 
1 CONT Issue Boite mac Cináeda ?
1 CONT Gille Coemgáin ?
1 CONT Giric ? 
1 CONT Royal House Alpin 
1 CONT Father Dub (Dub mac Maíl Coluim) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Cináed mac Duib (Modern Gaelic: Coinneach mac Dhuibh)[1] anglicised as Kenneth III, and nicknamed An Donn, "the Chief" or "the Brown",[2] (before 967–25 March 1005) was King of Scots from 997 to 1005. He was the son of Dub (Dub ma
1 CONC c Maíl Coluim). Many of the Scots sources refer to him as Giric son of Kenneth son of Dub, which is taken to be an error.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT The only event reported in Kenneth's reign is the killing of Dúngal mac Cináeda by Gille Coemgáin mac Cináeda, by the Annals of the Four Masters s.a. 999. It is not certain that this refers to events in Scotland, and whether one o
1 CONC r both were sons of this Kenneth, or of Kenneth II of Scotland, or some other person or persons, is not known.[4] Kenneth was killed in battle at Monzievaird in Strathearn by Malcolm II (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda) in 1005.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Whether Boite mac Cináeda was a son of this Kenneth, or of Kenneth II, is uncertain, although most propose this Kenneth. A son, or grandson of Boite, was reported to be killed by Malcolm II in 1032 in the Annals of Ulster.[6]
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth's granddaughter, Gruoch daughter of Boite (Gruoch ingen Boite meic Cináeda) — Shakespeare's Lady Macbeth — was wife firstly of Gille Coemgáin, Mormaer of Moray, and secondly of King Macbeth; her son by Gille Coemgáin, Lula
1 CONC ch (Lulach mac Gille Coemgáin), would briefly succeed Macbeth as King of Scotland. The meic Uilleim, descendants of William fitz Duncan by his first marriage, were probably descended from Kenneth.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Cináed mac Duib is the Mediaeval Gaelic form. 
1 CONT ^ The former is probable because later English-speaking sources called him "Grim"; Old Irish donn has similar meaning to Old English greimm, which means "power" or "authority"; see Skene, Chronicles, p. 98; Hudson, Celtic Kings, p
1 CONC . 105. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, p. 22; Smyth, pp. 220–221 and 225, prefers to assume that Kenneth had a son, Giric, who ruled jointly with his father; also ESSH, p.522, note 4. 
1 CONT ^ ESSH, p.520, note 5; Smyth, pp. 221–222, makes Gille Coemgáin the son of this Kenneth following ESSH, p. 580. 
1 CONT ^ Annals of Ulster and Chronicon Scotorum, s.a. 1005; the various versions of the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba and the Prophecy of Berchán agree on the site of the battle; ESSH, pp 522–524. Duncan, p. 22, suggests that the kille
1 CONC r's name should be read as Giric mac Cináeda, "Giric son of Kenneth", a brother of Malcolm. If not, this Giric was presumably also killed at about this time. 
1 CONT ^ This Cináed and Boite's son, Smyth, pp. 220–221; Duncan, p. 32. See also ESSH, p. 571, note 7. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, pp. 102–103. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT For primary sources see also External links below.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History A.D 500–1286, volume 1. Reprinted with corrections. Paul Watkins, Stamford, 1990. ISBN 1-871615-03-8 
1 CONT Duncan, A.A.M., The Kingship of the Scots 842–1292: Succession and Independence. Edinburgh University Press, Edinburgh, 2002. ISBN 0-7486-1626-8 
1 CONT Smyth, Alfred P. Warlords and Holy Men: Scotland AD 80-1000. Reprinted, Edinburgh: Edinburgh UP, 1998. ISBN 0-7486-0100-7 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT CELT: Corpus of Electronic Texts at University College Cork includes the Annals of Ulster, Tigernach, the Four Masters and Innisfallen, the Chronicon Scotorum, the Lebor Bretnach (which includes the Duan Albanach), Genealogies, an
1 CONC d various Saints' Lives. Most are translated into English, or translations are in progress. 
1 CONT (CKA) The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Constantine (Causantín) III King of Scots
1 CONT 997–1005 Succeeded by
1 CONT Malcolm (Máel Coluim) II 
