0 @N6000000002187690467@ NOTE Harald I of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Fairhair
1 CONT King of Norway
1 CONT Reign 872 – 930
1 CONT Coronation none
1 CONT Born c. 850
1 CONT Eastern Norway
1 CONT Died 933
1 CONT Rogaland, Norway
1 CONT Buried Karmsund
1 CONT Predecessor none
1 CONT Successor Eirik Bloodaxe
1 CONT Wife/wives Ragnhild Eriksdotter
1 CONT Åsa Håkonsdotter
1 CONT Svanhild Øysteinsdotter
1 CONT various concubines
1 CONT Issue 21 known sons
1 CONT 3 known daughters
1 CONT Sons include:
1 CONT Eirik Bloodaxe
1 CONT Bjørn Farmann
1 CONT Olaf Geirstadalf
1 CONT Haakon the Good
1 CONT Dynasty Fairhair dynasty
1 CONT Father Halfdan the Black
1 CONT Mother Ragnhild Sigurdsdotter
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Fairhair or Harald Finehair (Old Norse: Haraldr hárfagri, Norwegian: Harald Hårfagre), (c. 850 – c. 933) was the first king (872 – 930) of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Little is known of the historical Harald. The only contemporary sources mentioning him are the two skaldic poems Haraldskvæði and Glymdrápa, by Þorbjörn Hornklofi. The first poem describes life at Harald's court, mentions that he 
1 CONC took a Danish wife, and that he won a victory at Hafrsfjord. The second relates a series of battles Harald has won. He is not mentioned in any contemporary foreign sources. His life was described in several of the Kings' sagas, bu
1 CONC t the first of these were not written until the end of the 12th century, over 250 years after his death. Their accounts of Harald and his life differ on several points, and much of the content is clearly mythological. He is credit
1 CONC ed with having unified Norway into one kingdom. Modern historians assume that his rule was limited to the coastal areas of southern Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The saga descriptions
1 CONT
1 CONT The sagas tell us that Harald succeeded, on the death of his father Halfdan the Black Gudrødsson in A.D. 860, to the sovereignty of several small, and somewhat scattered kingdoms in Vestfold, which had come into his father's hands
1 CONC  through conquest and inheritance.
1 CONT
1 CONT The unification of Norway is, according to a tale, somewhat of a love story. The tale begins with a marriage proposal that resulted in rejection and scorn from Gyda, the daughter of Eirik king of Hordaland. She said she refused to
1 CONC  marry Harald "before he was king over all of Norway". Harald was therefore induced to take a vow not to cut nor comb his hair until he was sole king of Norway, and that ten years later, he was justified in trimming it; whereupon 
1 CONC he exchanged the epithet "Shockhead" or "Tanglehair" for the one by which he is usually known. Most scholars today regard this story as a literary tale inspired by the Romance stories popular at the courts, by the time Heimskringl
1 CONC a was written.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 866, Harald made the first of a series of conquests over the many petty kingdoms which would compose Norway, including Värmland in Sweden, and modern day south-eastern Norway, which had sworn allegiance to the Swedish king Erik
1 CONC  Eymundsson. In 872, after a great victory at Hafrsfjord near Stavanger, Harald found himself king over the whole country. His realm was, however, threatened by dangers from without, as large numbers of his opponents had taken ref
1 CONC uge, not only in Iceland, then recently discovered; but also in the Orkney Islands, Shetland Islands, Hebrides Islands, Faroe Islands and the northern European mainland. However, his opponents leaving wasn't entirely voluntary. Ma
1 CONC ny Norwegian chieftains who were wealthy and respected posed a threat to Harald; therefore, they were subjected to much harassment from Harald, prompting them to vacate the land. At last, Harald was forced to make an expedition to
1 CONC  the West, to clear the islands and the Scottish mainland of some Vikings who tried to hide there.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was long thought that Harald thus caused the Norse settlement of Iceland and beyond. According to this view, Iceland was settled by "malcontents" from Norway, who resented Harald's claim of rights of taxation over lands, which 
1 CONC the possessors appear to have previously held in absolute ownership. This view has been largely abandoned by modern historians, as archaeological finds have shown that the settlement of Iceland started before Harald's reign in Nor
1 CONC way.
1 CONT Norwegian petty kingdoms ca. 820 AD at the death of Gudrød the Hunter. The most important kingdoms were Vestfold (red), Hålogaland (purple), Alvheim (yellow) and Agder (green).
1 CONT
1 CONT Petty kingdoms ca. 860 AD at the death of Halfdan the Black. In red is the kingdom inherited by Harald Fairhair.
1 CONT
1 CONT Petty kingdoms ca. 872 AD (the unified kingdom shown in red) before the defining Battle of Hafrsfjord.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Harald I's division of the kingdom ca. 930 AD. The yellow areas are petty kingdoms assigned to Harald's sons and kinsmen, the red area remained Harald's territory as High King, the purple is the domain of the earls of Lade, o
1 CONC range is the domain the earls of Møre.
1 CONT The division of the kingdom after the Battle of Svolder (1000) between Sweden (yellow), Denmark (red) and the jarl of Lade (purple).
1 CONT
1 CONT Unified Norway during the reign of Saint Olav ca. 1020 AD. In pale red the Finnmarken ("Marches of the Sami") most of which paid tribute to the kings of Norway.
1 CONT Harald Haarfager later in his life.
1 CONT Harald Haarfager later in his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT The latter part of Harald's reign was disturbed by the strife of his many sons. He gave them all the royal title and assigned lands to them, which they were to govern as his representatives; but this arrangement did not put an end
1 CONC  to the discord, which continued into the next reign. When he grew old, Harald handed over the supreme power to his favourite son Eirik Bloodaxe, whom he intended to be his successor. Eirik I ruled side-by-side with his father whe
1 CONC n Harald was 80 years old. Harald died three years later due to age in approximately 933. The number of sons he left varies in the different saga accounts, from 11 to 20. Twelve of his sons are named as kings, two of them over the
1 CONC  whole country.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald's children with Åsa, daughter of Håkon Grjotgardsson, Earl of Lade:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Guttorm Haraldsson, king of Ranrike
1 CONT     * Halvdan Kvite (Haraldsson), king of Trondheim
1 CONT     * Halvdan Svarte (Haraldsson), king of Trondheim.
1 CONT     * Sigrød Haraldsson, king of Trondheim
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Gyda:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ålov Årbot (Haraldsdotter)
1 CONT     * Rørek Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Sigtrygg Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Frode Haraldsson
1 CONT     * (Torgils Haraldsson)¹
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Ragnhild Eiriksdotter of Jutland:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Eirik Bloodaxe, king of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Svanhild, daughter of Eystein Earl:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bjørn Farmann, king of Vestfold.
1 CONT     * Olaf Haraldsson Geirstadalf, king of Vingulmark, later also Vestfold.
1 CONT     * Ragnar Rykkel
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Åshild, daughter of Ring Dagsson:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ring Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Dag Haraldsson
1 CONT     * Gudrød Skirja
1 CONT     * Ingegjerd Haraldsdotter
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Snøfrid, daughter of Svåse the Finn:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Halvdan Hålegg
1 CONT     * Gudrød Ljome
1 CONT     * Ragnvald Rettilbeine
1 CONT     * Sigurd Rise
1 CONT
1 CONT Children with Tora Mosterstong, his maid:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Haakon the Good, king of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Other children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ingebjørg Haraldsdotter
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald was the grandfather of Harald II.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. This Torgils Haraldsson is identical to "Thorgest" in the Irish history. Snorre Sturlason, in his Heimskringla, claims that Torgils was Harald's son. This is now thought to be unlikely.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Fairhair
1 CONT Fairhair dynasty
1 CONT Born: c. 850 Died: c. 933
1 CONT Regnal titles
1 CONT New title King of Norway
1 CONT 872-930 Succeeded by
1 CONT Eric Bloodaxe
0 @N6000000002621242257@ NOTE Harald Hårfagre (ca. 850-933) (Haraldr hárfagri) var Norges konge ca. år 872-933.
1 CONT
1 CONT Som konge
1 CONT Han var i følge Snorre den første kongen som styrte over hele Norge, han vant mange slag og la under seg i tillegg til Norge øyene i vest og Värmland i Sverige. Det mest kjente slaget han vant er slaget i Hafrsfjord, trolig i år 8
1 CONC 72. Der underla han seg store deler av Vestlandet og Sørlandet.
1 CONT
1 CONT I følge et sagn gjengitt i Harald Hårfagres saga var Haralds motiv for rikssamlingen at han ønsket å ekte Gyda. Harald hadde forelsket seg i denne kongsdatteren (datter til Kong Eirik av Hordaland), som ble fostret opp av en rik b
1 CONC onde i Valdres. Han sendte sine sendemenn for �� hente henne, men hun ville ikke ha Harald, fordi han ikke var mektig nok. Hun lurte på hvorfor han ikke hadde lagt hele Norge under seg. Han ble slett ikke fornærmet av det hun sa, 
1 CONC men tok utfordringen, og lovte å ikke klippe seg før han hadde samlet hele landet under seg.
1 CONT Allerede for 200 år siden begynte historikerne å regne fortellingen som annet enn en fabel fra hentet ridderroman.
1 CONT Han var kjent for å styre landet med hard hånd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tilnavnet «Hårfagre»
1 CONT Tilnavnet «Hårfagre» fikk han etter at han hadde lovet Gyda å ikke klippe håret før hele Norge var samlet til ett rike. Gyda sa ogs�� at hun ville gifte seg med Harald dersom han fikk samlet Norge. Det gikk mange år, men da han en
1 CONC delig nådde målet, sendte han sine folk til Gyda og minnet henne på hva hun hadde sagt til ham. De tok henne med til Harald, og de giftet seg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Etter samlingen dro Harald til Ragnvald Jarl på Møre. Da var han ikke klipt på 10 år, men her kunne han endelig la håret falle. Det sies at Han lot håret falle på Bremsnes på Averøya. På det stedet st��r det i dag et stort tre med
1 CONC  en steinmur rundt, som et minne. Det var Ragnvald Jarl som da ga han tilnavnet «Hårfagre».
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Hårfagres død
1 CONT Da Harald var 80 år gammel, gav han kronen til sin sønn Eirik Blodøks, fordi han selv begynte å bli svak. Han levde enda tre år, men døde da sottedød (av sykdom). Han er hauglagt på Haug ved Karmsund.
1 CONT
1 CONT Familie og barn
1 CONT Hans far var Halvdan Svarte (Gudrødsson), hans mor var Ragnhild Sigurdsdatter. Han ble født ca. år 850, og døde i ca. år 933. Farsslekten kom ifølge Snorre fra Ynglingeætten, og var en mektig slekt, siden de skal stamme fra guden 
1 CONC Frøy selv.
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Hårfagre hadde mange barn, med forskjellige kvinner. Hvor mange han fikk er ukjent. Etter Haralds død, ble det å stamme fra Harald Hårfagre politisk opportunt: det ga arverett til kongemakten. Å ha Harald Hårfagre som oldef
1 CONC ar ga uendelig mye mer legitimitet til et maktprosjekt, enn å stamme fra en av de mange
1 CONT tilfeldige småkongene han la under seg. Det er mer enn sannsynlig at mange av de slektslinjene som senere tiders høvdinger viste til, hadde blitt redigert av hensyn til dette.
1 CONT
1 CONT Følgende oversikt over Haralds barn må derfor tas med en klype salt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Med Gyda Eiriksdatter:
1 CONT - Ålov Årbot
1 CONT - Rørek Haraldsson
1 CONT - Sigtrygg Haraldsson
1 CONT - Frode Haraldsson
1 CONT - Torgils Haraldsson
1 CONT
1 CONT Med Åsa Håkonsdatter:
1 CONT - Guttorm Haraldsson
1 CONT - Halvdan Svarte Haraldsson
1 CONT - Halvdan Kvite Haraldsson
1 CONT - Sigrød Haraldsson
1 CONT
1 CONT Med Ragnhild Eiriksdatter:
1 CONT - Eirik Blodøks
1 CONT
1 CONT Med Svanhild Øysteinsdatter:
1 CONT - Bjørn Farmann
1 CONT - Olav Haraldsson Geirstadalf
1 CONT - Ragnar Rykkel
1 CONT
1 CONT Med Åshild Ringsdatter:
1 CONT - Ring Haraldsson
1 CONT - Dag Haraldsson
1 CONT - Gudrød Skirja
1 CONT - Ingegjerd Haraldsdatter (kanskje Tora Mosterstong var hennes mor)
1 CONT
1 CONT Med Snøfrid Svåsedatter:
1 CONT - Sigurd Haraldsson Rise
1 CONT - Halvdan Hålegg
1 CONT - Gudrød Ljome
1 CONT - Ragnvald Rettilbeine
1 CONT
1 CONT Med Tora Mosterstong
1 CONT - Håkon den gode (Håkon Adalsteinsfostre)
0 @N6000000003220311657@ NOTE Regj. 890-940.
0 @N6000000003232674633@ NOTE Raids On Europe Reached Peak During Reign. 3 Years Before His Death He Gave Throne To His Son Eirik. Became King At Age 10. Described As Large, Strong And Handsome With Long Fair Hair. Gave Up His Other Wives When He Married Ragnh
1 CONC ild The Mighty. Grew Up Near Randsfjorden, Norge With Uncle Guttorm. Migration To Iceland Continued.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alias:<ALIA> Harald /Fairhair/, Hairbeauty or Luva According to Tom Bjørnstad, Norwegian genealogist (http://home.ringnett.no/bjornstad/royals/Norge.html) : "King Harald I Harfagre also known as "Harald Fairhair" the first to unif
1 CONC y Norway as one kingdom."  "Harold Fairhair's reign is officially recognized as the period 865-933, but this is likely wrong.  Modern studies of the history in Scandinavia reveals approximately 890 CE to be a more correct date to 
1 CONC the Battle of Hafrsfjord.  On this base we believe he must have died in approximately 940 CE. From "The building of a nation"
1 CONT
1 CONT (http://home.ringnett.no/bjornstad/royals /into.html): "The history of Norway as a nation begins more or less with the Battle of Hafrsfjord in approximately 890 CE, a battle that was won by Harold Fairhair (Harald Harfagre).  By w
1 CONC inning this battle he managed to unite Norge in one kingdom, as the first absolute monarch in all of Scandinavia, though the process to fulfill it did certainly last a lot longer. Harold Fairhair was, according to the folk tales k
1 CONC nown as the Nordic Sagas, the son of Halvdan the Black and his second wife, Asa of Agder.  Final proof of Harold Fairhair's existence do not exist, but it is highly probable due to proof of several of his sons existence. The most 
1 CONC famous being Eirik Blood-axe, the last Viking king of York, who was killed in the Battle of Stainmore in 954 CE. For a short time he had also been a kind of over king back in Norge, but he was expelled due to his ruggedness. Also 
1 CONC Hakon the Good, half brother of Eirik, is well known. He succeeded to the throne of Norge in approximately 938/9 A D. He was also called Hákon Æþelstan-foster, because he was supposedly brought up by the Royal Court of Æþelstan, K
1 CONC ing of England (924-939), to whom Harold Fairhair had sent him after a previous episode between the two kings ."
1 CONT
1 CONT From Norge. Sigvart Sorensen. New York. 1899: "Note: He was 10 years old when he became King.  He asked for the hand of Princess Gyda Eiriksson, but was turned down as she wanted a king who made the whole of Norway subject to him.
1 CONC  So Harald gathered an army and took over the north and west of Norge in 872 and was acknowledged as the King of Norway. He then married Gyda. He instituted taxes on land and possessions and placed an Earl over each district to co
1 CONC llect taxes, administer justice, and serve the king with 60 warriors in case of war.  Many objected and left for the Faroe Islands, Scottish Islands and Iceland.  Harald, therefore took over the Orkney and Shetland Islands.  He ha
1 CONC d six wives and many sons and when 80 years old became weak and turned the government over to son, Eirik.  He died three years later and is buried in a  mound at Hauge near Haugesand in Ryfylke.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some sources list his birth in 858.
