0 @N6000000002187965515@ NOTE King of Limerick; son of Sitric; d. 940. During the late 10th and early 11th centuries, a dynasty briefly appears whose kings were called king of the Isles (i.e., principally the Hebrides), and at least one of these kings (Godred,
1 CONC  who is called king of Man in Njal's Saga, an Icelandic source) appears to have ruled in Man.The use of the rare name "Lagman" among the later kings of Man also suggests that they regarded themselves as the successors of these kin
1 CONC gs of "the Isles". Prob. father of Godred/Goffraid who d. 989, king of the Isles. [Stewart Baldwin
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Sigtryg Sigtrygsson and Edgyth, Princess of England; m. Edgyth; father of Godfred of Man who m. ?? of Islay
0 @N6000000003635713136@ NOTE [4685] "Bloodline ... ", p 427-31
0 @N6000000002187592430@ NOTE Eadgifu of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eadgifu (b. 902, d. after 955) Ogiva or Edgifu, was a daughter [1] of Edward the Elder, King of Wessex and England, and his second wife Aelffaed. She was born in Wessex.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Marriage to the French King 
1 CONT 2 Flight to England 
1 CONT 3 Notes 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage to the French King
1 CONT She was the second wife of King Charles III of France,[1] whom she married in 919 after the death of his first wife, Frederonne; she was mother to Louis IV of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Flight to England
1 CONT In 922 Charles III was deposed and the next year taken prisoner by Count Herbert II of Vermandois, an ally of the present King. To protect her son's safety Eadgifu took him to England in 923 to the court of her half-brother, Athel
1 CONC stan of England.[2] Because of this, Louis IV of France became known as Louis d'Outremer of France. He stayed there until 936, when he was called back to France to be crowned King. Eadgifu accompanied him.
1 CONT
1 CONT She retired to a convent in Laon. Then, in 951, she left the convent and married Herbert III, Count of Vermandois.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ a b Lappenberg, Johann; Benjamin Thorpe, translator (1845). A History of England Under the Anglo-Saxon Kings. J. Murray, pp. 88-89.  
1 CONT ^ a b Williams, Ann; Alfred P. Smyth, D. P. Kirby (1991). A Biographical Dictionary of Dark Age Britain: England, Scotland, and Wales. Routledge, p. 112. ISBN 1852640472.  
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 50-20, 148-17. 
1 CONT Lappenberg, Johann; Benjamin Thorpe, translator (1845). A History of England Under the Anglo-Saxon Kings. J. Murray, pp. 88-89.  
1 CONT Williams, Ann; Alfred P. Smyth, D. P. Kirby (1991). A Biographical Dictionary of Dark Age Britain: England, Scotland, and Wales. Routledge, p. 112. ISBN 1852640472.  
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Frederonne Queen of Western Francia
1 CONT 919–922 Succeeded by
1 CONT Béatrice of Vermandois 
