0 @N6000000001783499391@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born to William I of Normandy, ruler of Normandy, and Sprota of Senlis. He was born probably between 932 and 935; he was still a boy when his father died in 942. His mother was a Breton concubine captured in war and bound t
1 CONC o William by a Danish marriage. After William died, Sprota became the wife of Esperleng, a wealthy miller.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was still a boy when his father died, and so he was powerless to stop Louis IV of France when he seized Normandy. Louis kept him in confinement in his youth at Lâon, but he escaped with the assistance of Osmund de Centevil
1 CONC le, Bernard de Senlis (who had been a companion of Rollo of Normandy), Ivo de Bellèsme, and Bernard the Dane (ancestor of families of Harcourt and Beaumont). In 968, Richard agreed to "commend" himself to Hugh, Count of Paris. He 
1 CONC then allied himself with the Norman and Viking leaders, drove Louis out of Rouen, and took back Normandy by 947. He later quarrelled with Ethelred II of England regarding Viking invasions of England because Normandy had been buyin
1 CONC g up much of the stolen booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was bilingual, having been well educated at Bayeux. He was more partial to his Norse and Danish subjects than to the French. During his reign, Normandy became completely Gallicized and Christianized. He introduced the feud
1 CONC al system and Normandy became one of the most thoroughly feudalized states on the continent. He carried out a major reorganization of the Norman military system, based on heavy cavalry. He also became guardian of the young Hugh, C
1 CONC ount of Paris, on the elder Hugh's death in 956
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Emma of Paris
1 CONT
1 CONT He married 1st (960) Emma of Paris, daughter of Hugh the Great, Duke of the Franks and Count of Paris. They were betrothed when both were very young. She died after 966, with no issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gunnor de Crepon
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Robert of Torigni, not long after Emma's death, Duke Richard went out hunting and stopped at the house of a local forester. He became enamoured of the forester's wife, Seinfreda, but she being a virtuous woman, sugges
1 CONC ted he court her unmarried sister, Gunnor de Crepon, instead. Gunnor became his bride, and her family rose to prominence. Her brother, Herefast de Crepon, may have been involved in a controversial heresy trial. Gunnor was, like Ri
1 CONC chard, of Norse descent, being a Dane by blood. Richard finally married her to legitimate their children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in Fecamp, France on November 20, 996 of natural causes. (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_I_of_Normandy)
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard I of Normandy was the leader of the Normans of Rouen [anachronistically, "Duke of Normandy"], 942-996.  A minor at the assassination of his father William in 942, it was largely during Richard's long period of rule that wh
1 CONC at eventually became the duchy of Normandy evolved from what was essentially a pirate principality into a feudal state. Richard is described by such a wide range of words (comes, marchio, consul, princeps, dux) by various sources 
1 CONC (sometimes of dubious authority) that it would be difficult to argue that there is a specific "title" by which he should be called [see Helmerichs 1997].
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Emma of France
1 CONT
1 CONT Daughter of Hughes le Grand, duke of France., in 960 [Flodoard's Annals, s.a. 960 (MGH SS 3, 405)], who was still alive 966 [GND 1: 129, n. 6, citing Fauroux (1961), #3], d.s.p. [Dudo iv, 85 (p. 163); GND iv, 18 (vol. 1, pp. 128-9
1 CONC )].
1 CONT
1 CONT Gunnor
1 CONT
1 CONT [Dudo iv, 125 (p. 163)], d. 4 or 8 January 1031 [Chron. Rothomag. RHF 10: 324 (year only); Ex Obituario Gemmeticensi, RHF 23, 417 (4 Jan.); Notæ Monasterii Montis Sancti Micaelis, RHF 23, 576 (8 Jan.)]. Robert de Torigny, in his a
1 CONC dditions to GND, stated that she was first Richard's mistress, and that the marriage occurred after the birth of the children. [GND (Rob. Tor.) viii, 36 (vol. 2, pp. 266-9)].
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard and Gunnor had the following children:
1 CONT Richard II, Duke of Normandy
1 CONT Robert, Archbishop of Roen (d. 1037) and Count of Évreux
1 CONT Mauger, count of Corbeil (d. after 1033)
1 CONT Robertus Danus, (d. bef. 985×989)
1 CONT 2 Unamed sons
1 CONT Emma of Normandy (d. 1052) , married  Æthelred II "the Unready", King of England; and Knud/Canute, (d. 1035), King of England, Denmark and Norway
1 CONT Hawise, married to Geoffrey I, Duke of Bretagne (Brittany). (d. 1034)
1 CONT Mathilde,  married to Eudes II, Count of Bloisd. (d. bef. 1005)
1 CONT
1 CONT He had several natural children by unknown mistresses.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was succeeded by his son Richard II in 996.
1 CONT <http://sbaldw.home.mindspring.com/hproject/prov/richa000.html
