0 @N6000000002187590648@ NOTE Crínán of Dunkeld
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Crínán of Dunkeld (died 1045) was the lay abbot of the diocese of Dunkeld, and perhaps the Mormaer of Atholl. Crínán was progenitor of the House of Dunkeld, the dynasty who would rule Scotland until the later 13th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crinán was married to Bethoc, daughter of King Malcolm II of Scotland (reigned 1005-1034). As Malcolm II had no son, the strongest hereditary claim to the Scottish throne descended through Bethóc, and Crinán's eldest son Donnchad 
1 CONC I (reigned 1034-1040), became King of Scots. Some sources indicate that Malcolm II designated Duncan as his successor under the rules of tanistry because there were other possible claimants to the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crinán's second son, Maldred of Allerdale, held the title of Lord of Cumbria. It is said that from him, the Earls of Dunbar, for example Patrick Dunbar, 9th Earl of Dunbar, descend in unbroken male line.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crinán was killed in battle in 1045 at Dunkeld.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Crinán as Lay Abbot of Dunkeld
1 CONT The monastery of Saint Columba was founded on the north bank of the River Tay in the 6th century or early 7th century following the expedition of Columba into the land of the Picts. Probably originally constructed as a simple grou
1 CONC p of wattle huts, the monastery - or at least its church - was rebuilt in the 9th century by Kenneth I of Scotland (reigned 843-858). Kenneth I brought relics of Columba from Iona to Dunkeld at the same time others were taken to K
1 CONC ells in Ireland, to protect them from Viking raids. Dunkeld became the prime bishopric in eastern Scotland until supplanted in importance by St Andrews since the 10th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT While the title of Hereditary Lay Abbot was a feudal position that was often exercised in name only, Crinán does seem to have acted as Abbot in charge of the monastery in his time. He was thus a man of high position in both cleric
1 CONC al and secular society.
1 CONT
1 CONT The magnificent semi-ruined Dunkeld Cathedral, built in stages between 1260 and 1501, stands today on the grounds once occupied by the monastery. The Cathedral contains the only surviving remains of the previous monastic society: 
1 CONC a course of red stone visible in the east choir wall that may be re-used from an earlier building, and two stone 9th century-10th century cross-slabs in the Cathedral Museum.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Hereditary lay abbot of Dunkeld and Dull. Crinan was the son of Duncan, Mormaer of Athol. He may have been descended from an earlier abbot of Dunkeld, Donnchadh, which means *brown warrior* in Gaelic. According to some sources, Cr
1 CONC inan was killed in battle against Macbeth
0 @N6000000002187915732@ NOTE Sigurd Hlodvirsson, Earl of Orkney
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Sigurd Hlodvirsson ("Sigurd the Stout") was the 14th Jarl of Orkney and a key figure in the Battle of Clontarf. He was born around AD 960, and is mentioned in the Orkneyinga Saga and other Medieval sources. According to the 1
1 CONC 3th century Njal's Saga, Gormflaith prompted her son, Sigtrygg Silkbeard, into getting Sigurd to fight against her former husband, Brian Ború:
1 CONT
1 CONT     "...she sent him to Earl Sigurd to ask for support... Sigtrygg sailed back to Ireland and told his mother that the jarl had joined him."
1 CONT
1 CONT The 12th century Irish source, the Cogadh Gaedhil re Gallaibh, records the events of the battle. The "foreigners and Leinstermen" were led by Brodir of the Isle of Man and Sigurd, and the battle lasted all day. Though Brian was ki
1 CONC lled in the battle, the Irishmen ultimately drove back their enemies into the sea. Sigurd also died that day at Clontarf, in 1014.
