0 @N6000000000929911862@ NOTE was killed by Macbeth
0 @N6000000001563195276@ NOTE Duncan I the Gracious, King of Scotland, added Strathclyde to the kingdom, and is thus considered to be the first king of a united Scotland. His reign, however, was a period of disatrous wars and internal strife; and ended in 1040
1 CONC  when he was defeated and killed in battle by Macbeth, Mormær of Ross and Moray who then became king. 
1 CONT Despite Shakespeare's depiction to the contrary, Macbeth was an honest monarch who was generous to the church; and as a grandson of Malcolm (II), had as legitimate a claim to the throne as did Duncan. As well, Macbeth's wife Gruoc
1 CONC h was a greatX2 granddaughter of Malcolm (I). Duncan's wife, on the other hand, was a relative of Siward, the Viking Earl of Northumbria; which helps explain why Siward assisted Duncan's sons in defeating Macbeth. 
1 CONT In all fairness, it should be borne in mind that Shakespeare based his work on Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles and that he did not intend Macbeth to be an historical documentary, but rather an entertaining play.
0 @N6000000001563195278@ NOTE (Medical):This was the same duncan who was murdered in Shakespeare's play "Macbeth"
0 @N6000000003796233479@ NOTE ==================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT FAIRBAIRN Genealogy
1 CONT URL:  http://home.austarnet.com.au/dfgoonan/FAIRBAIRNpg.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT  Fairbairn was supposed to derive from Fair (white, pale, handsome) and bairn (child), but this seems unlikely as surnames weren't granted to children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another more likely theory has the surname derive from a Viking Prince named Siward “the Fair” BEORN (bear). He was also known as Siward Fairbairn “of the Strong Arm”. His father was Siward "Digira" whose father was Hringo, King o
1 CONC f Upland who was also known as Earl Beorn (bear). The tales about Siward and his father were called “Fay Bairn” (fairy bear) and relate to Nordic legends.
1 CONT
1 CONT Siward "Digira" lived in England from about 995 until his death in 1056. King Edward “the Confessor” conferred the title of Earl of Northumbria (Northumberland) on the giant man.
1 CONT
1 CONT An enduring story tells of Siward’s encounter with Tostig, Earl of Huntington. Tostig apparently insulted Siward by throwing dirt on him when they met on a bridge. Siward had just left a meeting with King Edward and took no offenc
1 CONC e. They met again on the same bridge and this time Siward decapitated Tostig and carried his head to King Edward. Edward was so impressed that he awarded Siward the Earldom of Huntington in addition to Northumbria. (If Siward kill
1 CONC ed Tostig prior to 1056, then it would discredit sources that say that Tostig was defeated by his brother Harold at the Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Siward’s sister married Duncan, King of Scots. Siward’s eldest son was Osberne “Bulax” who was killed in battle with “Macbeth” on 27th July 1054. Osberne was said to have married the daughter of Lady Godiva. Siward’s second son Wa
1 CONC ltheof married Juditha, a niece of William “the Conqueror” who helped Waltheof regain the title of Earl of Northumbria in 1069. Their friendship didn’t last long though as King William had Waltheof beheaded outside Westminster in 
1 CONC 1076.
1 CONT
1 CONT Osberne “Bulax” had two sons named Siward Barn “the Red” and Siward Barn “the White” (Fairbarn). Siward “the White” became a refugee and fled to Scotland with Edgar “the Atheling” where he was greeted kindly by his cousin Malcolm 
1 CONC III, King of Scots. Siward and Malcolm III set about driving the Normans out of Northumbria. During battle Malcolm III had his horse killed under him and was partially crippled. Siward Fairbairn fought his way to the armoured King
1 CONC , took him under his arm and fought his way to safety. For this bravery he was knighted Sir Armstrange and given lands and a castle on the Scottish border. It is from this Siward Fairbairn that the Armstrongs of Mangerton claim de
1 CONC cent.
0 @N6000000001578847438@ NOTE Duncan I the Gracious, King of Scotland, added Strathclyde to the kingdom, and is thus considered to be the first king of a united Scotland. His reign, however, was a period of disatrous wars and internal strife; and ended in 1040
1 CONC  when he was defeated and killed in battle by Macbeth, Mormær of Ross and Moray who then became king.
1 CONT Despite Shakespeare's depiction to the contrary, Macbeth was an honest monarch who was generous to the church; and as a grandson of Malcolm (II), had as legitimate a claim to the throne as did Duncan. As well, Macbeth's wife Gruoc
1 CONC h was a greatX2 granddaughter of Malcolm (I). Duncan's wife, on the other hand, was a relative of Siward, the Viking Earl of Northumbria; which helps explain why Siward assisted Duncan's sons in defeating Macbeth.
1 CONT In all fairness, it should be borne in mind that Shakespeare based his work on Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles and that he did not intend Macbeth to be an historical documentary, but rather an entertaining play.
0 @N6000000001578847440@ NOTE (Medical):This was the same duncan who was murdered in Shakespeare's play "Macbeth"
0 @N6000000002319315059@ NOTE Duncan met his fate at the hands of Macbeth in 1040; himself slain by
1 CONT Malcolm (Ceann Mor or Bighead) who became King Malcolm III. Malcolm
1 CONT  married his second wife the English Princess Margaret, who had fled to
1 CONT Scotland at the coming of the Normans. She introduced many English
1 CONT  fashions and customs to Scotland and established a refined court life.
1 CONT  Margaret also imposed English religious practices on the Scottish
1 CONT clergy and
1 CONT her husband moved the cultural center of his kingdom to Lothian, away
1 CONT from
1 CONT  the Celtic north.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Unfortunately for the stability of Malcolm and Margaret's kingdom,
1 CONT however,
1 CONT the Scottish king's constant excursions into Northern England brought
1 CONT him the
1 CONT  enmity of the Norman William who forced him to pay homage at Abernethy
1 CONT in
1 CONT   1071. On one of his attacks on Northumberland in 1093, Malcolm was
1 CONT killed,
1 CONT  his sainted wife following him in death a few days later. Margaret was
1 CONT later
1 CONT  canonized for her benefactions to the Church including the rebuilding
1 CONT of the
1 CONT monastery at Iona.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT
1 CONT  He acceded on November 25, 1034. Duncan died on August 14, 1040 at
1 CONT Bothganowan
1 CONT   in Elgin and was interred on the Isle of Iona in Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Duncan (died 1040), Scottish king murdered by Macbeth; Shakespeare
1 CONT based his version of 'Macbeth' on Holinshed, who pictured Duncan as
1 CONT kind and honorable, but earlier historians disagree with this.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
