0 @N6000000002187590582@ NOTE Duncan I of Scotland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duncan I
1 CONT (Donnchad mac Crínáin) 
1 CONT King of Scots 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign 1034–1040 
1 CONT Birthplace Scotland 
1 CONT Died August 14, 1040 (aged 38)[1] 
1 CONT  Pitgaveny, near Elgin 
1 CONT Buried Iona ? 
1 CONT Predecessor Malcolm II (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda) 
1 CONT Successor Macbeth (Mac Bethad mac Findláich) 
1 CONT Consort Suthen 
1 CONT Issue Malcolm III (Máel Coluim mac Donnchada)
1 CONT Donalbane (Domnall Bán mac Donnchada) 
1 CONT Royal House Dunkeld 
1 CONT Father Crínán of Dunkeld 
1 CONT Mother Bethóc 
1 CONT
1 CONT Donnchad mac Crínáin (Modern Gaelic: Donnchadh mac Crìonain)[2] anglicised as Duncan I, and nicknamed An t-Ilgarach, "the Diseased" or "the Sick"[3] (died 14 August 1040)[1] was king of Scotland (Alba). He was son of Crínán, hered
1 CONC itary lay abbot of Dunkeld, and Bethóc, daughter of king Malcolm II of Scotland (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda).
1 CONT
1 CONT Unlike the "King Duncan" of Shakespeare's Macbeth, the historical Duncan appears to have been a young man. He followed his grandfather Malcolm as king after the latter's death on 25 November 1034, without apparent opposition. He m
1 CONC ay have been Malcolm's acknowledged successor or tánaise as the succession appears to have been uneventful.[4] Earlier histories, following John of Fordun, supposed that Duncan had been king of Strathclyde in his grandfather's lif
1 CONC etime, ruling the former Kingdom of Strathclyde as an appanage. Modern historians discount this idea.[5]
1 CONT
1 CONT Another claim by Fordun, that Duncan married the sister, daughter or cousin of Sigurd the Dane, Earl of Northumbria, appears to be equally unreliable. An earlier source, a variant of the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba (CK-I), give
1 CONC s Duncan's wife the Gaelic name Suthen.[6] Whatever his wife's name may have been, Duncan had at least two sons. The eldest, Malcolm III (Máel Coluim mac Donnchada) was king from 1057 to 1093, the second Donald III (Domnall Bán, o
1 CONC r "Donalbane") was king afterwards. Máel Muire, Earl of Atholl is a possible third son of Duncan, although this is uncertain.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT The early period of Duncan's reign was apparently uneventful, perhaps a consequence of his youth. Macbeth (Mac Bethad mac Findláich) is recorded as his dux, literally duke, but in the context — "dukes of Francia" had half a centur
1 CONC y before replaced the Carolingian kings of the Franks and in England the over-mighty Godwin of Wessex was called a dux — this suggests that Macbeth was the power behind the throne.[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1039, Duncan led a large Scots army south to besiege Durham, but the expedition ended in disaster. Duncan survived, but the following year he led an army north into Moray, traditionally seen as Macbeth's domain. There he was ki
1 CONC lled, at Pitgaveny near Elgin, by his own men led by Macbeth, probably on 14 August 1040.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Depictions in fiction
1 CONT Duncan is depicted as an elderly King in Macbeth by William Shakespeare. He is killed in his sleep by the protagonist, Macbeth.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ a b Broun, "Duncan I (d. 1040)". 
1 CONT ^ Donnchad mac Crínáin is the Mediaeval Gaelic form. 
1 CONT ^ Skene, Chronicles, p. 101. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, p. 33. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, p. 40. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, p. 37. 
1 CONT ^ Oram, David I, p. 233, n. 26: the identification is from the Orkneyinga saga but Máel Muire's grandson Máel Coluim, Earl of Atholl is known to have married Donald III's granddaughter Hextilda. 
1 CONT ^ Duncan, Kingship of the Scots, pp. 33–34. 
1 CONT ^ Broun, "Duncan I (d. 1040)"; the date is from Marianus Scotus and the killing is recorded by the Annals of Tigernach. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History AD 500 to 1286, volume one. Republished with corrections, Paul Watkins, Stamford, 1990. ISBN 1-871615-03-8 
1 CONT Broun, Dauvit, "Duncan I (d. 1040)", Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, Oxford University Press, 2004 accessed 15 May 2007 
1 CONT Duncan, A. A. M., The Kingship of the Scots 842–1292: Succession and Independence. Edinburgh University Press, Edinburgh, 2002. ISBN 0-7486-1626-8 
1 CONT Oram, Richard, David I: The King Who Made Scotland. Tempus, Stroud, 2004. ISBN 0-7524-2825-X 
1 CONT Duncan I of Scotland
1 CONT House of Dunkeld
1 CONT Born: unknown 14 August 
1 CONT Regnal titles 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Malcolm (Máel Coluim) II King of Scots
1 CONT 1034–1040 Succeeded by
1 CONT Macbeth (Mac Bethad) 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT King of Strathclyde 1018 - 1034 King of Scots 1034 - 1040 Although depicted by Shakespeare as having been killed by Macbeth, he was actually killed in the battle of Burghead, near Elgin (which battle may indeed have been against t
1 CONC he forces of Macbeth) Source: RoyaList
