0 @N6000000003949642639@ NOTE Duncan I (d. Aug. 1, 1040, near Elgin, Moray, Scot.), king of the Scots from 1034 to 1040. Duncan was the grandson of King Malcolm II (ruled 1005-34), who irregularly made him ruler of Strathclyde when that region was absorbed int
1 CONC o the Scottish kingdom (probably shortly before 1034). Malcolm violated the established system of succession whereby the kingship alternated between two branches of the royal family. Upon Malcolm's death, Duncan succeeded peaceful
1 CONC ly, but he soon faced the rivalry of Macbeth, Mormaor (subking) of Moray, who probably had a better claim to the throne. Duncan besieged Durham unsuccessfully in 1039 and in the following year was murdered by Macbeth. Duncan's eld
1 CONC er son later killed Macbeth and ruled as King Malcolm III Canmore (1058-93).  #FC55 in Ashley's "Kings & Queens"
0 @N6000000000437753369@ NOTE Murdered by Macbeth
0 @N6000000004533699971@ NOTE Notes for Duncan I King of Scotland:
1 CONT
1 CONT Killed by Macbeth
1 CONT
1 CONT Duncan was the son of Malcolm II's eldest daughter Bethoc and herhusband Crinan, Lay Abbot of Dunkeld. He was about 33 when hesucceeded his grandfather. Married to a cousin of Siward, Earl ofNorthumberland he may have favoured sou
1 CONC thern ways and this is perhapswhy he became unpopular with his subjects. In 1039 he did march southto besiege Durham but he was beaten off, with heavy losses. Duncanattempted to impose his overlordship over Moray (an independentdy
1 CONC nasty) by military force. He was then twice defeated by the Earl ofOrkney's son, Thorfinn, before being killed in battle by Macbeth, oneof his commanders, near Elgin, Morayshire on 14 August 1040 as madefamous by William Shakespea
1 CONC re.
0 @N6000000006296755713@ NOTE Donnchad mac Crínáin (Modern Gaelic </wiki/Scottish_Gaelic_language>: Donnchadh mac Crìonain)[2] anglicised as Duncan I, and nicknamed An t-Ilgarach, "the Diseased" or "the Sick"[3] (died 14 August 1040)[1] was king of Scotland </
1 CONC wiki/Kingdom_of_Scotland> (Alba </wiki/Kingdom_of_Alba>). He was son of Crínán </wiki/Cr%C3%ADn%C3%A1n_of_Dunkeld>, hereditary lay abbot </wiki/Lay_abbot> of Dunkeld </wiki/Dunkeld>, and Bethóc </wiki/Beth%C3%B3c>, daughter of kin
1 CONC g Malcolm II of Scotland </wiki/Malcolm_II_of_Scotland> (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda).
1 CONT Unlike the "King Duncan </wiki/King_Duncan>" of Shakespeare's </wiki/William_Shakespeare> Macbeth </wiki/Macbeth>, the historical Duncan appears to have been a young man. He followed his grandfather Malcolm as king after the latte
1 CONC r's death on 25 November </wiki/November_25> 1034 </wiki/1034>, without apparent opposition. He may have been Malcolm's acknowledged successor or tánaise </wiki/Tanistry> as the succession appears to have been uneventful.[4] Earli
1 CONC er histories, following John of Fordun </wiki/John_of_Fordun>, supposed that Duncan had been king of Strathclyde </wiki/King_of_Strathclyde> in his grandfather's lifetime, ruling the former Kingdom of Strathclyde </wiki/Kingdom_of
1 CONC _Strathclyde> as an appanage </wiki/Appanage>. Modern historians discount this idea.[5]
1 CONT Another claim by Fordun, that Duncan married the sister, daughter or cousin of Sigurd the Dane </wiki/Sigurd_the_Dane>, Earl of Northumbria </wiki/Earl_of_Northumbria>, appears to be equally unreliable. An earlier source, a varian
1 CONC t of the Chronicle of the Kings of Alba </wiki/Chronicle_of_the_Kings_of_Alba> (CK-I), gives Duncan's wife the Gaelic </wiki/Scottish_Gaelic_language> name Suthen.[6] Whatever his wife's name may have been, Duncan had at least two
1 CONC  sons. The eldest, Malcolm III </wiki/Malcolm_III_of_Scotland> (Máel Coluim mac Donnchada) was king from 1057 to 1093, the second Donald III </wiki/Donald_III_of_Scotland> (Domnall Bán, or "Donalbane") was king afterwards. Máel Mu
1 CONC ire, Earl of Atholl </wiki/M%C3%A1el_Muire,_Earl_of_Atholl> is a possible third son of Duncan, although this is uncertain.[7]
1 CONT The early period of Duncan's reign was apparently uneventful, perhaps a consequence of his youth. Macbeth </wiki/Macbeth_of_Scotland> (Mac Bethad mac Findláich) is recorded as his dux, literally duke </wiki/Duke>, but in the conte
1 CONC xt - "dukes of Francia" had half a century before replaced the Carolingian </wiki/Carolingian> kings of the Franks and in England the over-mighty Godwin of Wessex </wiki/Godwin_of_Wessex> was called a dux - this suggests that Macb
1 CONC eth was the power behind the throne </wiki/Power_behind_the_throne>.[8]
1 CONT In 1039, Duncan led a large Scots army south to besiege Durham </wiki/Durham>, but the expedition ended in disaster. Duncan survived, but the following year he led an army north into Moray </wiki/Mormaerdom_of_Moray>, traditionall
1 CONC y seen as Macbeth's domain. There he was killed, at Pitgaveny near Elgin </wiki/Elgin,_Moray>, by his own men led by Macbeth, probably on 14 August </wiki/August_14> 1040 </wiki/1040>.[9]
