0 @N6000000002319315216@ NOTE http://sunsite.berkeley.edu/OMACL/Heimskringla/halfdan.html   ??
1 CONT
1 CONT Halfdan the Black
1 CONT
1 CONT Halfdan Svarte is known as Halfdan the Black in   English. He was a
1 CONT king of many territories prior to  the foundation of Norway, but he is
1 CONT perhaps better  known for being the father of the famous first king  of
1 CONT all Norway, King Harald Hsrfagre (Harold   Fairhair). Is it true that
1 CONT Halfdan Svarte really existed, or is he nothing   but wishful thinking
1 CONT by the Norwegians and the authors of the Norse   sagas? And what do we
1 CONT really know about him?
1 CONT
1 CONT  Not nearly enough I'm afraid! We don't even know if he did exist, but
1 CONT it is
1 CONT  very likely that he did. The different sagas that exist are about the
1 CONT only
1 CONT  sources we have, except for this huge burial mound sited at the farm
1 CONT Stein
1 CONT  in Hole then. However this mound need to be excavated if we are to get
1 CONT  any further information about his existence. In fact we need to
1 CONT excavate to
1 CONT  even extend the history of this nation, because a important part of it
1 CONT ends at
1 CONT   the very top of this huge burial mound.
1 CONT
1 CONT So far is Halfdan Svarte nothing but mythology. But this may change in
1 CONT the
1 CONT   near future, because archaeologists will start on a preliminary
1 CONT excavation of
1 CONT   the mound at Stein during the fall of 1997. Perhaps this eventually
1 CONT will lead
1 CONT  to a full excavation and so we will finally be able to decide if the
1 CONT sagas told
1 CONT us the truth or not. In the mean time we will have to stick to theories.
0 @N6000000002187913513@ NOTE Halfdan Hvitbeinn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Halfdan Hvitbeinn (Old Norse: Hálfdan hvítbeinn) was the son of Olof Trätälja of the House of Yngling according to Heimskringla. He lived around A.D. 710.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father was sacrificed to Odin by the Swedish settlers in Värmland because of a famine. Some Swedes, however, realised that the famine was brought by overpopulation and not by the fact that the king had been neglecting his reli
1 CONC gious duties.
1 CONT
1 CONT Consequently, they resolved to cross the Ed Forest and settle in Norway and happened to end up in Soleyar where they killed king Sölve and took Halfdan prisoner. The Swedish expatriates elected Halfdan king as he was the son of th
1 CONC eir old king, Olof. Halfdan subjugated all of Soleyar and took his army into Romerike and subjugated that province as well.
1 CONT
1 CONT Halfdan was to become a great king, who married Åsa, the daughter of king Eystein, the ruler of Oppland and Hedmark. They had two sons, Öystein Halfdansson and Gudröd.
1 CONT
1 CONT Halfdan conquered a large part of Hedemark, Toten, Hadeland and a part of Vestfold. When his brother Ingjald Olofsson died, he inherited Wermelandia. Halfdan died of old age in Toten and was transported to Vestfold where he was bu
1 CONC ried under a mound in Skiringssal.
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halfdan_Hvitbeinn"
1 CONT
1 CONT Categories: Värmland | Viking Age
0 @N6000000003051217121@ NOTE Eystein, we are told, was succeeded by his son Halfdan the Generous and the Stingy of Food. Halfdan gained this title by paying his men generously in coin but poorly in food. He died of a malady, was duly buried in a mound beside 
1 CONC his father Eystein, and was succeeded by his son Gudrod. Gudrod is considered to be a historical personage, although the tales that are told about him are no doubt at least partly legendary.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Wikipedia, "Halfdan the Old", retrieved 20 Jul 08]
1 CONT Halfdan the Old of Gór's lineage
1 CONT The Orkneying saga does not speak at all of Nór's descendants, but introduces instead a figure named Halfdan the Old as the son of Sveidi (Sveiði) the Sea-king, who is called Svadi (Svaði) in the Ættartolur. Sveidi/Svadi in both t
1 CONC exts was son of Heiti, son of Gór who was Nór's brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT This second Halfdan the Old is father of Jarl Ívar of the Uplands who married a daughter of a certain Eistein and so became father of Eystein the Clatterer (Eysteinn Glumra) who was father of Jarl Rögnvald of Møre and of Rögnvald'
1 CONC s brother Sigurd, and also of two daughters: Svanhild who was one of King Harald Fairhair's wives and another daughter named Malahule. According to various sources, Jarl Rögnvald had three illegitimate sons: Hallad (Hallaðr), Hrol
1 CONC laug (Hrollaugr), and Torf-Einarr. Later, by his wife Ragnhild (Ragnhildr) daughter of Hrólf Nose (Hrólf Nefja), Rögnvald was father of three legitimate sons: Hrólf, Ívar, and Thórir (Þórir) the Silent. Hrólf, also called Ganger-H
1 CONC rólf (Gongu-Hrólfr 'Hrólf the walker'), Icelandic/Norwegian historians identify him as the Rollo who conquered Neustria which was then renamed as Normandy, but that identification seems very doubtful. Thórir inherited his father's
1 CONC  lands. The first four Jarls of Orkney were successively Rögnvald's brother Sigurd, Sigurd's son Guthorm (Guttormr), Rögnvald's son Hallad, and Rögnvald's son Turf-Einar. From Turf-Einar the later Jarls descended. Hrollaug and his
1 CONC  wife and sons settled in Iceland.
0 @N6000000002244367164@ NOTE http://sunsite.berkeley.edu/OMACL/Heimskringla/halfdan.html   ??
1 CONT
1 CONT Halfdan the Black
1 CONT
1 CONT Halfdan Svarte is known as Halfdan the Black in   English. He was a
1 CONT king of many territories prior to  the foundation of Norway, but he is
1 CONT perhaps better  known for being the father of the famous first king  of
1 CONT all Norway, King Harald Hsrfagre (Harold   Fairhair). Is it true that
1 CONT Halfdan Svarte really existed, or is he nothing   but wishful thinking
1 CONT by the Norwegians and the authors of the Norse   sagas? And what do we
1 CONT really know about him?
1 CONT
1 CONT  Not nearly enough I'm afraid! We don't even know if he did exist, but
1 CONT it is
1 CONT  very likely that he did. The different sagas that exist are about the
1 CONT only
1 CONT  sources we have, except for this huge burial mound sited at the farm
1 CONT Stein
1 CONT  in Hole then. However this mound need to be excavated if we are to get
1 CONT  any further information about his existence. In fact we need to
1 CONT excavate to
1 CONT  even extend the history of this nation, because a important part of it
1 CONT ends at
1 CONT   the very top of this huge burial mound.
1 CONT
1 CONT So far is Halfdan Svarte nothing but mythology. But this may change in
1 CONT the
1 CONT   near future, because archaeologists will start on a preliminary
1 CONT excavation of
1 CONT   the mound at Stein during the fall of 1997. Perhaps this eventually
1 CONT will lead
1 CONT  to a full excavation and so we will finally be able to decide if the
1 CONT sagas told
1 CONT us the truth or not. In the mean time we will have to stick to theories.
